HT_DATA.load("detail", {"FITJMJjASUs":{"wy":"HN recommends it repeatedly as one of the best and most entertaining programming talks, and as the talk that makes the Y combinator click, even for people who do not use Ruby.","q":{"x":"perhaps the most entertaining and interesting technical talk I've ever seen.","i":10562564,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":47747123,"author":"peterlk","month":"2026-04","story":"All elementary functions from a single binary operator","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Derivation of the Y combinator from Ruby lambdas","quote":"Reminds me a bit of the coolest talk I ever got to see in person","text":"Reminds me a bit of the coolest talk I ever got to see in person: [VIDEO] It’s a derivation of the Y combinator from ruby lambdas"},{"id":43637738,"author":"sva_","month":"2025-04","story":"Baking the Y Combinator from Scratch","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Humorous one-hour explanation of the Y combinator, language-agnostic","quote":"Great watch even though I never coded a line of Ruby.","text":"If anyone wants this in a humorous 1 hour format, watch the legendary Jim Weirich (rip) explain it here: [VIDEO] Don't be discouraged that it is in Ruby as the concepts are completely general. Great watch even though I never coded a line of Ruby."},{"id":24602476,"author":"caseyohara","month":"2020-09","story":"All you need is λ, part one: booleans","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic talk on lambda calculus and the Y combinator","quote":"on lambda calculus and the (little 'c') Y combinator is fantastic.","text":"On the same topic, Jim Weirich's 2012 talk \"Y Not - Adventures in Functional Programming\" on lambda calculus and the (little 'c') Y combinator is fantastic. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17169201,"author":"dmvaldman","month":"2018-05","story":"Y Not? – Adventures in Functional Programming (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Same lecture with slides alongside the talk","quote":"Still one of the best programming talks I've ever seen.","text":"This video (similar lecture, different audience) has the slides alongside the talk: [VIDEO] Still one of the best programming talks I've ever seen."},{"id":16840747,"author":"webdevatlurk","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of two talks that blew the commenter's mind","quote":"The first two talks that really blew my mind are","text":"Finally made an account to post in this thread. I'm rather early in my career doing mostly Ruby, Python and JavaScript things. As you might expect, I consume mainly Ruby, Python and JavaScript related talks. The first two talks that really blew my mind are: - K Lars Lohn's PyCon Keynote from 2016: [LINK] - Jim Weirich's (RIP) \"Y Not\" talk from RubyConf 2012: [VIDEO] There are many other presenters who I have a good opinion of: - Raymond Hettinger: his presentation/teaching style is something I'd like to model my own after, also he gave the first talk on writing proper threaded/concurrent python that I was able to understand and make use of. - Brandon Rhoades: another speaker with a presentation style that I've found easy to follow, also he takes a little shot at the dd utility about 18 minutes into [LINK] ) - Sandi Metz: I started out in Python land and moved to Ruby land, where I was introduced to Sandi's talks. She doesn't talk about incredibly complex topics, but she's got insight into some really basic things that's helpful to new people who can't see the forest through the trees. - Robert Martin: I gather that his OO principles are not universally revered, but I find his talks useful. - Gary Bernhardt: his talks are interesting and entertaining in ways that most are not The list goes on, but I can't think of them all right now."},{"id":13203122,"author":"JamilD","month":"2016-12","story":"What is a Y Combinator?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Incredible talk and demonstration on the Y Combinator","quote":"Jim Wierich gave an incredible talk on the Y Combinator at RubyConf 2012.","text":"For those (like me) that prefer learning through talks and demonstrations, Jim Wierich gave an incredible talk on the Y Combinator at RubyConf 2012. Video: [VIDEO] Code: [LINK]"},{"id":10562564,"author":"careersuicide","month":"2015-11","story":"Writing code doesn't mix well with oration","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining live-coded derivation that makes the topic approachable","quote":"perhaps the most entertaining and interesting technical talk I've ever seen.","text":"Jim Weirich's \"Y Not - Adventures in Functional Programming\"[1] is perhaps the most entertaining and interesting technical talk I've ever seen. It would absolutely not be as cool or as fun without the live coding. Additionally, the incremental process by which he arrives at the conclusion makes the topic much more approachable. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7270423,"author":"VeejayRampay","month":"2014-02","story":"So very sad to hear about the passing of Ruby legend Jim Weirich","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Showcases Jim Weirich's craft as a teacher","quote":"This talk is a must-see, really shows off Jim Weirich's craft and overall ability to be a great pedagogue.","text":"[VIDEO] This talk is a must-see, really shows off Jim Weirich's craft and overall ability to be a great pedagogue."},{"id":6710858,"author":"mekishizufu","month":"2013-11","story":"What is a y-combinator? (2008)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Walks through the Y combinator and functional programming","quote":"If you are curious about the topic, I strongly recommend watching these videos","text":"If you are curious about the topic, I strongly recommend watching these videos: Y Not- Adventures in Functional Programming by Jim Weirich [VIDEO] Programming with Nothing by Tom Stuart [LINK]"},{"id":33414257,"author":"jiripospisil","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: What piece of code/codebase blew your mind when you saw it?","stance":"rec","why":"Functional programming presentation that blew the commenter's mind","quote":"I remember my mind was blown a few times while watching Jim Weirich's presentation on functional programming","text":"Well, .NET's garbage collector certainly springs to mind. Not exactly in a good way. You know you have something special on your hands when GitHub refuses to show you a highlighted version of a hand written C++ file ( [LINK] ). Other than that, I remember my mind was blown a few times while watching Jim Weirich's presentation on functional programming all those years ago ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":32637046,"author":"sva_","month":"2022-08","story":"An intriguing discovery of Computer Science: The Y combinator [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Derives the Y combinator from first principles in a way that clicks","quote":"A good video that made it click for me is the following","text":"A good video that made it click for me is the following, which derives the ycombinator from 1st principles: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18218671,"author":"dwrodri","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Live demo deriving the Y Combinator from first principles","quote":"the speaker derives the Y Combinator from first principles","text":"Here is a good one that come to mind: 1. This one is alive demo where the speaker derives the Y Combinator from first principles. A really interesting exposition of functional programming at its finest. Link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17468012,"author":"dnsco","month":"2018-07","story":"Deriving the Y Combinator in JavaScript","stance":"rec","why":"Great introduction to the Y Combinator and lambda calculus","quote":"is also a great introduction to the Y Combinator and Lambda Calculus.","text":"Jim Weirich's 2012 Ruby Conf talk is also a great introduction to the Y Combinator and Lambda Calculus. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12637633,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite tech talk on functional programming","quote":"Y Not - Adventures in Functional Programming by Jim Weirich","text":"Y Not - Adventures in Functional Programming by Jim Weirich [VIDEO] The Coming Civil War over General Purpose Computing by Cory Doctorow [LINK] Cybersecurity as Realpolitik by Dan Geer [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":8378399,"author":"HCIdivision17","month":"2014-09","story":"Excitement Over Gravity Waves Comes Crashing Down","stance":"rec","why":"Bootstraps computation from lambda calculus via the Y combinator","quote":"one of my favorite videos - I still don't grok it, but I'm getting closer!","text":"I mean, sure, it's going to be turtles the whole way down if you're looking to define the universe without resorting to physical quantities (that is, dimensioned units). We don't have anything like a Grand Unified Theory yet, wherein we define Physics by means of some elemental mathematics (in the form that we can bootstrap computation from lambda calculus via things like the Y Combinator [0]). Besides, we occasionally give dimensional names to dimensionless quantities (my favorite being the engineering unit the inverse radian) [1]. It's a useful abstraction, and it seems confusing to complain about it. So we resort to what we know, which is what we see. We can't see more than the surrounding universe, but we can try extrapolating from what seems to work. Attempting to stifle that inquiry seems a bit... I dunno, pointlessly antagonistic. This article shows science in action, doing what it does best: taking observations and reconciling them with other observations via theory, and then fixing theory to match. It seems our universe follows certain equations which are themselves dimensionful, so I'm not sure why we wouldn't attempt to benchmark the universe against itself - we don't have much else to use. Even dimensionless numbers are only marginally useful, like alpha; it may \"exist\" and manifest as our universe, but it's not especially predictive. [0] [VIDEO] (one of my favorite videos - I still don't grok it, but I'm getting closer!) [1] [LINK]"},{"id":8192575,"author":"friendzis","month":"2014-08","story":"Tech Talks","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent talk on functional programming and lambda calculus","quote":"an excellent talk about functional programming and lambda calculus","text":"Since it is not readme based, I suggest including Y-Not[1] - an excellent talk about functional programming and lambda calculus [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6967840,"author":"al2o3cr","month":"2013-12","story":"Emacs users are like Terry Pratchett’s Igors","stance":"rec","why":"Derives the Y combinator in Ruby","quote":"Here's a related talk about deriving the Y combinator in Ruby by Jim Weirich","text":"+1 on the lambda calculus stuff. Here's a related talk about deriving the Y combinator in Ruby by Jim Weirich: [VIDEO] (skip to about 28:00 if you're impatient) There are additional versions of this same presentation circulating with the code in Javascript and (I believe) Clojure. Full disclosure: Jim is a co-worker of mine."},{"id":5005803,"author":"tesmar2","month":"2013-01","story":"Three Little Birds – Deriving the Y Combinator","stance":"rec","why":"Live derivation of the Y combinator in pure Ruby","quote":"it was something amazing to watch happen live.","text":"Jim Weirich derives the Y combinator in pure Ruby here: [LINK] EDIT: Correct video [VIDEO] It is some of the ugliest ruby code I have ever seen, but it was something amazing to watch happen live."},{"id":10449897,"author":"HCIdivision17","month":"2015-10","story":"An Interactive Guide to Ambiguous Grammar","stance":"reference","why":"Shows self-reference being worked out of a function step by step","quote":"a talk that slowly warped and wrapped a statement to build up the Y-combinator","text":"Reading this, I was struck by how it reminded me of a talk that slowly warped and wrapped a statement to build up the Y-combinator. The process takes a self-referential function and slowly works the self out of it. Honestly, I'm not at all sure turning a function anonymous is actually similar to making a sentence passive, but maybe re-watching that video now I'll have that je ne sais quoi that'll let me actually grok the process. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7273936,"author":"e12e","month":"2014-02","story":"Y Not – Adventures in Functional Programming (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Confreaks.com seems to be having some troubles, I'm guessing this is the same talk","text":"Confreaks.com seems to be having some troubles, I'm guessing this is the same talk: Ruby Conf 12 - Y Not- Adventures in Functional Programming by Jim Weirich (uploaded by Confreaks): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"QmOF0crdyRU":{"wy":"HN recommends it repeatedly to people struggling with motivation, distraction or phone and internet addiction, as a science-based guide to dopamine.","q":{"x":"I'm obsessed with this podcast by Andrew Huberman (Stanford Neuroscientist) because he's talking about a scientific basis for stuff that has \"felt\" true to me for a while.","i":33398498,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":38997576,"author":"sebmellen","month":"2024-01","story":"Could inflammation be the cause of myriad chronic conditions? (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear, nuanced public explanation of dopamine","quote":"this is a very clear take on dopamine","text":"I don't understand this comment at all. Huberman, to me, has provided the most nuanced understanding of dopamine and dopaminergic effects accessible to the broader public. There's a lot of clickbait around him, and no doubt many \"influencer doctors\" who make a bad name for themselves, but this is a very clear take on dopamine: [VIDEO] . I wouldn't call it babble. (Huberman is also not a physician, but a PhD, which is an important distinction.)"},{"id":33398498,"author":"waprin","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: How did you find joy in life?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Scientific basis for baseline dopamine and motivation","quote":"I'm obsessed with this podcast by Andrew Huberman (Stanford Neuroscientist) because he's talking about a scientific basis for stuff that has \"felt\" true to me for a while.","text":"This is an area I'm pretty passionate about. So first of all, it's pretty clear that you _do_ have some interests. You mention Feynman and Kazantzakis. That sounds like you're interested in the history of science and literature. That sounds pretty cool. Many people will recommend going to a professional which I won't try to dissuade you from but that's not mutually exclusive with examining your lifestyle. I'm obsessed with this podcast by Andrew Huberman (Stanford Neuroscientist) because he's talking about a scientific basis for stuff that has \"felt\" true to me for a while. Your food diet affects how you feel but your information diet does massively as well. There is a neurochemical basis for this with your baseline dopamine levels, that sets your motivation. [VIDEO] I also recommend reading the book Dopamine Nation. Too much social media will fry your brain. If you're checking Hacker News more than a handful times of day, work on doing less of that. If you're addicted to Reddit, Twitter, or pornography, work on doing less of that. Exercise. Spend time in the outdoors. Focus hard on sleep hygiene. Eat less sugar and processed foods. You can not change all this stuff overnight but if you can take baby steps it will help massively. You will not feel better tomorrow or next week but if you take tiny steps you will feel a tiny bit better in a few months. I would also highly recommend learning about mindfulness, meditation, and yoga. A lot of dissatisfaction is aversion to a feeli"},{"id":32227676,"author":"asauce","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: I'm in a rut. How did you get out of yours?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Huberman podcast explaining how dopamine drives motivation and focus","quote":"I really recommend everyone to listen to Andrew Huberman's podcast [0] on Dopamine and how it affects motivation, focus, and satisfaction.","text":"I really recommend everyone to listen to Andrew Huberman's podcast [0] on Dopamine and how it affects motivation, focus, and satisfaction. Many people see Dopamine as just a pleasure chemical, but it actually directly influences motivation and focus. We also all have a somewhat set amount of dopamine in a day, so participating in activities that release a short peak of dopamine (drugs, masturbation, etc) means that we have less dopamine, and therefore less motivation for the rest of the day. I was also recently in a low motivation rut, however after making some lifestyle changes I feel much more motivated throughout the day. I stopped getting high, cut out all porn, and added 2 min of a cold water at the end of my regular shower. It was tough to start, but 100% worth it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29297795,"author":"Spinnaker_","month":"2021-11","story":"Dopamine Fasting: A Maladaptive Fad (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long Huberman guide to dopamine baselines and spikes","quote":"If you have 2 hours there is no better guide than Andrew Huberman","text":"The author is attacking a position that doesn't really exist. You obviously don't fast from dopamine in general. You fast from dopamine peaks. Continuous spiking of dopamine causes your baseline levels to drop, and you feel shitty and unmotivated. A dopamine fast (of the spikes) is intended to let the levels rise back. If you have 2 hours there is no better guide than Andrew Huberman: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39460290,"author":"StuffMaster","month":"2024-02","story":"Stop postponing things by embracing the mess","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman episodes on dopamine and focus","quote":"+1 for Andrew Huberman Controlling Your Dopamine For Motivation, Focus & Satisfaction","text":"+1 for Andrew Huberman Controlling Your Dopamine For Motivation, Focus & Satisfaction: [VIDEO] ADHD & How Anyone Can Improve Their Focus: [LINK]"},{"id":37414397,"author":"xwowsersx","month":"2023-09","story":"Cal Newport: Overstimulation Is Ruining Your Life","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman podcast on dopamine, motivation and focus","quote":"An insightful podcast from Andrew Huberman","text":"An insightful podcast from Andrew Huberman: \"Controlling Your Dopamine For Motivation, Focus & Satisfaction | Huberman Lab Podcast #39\" — [VIDEO] And a clip: \"Andrew Huberman - What Overusing Social Media Does To Your Brain\" — [LINK]"},{"id":32357955,"author":"8vectors","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Solution for Distraction?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the science of dopamine spikes and reducing stacked pleasures","quote":"This video explains the science behind it.","text":"It would probably help if you reduced dopamine spikes and made them more intermittent (less regular). If you stack multiple pleasures together it could be making it harder to focus. (Like drinking + watching something you enjoy + snacks, or smoking weed + playing video games). [VIDEO] This video explains the science behind it. Make your dopamine spikes more random and intermittent and try to reduce the peak on them by sometimes enjoying your pleasures separately instead of stacking them all at the same time."},{"id":31288310,"author":"danenania","month":"2022-05","story":"Why do you waste so much time on the internet?","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth Huberman episode on dopamine, stimulation and habit change","quote":"Andrew Huberman goes into this stuff in depth in his podcast, especially this episode","text":"The positive side of plasticity is that your brain can also re-adapt fairly quickly to a lower (healthier) level of stimulation if you can get over the initial hump. My understanding is that most of this is regulated by dopamine, and your dopamine system needs time to reset in order to adapt to new patterns. In the meantime, it's normal to feel bored or lacking in motivation; that's part of the reset process and you just need to wait it out. Personally, I've found it's easiest to change habits and patterns via some sort of healthy distraction that takes my mind off whatever I'm trying to change. Travel, getting into a new hobby/project, or starting a really good book are all great for this. Andrew Huberman goes into this stuff in depth in his podcast, especially this episode: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29298629,"author":"simple10","month":"2021-11","story":"Dopamine Fasting: A Maladaptive Fad (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman on using dopamine in pursuit of goals and the post-goal come down","quote":"Andrew Huberman from Stanford has an excellent podcast [2] on dopamine.","text":"Has anyone here tried 24 hours isolation [1] to reset perception of time? It's related to dopamine fasts but reportedly more profound. Andrew Huberman from Stanford has an excellent podcast [2] on dopamine. He goes in depth on the importance of using dopamine in the pursuit of goals with a caution around the come down period after achieving goals. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28781005,"author":"slothtrop","month":"2021-10","story":"Facebook is an addiction treadmill most may never be able to quit","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman videos on dopamine and addiction","quote":"I've been enjoying Huberman's videos on the topic of dopamine and other things","text":"Also mine. Have gone long stretches between relapses during which time I've felt noticeably better. What typically happens is I'll rationalize its use after a spell, but will find I'll want to watch the next day, and the next. Particularly if sleep suffers, just to get some deliverance. From what I've learned, large sustained dopamine hits lead to deficits in baseline levels. I could intuit early on that some of my negative experience (including sleep) was linked to consumption, but managed to convince myself each time that I \"just need to better manage it\". Even if the truth stares you in the face it's hard. I like to think of it as a \"mild\" addiction owing to frequency (it wasn't always) but still an addiction, which is like playing with fire. It can always get worse. I've been enjoying Huberman's videos on the topic of dopamine and other things - [VIDEO]"},{"id":28850679,"author":"kashyapc","month":"2021-10","story":"Dopamine, Smartphones and You: A battle for your time","stance":"rec","why":"Two-hour Huberman episode on dopamine, with a segment on smartphones","quote":"The segment on smartphones and how they alter our dopamine circuits starts at 01:15:28","text":"On understanding dopamine, FWIW, I recommend two excellent resources: one is Robert Sapolsky's 720-page tome, Behave —he gives a robust intro to dopamine in the second chapter, \"One Second Before\", and expands on it further in the book. The other is prof. Andrew Huberman's podcast. He recently did a 2-hour episode[1] on dopamine. The segment on smartphones and how they alter our dopamine circuits starts at 01:15:28[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28697008,"author":"mickelsen","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: Have you ever spend days unable to do *any* work?","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman episode on dopamine peaks and maintenance, useful for work paralysis","quote":"Check out the latest episode from Huberman Labs regarding dopamine peaks and maintenance","text":"Check out the latest episode from Huberman Labs regarding dopamine peaks and maintenance: [VIDEO] Also an earlier one about addiction, good tips to apply here or similar situations. And it's OK to take some time off and switch to other activities, while your brain replenishes your desire to work on the background. I did, for a couple of months. I was actually in the same spot as you and the guy from another post ( [LINK] ), even thought I was getting some sort of early dementia, as my memory was also affected. The unable to start or keeping are also signs of burnout and ADHD. You could try asking others in your team to give you some extra hand-holding, motivation or accountability. Some bumps in the road are solvable with a bit of encouragement, but it's hard to open up given that delivering what they ask from you is the expected thing. Sometimes even when you want to proactively tackle on these matters, it's a process that takes a while. That rejection you feel right now is totally common, and it's could be a sign that you should stop trying for now, it doesn't mean that you won't like it / be able to do it in the future, just a protection mechanism from your body overreacting."},{"id":33762831,"author":"throwaway0asd","month":"2022-11","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in an explanation of dopamine baselines and motivation","quote":"","text":"Motivation and motivated people are products of certain behaviors. * The primary driver of motivation in animal brains (even in small worms) is a healthy dopamine cycle. In the brain there exists a dopamine baseline by which regular drive and motivation occur. There are also dopamine spikes when a pleasurable moment occurs. A dopamine spike is always followed by a dopamine crash and that dopamine crash will always occur in the same ratio below baseline that the spike occurred above baseline. Driven people are able to maintain a near constant high baseline and recognize their depressive spikes with healthy and positive responses. Drug use, except for caffeine, almost always damages and/or destroys either the dopamine cycle or permanent dopamine production. A primary motivator behind the consumption of drugs, particularly combinations of drugs, is chasing a dopamine high that has already left the brain. * Openness is one of the big 5 of personality indicators. Openness is the willingness to try new experiences and creativity falls under openness. Openness is not in any way correlated with extroversion. These are not necessarily alpha personalities, as in their goal is probably not to compete but rather to be productive and industrious and probably original. This is an important distinction because alpha personalities qualify their performance in comparison to others while industrious and/or creative people quality their performance in response to their output even when there ar"},{"id":32383529,"author":"snthpy","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: How do you manage to do FOSS personal projects?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as background for dopamine and motivation","quote":"","text":"Interesting. May be a correlation rather than causation thing. For example if your dopamine levels are elevated for some unrelated reason then you may be experiencing more motivation hence the work on your FOSS projects and you may also experience increased sex drive at the same time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31434323,"author":"hericium","month":"2022-05","story":"I'm an addict","stance":"reference","why":"Huberman episode on controlling dopamine","quote":"","text":"Also from Huberman: \"Controlling Your Dopamine For Motivation, Focus & Satisfaction\" [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29603944,"author":"joshink1","month":"2021-12","story":"Internet addiction and the habit of book reading","stance":"reference","why":"Huberman on dopamine budgeting and information diet for focus","quote":"Dr. Huberman claims that he has treated this with dopamine budgeting, information diet, etc","text":"Dopamine exhaustion from over-stimulation may be a reason for the common “can’t focus” complaints. Dr. Huberman claims that he has treated this with dopamine budgeting, information diet, etc…: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AKGrmY8OSHM",1],["hFL6qRIJZ_Y",1],["I8Xc2_FtpHI",1],["ZXJWO2FQ16c",1],["3BA5bw1EV5I",1],["5BXtgq0Nhsc",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["4-oV42OMQoE",1],["ORp3q1Oaezw",1],["nm1TxQj9IsQ",1],["ZRV3pBuPxEQ",1]]},"eG0KrT6pBPk":{"wy":"Cited repeatedly to rebut 'just don't use Facebook' arguments and to explain why tracking becomes hard to escape at critical mass.","q":{"x":"I would highly encourage anyone who thinks that the solution is as simple as \"don't use XYZ\" to see what Moxie Marlinspike had to say","i":6795569,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":20088926,"author":"CodeMage","month":"2019-06","story":"Open Up vs. Break Up","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how technology reshapes society so declining to participate has costs","quote":"I would definitely recommend watching it in full, but the key takeaway from it","text":"> I think part of the difference in HN opinion color is somewhat related to transparency. It's not that I disagree with you on the topic of transparency, it's that I think we can't really compare opinions on two very different topics as if they were the same topic. People complaining about too many subscription content providers are not really arguing for or against a monopoly. It's not the same discussion. > And I'd say Google and Facebook haven't done a sufficient job in proactively informing the public about what they collect, how they generate revenue from it, and what's currently stored about a user. Granted, but I'm pretty sure that even if they did so, it wouldn't make much difference. I always refer people to Moxie Marlinspike's DEF CON keynote called \"Changing threats to privacy\" [1]. I would definitely recommend watching it in full, but the key takeaway from it -- for the purposes of this discussion -- is that it all boils down to choice. He talks about the way technology changes the fabric of the society, how it changes the way people interact, communicate and coordinate, and declining to participate in this new way of doing things exposes you to the \"no network effect\". This is why it's not enough for Google and Facebook to merely inform people about what they collect and how they use it. As long as they're practically monopolies, they can disclose and inform and then go on just the same, because there's no other viable alternative and they'll work hard to ensure "},{"id":6795569,"author":"CodeMage","month":"2013-11","story":"Surveillance as a Business Model","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why simply avoiding a service is not a real privacy solution","quote":"I would highly encourage anyone who thinks that the solution is as simple as \"don't use XYZ\" to see what Moxie Marlinspike had to say","text":"I would highly encourage anyone who thinks that the solution is as simple as \"don't use XYZ\" to see what Moxie Marlinspike had to say about \"Changing threats to privacy\" at Defcon 18: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6158150,"author":"yourcelf","month":"2013-08","story":"The Public-Private Surveillance Partnership","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic talk on public-private surveillance and tracking devices","quote":"Moxie Marlinspike gave a fantastic talk on this subject in 2010","text":"Moxie Marlinspike gave a fantastic talk on this subject in 2010, even down to the government mandated tracking device vs mobile phone: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4869286,"author":"losvedir","month":"2012-12","story":"Steps to Anonymous and Secured Communication","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains OTR's deniability advantage over PGP signatures","quote":"At least, that's what I gleaned from this wonderful video[1]","text":"> Or, maybe, IM with OTR without logging, but that . . . might also fall prey to crazy counterparty either not disabling logging or intentionally recording and blackmailing. I thought this was one of the benefits of OTR over, say, PGP. That is, with PGP if you sign a message the counterparty can wave it around and say \"look at what this poerson said\", while with OTR, since the encryption is done with a shared key, it could be just as likely that the counterparty made it up. At least, that's what I gleaned from this wonderful video[1] someone linked to on HN yesterday. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18948773,"author":"CodeMage","month":"2019-01","story":"Tech Companies Manipulate Our Personal Data","stance":"rec","why":"Rebuts the idea that alternatives to social media make opting out easy","quote":"I suggest watching Moxie Marlinspike's keynote for Defcon 18","text":"> There are plenty of alternatives to social media - email, SMS, phone calls, and even decentralized options. I suggest watching Moxie Marlinspike's keynote for Defcon 18: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12810981,"author":"CodeMage","month":"2016-10","story":"CoinTent: A Sustainable Ad-Free Web","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why simply not participating is not a valid option","quote":"It explains why \"simply don't participate in X\" is not really a valid option.","text":"I would encourage you to watch the Defcon 18 talk by Moxie Marlinspike, called \"Changing threats to privacy\". It explains why \"simply don't participate in X\" is not really a valid option. Here's the link to the talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12589794,"author":"sygma","month":"2016-09","story":"Facebook Ordered to Stop Collecting Data on WhatsApp Users in Germany","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on the social alienation of declining to use dominant services","quote":"Here's a relevant talk [0] by Moxie Marlinspike talking about that.","text":"> Using the messaging service is a voluntary decision Yes, it is voluntary, but it would be socially alienating not to be on that service. Here's a relevant talk [0] by Moxie Marlinspike talking about that. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12362430,"author":"CodeMage","month":"2016-08","story":"“How do I choose not to share my account information with Facebook?”","stance":"rec","why":"Explains better than the commenter why opting out is not a solution","quote":"I could try to explain, but Moxie Marlinspike did it a lot better","text":"There's a reason most people don't like hearing that: it's not a solution. I could try to explain, but Moxie Marlinspike did it a lot better: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6876941,"author":"lucb1e","month":"2013-12","story":"Facebook's AI lab","stance":"rec","why":"Argues network effects remove real choice about using Facebook or a mobile phone","quote":"He talks about how we do not really have a free choice when it comes to things like having a Facebook account or having a mobile phone.","text":"Define: freely I think Moxie Marlinespike's talk at Defcon 18 (2010) was a really good one: [VIDEO] He talks about how we do not really have a free choice when it comes to things like having a Facebook account or having a mobile phone. Namely due to the network effect, you don't really have a choice if you want to be a normal member of society."},{"id":4822165,"author":"jiggy2011","month":"2012-11","story":"I Don't Want To Be Part of Your Ecosystem","stance":"rec","why":"Articulates why tracking becomes hard to escape at critical mass","quote":"It is articulated quite well here","text":"Lots of people don't care about voting in elections, giving to charity, partaking in public demonstrations or looking after their health either. The problem is not so much whether people mind if their location is tracked or whether they have control over their hardware etc. It's more that once these things reach a critical mass it is very difficult to start to go back if we don't like where we are or indeed for an individual to make a choice to live outside of this stuff. It is articulated quite well here: [VIDEO] At around 09:00"},{"id":19006198,"author":"giornogiovanna","month":"2019-01","story":"Mark Zuckerberg Speaks Down to Users and Misses the Point","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for platforms becoming a de facto requirement for society","quote":"","text":"But at some point, it becomes a basic requirement to participate in society, and that gives the people who want out an impossible choice, not by any virtue of the platform, but by the platform hijacking the virtue of society.[0] [0]: [VIDEO] \"Changing threats to privacy\""},{"id":10017630,"author":"nitrogen","month":"2015-08","story":"New Trans-Pacific Partnership Leak Means ‘Significant Overhaul’ for Canadian Law","stance":"reference","why":"Applies the network-effect argument about opting out of technology to culture","quote":"See Moxie Marlinspike's presentation about this concept applied to technology","text":"When effective cultural participation requires keeping up to date with at least some of Hollywood's products, choosing not to view/listen is the same as choosing to be just that much more socially disconnected. See Moxie Marlinspike's presentation about this concept applied to technology: [VIDEO] When one oligopoly owns the vast majority of contemporary popular culture, we, the populace, should be expecting a lot more in return."},{"id":6207406,"author":"acqq","month":"2013-08","story":"Google Blindness","stance":"reference","why":"Moxie Marlinspike's 2010 talk on how data collection arrived at today's surveillance","quote":"How we got here: Moxie Marlinspike, 2010","text":"The point is, Google isn't just \"selling space.\" They collect the data about you, admitting themselves that they prefer to never delete anything. The key word is \"targeting,\" remember? In process, they still know more about you than your mother or partner. Somewhere in their guts, there is a collection of data about you, the depth of it not even imaginable before. How we got here: Moxie Marlinspike, 2010: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5882150,"author":"rasengan0","month":"2013-06","story":"NSA-proof encryption exists. Why doesn’t anyone use it?","stance":"reference","why":"Article echoes the talk's narrative","quote":"the writer of the article almost echoes the exact same narrative","text":"Hmm...the writer of the article almost echoes the exact same narrative of Defcon 18 Changing threats to privacy - Moxie Marlinspike [VIDEO]"},{"id":5266576,"author":"throa8923489","month":"2013-02","story":"I used Google Glass","stance":"reference","why":"Moxie's fascism versus social democracy framing applied to Glass","quote":"We thought we would get facism, instead we got social democracy.","text":"Reminds me of this talk [VIDEO] by moxie. We thought we would get facism, instead we got social democracy. We will be able to deal with this, though, using appropriate laws. Europe will certainly go down that route, the US will probably follow. For example, using the camera to take pictures or videos that are stored or shared (vs. just processed by some augmented reality algorithms) could enable a small lamp on the frame of the glasses. Just like a video recorder, but without the ability to (easily!) turn it off."},{"id":4862950,"author":"spindritf","month":"2012-12","story":"Julian Assange: Cryptographic Call to Arms","stance":"reference","why":"Moxie's point on fascism versus social democracy","quote":"we were preparing for fascism but got social democracy[1].","text":"> The world is not sliding, but galloping into a new transnational dystopia. This development has not been properly recognized outside of national security circles. I agree with Moxie Marlinspike on that, we were preparing for fascism but got social democracy[1]. Assange is still preparing for fascism. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4820357,"author":"krapp","month":"2012-11","story":"Student Suspended for Refusing to Wear a School-Issued RFID Tracker","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on whether a surveillance society could work","quote":"tl;dw -- it does.","text":"wonder if a surveillance society could work if everyone was allowed access to the information. [VIDEO] tl;dw -- it does."}],"r":[["DoeNbZlxfUM",2],["6h3RJhoqgK8",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1]]},"qJEWOTZnFeg":{"wy":"Recommended as the best account of Blender's history and its user-focused approach as a free software success story.","q":{"x":"I highly encourage to watch the whole interview","i":19561821,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":45288774,"author":"solarkraft","month":"2025-09","story":"Ton Roosendaal to step down as Blender chairman and CEO","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Blender as a FOSS success story.","quote":"I loved watching the BlenderGuru interview","text":"Blender is one of those shining FOSS successes we should all learn from. I loved watching the BlenderGuru interview: [VIDEO] Thank you, Ton!"},{"id":45289104,"author":"invader","month":"2025-09","story":"Ton Roosendaal to step down as Blender chairman and CEO","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ton Roosendaal reflects on Blender's journey from Amiga to crowdfunding.","quote":"I would highly recommend","text":"Ton is a legend and a personal hero of mine. He is a great innovator and an open-source superstar who disrupted the entire industry, traditionally dominated by big corporations. The entire Blender story is captivating - from modeler and raytracer prototypes on Amiga to the Internet bubble startup to the famous crowdfunding campaign to free Blender... It is a story that deserves a movie. For anyone interested in open-source history who is not familiar with the Blender story, I would highly recommend Ton's interview with Blender Guru, where he reflects on his journey - [VIDEO]"},{"id":34176265,"author":"solarkraft","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: What was the best software that you used during 2022?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Blender's user-driven approach to software development.","quote":"The interview with BlenderGuru is great one","text":"IIRC Ton Rosendaal, Blender's creator, still leads the project, but while listening very closely to its users and their pains and a very interesting approach to software development. The interview with BlenderGuru is great one: [VIDEO] The last major redesign was started by a vision shared by the interviewer, who seems to be one of the many deeply invested artists. (This is all very much \"IIRC\", based on stuff I've peripherally heard or seen years ago)"},{"id":31421058,"author":"solarkraft","month":"2022-05","story":"Gimp 3 Beta Released","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Blender's iterative development focused on usability for creatives.","quote":"Great interview with Blender Guru","text":"The Blender story is quite an impressive one of iterative development. Ton Rosensaal very explicitly doesn't want technology for its own sake and wants to \"get it out of the door\", but does it all while keeping an eye on it being usable for creatives (now more than before, but the ground work was laid long ago, see everything being animatable) Great interview with Blender Guru: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27085039,"author":"greshario","month":"2021-05","story":"Blender Foundation Annual Report 2020 [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ton Roosendaal says money is only a tool for creating.","quote":"I reccomend watching this interview with him","text":"I reccomend watching this interview with him - at least the parts where he talks about money. It's incredibly refreshing to hear someone at the top of their game in the tech sphere say they're not interested in money except as a tool to help them create. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19561821,"author":"halirutan","month":"2019-04","story":"Why Wolfram Tech Isn’t Open Source","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How Blender became open source rather than abandonware.","quote":"I highly encourage to watch the whole interview","text":"Your last sentence reminded me of an interview with the Ton Rosendaal, the creator of Blender. He talks extensively about how Blender became OSS and not abandonware. I highly encourage to watch the whole interview (you need at least 5 mins to get used to his accent), but the important part starts around minute 14 [VIDEO]"},{"id":24340873,"author":"Lievelingsduif","month":"2020-09","story":"Gimp project gets $68.34 monthly","stance":"rec","why":"Blender's history, including the crowdfunded move to open source.","quote":"interesting interview between Ton Roosendaal and Blender Guru about the history","text":"Blender already has a solid and enthousiastic user base before the 2.7 UI update. Blender was also the forerunner in having a kickstarter-like scheme to raise enough money to make it opensource. There's an interesting interview between Ton Roosendaal and Blender Guru about the history. [VIDEO] The Blender guys were also a lot less hostile towards people trying to make improvements to stuff like UX. One of the things that GIMP also desperately needs is a name change, while Blender is an odd choice it at least doesn't conjur up sexual images."},{"id":20140084,"author":"dorena","month":"2019-06","story":"Blender Is Free Software","stance":"rec","why":"Ton Roosendaal's plans for Blender.","quote":"if you want to know more about him and his plan for Blender watch this interview","text":"I‘m using blender every day and absolutely love it. the fact that it’s run by such a genuine and inspirational guy like Ton makes me like blender even more :) if you want to know more about him and his plan for Blender watch this interview [VIDEO] or the tour of their new office (it’s really funny) [LINK]"},{"id":16663307,"author":"dustinmoorenet","month":"2018-03","story":"Blender’s Prehistory – Traces on Commodore Amiga (1987-1991)","stance":"rec","why":"Long interview explaining Blender's history with Ton Roosendaal.","quote":"The history of Bender was pretty well explained in a long interview with Ton Roosendaal","text":"The history of Bender was pretty well explained in a long interview with Ton Roosendaal by Andrew Price of Blender Guru. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32813808,"author":"Qualadore","month":"2022-09","story":"Ladybird: A new cross-platform browser project","stance":"reference","why":"Blender's approach of prioritizing users over developers.","quote":"approach of heavily prioritizing users over developers","text":"> We should go back to the roots, forget about competing, focus on what's the best not for the end users but on what's fun for the FOSS developers, and bring back the hobby on open source projects. I prefer Blender's approach of heavily prioritizing users over developers [0]. It's been a huge success. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32449913,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-08","story":"Autodesk criticised by 35 named architects (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Ton criticizes Autodesk licenses and champions free software.","quote":"Nobody reads those licenses. Nobody.","text":"Firms like NVidia, AMD, Facebook, Amazon, Unity, Epic Mega Grants, Adobe, Microsoft, Intel, and Apple chucking money at Blender is a hell of a lot better for users and society and education and industry than the featureless monolith that is Autodesk chucking plaster at Ton Roosendaal. ;) [LINK] >... of public interest, we have to act in that sense. I can't simply say, \"Oh, I'm going to support Autodesk from now on\". And then, you can't. You can bring me to court and I will most certainly lose this. Plus, what people don't know maybe, but the Blender code is not owned by the Blender Foundation. [CRASH RUMBLE THUD] What??! That was Autodesk! [VIDEO] >\"Money doesn't interest me\" - Ton Roosendaal interview. Interview with Ton Roosendaal, the man behind the open source 3D software Blender! 46:52: [Q: What do you think of Autodesk?] [VIDEO] >A: They're my best friends! Yah ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha! >[...52:46] Nobody reads those licenses. Nobody. [And this one is quite bad, isn't it?] It's horrible. [Yeah.] They can visit you at home if you use Autodesk, and check your computer, what's on it. [That's a good quote.] >Some people ask me, hey Ton, if I use Blender, the art I make, is that my property, can I do whatever I like with it? Of course! That's open, the Free Software Foundation philosophy, right? We are making free software. Do whatever you do with the content. >Autodesk, on the other hand, they also have their educational versions. In the license it says that everything yo"},{"id":26543477,"author":"alexc05","month":"2021-03","story":"Don't End the Week with Nothing (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> So let me ask ... when you're listening to podcasts, do you ever take anything away from them that is actionable? Do you take notes? Do you summarise? I can answer this a little from my perspective. There are three major inspirations for tech podcasts that I use personally. The first two: [LINK] and [LINK] are both in the format of \"veterans talk to each other about their careers and the things they've learned\". They always roll in the format of two old friends catching up, but in a guided way in terms of their life, experiences and learnings from along the way. These are the ones that I want to emulate, but with a focus on programming for enterprise, business and startups. I'm personally most excited in talking to what I think of very respectfully as \"cogwheel\" or \"workhorse\" developers. I think developers are vastly under-celebrated when compared to other creatives ... like some person who can cry on demand gets a tiny golden statue and applause ... but the person who re-writes 1500 lines of SQL into a one liner and reduces a week long job down to seconds DOESN'T?! You're telling me I have to go out and buy my OWN tiny little golden statue and play an applause loop on my cellphone? That's bullshit! At least that's where I want to start, I've seen some interviews in the last few days with people like Ton Roosendaal - the creator of blender - [VIDEO] After I've developed the interview skills to talk to the people who genuinely make me feel a little like a programming fanboy"},{"id":20499604,"author":"Ralfp","month":"2019-07","story":"Ubisoft joins Blender Development Fund to support open-source animation","stance":"reference","why":"Ton on the small 3D software market and Maya's tiny dev team.","quote":"Ton Roosendaal (Blender creator) estimated in 2018 interview that 3D software market is very small","text":"Ton Roosendaal (Blender creator) estimated in 2018 interview that 3D software market is very small, and pulled up Autodesk's (3DsMax/Maya, etc) business results from that time, which amounted to 25000 - 30000 yearly licenses. He also claimed that Maya has \"maybe 20\" developers behind with things being similar for other products. [VIDEO] (jump to 1h mark)"}],"r":[["y7HrM-fk_Rc",1],["7FeqF1-Z1g0",1],["wxL4ElboiuA",1]]},"wo84LFzx5nI":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for its source-based history of OOP, though some dispute its account of Alan Kay.","q":{"x":"which really is hunting back and back for the cosmogenesis of objects, and covers so much terrain.","i":46042045,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46042045,"author":"jauntywundrkind","month":"2025-11","story":"Fifty Shades of OOP","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hunts for origins of objects and covers much terrain","quote":"which really is hunting back and back for the cosmogenesis of objects, and covers so much terrain.","text":"I'd love a deeper dive on how Objects work in NeXTSTEP. From their brochures, they talk about objects being persistent and distributable, that in a workgroup of people everyone can rely on the objects being up to date. I've always been so curious what the broader technical ecosystem looks like here. Presumably there are still processes running on systems. But these processes have lots of objects in them? And the objects are using Mach message passing to converse with other processes elsewhere? Within an application, are objects communicating across Mach too? There's so much high level rhetoric about. Such as this bit. But I'd love a real technical view at what was happening, what objects really were here. [LINK] [LINK] This is a fun work. It feels like the brief outline for a Speaking for the Dead for OOP. Huge amount of things to lots of different people over time. Seconding @rawgabbit's recommendation for Casey Muratori's The Big OOPs: Anatomy of a Thirty-five-year Mistake, which really is hunting back and back for the cosmogenesis of objects, and covers so much terrain. Objectogenesis? [VIDEO]"},{"id":46044719,"author":"fmjrey","month":"2025-11","story":"Fifty Shades of OOP","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recommended for understanding OOP's conflated aspects","quote":"I can only recommend the big OOPS video","text":"Here is an example of a 2006 rant that qualifies: [LINK] OO conflates many different aspects that are often orthogonal but have been conflated together opportunistically rather than by sound rigor. Clearly most languages allow for functions outside classes. It's clearly the case today especially with FP gaining momentum, but it's also clear back then when Java and the JVM were created. I think smalltalk was the only other language that had this limitation. Like others in this thread, I can only recommend the big OOPS video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48632181,"author":"Rendello","month":"2026-06","story":"Code duplication is far cheaper than the wrong abstraction","stance":"rec","why":"Digs into viewpoints on OOP and how it became dogma","quote":"digs into the different ideas and viewpoints surrounding OOP and how it coalesced into a dogma","text":"That's one of the things Mike was pushing back on (in the context of C++ / game dev; there's a good example ~20 min in). In case you haven't seen it, Casey Muratori's The Big OOPs: Anatomy of a Thirty-five-year Mistake digs into the different ideas and viewpoints surrounding OOP and how it coalesced into a dogma: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48067595,"author":"eska","month":"2026-05","story":"Bjarne Stroustrup: How do I deal with memory leaks?","stance":"rec","why":"History shows C++ design flaws were knowable","quote":"Sounds like you would appreciate this talk","text":"Sounds like you would appreciate this talk: [VIDEO] Personally I use C or something, anything other than C++ really, if I need something more ergonomic or provably correct. Many excuse C++’s design history with “they didn’t know better”, but the oft forgotten history explained in that video shows otherwise."},{"id":46932597,"author":"afiori","month":"2026-02","story":"I Write Games in C (yes, C)","stance":"rec","why":"Pragmatic historical definition of OOP as subtype polymorphism","quote":"I enjoyed the pragmatic historical definition of OOP given in","text":"I enjoyed the pragmatic historical definition of OOP given in [VIDEO] where essential it can be seen as subtype polymorphism"},{"id":46319214,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2025-12","story":"Your job is to deliver code you have proven to work","stance":"rec","why":"Traces where OOP ideas came from and who","quote":"Where the different ideas in OOP came from, how they came together through which programming languages, who was working on what, and when, and why. It's interesting.","text":"> \" Why are we wasting so much time on abstractions like .. objects? \" Aside: earlier this year Casey Muratori did a 2.5 hour conference talk on this topic - why we are using objects in the way they are implemented in C++ et al with class hierarchies and inheritance and objects representing individual entities? \" The Big OOPs: anatomy of a 35 year mistake \"[1]. He traces programming history back to Stroustrup learning from Simula and Kirstan Nygaard, back to C.A.R. Hoare's paper on records, back to the Algol 68 design committee, back to Douglas T. Ross's work in the 1950's. From Ross at MIT in 1960 to Ivan Sutherland working on Sketchpad at MIT in 1963, and both chains influencing Alan Kay and Smalltalk. Where the different ideas in OOP came from, how they came together through which programming languages, who was working on what, and when, and why. It's interesting. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46041897,"author":"rawgabbit","month":"2025-11","story":"Fifty Shades of OOP","stance":"rec","why":"Traces OOP history to original documents","quote":"Muratori traced the history of OOP to the original documents.","text":"Muratori traced the history of OOP to the original documents. Skip to the 1:18 mark if you want to skip to his findings. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45326580,"author":"OhMeadhbh","month":"2025-09","story":"How to stop functional programming (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview for those without a CS background","quote":"it's a good overview for people without a computer science background.","text":"This is the video that's been going around. It's a bit long and could have been compacted into a 20 minute video, but if you have the time, it's a good overview for people without a computer science background. \"Casey Muratori – The Big OOPs: Anatomy of a Thirty-five-year Mistake – BSC 2025\" [VIDEO] But if your point is there aren't any written articles about stuff like this, I agree. If they're out there, they're a bit outside the mainstream."},{"id":45049401,"author":"hiker","month":"2025-08","story":"Object-oriented design patterns in C and kernel development","stance":"rec","why":"History of OOP","quote":"You might also want to watch Casey's great talk on the history of OOP","text":"Plexes are first mentioned in 1960 [LINK] and the paper even starts with a critique of the efficiency of Lisp's approach for representing data with cons pairs (citing McCarthy's paper from the same year). You might also want to watch Casey's great talk on the history of OOP [VIDEO]"},{"id":44600453,"author":"abetusk","month":"2025-07","story":"The Big Oops: Anatomy of a Thirty-Five-Year Mistake [video]","stance":"rec","why":"History of OOP and early ideas behind ECS","quote":"I found this talk to be great.","text":"I found this talk to be great. It goes through the history of OOP and how some of the ideas for the more modern ECS were embedded in the culture at the formation of OOP in the 1960s to 1980s but somehow weren't adopted. It was pretty clear, even 20 years ago, that OOP had major problems in terms of what Casey Muratori now calls \"hierarchical encapsulation\" of problems. One thing that really jumped out at me was his quote [0]: > I think when you're designing new things, you should focus on the hardest stuff. ... we can always then take that and scale it down ... but it's almost impossible to take something that solves simple problems and scale it up into something that solves hard [problems] I understand the context but this, in general, is abysmally bad advice. I'm not sure about language design or system architecture but this is almost universally not true for any mathematical or algorithmic pursuit. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44628854,"author":"ndiddy","month":"2025-07","story":"Java was not underhyped in 1997 (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"History of OOP back to the 1960s debunking the myth","quote":"There's a really good talk about the history of OOP going back to the early 60s","text":"There's a really good talk about the history of OOP going back to the early 60s that goes over this point. The whole \"90s style OOP isn't OOP as it was originally intended\" thing is largely a myth. When Kay and other OOP pioneers were describing Smalltalk and OOP to professional developers, they all used 90s Java style \"compile-time hierarchy of encapsulation that matches the domain model\" OOP to explain it. Smalltalk-80: The Language , a book by some of the original designers of Smalltalk, even uses the same shape class inheritance example you'll see in every introductory Java textbook. Here's a link to the talk, timestamped to when he goes over this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48107132,"author":"theteapot","month":"2026-05","story":"Learning Software Architecture","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"but then I watched this [2] and now I'm in crisis","text":"Nurse Practitioner? I would say SOLID [1] is a good start, but then I watched this [2] and now I'm in crisis and code anymore. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45483683,"author":"zengid","month":"2025-10","story":"Callbacks in C++ using template functors (1994)","stance":"reference","why":"Where the commenter learned of Hickey's paper","quote":"Heard about this watching Casey Muratori's \"The Big OOPs\" talk [0].","text":"Heard about this watching Casey Muratori's \"The Big OOPs\" talk [0]. Thought it couldn't be _that_ Hickey, but turns out it was! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45541462,"author":"_aavaa_","month":"2025-10","story":"Multi-Core by Default","stance":"reference","why":"ECS redraws encapsulation boundaries","quote":"It’s a redrawing of encapsulation boundaries which can be applied to systems broadly.","text":"ECS is not a game specific thing. It’s a redrawing of encapsulation boundaries which can be applied to systems broadly. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44621015,"author":"romaniv","month":"2025-07","story":"The Big Oops: Anatomy of a Thirty-Five-Year Mistake [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"That narrative is demonstrably false.","text":"From the video: \"It's like, yeah, he said that in 2003, right? He said that after a very long time. So why did he say it? It's because 10 years earlier, he was already saying he kind of soured on it.\" [VIDEO] He mentions Alan Kay about dozen times and uses quotes and dates to create a specific narrative about Smalltalk. That narrative is demonstrably false."},{"id":44635773,"author":"Mathnerd314","month":"2025-07","story":"The Big Oops: Anatomy of a Thirty-Five-Year Mistake [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"So yeah, Casey has already admitted he has no understanding of Alan Kay's writings.","text":"Casey says he “didn’t really cover Alan Kay” [VIDEO] To me that says that Kay wasn’t a major focus of his research. That seems to be reflected in the talk itself: I counted 6 Bjorne sources, 4 Alan Kay sources, 2 more related to Smalltalk, and about 10 focused on Sketchpad, Douglas Ross, and others. By source count, the talk is roughly 18% about Alan Kay and 27% about Smalltalk overall - not a huge part. As far as the narrative, probably the clearest expression of Casey's thesis is at [VIDEO] \"Alan Kay had a degree in molecular biology. ... [he was] thinking of little tiny cells that communicate back and forth but which do not reach across into each other's domain to do different things. And so [he was certain that] that was the future of how we will engineer things. They're going to be like microorganisms where they're little things that we instance, and they'll just talk to each other. So everything will be built that way from the ground up.\" AFAICT the gist of this is true, Kay was indeed inspired by biological cells and that is why he emphasized message-passing so heavily. His undergraduate degree was in math + bio, not just bio, but close enough. As far as specific discussion, Casey says, regarding a quote on inheritance: [VIDEO] \"that's a little bit weird. I don't know. Maybe Alan Kay... will come to tell us what he actually was trying to say there exactly.\" So yeah, Casey has already admitted he has no understanding of Alan Kay's writings. I don't know what else you wa"}],"r":[["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1]]},"2tdiKTSdE9Y":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a well-told chase of a weird fact that lands a rare interview with Lovelock at age 101.","q":{"x":"The whole story is worth a watch since Tom Scott does an admirable job of explaining","i":32254410,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32254410,"author":"techdragon","month":"2022-07","story":"James Lovelock has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tom Scott chases a weird fact and lands an amazing Lovelock interview.","quote":"The whole story is worth a watch since Tom Scott does an admirable job of explaining","text":"Despite everything else he accomplished in his quite storied professional career as one of the last independent scientists... Including his work on the Viking mars landers! [LINK] To me he will always be the man who invented his own microwave to thaw frozen hamsters. The whole story is worth a watch since Tom Scott does an admirable job of explaining how he went digging up a weird fact expecting to debunk it only to wind up recording an amazing interview with James Lovelock [VIDEO] but if you’re impatient the interview with lovelock about cryopreserved hamsters and his \"microwave\" starts here [VIDEO]"},{"id":47263927,"author":"radishingr","month":"2026-03","story":"Chimpanzees Are into Crystals","stance":"rec","why":"Summarizes Percy Spencer's oven and Lovelock's small microwave for cryorevival.","quote":"did a pretty good summary of the Percy Spencer microwave","text":"The Tom Scott video( [VIDEO] ) did a pretty good summary of the Percy Spencer microwave (a giant commercial oven) and the later parallel development by J. E. Lovelock of a small microwave to reheat small mammals for experiments with cryorevival (replacing lamps and hot paddles)."},{"id":36446454,"author":"H8crilA","month":"2023-06","story":"Scientists unfroze rat organs and transplanted them","stance":"rec","why":"Covers the man who thawed frozen small animals with microwaves.","quote":"Tom Scott made a great video about it","text":"Tom Scott made a great video about it, he even got to the guy who started using microwaves for thawing entire small animals. Before microwaves (diathermic heating) they would use a hot piece of metal, like a large spoon. [VIDEO] And yes, you can unfreeze a rock solid rat, then see it walk around. This is why cryogenics was hyped so much, because it sort of works on small animals."},{"id":34687386,"author":"zeristor","month":"2023-02","story":"SimEarth, Finding Gaia in an Afternoon","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott's microwave video with a Lovelock interview.","quote":"Tom Scott’s video on Microwaves, in which James Lovelock is interviewed","text":"This where I can post Tom Scott’s video on Microwaves, in which James Lovelock is interviewed: [VIDEO] Spoiler warning, well you wouldn’t watch it if I didn’t tell you would you."},{"id":34940736,"author":"cynwoody","month":"2023-02","story":"How to date a recording using background electrical noise","stance":"rec","why":"Microwaving frozen hamsters back to life, with Lovelock interviewed at 101.","quote":"Tom Scott is one talented YouTuber.","text":"Yes, Tom Scott is one talented YouTuber. See, for example, his video on microwaving frozen hamsters back to life[0], in which he interviews the 101-year-old J. E. Lovelock, the scientist who did the work back in the fifties. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33357798,"author":"mabbo","month":"2022-10","story":"A singular scientist: James Lovelock","stance":"rec","why":"Wonderful video interviewing a remarkably coherent 100-year-old Lovelock.","quote":"Tom Scott interviewed him last year in a really wonderful video about microwaves.","text":"Tom Scott interviewed him last year in a really wonderful video about microwaves. [VIDEO] > I found an article that said \"The microwave was invented to heat hamsters humanely in 1950s experiments.\" And I thought, no it wasn't. ...was it? At over 100 years old, the man was remarkably coherent and healthy. Quite sad to hear he's passed now."},{"id":32251600,"author":"bambax","month":"2022-07","story":"James Lovelock has died","stance":"rec","why":"Features an interview with Lovelock about microwave ovens.","quote":"features a nice interview with Lovelock","text":"This Tom Scott's video from last year about microwave ovens features a nice interview with Lovelock: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31288393,"author":"_Algernon_","month":"2022-05","story":"Faecal transplants reverse hallmarks of ageing","stance":"rec","why":"Related video about thawing frozen animals with microwaves.","quote":"Edit to add an interesting related video","text":"That's purely a size thing iirc. Lot quicker to get enough heat into a mouse to thaw it, than a human. Edit to add an interesting related video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27425608,"author":"broof","month":"2021-06","story":"Frozen in Siberian Permafrost for 24,000 Years, Microscopic Animal Comes to Life","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how microwaves were first used to thaw frozen animals.","quote":"There was a really interesting video about how microwaves were first used to test this","text":"They already studied this. It sometimes worked with small animals like mice, but fails as organisms get larger. There was a really interesting video about how microwaves were first used to test this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42446991,"author":"pauloday","month":"2024-12","story":"FastVideo: a lightweight framework for accelerating large video diffusion models","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Someone wrote the following comment then deleted it. I spent 30 minutes on my response and wanted to post it anyway. Apologies if the original comment was deleted by a mod, I hope this is OK to post. ---QUOTE--- My \"test\" for video generation turning movie making on its head is when a model can add the missing Tom Bombadil chapters to Peter Jackson's LOTR movies. Probably 20 - 30 minutes of HD, aesthetically synced, scripted etc with minimal editing after a detailed prompt and source material. Qualifier - the AI just has to follow the book script, third party tools ok to use for lip syncing and audio :) I said 5 years away last year. Feels like it might be more like 1 - 2 years. What do you think? ---END QUOTE--- My response: I think we're getting into diminishing returns territory with this AI stuff. These video/image generators are impressive but they don't \"understand\" physical reality and probably never will without a breakthrough. You can see this in the demo videos, the best looking ones are glorified still images and the worst are whenever something physical happens, like the lemon being picked up or the guy eating cereal. These examples may get better, but I really doubt they'll ever look like real unaltered camera footage without adding an understanding of how our physical reality works into the model somehow. For the script generation, Fellowship of the Ring is not a movie script and requires serious interpretation and planning to be converted into one. Especially i"},{"id":42155246,"author":"blue_pants","month":"2024-11","story":"Biological Miracle – Wood Frog","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with Lovelock on freezing and reviving hamsters.","quote":"And there's a Tom Scott's interview with James Lovelock","text":"From [LINK] \"In the mid-1950s, Lovelock experimented with the cryopreservation of rodents, determining that hamsters could be frozen and revived successfully.[14] Hamsters were frozen with 60% of the water in the brain crystallised into ice with no adverse effects recorded. Other organs were shown to be susceptible to damage.[15]\" And there's a Tom Scott's interview with James Lovelock: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41848334,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-10","story":"A History of Microwave Ovens","stance":"reference","why":"Interviews Lovelock about the frozen hamsters.","quote":"Tom Scott has a video on microwaves where he interviews James Lovelock about the frozen hamsters","text":"Tom Scott has a video on microwaves where he interviews James Lovelock about the frozen hamsters: * [VIDEO] Lovelock passed away a year later (in 2022): * [LINK]"},{"id":41848473,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-10","story":"A History of Microwave Ovens","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott interviews Lovelock about reanimating cryogenically frozen hamsters.","quote":"Mentions the Tom Scott video where he interview Lovelock","text":"> A history of microwave ovens without mention of reanimating cryogenically frozen hamsters? There is: > 1954: James Lovelock et al. reanimated cryogenically-frozen hamsters using an ad-hoc microwave oven. Mentions the Tom Scott video where he interview Lovelock (who passed a year later, in 2022): * [VIDEO]"},{"id":40928295,"author":"jessekv","month":"2024-07","story":"Rich People Are Freezing Themselves to Stay Wealthy Forever","stance":"reference","why":"Shows small mammals have been revived from freezing.","quote":"Actually, this has been done. Although that size seems to be about the limit.","text":"> even revive a mouse Actually, this has been done. Although that size seems to be about the limit. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40732562,"author":"peterfirefly","month":"2024-06","story":"Discovery of new white blood cell function","stance":"reference","why":"Cites the video line about hamster size alongside a research paper.","quote":"A hamster is an acceptable size.","text":"[VIDEO] \"A hamster is an acceptable size.\" [LINK] \"Studies on golden hamsters during cooling to and rewarming from body temperatures below 0° C. III. Biophysical aspects and general discussion\""},{"id":40384511,"author":"Khelavaster","month":"2024-05","story":"Frozen human brain tissue brought back to life in major breakthrough","stance":"reference","why":"Lovelock froze and microwave-thawed whole hamsters.","quote":"Lovelock was quick-freezing and microwave-thawing entire living hamsters in the mid 1900s.","text":"Lovelock was quick-freezing and microwave-thawing entire living hamsters in the mid 1900s. If it works on a whole hamster brain, it probably works in a sliver of human brain tissue. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37786373,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2023-10","story":"Ask HN: Does your microwave interfere with Bluetooth? Mine does","stance":"reference","why":"Microwave invented to revive frozen hamsters humanely.","quote":"","text":"> \" But this would have been the 1950s(?), so I'd assume they empirically determined a balance of functionality + technical feasibility for water, called it a lunch, and went to have five martinis in a practical fashion. \" Or maybe they just wanted to revive frozen hamsters more humanely: \"I promise this story about microwaves is interesting.\" by Tom Scott, about scientist James Lovelock - [VIDEO]"},{"id":34062226,"author":"crote","month":"2022-12","story":"What ever happened to the first cryogenically frozen humans?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for 1950s rodent freezing and microwave reanimation.","quote":"","text":"In the 1950s scientists were freezing small rodents and reanimating them using a primitive microwave. [0] If vitrifying and reviving a single rodent organ is the state-of-the-art 70 years later, I am not exactly impressed by their progress. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30815139,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2022-03","story":"Test Microwave for Radiation Leakage","stance":"reference","why":"Includes an interview with James Lovelock about reanimating frozen hamsters.","quote":"includes a brief interview with James Lovelock last year at age 101","text":"> \" I kind of doubt an independent inventor could bring this to market with today's startup climate. \" Especially the kind of inventor who created microwaves for experiments with reanimating frozen hamsters, cough James Lovelock. (Tom Scott's video \"I promise this story about microwaves is interesting\" which includes a brief interview with James Lovelock last year at age 101 - [VIDEO] )"},{"id":28770958,"author":"antiSingularity","month":"2021-10","story":"2450MHz radiation induces stress and anxiety like behavior in rats (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Covers microwaving frozen mice as an early magnetron application.","quote":"microwaving frozen mice was one of the very first non-radar applications of the microwave magnetron.","text":"Oddly enough, microwaving frozen mice was one of the very first non-radar applications of the microwave magnetron. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4QXZ2RzN_Oo",1],["j3mhkYbznBk",1],["4jEz03Z8azc",1],["zFhYJRqz_xk",1],["HaEPXoXVf2k",1],["eg3_kUaYFJA",1],["RzWB5jL5RX0",1],["wGt4XwBbCvA",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["st571DYYTR8",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1],["If61baWF4GE",1]]},"Q2UnOryQiIY":{"wy":"Several HN commenters say it was memorable or life-changing and use it to argue against seed oils.","q":{"x":"This lecture on vegetable oils is one of the most memorable lectures I've ever watched","i":25264408,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":34533262,"author":"jononomo","month":"2023-01","story":"Chipotle Goes on Hiring Spree of 15,000 Amid Strong Growth","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on seed oils that the commenter says changed their life.","quote":"This talk changed my life","text":"Seed oils did not exist in the human diet prior to about 1920. They are the most ultra-processed possible things you can consume. This talk changed my life: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32389805,"author":"jononomo","month":"2022-08","story":"The Latest Study on Red Meat and Heart Disease: A Red Herring","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk by Teicholz that made a massive impression on the commenter.","quote":"I came across this talk by her a few years ago and it made a massive impression on me","text":"Oh, wow -- I love Nina Teicholz! I came across this talk by her a few years ago and it made a massive impression on me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25264408,"author":"jononomo","month":"2020-12","story":"My Hunt for the Original McDonald’s French-Fry Recipe","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Memorable lecture on the implications of cooking with vegetable oil.","quote":"This lecture on vegetable oils is one of the most memorable lectures I've ever watched","text":"Just as long as you're aware of the implications of using vegetable oil for your french fries. This lecture on vegetable oils is one of the most memorable lectures I've ever watched: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26128206,"author":"teleforce","month":"2021-02","story":"50 Years Ago, Sugar Industry Quietly Paid Scientists to Blame Fat (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting story of the war on fat in the US.","quote":"provides interesting story about the war on fat in the US","text":"This video on \"Vegetable Oils: The Unknown Story\" provides interesting story about the war on fat in the US, by the author of the book \"The Big Fat Surprise\" [1][2]. According to the author, she was about to name her book \"The Big Fat Lie\", but eventually decided against it. [1] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":25788583,"author":"teleforce","month":"2021-01","story":"Testosterone levels show steady decrease among young US men","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by the author of The Big Fat Surprise on vegetable oils.","quote":"Please check this video on Vegetable Oils: The Unknown Story by the author of the book","text":"Please check this video on Vegetable Oils: The Unknown Story by the author of the book \"The Big Fat Surprise\" [1][2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":25430262,"author":"teleforce","month":"2020-12","story":"A palm oil alternative could help save rainforests","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by the author of The Big Fat Surprise on vegetable oils.","quote":"Please check this video on Vegetable Oils: The Unknown Story by the author of the book","text":"Most of the people are ignorant about the things and fat they put inside their body (that includes me). Please check this video on Vegetable Oils: The Unknown Story by the author of the book \"The Big Fat Surprise\" [1][2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":17794606,"author":"crphilipp","month":"2018-08","story":"Dietary carbohydrate intake and mortality: a prospective cohort study","stance":"rec","why":"Teicholz's argument against vegetable oils and the low-fat diet.","quote":"Please take a look at this videos,","text":"I have read her book, and it is fascinating. Go ahead and make your assumptions about her motives, but I believe you are doing yourself a disservice. Before the 20th century, we ate a lot of saturated fats, and it wasn't until the creation of vegetable oils (which are made through extensive industrial processes), was there a move to start restricting saturated fat. The current low-fat diet is an experiment that has America has been on for forty some years. Please take a look at this. [LINK] And in general, the American population has been following this diet, and we are fatter than ever before, suffering from Type 2 Diabetes. And the \"science\" behind our current health policy is driven by organizations like the American Soybean Asociation who are more concerned with making money and less on science. And when you start talking about money, the science research will follow the money, because the scientists need to eat. Please take a look at this videos, [VIDEO] [LINK] I would read her book before you pass judgment."}],"r":[["HB5TrK7A4pI",1],["AF8d72mA41M",1],["tc4ROCJYbm0",1],["Sg4U4r_AgJU",1]]},"3ZTGwcHQfLY":{"wy":"HN cites it constantly in US manufacturing debates as a vivid demonstration of how much domestic industrial capability has been lost.","q":{"x":"Here's a great video by Smarter Every Day that came out a few months ago","i":44936651,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44936651,"author":"ndiddy","month":"2025-08","story":"The decline of high-tech manufacturing in the United States","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Destin's attempt to make a grill scrubber entirely in the US reveals lost manufacturing skills.","quote":"Here's a great video by Smarter Every Day that came out a few months ago","text":"Here's a great video by Smarter Every Day that came out a few months ago: [VIDEO] . It's about his experience with the atrophying US industrial skillset while he tries to make a grill scrubber entirely in the United States. He brings up a few good points about manufacturing that I personally found compelling: 1. During Covid, medical supplies were in short supply, to the point where the US government was flying in individual airplanes from China full of N95 masks and face shields. The guy making the video knew that Alabama (where he lives) wouldn't be on the priority list for where the masks and face shields would get distributed. His community's initial response was to 3D print face shields and donate them to healthcare facilities, but they knew that they had to step up to actual manufacturing to produce the face shields in necessary volumes. He was only able to find a single aging technician in Alabama capable of producing injection molding tooling, which he then was able to use to mass produce enough face shields to supply Alabama and neighboring states. If that one guy had chosen to retire before Covid, they would have not been able to make the injection mold locally. At a time where global supply chains were massively disrupted, they would have been screwed. 2. When an American company gets their products made overseas, the factory overseas that manufactures the product owns the tooling. This means that they're able to fill your order during the day, then at night make e"},{"id":47585605,"author":"fooker","month":"2026-03","story":"Why the US Navy Won't Blast the Iranians and 'Open' Strait of Hormuz","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how hard a simple product is to make with a US supply chain.","quote":"trying to manufacture something as simple as a grill scrubber with a US supply chain would help you understand how bad it is","text":"> We make plenty of stuff at scale Maybe this video of a rather famous YouTuber trying to manufacture something as simple as a grill scrubber with a US supply chain would help you understand how bad it is? [VIDEO]"},{"id":46274265,"author":"legacynl","month":"2025-12","story":"Roomba maker goes bankrupt, Chinese owner emerges","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why US manufacturing of such products is nearly impossible now.","quote":"That guy from smarter every day had a great youtube video about why that's basically impossible in the US right now","text":"That guy from smarter every day had a great youtube video about why that's basically impossible in the US right now: [VIDEO] I'm not a fan of big coorps either, but these changes happened gradually over time, starting way back in probably the 50/60s. The blame is with the companies and the US government back then for allowing this critical knowledge to be lost. But Roomba didn't exist back then, and the problem is bigger than a single company. The only real solution is for the US government to invest heavily in regaining this capability for the coming 25 years. But doge and trump axed any chance of this happening so though luck i guess."},{"id":45438485,"author":"Corrado","month":"2025-10","story":"Ford CEO on his 'epiphany' after talking to his Gen Z factory workers","stance":"rec","why":"Highlights the decline of US blue collar manufacturing skills.","quote":"This video[0] about building a grill scrubber in America really highlighted the problem for me.","text":"I think we are definitely losing our blue collar skills. This video[0] about building a grill scrubber in America really highlighted the problem for me. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44442307,"author":"aeonik","month":"2025-07","story":"They tried Made in the USA – it was too expensive for their customers","stance":"rec","why":"Details the challenges of making a product in the US","quote":"He details all the challenges, and it's a pretty good watch.","text":"SmarterEveryday is trying this right now too. He details all the challenges, and it's a pretty good watch. The grill brush they made is a bit on the expensive side, but I bought one. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44334720,"author":"j_bum","month":"2025-06","story":"BYD begins testing solid-state EV batteries in the Seal","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates the institutional knowledge gap in US manufacturing","quote":"does a good job of demonstrating the institutional knowledge gap","text":"Agreed. Maybe you’ve seen this, but Destin Sandlin’s recent video on this topic does a good job of demonstrating the institutional knowledge gap [0]. He also was a guest on the Search Engine podcast to discuss the same topic earlier this year [1]. I enjoyed this more than the video. I think there’s some nuance and slight nationalism to take with a grain of salt, but the point is extremely well demonstrated. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":48127130,"author":"subscribed","month":"2026-05","story":"Bambu Lab is abusing the open source social contract","stance":"reference","why":"Process of trying to build a simple item outside China.","quote":"here's the process Smarter Every Day went through for a VERY simple item","text":"But that's not exactly the Chinese fault. Actually try building something somewhere else - here's the process Smarter Every Day went through for a VERY simple item, nowhere the scope or complexity of the 3d printer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48343200,"author":"wcfrobert","month":"2026-05","story":"Leo's first encyclical attacks technological messianism","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates atrophied US manufacturing.","quote":"here's a video from Smarter everyday on how atrophied manufacturing has become in the US","text":"This is so accurate wtf!? - \"United States is a service and information economy\": Finance, real estate, insurance, SaaS. - \"Nearly all the manufacturing industries have slipped away\": here's a video from Smarter everyday on how atrophied manufacturing has become in the US: [I Tried to Make Something in America]( [VIDEO] ) - \"Technological power in the hands of very few\": AWS, Google Cloud, Azure. - \"People have lost the ability to set their own agendas or knowledgeably question those in authority [...] unable to distinguish between what feels good and what's true\": Fun house mirror of Twitter/X. Attention economy. Algorithm that maximizes engagement. AI Slop. Deepfakes. War in Iran. - \"our critical faculties in decline\": NYT Article [Why U.S. Test Scores Are in a Generation-Long Decline]( [LINK] ) - \"the 30-second sound bites now down to 10 seconds or less\": TikTok, Instagram reels - \"credulous presentations on pseudoscience and superstition\": RFK Jr, Tate, Fuentes, conspiracies, Qanon, Vaccine hesitancy."},{"id":47438245,"author":"petterroea","month":"2026-03","story":"Denmark was reportedly preparing for full-scale war with the US over Greenland","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment manufacturing without China that failed.","quote":"As an experiment he tried to manufacture something without China, but was unable to","text":"They are pretty happy with having superiority on high tech manufacturing and robotics. You basically cannot manufacture something without using China - even if you try. I don't think they consider the TSMC EUV monopoly a long term threat. Doing good on AI as well, you bet the OSS chinese models causing stock panic in the US makes them laugh. On the topic of manufacturing outside China, the YouTuber \"Smarter Every day\" (Destin Sandlin) has a series on manufacturing and feels strongly about manufacturing having moved out of the country. As an experiment he tried to manufacture something without China, but was unable to: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46884379,"author":"morbusfonticuli","month":"2026-02","story":"Lessons Learned Shipping 500 Units of My First Hardware Product","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment making a grill brush in the USA showed lost manufacturing knowledge.","quote":"who tried to manufacture a a grill brush completely in USA","text":"This reminded of the YouTube guy of \"Smarter Every Day\" who tried to manufacture a a grill brush completely in USA and still be competitive in the marketplace (i.e. China) [0]. It turned out that USA / \"the west\" lost the engineering knowledge to manufacture \"stuff\" (in this case injection molding and other procedures): Nowadays, we simply create the plans and schematics (e.g. CAD files) and let the Chinese do the building. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45973303,"author":"nostrademons","month":"2025-11","story":"Chuck Moore: Colorforth has stopped working [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary showing the US supply chain is largely gone.","quote":"I'm reminded of the Smarter Scrubber documentary that found that basically the whole supply chain was gone","text":"I wonder sometimes if there's an earlier level of technology that society could basically \"checkpoint\" at and freeze, and then build off of. Capitalism today feels like it's hit the Red Queen Paradox - it goes around and around to keep the money flowing, but with very little actual progress. Indeed, most people seem to feel like the world is getting worse for all that work, and that many of the innovations of the last ~10-15 years are \"fixing\" things that weren't problems to begin with while creating new problems. And yet because all the substrate is shifting around, even if you don't break something someone else will. Could we go back to a world of redundant interchangeable parts where if somebody breaks something, you just cut them off and use a substitute that works just as well? Or maybe that's well and truly gone and we're just fated to another dark age. I'm reminded of the Smarter Scrubber documentary that found that basically the whole supply chain was gone and it was impossible to make something useful in America. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45497024,"author":"bityard","month":"2025-10","story":"Sharpie found a way to make pens more cheaply by manufacturing them in the U.S.","stance":"reference","why":"Background on lost US manufacturing base.","quote":"","text":"It is insanely hard to build basic things in the US anymore, even if there is a market, even if the market will pay your prices, even if you have the capital to start, and even if you can hire reliable workers. And those are all HUGE ifs. The biggest problem is that the base underlying our manufacturing capability has almost completely evaporated. You need tool and die companies to build parts for your manufacturing machines, but almost none are left. There are only a few plastic injection manufacturing companies left, and they are either fully booked or on the path to shutting down. (No, you can't 3D print products for the mass market, it's way too slow and expensive.) Young people think of trades and manufacturing jobs as fall-backs that only drop-outs who couldn't hack college get into. You will make many times more money as a salescritter convincing people to buy things they don't need than using decades of engineering experience to build the things they do. It's a popular misconception that China's manufacturing advantage is cheaper labor. This was _maybe_ true for a while, their advantage right now is that they have all the things mentioned above: they have the tool and die shops, they have the supply chains, and they have workforce. They can go from product idea to shipping in a week. I do not celebrate the Chinese government for much, but making sure they had a robust manufacturing base from top to bottom was the biggest and most important thing they have ever done. T"},{"id":45498058,"author":"jjk166","month":"2025-10","story":"Sharpie found a way to make pens more cheaply by manufacturing them in the U.S.","stance":"reference","why":"Example of failing to make a grill brush with US-only parts.","quote":"","text":"> You need tool and die companies to build parts for your manufacturing machines, but almost none are left. There are only a few plastic injection manufacturing companies left, and they are either fully booked or on the path to shutting down. > Young people think of trades and manufacturing jobs as fall-backs that only drop-outs who couldn't hack college get into. As an engineer running a factory in New Jersey doing, among other things, injection molding, this is way off base. There are plenty of tool and die shops. While they do exist, injection molding job shops are a miniscule fraction of the injection molding market. The machines are so cheap and so easy to use that everyone in-houses their molding operations. I don't personally see a shortage of young people in manufacturing; I'm in my mid 30s and I'm the old man in my department, and my past employers and the various companies I interact with seem to have similar demographic structures. Our job postings are flooded with kids fresh out of school applying for jobs. My job requires a degree; and it is quite rare to find someone who does not hold a degree compared to say software development. A lot of manufacturing companies will pay to send mid-career employees to school. Post secondary degrees are not uncommon. > Destin from Smarter Every Day encountered all of this when he tried to make a better grill brush using only a handful of parts and _still_ did not manage to make the whole thing using only US-originated parts. It"},{"id":45354690,"author":"speff","month":"2025-09","story":"Denmark wants to push through Chat Control","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for lost tribal manufacturing knowledge.","quote":"","text":"Manufacturing: > The United States is the world's second-largest manufacturer after the People's Republic of China with a record high real output in 2024 of $2.913 trillion [0] I believe the US' manufacturing capability is the core of your comment and I also believe it's incorrect. Sure we don't manufacture fast-fashion or junk products and we may have lost quite a bit of tribal knowledge[1] with respect to that. But it's nothing that can't be re-gained. And the benefits China and the Russia get from their spying programs? Americans by-and-large simply do not care about them. Denmark can do whatever they want with their tech as long as their citizens approve. Like you said, they're in a different position given their geographic location - thus, they have different priorities. But Americans do not feel like they have such an existential threat so they are (generally) not willing to give up their privacy. Whether \"reality\" catches up to your predictions remains to be seen. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44805046,"author":"bsder","month":"2025-08","story":"TSMC says employees tried to steal trade secrets on iPhone 18 chip process","stance":"reference","why":"Attempt to make a grill scrubber in the US went poorly","quote":"SmarterEveryDay attempted to make a grill scrubber in the US.","text":"The problem is that these jobs are hard and the pay is crap. SmarterEveryDay attempted to make a grill scrubber in the US. [VIDEO] tl;dr He did it, but it went very poorly."},{"id":44912575,"author":"JimmyBiscuit","month":"2025-08","story":"Open hardware desktop 3D printing is dead?","stance":"reference","why":"Making things without sourcing internationally is nearly impossible","quote":"Trying to make a thing and not sourcing stuff internationally is almost impossible","text":">As a creator, how am I supposed to create, when all avenues lead to \"source it in China\"? That... Is huge. Trying to make a thing and not sourcing stuff internationally is almost impossible: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44937701,"author":"Caligatio","month":"2025-08","story":"The decline of high-tech manufacturing in the United States","stance":"reference","why":"Part of a series on US loss of tooling and manufacturing spin-up ability.","quote":"SmarterEveryDay has a whole series[1] on manufacturing that covers how the US is losing/lost the ability to create tooling.","text":"SmarterEveryDay has a whole series[1] on manufacturing that covers how the US is losing/lost the ability to create tooling. I believe the \"Smarter Scrubber\" episode[2] is the one that explicitly talks about how not only has the US lost manufacturing capacity but it also lost the ability to spin up manufacturing. The tool and die episode also touches on it[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44941262,"author":"nabla9","month":"2025-08","story":"Promises of a US manufacturing Renaissance leave experts scratching their heads","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as hinting that domestic manufacturing does not bring many jobs.","quote":"Even Destin Sandlin (Smarter Every day guy) hinted it in a recent video","text":"It's not just Trump, it's also US public who thinks that domestic manufacturing brings jobs. Even Destin Sandlin (Smarter Every day guy) hinted it in a recent video [VIDEO] Manufacturing as share of jobs declines even in those countries that are big at manufacturing. China, Germany, Japan, South Korea. China buys half of worlds robots. Manufacturing can create only very low paying jobs that people do in third countries. You can bring more manufacturing but not much more jobs. Most likely the lost comparative advantage means net welfare loss if you force it."},{"id":45032651,"author":"robocat","month":"2025-08","story":"We regret but have to temporary suspend the shipments to USA","stance":"reference","why":"Shows difficulty finding US-made bolts.","quote":"talks about the trouble he had finding US made bolts","text":"Good imperial bolts. Does the US make the machines that make the bolts any more? And what if you needed metric bolts? \"I Tried To Make Something In America (The Smarter Scrubber Experiment)\" [VIDEO] talks about the trouble he had finding US made bolts and I seem to remember he found out he'd been scammed and was sold Chinese bolts anyway? (Edit: Skip to around 17m35s)"},{"id":44640436,"author":"yegle","month":"2025-07","story":"What went wrong inside recalled Anker PowerCore 10000 power banks?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This seems to be startlingly contrast to all the talks about \"US manufacturers are absolutely doomed w/o Chinese vendors' great products and quality control\" when Trump increased the tariffs. I can only find [VIDEO] but earlier this year it was all over YouTube."}],"r":[["ispyUPqqL1c",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["YcUycQoz0zg",1],["EmKQsSDlaa4",1],["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["mScpHTIi-kM",1],["_0QMKFzW9fw",1],["kUfF2MTnqAw",1],["X0VH78ye4yY",1]]},"LxgMdjyw8uw":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an articulate, uplifting counterweight to climate doomerism, though one critic calls it a form of denialism and another notes it omits nuclear.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this video from the wonderful Kurzgesagt science video series","i":30957704,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":36406527,"author":"willio58","month":"2023-06","story":"53% of parents say climate change affects their decision to have more kids","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Touches on climate fears and whether to have children.","quote":"Kurzgesagt has a great video that touches on this","text":"I posted about this before and one commenter said something that stayed with me. Something along the lines of if all the people that feared having children due to climate change didn't have children, the only people having children would be those who had no fear of climate change. Kurzgesagt has a great video that touches on this - [VIDEO]"},{"id":30932667,"author":"Nagyman","month":"2022-04","story":"IPCC’s starkest message yet: extreme steps needed to avert climate disaster","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Uplifting, well articulated case that positive climate change is happening.","quote":"Here's some uplifting news in a well articulated video worth watching","text":"Climate change is a scale, from bad to really really bad, and positive change is already happening. Here's some uplifting news in a well articulated video worth watching: [VIDEO] Apathy is their weapon, and it's very effective."},{"id":30957704,"author":"civilized","month":"2022-04","story":"The only thing we should be talking about is the climate crisis","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reasons for hope as trends point in the right direction.","quote":"I highly recommend this video from the wonderful Kurzgesagt science video series","text":"I agree that we should be giving a lot more attention to climate change. I don't think it's the only thing we should be talking about, but we should be regularly asking ourselves if there is more that we can do (and there certainly is). I highly recommend this video from the wonderful Kurzgesagt science video series: [VIDEO] Climate change is bad and we need to take it seriously, but there is a lot of reason for hope. A lot of trends are pointing in the right direction and we need to support those trends. Climate policy is still in a bad state, in large part because of the gerontocracy, but younger people are entering politics with a different view."},{"id":39734690,"author":"instagib","month":"2024-03","story":"Liebreich: Net Zero Will Be Harder Than You Think – and Easier. Part II: Easier","stance":"rec","why":"More positive, less doomerist climate update","quote":"here is a decent video and a follow up more positive one I found","text":"If you don’t mind YouTube, here is a decent video and a follow up more positive one I found. It does mention concrete and many other forms of co2 production we haven’t figured out yet. 2022 Kurzgesagt in a nutshell. [LINK] 2023 less doomerism climate change update: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35238422,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-03","story":"IPCC climate crisis report delivers ‘final warning on 1.5C’","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Make sure that you watch the second one if you watch the first one.","text":"From Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell: Can YOU Fix Climate Change? [LINK] and We WILL Fix Climate Change! [VIDEO] Make sure that you watch the second one if you watch the first one."},{"id":32181802,"author":"bckr","month":"2022-07","story":"Most Americans think NASA’s $10B space telescope is a good investment","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"The future is not full of only despair[] .","text":"There's this idea that we should be sad about this. I say, if you're sad about it, I understand. I feel a little sad, too. But I'm also curious about what comes next. What will only get a chance to live thanks to the things that have died? What will humans be able to do in the aftermath? It sounds morbid, but if you just let yourself play with these thoughts they can lead somewhere different and interesting. The future is not full of only despair[] . [] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32176783,"author":"AussieWog93","month":"2022-07","story":"The world is awful, the world is much better, the world can be much better","stance":"reference","why":"Argues worst outcomes already avoided and 2C may be limited.","quote":"At least, according to Kurzgesagt.","text":">Climate change is not being addressed seriously currently, at all. And the trend is towards it not being addressed. While we could be doing better, we've made immense strides over the past ten years, to the point where we've already avoided the worst outcomes and could possibly even limit the rise to 2°C and avoid most harm altogether. At least, according to Kurzgesagt. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30953100,"author":"wdr1","month":"2022-04","story":"Nuclear power helped prevent ~2M deaths in the last 50 years","stance":"reference","why":"Hopeful look at climate solutions, though omitting nuclear power.","quote":"Kurzgesagt just did a great video[1] on what we should be hopeful for addressing climate change","text":"It baffles me how it's completely normal to avoid any mention of nuclear power when discussing climate change. Kurzgesagt just did a great video[1] on what we should be hopeful for addressing climate change, but again, absolutely zero mention of nuclear power. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31031718,"author":"gnramires","month":"2022-04","story":"Americans’ views on the war reveal a striking generational divide","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think in this case it's more than that... Russia (or at least Putin) is trying to impose a certain worldview that's very regressive: pro-oil, heavy censorship, against several human rights (including LGBT), autocratic regime. I think there's a lot of merit to resisting this regime, resisting being taken by force and turned into the opposite of what they want (which was to join more progressive European countries, from what I gather). It's all connected... if you let oil-hungry autocrats rule the world, climate change is completely out of the window. Also human rights and ideals we should all stand for. I do want peace as soon as possible. I believe a compromise might be necessary. I think Putin's worldview has been shown critically dated, so hopefully Russians can rise against this sort of government eventually. It's very difficult to rise up when your life is threatened by the regime though, but reason and truth tend to find their way through the cracks. --- I am specially moved because of the parallels to Nazi invasions. Doing nothing proved a grave mistake, not standing up for human rights, giving fascists the benefit of the doubt cost so many lives. I've said \"never again\" too. It's easy to fact-check the fascist lies in this case. I suggest any new generations read Anne Frank's diary if you can. --- Also: don't believe narratives of doom. There's no doom incoming immediately, not from nuclear war (it's been shown it would doom civilization at least), not from climate c"},{"id":36087660,"author":"runarberg","month":"2023-05","story":"Emissions are no longer following the worst case scenario","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The YouTube channel Kurzgesagt ( ) is guilty if this form of denialism.","text":"I call these sort of arguments Climate Optimism and I consider it a form of climate denial. The central thesis is that the climate crisis has already been solved with technology and capitalism. The worst offenders (like Max Roser of Our World in Data [LINK] ) overemphasize success in western countries and hint the problem lies in China and India not doing enough. A more run of the mill climate optimist claims that the efforts we’ve done so far are enough to avert disaster, and we just need to do more of the same. The YouTube channel Kurzgesagt ( [VIDEO] ) is guilty if this form of denialism."}],"r":[["yiw6_JakZFc",2],["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["CbIZU8cQWXc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["rvskMHn0sqQ",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["fD-R35DSSZY",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["bLk1cjZ4ll0",1]]},"lHcTKWiZ8sI":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a short, accessible primer on social credit and surveillance-driven gamification, linking it in many privacy and China threads.","q":{"x":"It's 7.5 minutes long, and well worth the watch","i":15940927,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":16780828,"author":"viraptor","month":"2018-04","story":"China’s experiment in ranking and monitoring citizens has started","stance":"rec","why":"Covers gamification and social pressure side rarely discussed elsewhere.","quote":"I recommend watching","text":"There's an interesting video from Extra Credits about this. It's based on early announcements and repeats some of the popular points, but it addresses the gamification and social pressure side of the issue which I haven't seen discussed that much elsewhere. I recommend watching: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16441931,"author":"hagbardceline","month":"2018-02","story":"Preparing for Malicious Uses of AI","stance":"rec","why":"A great primer on Sesame Credit, worth watching even if familiar.","quote":"a great primer, and even if you know all about it, it is still great to watch","text":"I believe much of this can be dealt with, if we start RIGHT NOW to address a serious issue in our systems - the lack of a way to represent Morals and Ethics (the When and the Why) in the systems we are building. This needs to provide important input to, and thus shape the DIKW(D) pyramid. I've been doing some work with the IEEE on this - and I'm looking here on ycombinator to get some real-world feeedback on what people are thinking and concerned about. I have some (personal) ideas that might work to address the concerns I'm seeing. { NOTE Some of this is taken from a previous post I wrote (but kinda missed the thread developing, I was late I don't think anyone read it). It is useful for this thread, so a modification of that post follows.} First, I think you need a way to define 'ethics' and 'morals', with a 'ontology' and a 'epistemology' to derive a metaphysic for the system (and for my $.02, aesthetics arises from here). Until we can have a bit of rigor surrounding this, it's a challenge to discuss ethics in the context of an AI, and AI in the context of the metaphysical stance it takes towards the world. This is vital, as we need to define what 'malicious use' IS . This is still an area (as the thread demonstrates) of serious contention. Take sesame credit (a great primer, and even if you know all about it, it is still great to watch: [VIDEO] ). Now here is a question for you: Is it wrong for the government to create a system that uses social pressure, rather than retrib"},{"id":15940927,"author":"maltalex","month":"2017-12","story":"Inside China's Vast New Experiment in Social Ranking","stance":"rec","why":"Short game-design take on Sesame Credit as gamified obedience.","quote":"It's 7.5 minutes long, and well worth the watch","text":"Extra Credits made a video about Sesame Credit a couple of years ago. It's 7.5 minutes long, and well worth the watch: [VIDEO] Being a game design channel, they discuss Sesame Credit as obedience to the party, gamified."},{"id":15799672,"author":"heroprotagonist","month":"2017-11","story":"Tencent’s Just Getting Started on Online Advertising","stance":"rec","why":"Most easily-engaged piece on implications of social credit.","quote":"most easily-engaged piece I've seen on implications of social credit","text":"Beyond that, consider what Tencent are doing with a \"social credit score\" to compete with Alibaba's Sesame Credit. It will be scary when these systems eventually migrate to or evolve in the US. Tencent social credit: * [LINK] Sesame Credit video from Extra Credits (almost a couple years old now, but most easily-engaged piece I've seen on implications of social credit): * [VIDEO]"},{"id":10871778,"author":"samstave","month":"2016-01","story":"China Shows How Surveillance Leads to Intimidation and Software Censorship","stance":"rec","why":"Shows China using gamification to control populations.","quote":"Check out this nightmare of a system China is implementing to use gamification to control populations","text":"Check out this nightmare of a system China is implementing to use gamification to control populations: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10882606,"author":"joeyespo","month":"2016-01","story":"Why privacy is important, and having “nothing to hide” is irrelevant","stance":"rec","why":"Dystopian picture of surveillance mixed with gamification.","quote":"it paints a rather dystopian picture of what's possible when you mix surveillance and gamification.","text":"I'm sure there are more direct examples, but I watched a video about Sesame Credit recently and it paints a rather dystopian picture of what's possible when you mix surveillance and gamification. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10793933,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-12","story":"\"Free Basics\" - Myths and Facts","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how Sesame Credit turns friends against each other.","quote":"","text":"> anti-free-basics mindset Ahh, the usual attempt to create factions. > Facebook isn't stopping anyone from providing internet access anywhere. The very idea of \"free basics\" is about normalizing the idea that Facebook can prevent who people can access. Yes, other providers already exist (removing any need for \"free basics\"), which is why Facebook is abusing their position as a social network to manipulate people into supporting their plan. They can do this because being a gatekeeper of social interaction is one of the most powerful political tools known to man. > And they certainly don't gain this power by providing free basics. If you read any of the other threads on this topic, you will find examples Facebook's tactics such as lying to users saying their friends support \"free basics\". That is their existing power as a social gatekeeper. The entire point of this new plan is to extend their gatekeeper position into one of the largest untapped markets on the planet. Without even considering how this position would allow Facebook to monopolize the market, their power as a gatekeeper to monitor social interaction (why do you think they MitM encryption?) and manipulate public opinion is so powerful it should be seen as a threat to national sovereignty . You probably think this is hyperbole, but I'm deadly serious. As I mentioned in a previous thread, the end game of this power to use friends against each other already exists in China under the name Sesame Credit. I recommend wat"},{"id":16602947,"author":"Dove","month":"2018-03","story":"China to bar people with bad 'social credit' from planes, trains","stance":"reference","why":"Game designer analysis of ideological warfare via gaming techniques.","quote":"Here's some analysis from a game designer from 2015","text":"China has been working on this for a while, and barring people from transportation is not the scary bit. That's last-century scary. No, it's the emergence of a new sort of ideological warfare making use of the recent knowledge developed in gaming industry that is the scary bit. This is a new kind of threat, something we as humans haven't learned how to deal with. Here's some analysis from a game designer from 2015: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16017963,"author":"Pica_soO","month":"2017-12","story":"34th Chaos Communication Congress Live Streams","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an episode on Sesame Credit.","quote":"There was a extra credit episode on that.","text":"There was a extra credit episode on that. Yes, that is purest evil. The sort that laughs alone, at night in front of a mirror, because even other evil shuns such company. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15016649,"author":"arethuza","month":"2017-08","story":"China's Plan for World Domination in AI Isn't So Crazy","stance":"reference","why":"Video describing a similar social credit system.","quote":"Video describing a similar system (linked to from the wiki page)","text":"I've seen the \"Social Credit System\" discussed: [LINK] Video describing a similar system (linked to from the wiki page): [VIDEO] Possibly one of the scariest concepts I've seen in a while."},{"id":14749190,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2017-07","story":"Sesame Credit","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for social scoring used to enforce conformity.","quote":"Extra Credit's episode about Sesame Credit","text":"Extra Credit's episode about Sesame Credit: [VIDEO] So, where else is there a widespread system of social media scores used to enforce conformity? Right here! The only difference is that there is a greater diversity factions influencing this in the west. From what I've seen, we are all being very effectively conditioned to hate \"the other.\" [LINK]"},{"id":14043296,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2017-04","story":"The Town of Innisfil partners with Uber for transit instead of buses","stance":"reference","why":"Video about Chinese government attempt to rate citizens.","quote":"This Extra Credits video is about an attempt by the Chinese government to implement this","text":"What if we begin to rate citizens as Apps rate users: will someone get blacklisted because he got a bad rating? This Extra Credits video is about an attempt by the Chinese government to implement this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13058471,"author":"htaunay","month":"2016-11","story":"China’s New Tool for Social Control: A Credit Rating for Everything","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the earlier video about Sesame Credit.","quote":"","text":"When Extra Credits \"Propaganda Games\" video came out about a year ago[1], I was relived when I read that \"Sesame Credit\" was a complete fabrication, and even questioned EC for posting such a video without source checking. Apparently is wasn't completely fabricated after all. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12522915,"author":"krick","month":"2016-09","story":"Google's plan to deter aspiring ISIS recruits","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of loyalty-point programs in China.","quote":"","text":"I'd imagine all these pro-privacy guys cheering right now, shouting \"we told you where this is going!\" if only it wouldn't be so terrifying and not funny at all. First they collect your personal data to show you ads, and people don't mind, because why not? Then they monopolize the market more and more, and basically all your data ends up in their hands, making them know you literally better than yourself do (as you don't normally quantify your life and are not watching over yourself really well). And people don't mind again, because \"I have nothing to hide\". Then they are officially profiling their clients to use the data for political purposes. First this might be ads, then some more. And people won't mind again, because it's unacceptable for USA citizen not to hate ISIS (but it doesn't really matter what is the most iconic black sheep at the moment). Then (at the moment, actually, but just in China[1] for now) the most used social network is running \"citizen loyalty bonus-points\" program. And people are accepting still, because it's all so cool and nice and what not. And even though it might seem terrifying to some, I don't think \"the people\" will ever actually regret this: the \"acceptable norm\" will just become narrower and narrower, and everything else will be treated as a criminal offense, a sickness that must be cured. It makes me think that this is just how life goes, the natural way an organism evolves. But the thought of it makes me feel sick anyway. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":11425034,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-04","story":"Rise of Ad Blocking Is the Ad Industry's Fault, Says Outgoing FTC Commissioner","stance":"reference","why":"Warns of credit scores tied to friends' products.","quote":"","text":"Ads as content isn't that bad of a problem. Consider a review or a show that uses a product for legitimate reasons . The (far) bigger problem will be using your friends against you[1]. Advertising by making various \"credit scores\" dependent on your friends using the \"right\" products will be very hard to counter. I suggest fighting it now. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11227464,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-03","story":"Wealth Inequality Is Even Worse in Reputation Economies","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a source on how Sesame Credit works.","quote":"","text":"No, that's not quite how Sesame Credits work in China.[1][2] The system computes a social score for each citizen. But it's not run by the Government or a bank. It's run by Tencent and Alibaba. It tracks what you say online, what you buy, and who your friends are. Having a low-scoring friend brings your score down. New feature: Baihe, China's largest dating service, now uses Sesame Credit scores in matching. It's voluntary now, but will become mandatory in 2020. This is the successor to the Dang'an, a permanent record kept for each citizen of China since it went Communist. The record was typically maintained in a book by the employer, in conjunction with the Public Security Organization. The Dang'an system became less relevant as more non-state employers appeared. Now it's getting an upgrade. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11309020,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-03","story":"Apple Encryption Engineers, If Ordered to Unlock iPhone, Might Resist","stance":"reference","why":"Source for positive reinforcement systems based on friends' behavior.","quote":"","text":"All it will take to \"imprison the mind\" is convincing enough people to use a system that gives positive reinforcement when your friends act \"correctly\". Normal social behavior will do the rest. China (with Tencent) is already trying this, and Facebook has at least explored this area[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":10834212,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-01","story":"Looking Beyond the Internet of Things","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates a nasty end-game of surveillance-driven business models.","quote":"","text":"> It smacks of a solution in desperate need of a problem That's because you're looking at it from the wrong perspective. This kind of junk makes sense when the problem is \"how can we trick people into giving over their personal data\". It doesn't have to actually work (that is, save energy or offer some significant convenience) as long as it lets you generate a database of when people spend time in their home. It's telling that insurance companies are already mentioned - as if that was a good thing. Aral Balkan's observation[1] that a lot of modern business plans are based on surveillance (\"it's about the data\"), Al Jazeera recent investigation[2] of this power grab is surprisingly detailed. > that cannot be allowed to be a thing I completely agree. I only hope this can be fought before we end up with one of the nastier end-games[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10842079,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-01","story":"Dell Computers Has Been Hacked","stance":"reference","why":"Describes how public ratings use your friends against you.","quote":"See Extra Credit's description[1] of how this works.","text":"There is a huge difference between a \"private\" rating system and a public rating that uses your friends against you as manipulation. See Extra Credit's description[1] of how this works. We aren't there yet , but consider that Facebook wants to use your social network associations in your credit score[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":10793604,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-12","story":"Facebook is misleading Indians with its full-page ads about Free Basics","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the end game of using friends' support as manipulation.","quote":"China already showed us the end game of this tactic with Sesame Credit.","text":"> convince you that all your friends are supporting their proposals This is a serious problem, because China already showed us the end game of this tactic with Sesame Credit. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gQnmP7UD_zk",1],["XI5frPV58tY",1],["8b7erU_DOfE",1],["VI_riscmviI",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1]]},"lhYGXYeMq_E":{"wy":"Recommended to people who find it inspiring or perspective-changing on pursuing goals, open-ended search and AI beyond optimization.","q":{"x":"It really changed my perspective on achieving my long term goals","i":27251958,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27251958,"author":"ZephyrBlu","month":"2021-05","story":"NNCP: Lossless Data Compression with Neural Networks (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed commenter's perspective on achieving long-term goals","quote":"It really changed my perspective on achieving my long term goals","text":"> The signal might be good but is it the signal you should be focusing on? There is an amazing amount of interesting stuff being discovered all the time. But is it helping you achieve concrete goals? My point is that putting everything through the filter of \"does this directly help me achieve my goals?\" isn't the best way to pursue your goals, assuming we're talking about big goals where the path to achieving them isn't clear. I recommend checking out this podcast episode called \"Why Greatness Cannot be Planned\". It really changed my perspective on achieving my long term goals: [VIDEO] Also on Twitter, yeah some people don't only tweet things in their domain. But I've found that some people do reliably stick to a single domain. Though I don't really follow science people on Twitter."},{"id":26007746,"author":"visarga","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: Is the Brain a Computer or an Antenna?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Open-endedness in brain and AI, contrasting evolution with optimization","quote":"I found this video about open endedness (contrasting evolution vs optimization) in the brain and AI very inspiring","text":"I think it's mainly a competitive & cooperative self replicator. The brain exists because previous generations did well. Do we need to infuse \"antenna magic\" into our brains, aren't they marvelous enough? I found this video about open endedness (contrasting evolution vs optimization) in the brain and AI very inspiring: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39669351,"author":"mikhailfranco","month":"2024-03","story":"Abandoning Objectives: Evolution with the Search for Novelty Alone (2011) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent long-form MLST interview with Stanley","quote":"Here are some excellent long-form podcast interviews with Stanley on MLST","text":"Lehman & Stanley concerning how explicit objectives encourage effort on exploit rather than explore. AI is progressing using objective functions that can be expressed in a concise form (energy minimization, gradient descent, etc.). A similar mechanism has been at work in industry since Ford and Winslow, from time-and-motion to jit-supply-chain, all under the short-term profit incentive. These are narrow known objectives with a desire to get relatively quick results. But perhaps the big leaps are not achieved by climbing local hills. The observation harks back to Thomas Kuhn, who described the activities of normal science and great leaps - scientific recolutions. Obviously there are further philosophical implications, but also economic and administrative cliffs - financialization of the economy, bloat in university admin, group-think in academic tracks, (US) healthcare administration - all lead to temporary money extraction, with cautious risk-averse investments in bits. But there is not so much free-riding backing of mavericks, long-shot bets on contrarian ideas, and speculative gambling on profound, but expensive and difficult, inventions from atoms (see Peter Thiel - \"courage is in far shorter supply than genius\" ). Why Greatness Cannot Be Planned is Stanley's latest book (2015). Here are some excellent long-form podcast interviews with Stanley on MLST: Is getting a pay rise a step to becoming a billionaire? #38 Prof. KENNETH STANLEY - Why Greatness Cannot Be Planned (2021)"},{"id":32747113,"author":"visarga","month":"2022-09","story":"Campus College of Letters and Science plans to limit high-demand majors","stance":"rec","why":"Great, very long video on open-endedness with Kenneth Stanley","quote":"A great video about open-endedness (warning - very long)","text":"You're right, the search space is deceptive and going directly towards the goal might lead to a dead end. Only in hindsight we can tell which stepping stones were essential. Immediate results are the best, but in the meantime we need to focus on novelty and interestingness. A great video about open-endedness (warning - very long) with Kenneth Stanley, author of the book \"Why Greatness Cannot be Planned\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":27062803,"author":"visarga","month":"2021-05","story":"Crazy New Ideas","stance":"rec","why":"Covers openendedness, serendipity, tyranny of objectives and evolution of ideas","quote":"They talk about \"openendedness\", serendipity, the tyranny of objectives, the evolution of ideas (and biology)","text":"This video is relevant. They talk about \"openendedness\", serendipity, the tyranny of objectives, the evolution of ideas (and biology), inventing new problems not just solving problems. There are important implications in AI. Kenneth Stanley - Why Greatness Cannot Be Planned on Machine Learning Street Talk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26933609,"author":"visarga","month":"2021-04","story":"Feynman: I am burned out and I'll never accomplish anything (1985)","stance":"rec","why":"Illuminating discussion of open-endedness as a revolt against objectives","quote":"I found this discussion about open-endedness being very illuminating","text":"> Do things that don't matter. Aside from the doors it can unlock, it's just damn fun. I found this discussion about open-endedness being very illuminating about doing stuff that are just fun. A revolt against objectives. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26933796,"author":"bvsrinivasan","month":"2021-04","story":"Feynman: I am burned out and I'll never accomplish anything (1985)","stance":"rec","why":"Very long interview where Stanley addresses criticisms of his idea","quote":"Stanley addresses some strong and subtle criticisms here","text":"In all probability, Youtube's algorithm will already suggest this to anyone who watches the talk, but there is a (very) long, ML Street Talk interview on this idea here [VIDEO] Stanley addresses some strong and subtle criticisms here but I actually preferred the book the most. The book is a bit repetitive but has some very good ideas in the appendix."},{"id":26139263,"author":"visarga","month":"2021-02","story":"DeepMind achieves SOTA image recognition with 8.7x faster training","stance":"rec","why":"Long discussion on evolution of ML ideas and Stanley's book","quote":"Link to a long discussion about the evolution of ML ideas","text":"> Anyways, my point is, none of this required math or genius or particularly demanding thought. It was basically let's tinker with this until we find a way that's better, using guess and check. No equations needed. I get that you'd like to have a clear theoretical basis for what works and we're far from there. But in the meantime we're stumbling in the dark, discovering tricks and forming intuitions, not knowing even where the road is going to lead us. This is an evolutionary process of ideas, similar to biological evolution that managed to make us. If you know where you're going you can optimise your actions but when you don't even know what might be useful later on, then all attempts are good. They increase diversity and discover blind spots. Some of them will be the stepping stones for the future, but we can't say in advance which and how. Link to a long discussion about the evolution of ML ideas and the book \"Why greatness cannot be planned\" by Kenneth Stanley - [VIDEO]"},{"id":39184431,"author":"gradschoolfail","month":"2024-01","story":"Lessons from history's greatest R&D labs","stance":"reference","why":"Ken Stanley explaining his concept that greatness cannot be planned","quote":"Here is Ken talking about the concept","text":"@publicdaniel asked below about the relevance of openai’s Ken Stanley of “greatness cannot be planned” fame. @jph00 Can you comment on the similarities and difference of your approach as you understand it, given that Ken is or was a research manager at OpenAI? Here is Ken talking about the concept [VIDEO] [LINK] “ collaboration can sometimes thwart innovation by tacitly forcing its participants into an objective-driven mindset.”"}],"r":[["2pWv7GOvuf0",1],["-KxjVlaLBmk",1],["iOWamCtnwTc",1],["iDulhoQ2pro",1],["Gh-Tff7DdzU",1],["JLb5hZLw44s",1],["3x1b_S6Qp2Q",1]]},"Y3n3c_8Nn2Y":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as the standard example of how much a tiny program can do, in contrast to bloated modern software.","q":{"x":"not very many 64Ks have reached the amazingness","i":15773902,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15773902,"author":"userbinator","month":"2017-11","story":"Terrain rendering in fewer than 20 lines of code","stance":"strong_rec","why":"An 11 minute 64k demo considered among the most amazing.","quote":"IMHO not very many 64Ks have reached the amazingness of this 11 minute one from 2000","text":"IMHO not very many 64Ks have reached the amazingness of this 11 minute one from 2000: [VIDEO] ...and for 4K, this one from 2009: [LINK]"},{"id":11850025,"author":"pkrefta","month":"2016-06","story":"Source code for 4kb demoscene production “Elevated” released","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A 64k intro that humbled a young programmer.","quote":"64k fr-08 by farbrausch told me that I know nothing about programming ;)","text":"In year 2000 when I was 16 years old 64k fr-08 by farbrausch told me that I know nothing about programming ;) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24017461,"author":"userbinator","month":"2020-08","story":"Niklaus Wirth was right and that is a problem","stance":"rec","why":"Among personal favourite demos showing what computers can do efficiently.","quote":"award-winning 4k or 64k demos should be required watching for every software developer.","text":"One only has to look at the demoscene to realise that limitations inspire creativity and efficiency. It's just a form of art, but gives a glimpse at what computers are capable of, if only we would try to use them more efficiently. I think award-winning 4k or 64k demos should be required watching for every software developer. Here are some personal favourites which have also appeared on HN: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14998414,"author":"userbinator","month":"2017-08","story":"Papers I like","stance":"rec","why":"Memorable demo from the Farbrausch group.","quote":"This one is probably the most memorable","text":"I discovered #4 through him and I agree both with his summary and the paper itself --- canonical Huffman is astoundingly simple if you look at it in the right way, yet almost everyone doesn't. ryg is also a member of the Farbrausch demogroup, which has released some amazing demos. This one is probably the most memorable: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16347269,"author":"xtrapolate","month":"2018-02","story":"In web design, everything easy is hard again","stance":"reference","why":"Example of what 64k can achieve, contrasted with bloated web frameworks.","quote":"Here's what they've pulled off in 2000","text":"When I'm thinking about bloated web frameworks and inefficient libraries, I often come back to memories of 64k demos released by groups such as Farbrausch. Here's what they've pulled off in 2000: [VIDEO] - as somewhat of a \"contrast\", jQuery alone (3.0.0-alpha1, minified) weighs 84522 bytes. Use (what you deem as) the right tool for the job. As to how we've got to where we are today, there are lots of reasons - but personally, I feel it's pointless to constantly talk about it. Whether things are more complex than they used to be is up for debate, it's entirely subjective. It's natural for things to evolve given the rising popularity and increased demands - but they evolve organically. People flock to frameworks because there's abstraction, and the \"dirty work\" has been offloaded to a third-party. This saves people time and effort. It's a bet - frameworks come and go, maintainers disappear; that's just the nature of it."}],"r":[["_YWMGuh15nE",1],["TQB0KK2rxcw",1],["yHXx3orN35Y",1],["jB0vBmiTr6o",1]]},"DEcwa68f-jY":{"wy":"Recommended as an approachable introduction to CRDTs and the local-first idea behind apps like Actual.","q":{"x":"the same one who made a great talk on using CRDTs called CRDTs for Mortals","i":38055185,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38055185,"author":"satvikpendem","month":"2023-10","story":"Actual is going open-source","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk that inspired local-first apps.","quote":"the same one who made a great talk on using CRDTs called CRDTs for Mortals","text":"Worth noting that the author, James Long, is the same one who made a great talk on using CRDTs called CRDTs for Mortals [0], and he uses them in Actual as well. He was a great source of inspiration when I was looking into using them for my offline-first, local-first apps [1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":28721814,"author":"azteceagle","month":"2021-10","story":"Downsides of Offline First","stance":"rec","why":"Promising simple sync implementation by James Long.","quote":"Something that we have researched and think it has a lot of potential","text":"We are in the same need right now. Something that we have researched and think it has a lot of potential, at least for us is James Long sync implementation. You can check out his talks and demos at: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] And his demo implementation (and annotated fork): * [LINK] * htps://github.com/clintharris/crdt-example-app_annotated/blob/master/NOTES.md I wonder why there isn't some open source engine based on this at least for CRUD apps since it has a lot of potential and it is really \"simple\" to implement and even understand."},{"id":25566488,"author":"raihansaputra","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: What are some concrete examples of good SPA's?","stance":"rec","why":"Offline-first web apps with CRDT syncing.","quote":"Offline first PWA/Web App, with CRDT for syncing","text":"Yeah. The trick to managing the offline states is to have it as cache. So updating and solely relying on the server, and only serve when offline. On the interactivity side, I feel that a lot of tools that I use daily justifies the use of SPA/PWA. Definitely can be passable with HTML SSR, but there's not much examples of it yet. Todoist: No hassle offline handling. It will sync on it's own. Twitter: They do intercept everything and handle/queue requests when the client is offline. It's not perfect though. Roam: Again. Offline syncing, mainly offline search/document creation. This is a bit unique though as I think the server basically is just a sync logic. I feel that the SPA stage is definitely a growing phase instead of a destination to be. There's a lot of problems that are not solved elegantly yet, and it's and the boundaries. But I feel that SPA and all of it's learnings will be an example of how the next step will be like. And with .wasm coming, there's a lot of possibility of different architectures/how to build apps. Some talks that refers to why/how SPA makes sense for some problems/usecases: Offline first PWA/Web App, with CRDT for syncing: [VIDEO] (James Long's CRDT For Mortals talk) State management framework oriented for offline/PWA use: [LINK]"},{"id":23522358,"author":"raihansaputra","month":"2020-06","story":"One word in PostgreSQL unlocked a 9x performance improvement","stance":"rec","why":"Explains problem with wall clock timestamps and CRDTs.","quote":"Really useful if anyone else wonders what's the problem with wall clock timestamps.","text":"The author has a conference talk talking about CRDT and the timestamp technique he uses. Really useful if anyone else wonders what's the problem with wall clock timestamps. dotJS 2019 - James Long - CRDTs for Mortals (20:44): [VIDEO] EDIT: btw, out of all the comments on the timestamp here, seems like only yours is not complaining about the format, and even points out that the article did not go in depth yet to specify the actual timestamp that he uses."},{"id":38055190,"author":"satvikpendem","month":"2023-10","story":"Actual is going open-source","stance":"reference","why":"Talk describes grow-only sets approach.","quote":"Based on the author's talk [0], looks like it's grow-only sets.","text":"Based on the author's talk [0], looks like it's grow-only sets. I haven't looked at the code though so it might have changed since he gave that talk in 2019. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xqGAC5QCYuQ",1],["otbnC2zE2rw",1],["KZeIEiBrT_w",1],["6reLWfFNer0",1]]},"J-M98KLgaUU":{"wy":"It is the standard answer when someone asks what happened to drone delivery.","q":{"x":"I think that Wendover has the best summary","i":36765824,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36765824,"author":"CSMastermind","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: What ever happened to the “coming wave” of delivery drones?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best summary of why drone delivery stalled, especially the last-foot problem.","quote":"I think that Wendover has the best summary","text":"I think that Wendover has the best summary: [VIDEO] Here's the tl;dr: The \"last foot\" problem is the biggest killer. Getting a drone into the air and to its target is not the hard problem - getting a package safely to the ground at someone's home is. It would require either a very specific neighborhood or a big advance in computer vision and AI tech. Where we have seen success in this space are places with dedicated delivery zones in controlled environments where existing transport infrastructure is not a good alternative. Also this quote: \"most people are simply willing to wait two or three days to receive their package while ALL customers wants their packages delivered as cheaply and simply as possible.\" Uber eats and door dash largely solve the problem that drone delivery was supposed to solve and if you're going to have autonomous delivery doing it via ground robots is much easier than trying to do it from a drone."},{"id":37590674,"author":"tim333","month":"2023-09","story":"FAA Authorizes Zipline to Deliver Commercial Packages Using Drones","stance":"rec","why":"Why drone delivery has not taken off despite a decade of hype.","quote":"There's quite a good video on","text":"There's quite a good video on \"Drone Delivery Was Supposed to be the Future. What Went Wrong?\". It's been a decade since Bezos launched drone deliveries but it hasn't taken off for a number or reasons including air traffic control around airports, people living in flats and the difficulties of getting a drone to find a safe spot. On the other hand Uber Eats type guys on ebikes have proved a practical solution. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32644942,"author":"coppolaemilio","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: What happened to drone delivery service?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains no-fly zones, cost and drop-off problems for drone delivery.","quote":"This mini-documentary explains it pretty well","text":"This mini-documentary explains it pretty well: [VIDEO] TL;DW: No-fly zones, costs, and drones not being intelligent enough to detect where they are going to drop off packages. It is cheaper and easier to do the last mile via traditional methods than dealing with all the risks and limitations of flying."},{"id":31151600,"author":"ketzu","month":"2022-04","story":"Drones have transformed blood delivery in Rwanda","stance":"rec","why":"Why drone delivery struggled in the US and where it works.","quote":"It was really interesting to watch.","text":"They also have a more recent video on general drone delivery: [VIDEO] It was really interesting to watch. General idea discusses the problems it faced in the US in seemingly optimal circumstances (airspace restrictions, density, existing delivery networks) and how it does live and help out in other places, highlighting zipline as an example."},{"id":35287276,"author":"NeoTar","month":"2023-03","story":"German monks create first powdered beer","stance":"reference","why":"Argues drone delivery is a mostly bad or niche idea.","quote":"Drone delivery is a mostly-bad or niche idea","text":"It makes it more efficient for delivery by most things. Drone delivery is a mostly-bad or niche idea: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IkdziSLYzHw",1],["7k1ehaE0bdU",1],["YMqgiXLjvRs",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["OxhTQdcieQE",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["baFaEvBywGc",1],["obCjODeoLVw",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["9NOncx2jU0Q",1],["Ak4on5uTaTg",1],["gfh-VCTwMw8",1],["t_rzYnXEQlE",1],["0SARbwvhupQ",1]]},"9W74aeuqsiU":{"wy":"HN points to it as a well-researched, accessible overview of PFAS in PFAS-related threads.","q":{"x":"he does his homework","i":31776260,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":44605940,"author":"nayuki","month":"2025-07","story":"French villages have no more drinking water. The reason? PFAS pollution","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among additional good PFAS videos.","quote":"Additional good videos","text":"Additional good videos: * Vox: [LINK] * MinuteEarth: [LINK] * MinuteFood: [LINK] * LastWeekTonight (John Oliver): [VIDEO] (2021) * LastWeekTonight (John Oliver): [LINK] (2024) * DW (Deutsche Welle) Documentary: [LINK]"},{"id":33853506,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-12","story":"The U.S. military is poisoning Okinawa","stance":"rec","why":"Covers PFAS variants and limited EPA bans.","quote":"There is a video by John Oliver talking about PFAS and the other hundred or so variants of these compounds.","text":"I thought that too. There is a video by John Oliver talking about PFAS and the other hundred or so variants of these compounds. [1] There are other videos talking about how companies can still use these compounds because the EPA only outlawed about 3 of the 130 or so. [1] - [VIDEO] [video]"},{"id":31776260,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2022-06","story":"EPA: 'Forever chemicals' pose risk even at low levels","stance":"rec","why":"Well-researched episode on PFAS.","quote":"he does his homework","text":"John Oliver (I know he's not everyone's cup of tea, but he does his homework) did an episode on these, last year: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28847362,"author":"725686","month":"2021-10","story":"Why birds can fly over Mount Everest (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting segment on PFAS in teflon and repellent fabrics.","quote":"John Oliver talks about PFAS, which was very interesting.","text":"Last week, on Last Week Tonight, John Oliver talks about PFAS, which was very interesting. Not only teflon, but all kind of repellent fabrics like Gore-tex: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31296729,"author":"strogonoff","month":"2022-05","story":"Shaving is an example of how consumer products extract more money","stance":"reference","why":"Author likely understates PFAS risk.","quote":"the author is likely significantly understating that particular point[0].","text":"> Also, claiming that [PFAS] \"seem to be poisonous\" without any qualifiers is a little misleading. To my knowledge they're only poisonous during manufacturing (ie. the precursor chemicals) and when they get too hot. It’s a bit difficult to exactly measure the effects of PFAS and how much of it makes it poisonous—when a study sought a control group of people without any in their blood for a study, they were not able to find a single living person on this planet that fits the definition anymore—but even with what we know the author is likely significantly understating that particular point[0]. Even if we ignore the poison released into the environment during manufacturing, I am yet to see a used Teflon pan that has no scratches (and that’s just the ones visible with naked eye). Combine with the fact that non-Teflon pans are becoming nearly impossible to find in many retail stores, and it becomes somewhat concerning. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31079000,"author":"bialpio","month":"2022-04","story":"3M tape with anisotropic z-axis electrical conductivity","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver piece on PFAS.","quote":"John Oliver also did a piece about PFAS","text":"Not sure if you'll consider this a more scientific source, but John Oliver also did a piece about PFAS: [VIDEO] . The list of issues that the wiki article claims are \"high certainty\" is also quite concerning to me: [LINK] At the very least, seems that the production is being ramped down - quick skim of the article yielded this quote: \"Their [PFASs'] production has been regulated or phased out by manufacturers, such as 3M, DuPont, Daikin, and Miteni in the US, Japan, and Europe.\""}],"r":[["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["dnHdqPBrtH8",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["P7GKK3liv8M",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["kdz6FeQLuHQ",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["Y99Wj-NStok",1],["-opBifFfsMY",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1],["pLPpl2ISKTg",1],["0XlO39kCQ-8",1],["1ffBJ4sVUb4",1]]},"BEG-ly9tQGk":{"wy":"Recommended as an entertaining example of rediscovered lost skills, though some commenters note it is contested.","q":{"x":"Lars Andersen taking archery to the next level by using forgotten skills he learned by looking at old art and various texts. Worth a watch!","i":8947280,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":33299200,"author":"mikkergp","month":"2022-10","story":"Archery World Record: Most arrows through a keyhole [video]","stance":"rec","why":"One of many archery feat videos on his channel","quote":"He has lots of videos in his YouTube channel showing various feats of archery, this is a pretty good one","text":"He has lots of videos in his YouTube channel showing various feats of archery, this is a pretty good one: [VIDEO] Maybe he faked all the videos and got the Guinness book of world records to put a fake record on their website, but they doesn’t seem like the simplest explanation."},{"id":16801918,"author":"gbtw","month":"2018-04","story":"What Medieval England Can Teach Us about Software Development","stance":"rec","why":"Series showing much conventional archery knowledge is wrong or incomplete","quote":"much what we know is either wrong or incomplete about archery. Lars Anderson made a nice series of videos on this","text":"Not to shit on your nice story :) but much what we know is either wrong or incomplete about archery. Lars Anderson made a nice series of videos on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16719506,"author":"xamuel","month":"2018-03","story":"Million-dollar Strads fall to modern violins in blind ‘sound check’","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining video on rediscovered archery technique","quote":"this entertaining video argues that archery might be an example","text":"Don't know how historically accurate it is, but this entertaining video argues that archery might be an example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8947280,"author":"rickdale","month":"2015-01","story":"Inside the world of longsword fighting","stance":"rec","why":"Archery skills revived from old art and texts","quote":"Lars Andersen taking archery to the next level by using forgotten skills he learned by looking at old art and various texts. Worth a watch!","text":"Semi-Related to this is Lars Andersen taking archery to the next level by using forgotten skills he learned by looking at old art and various texts. Worth a watch! [VIDEO]"},{"id":30394489,"author":"jart","month":"2022-02","story":"What do you really get from IDE-driven development?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I've always thought Lars Andersen's archery did a pretty good job making this point.","text":"I've always thought Lars Andersen's archery did a pretty good job making this point. [VIDEO] What will the crossbow wielding programmers of the future look like and who will be selling the crossbows?"},{"id":8936722,"author":"discardorama","month":"2015-01","story":"Lars Andersen: a new level of archery [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows arrows piercing chainmail","quote":"They do show his arrows piercing chainmail","text":"> Weaker stuff will not punch through even the worst armour at any kind of decent range. They do show his arrows piercing chainmail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8973726,"author":"nkurz","month":"2015-01","story":"In which I am crabby about viral archery videos","stance":"reference","why":"Video description addresses criticisms of the archery claims","quote":"Andersen's description on the original posting of the video addresses many of these criticisms.","text":"Andersen's description on the original posting of the video addresses many of these criticisms. I can't figure out how to link to it directly as text, but you can go to [VIDEO] , stop the video, and then click \"Show More\". Or view the copy below. --------------------------------------------------------- Published on Jan 23, 2015 The ultimate archery trick. Proving that Hollywood archery is not historical. Press release: [LINK] . Podcast about how I started archery: [LINK] . DO I HIT EVERYHING? I use a LOT of time practicing, and every time I set out to learn a new skill, a new trick or how to handle a new type of bow or arrow, it takes a long time, with plenty of misses. When I got the idea of grabbing an arrow in flight and firing before I landed, it took me months to learn. For a long time, arrows flew everywhere! But there's no trick in the video that I haven't done many times (except for splitting the arrow in flight – after I'd done that once I finished the video). The one with hitting the blade I've only done three times, though. All that running hurt my knees. ;-) ON MY BOWS AND POWER Many people talk about how what I do is only possible because I use bows that are less powerful than English longbows. They are correct. I'm 50 years old, and have been doing archery for only ten years. I'll never be able to shoot really fast with 100 lbs+ war bows. I tried, but it just produced injuries. Had I started at age 10, it would have been a different story. ;-) There is also a t"}],"r":[["3kEpZWGgJks",1]]},"IRVdiHu1VCc":{"wy":"It comes up in threads on career choice, trades, handiness and the 'follow your passion' advice.","q":{"x":"This guy is a big proponent of more down to earth careers and I agree with everything he says","i":9365140,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9365140,"author":"alexashka","month":"2015-04","story":"Best, Brightest – and Saddest?","stance":"rec","why":"Advocates down-to-earth careers beyond the intellectual track.","quote":"This guy is a big proponent of more down to earth careers and I agree with everything he says","text":"This is actually an even more extreme trend among some of the asian immigrant folks I've been in school with - their parents can be incredibly close-minded, overbearing and abusive. The pressure kids feel is largely fictional, in the sense that the world ISN'T going to end if they 'only go to college'. For example when I graduated highschool and went straight to college and some of the folks I knew stayed behind for an extra year to get their grades up - my parents in unison with me, thought they were failures. Looking back - it was smart. I am not working in the field that I studied, so the joke is on me :) Another thing to realize is that there are a number of jobs that are not intellectual that can be quite fulfilling, honestly. [VIDEO] This guy is a big proponent of more down to earth careers and I agree with everything he says, even though I'm a programmer. In short - the pressures are due to having a narrow view of the world and what success means. If you widen it to include working construction or going to college to become an ultrasound technician - it's not so bad all of a sudden. Parents just have to stop pushing their lack of achievements and phobias on to their kids - easier said than done of course. Solution? Incentivize intelligent people to breed more and try-hards and ignoramuses to breed less and the problem will be solved, in some number of decades :)"},{"id":5459253,"author":"InclinedPlane","month":"2013-03","story":"What Being a Handyman Has Taught Me About Male Insecurity","stance":"rec","why":"Mike Rowe on why fewer people are handy with their hands.","quote":"here's an excellent TED talk from Mike Rowe (from Dirty Jobs) on the subject","text":"I took AP classes in college, and I work in the software industry, but I still know how to work with my hands and I'm very happy to have that knowledge. It's not that hard, if you feel bad about not knowing anything, go learn, go take a class or two, go read a book or two, go ask a friend, or use the internet, there are a million excellent resources out there. Pick a simple project and do it, then do another project. Stocking a basic toolbox is extraordinarily inexpensive, there's really no reason not to have one. I think the reason so many people aren't as handy as they used to be has to do more with culture than with anything else, here's an excellent TED talk from Mike Rowe (from Dirty Jobs) on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5013741,"author":"shantanubala","month":"2013-01","story":"All My Life I’ve Been Told I Was Special. It Was A Lie.","stance":"rec","why":"Mike Rowe on how people choose careers and parental influence.","quote":"I think Mike Rowe explains it best","text":"I don't think anyone knows what they really like when they're young, and I believe even more firmly that parents are usually a better source of influence than peers. These are obviously generalizations, but I think Mike Rowe explains it best: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3732546,"author":"pbj","month":"2012-03","story":"Low pay low stress jobs/lifestyle","stance":"rec","why":"Mike Rowe on valuing hands-on, unglamorous jobs.","quote":"You might want to check out Mike Rowe's Ted talk, he touches on some of this.","text":"You might want to check out Mike Rowe's Ted talk, he touches on some of this. [VIDEO] I'm in the process of changing careers now. I tried doing the IT thing for several years and ended up hating it. There was a lot of poor work/life balance, stress with meeting deadlines, poor job security (I'm not a developer), etc. This was with several different companies so I don't think it was just poor luck. My passion is working with computers & technology but I realized one day that it just wasn't worth it for me to be so stressed out. So I spent some time researching careers and I'm now going back community college for something health related. The job security is outstanding, the stress is fairly low, and I actually get to directly help improve the lives of others every day. The pay isn't anything crazy but it's definitely enough to live comfortably on if you just want a modest lifestyle."},{"id":17526038,"author":"wcchandler","month":"2018-07","story":"‘Find Your Passion’ Is Awful Advice","stance":"reference","why":"Mike Rowe addresses the follow-your-passion advice around the 12 minute mark.","quote":"Just rewatched his TED talk and he brings it up around ~12 minutes in","text":"Mike Rowe has a short video on this: [LINK] Just rewatched his TED talk and he brings it up around ~12 minutes in: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9206098,"author":"raverbashing","month":"2015-03","story":"Antiwork – a radical shift in how we view “jobs”","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as relevant to the discussion of work and jobs.","quote":"Relevant","text":"Exactly As much as I'd like to work in a antiwork position, it's very complicated society-wise (especially due to the fact that there aren't that many jobs) Relevant [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["En_2T7KH6RA",1],["2Q26XIKtwXQ",1],["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["nBNTAmLRmUg",1],["qPKj0YnKANw",1]]},"drv3BP0Fdi8":{"wy":"HN recommends it in depression threads as a practical, non-medication approach with concrete takeaways.","q":{"x":"Two points I remember clearly taking from this lecture","i":30927294,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30927294,"author":"hsn915","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Beating depression with or without anti-depressants?","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture with practical takeaways like omega-3, walking, sunlight, social connection","quote":"Two points I remember clearly taking from this lecture","text":"I can't say I had full on depression, but I did go through periods where I spent more time in bad moods than good moods, and I didn't have a clear idea of where I was going in life. I remember watching this video [VIDEO] Two points I remember clearly taking from this lecture: - Omega-3. Make sure they are 1) high quality 2) high EPA content 3) over 1000mg of EPA per day - Walking. If you can spend more than one hour walking, do it. The other things I sort of knew, like sunlight and social connection. They are also very important, obviously. So, try to find a way, any way, to have some sort of social connection. Nothing deep. Just very casual and occasional. I used to go to meetup.com and look for things that interested me. At the time it was: pickup soccer, and language exchange. Not only I was interested in them, but they don't require social skills to participate in. You don't need to approach anyone. For language exchange, there's usually seat arrangement so the organizers will decide for you who you will talk to. So it's not like you're in a bar and have to work up the courage to approach people. This is very important. Same with soccer. There's no social skill required. The organizer will manage assigning you to a team. One thing to keep in mind: when you go to a meetup, do not talk about having depression. It's not a positive/welcoming topic. Keep it light and casual. And finally, this is just my personal take. It's possible that your job itself is a source of depressio"},{"id":29778191,"author":"rancar2","month":"2022-01","story":"Loving Someone with Depression","stance":"rec","why":"Approaches to treating depression without medication","quote":"here is a good TED talk on approaches without medication","text":"There are programs, non-profits, and alternative treatments depending on the country/region/state/city. If you are able to send US text messages for free to/from the US, you can use X2AI’s chatbot for free by texting “YC” to (650) 825-9634. It will at least get you access to CBT coaching, which has shown to help. Medication (once one finds the right one for themselves) helps many, but here is a good TED talk on approaches without medication: [VIDEO] Disclosure: I know the founder."},{"id":27545105,"author":"jointpdf","month":"2021-06","story":"I will never not talk about my depression","stance":"rec","why":"How lifestyle changes can help treat depression","quote":"For understanding how lifestyle changes can help treat depression, I recommend this","text":"On the topic of destigmatization, I recommend this Robert Sapolsky lecture (on the neurobiology of depression) to everyone to understand just how physical and systemic depression is: [LINK] . For understanding how lifestyle changes can help treat depression, I recommend this: [VIDEO] . He contradicts the author on the point of exercise—it is one of the most potent medicines (though hard to administer to yourself when you’re depressed). Reflecting on my own (extensive) experiences with depression and its co-conspirators, I think there are two deep fears that inhibit seeking or sticking with treatment: 1. What happens if I don’t get better? Then I’m hopeless. 2. What happens if I do get better (and it’s not enough, or I ruin my progress with a relapse)? I don’t have a definitive answer to either, but reasonable responses include: trying and failing feels better than languishing/surrendering (not trying is the only thing that’s likely hopeless), it is (or should be ) OK to make nonlinear progress in managing chronic illness (depression or otherwise), and the universe/existence is at once magnificent and meaningless so just try to relax and enjoy the ride with your fellow creatures."},{"id":8563839,"author":"Lifescape","month":"2014-11","story":"The poetry and brief life of a Foxconn worker: Xu Lizhi (1990-2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on understanding and coping with depression via lifestyle","quote":"This helped me","text":"Maybe depression can't be 'fixed', but you can learn to understand it and find ways to ignore it. This helped me: [VIDEO] It's different for everyone so it might not make a difference."},{"id":16217228,"author":"schackbrian","month":"2018-01","story":"Does Depression Have an Evolutionary Purpose? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Ilardi argues depression is a disease of modernity","quote":"Dr. Stephen Ilardi from the University of Kansas takes the opposite position.","text":"This reminded me of a Scientific American article from nine years ago about the evolutionary roots of depression. It says that depression is a useful adaptation: [LINK] Dr. Stephen Ilardi from the University of Kansas takes the opposite position. In his book, The Depression Cure, he says that depression is a disease of modernity. \"[O]ur bodies were never designed for the sleep-deprived, poorly nourished, frenzied pace of twenty-first century life.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":12441553,"author":"rak00n","month":"2016-09","story":"Most Depressed Adults in the U.S. Remain Untreated","stance":"reference","why":"Argues hunter-gatherer genes mismatch sedentary isolated modern life","quote":"The genes we got from our hunterer gatherer ancestors cannot sustain sedentary isolated life we live today.","text":"I saw this TedTalk[0] earlier that vouches this idea. The genes we got from our hunterer gatherer ancestors cannot sustain sedentary isolated life we live today. [0] [VIDEO] ."}],"r":[["pgoetgxecw8",1],["IJiHDmyhE1A",1],["-suTmUX4Vbk",1]]},"jqjacHSTd48":{"wy":"HN commenters call it a passionate and educational history of locks and physical security, and a good starting point for the subject.","q":{"x":"It's a great place to start.","i":8476426,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":12928468,"author":"brownbat","month":"2016-11","story":"China’s ‘Artificial Sun’ achieves fusion breakthrough","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing discussion on European views of American lock inventions.","quote":"Schuyler Towne has an amazing discussion of this","text":"And the US in the early 1800s. Schuyler Towne has an amazing discussion of this, how Europeans routinely were laughing at American inventions... until they suddenly very much weren't. It's in this talk. [VIDEO] I'd link directly to the relevant five minutes but it's hard to justify doing that sort of harm, depriving you of the other great 45 minutes there. (Ok, let's say it's 34ish to 41ish. Still, that entire thing is probably better than the other way you were going to spend some 50 minute block today.)"},{"id":8476426,"author":"steakejjs","month":"2014-10","story":"A difficult lock to pick [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Passionate, educational history of locks; great starting point.","quote":"It's a great place to start.","text":"If you ever listen to people who really love locks talk about locks they all seem to say one thing. \"All locks are pickable [or attackable]\". This just means that this particular lock is abnormal and most lock-people don't have the experience to attack it, not that it is secure. If you are semi-interested in locks, I really recommend listening to Schuyler Towne talk about locks. He's one of those people who is very passionate about what he does in a way that interest in locks really rubs off onto you in a really educational and relatable way. Here's a longer talk about the history of locks by him from 2012. It's a great place to start. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23336301,"author":"brownbat","month":"2020-05","story":"Guerrilla Public Service Redux (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"History of physical security and assumptions about perfect security.","quote":"Schuyler Towne has (several?) great talks on this","text":"Schuyler Towne has (several?) great talks on this, going through the history of physical security and our assumptions about perfect security vs. social and cultural symbols. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8233262,"author":"emhart","month":"2014-08","story":"3-D Printed Keys Can Pick High-Security Locks in Seconds","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter's own talk on why people lock doors.","quote":"","text":"I've researched the \"why\" and a couple years ago had the opportunity to discuss some of the broader ideas in the history & anthropology of locks. It was actually titled \"Why do you lock your door?\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2LyzBoHo5EI",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["Hatav_Rdnno",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1]]},"knUitQQnpJo":{"wy":"HN readers recommend it as a thorough, practical introduction to Postgres fundamentals and optimization for application developers.","q":{"x":"I found this to be great introductory that touches on all the major points to keep in mind with Postgres.","i":12470200,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12470200,"author":"elmigranto","month":"2016-09","story":"Things I Hate About PostgreSQL (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great, up-to-date introduction covering vacuuming, MVCC, replication, and backups.","quote":"I found this to be great introductory that touches on all the major points to keep in mind with Postgres.","text":"I found this to be great introductory that touches on all the major points to keep in mind with Postgres. It's pretty up-to-date too! [VIDEO] It isn't quite what you ask for, but should be enough to get you high-level overview of major systems and contains solid practical advices (on vacuuming, mvcc, replication, backups, etc…), so you know what to google for deeper understanding."},{"id":11921324,"author":"insertnickname","month":"2016-06","story":"PG Casts – Postgres Screencasts","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers the basics of setting up Postgres and more.","quote":"I highly recommend this tutorial by Christophe Pettus from PyCon","text":"For this, I highly recommend this tutorial by Christophe Pettus from PyCon: [VIDEO] It covers the basics of setting up Postgres as well as other things."},{"id":12353755,"author":"ceyhunkazel","month":"2016-08","story":"Practical Guide to PostgreSQL Optimizations","stance":"rec","why":"PostgreSQL basics and optimization.","quote":"I recommend following Christophe Pettus - PostgreSQL Proficiency for Python People - PyCon 2016 video for postgresql basics and optimization","text":"I recommend following Christophe Pettus - PostgreSQL Proficiency for Python People - PyCon 2016 video for postgresql basics and optimization [VIDEO]"}]},"9R5OWh7luL4":{"wy":"HN cites it as the classic demonstration of survivorship and confirmation bias in betting, trading and prediction claims.","q":{"x":"Here's an entertaining video from Derren Brown that shows how to use this technique to convince people you have psychic powers","i":8324346,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8596664,"author":"Fuzzwah","month":"2014-11","story":"The Day I Lost a Shit-ton of Money, Part I","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Derren Brown's horse-betting special","quote":"This reminds me of Derren Brown's awesome \"The System\" special","text":"This reminds me of Derren Brown's awesome \"The System\" special: [VIDEO] More info for anyone who hasn't had the pleasure of knowing who Derren Brown is: [LINK] Derren Brown is a British illusionist, mentalist, trickster, hypnotist, painter, writer, and sceptic. He is known for his appearances in television specials, stage productions, and British television series such as Trick of the Mind and Trick or Treat. Since the first broadcast of his show Derren Brown: Mind Control in 2000, Brown has become increasingly well known for his mind-reading act. He has written books for magicians as well as the general public. Though his performances of mind-reading and other feats of mentalism may appear to be the result of psychic or paranormal practices, he claims no such abilities and frequently denounces those who do. Brown states at the beginning of his Trick of the Mind programmes that he achieves his results using a combination of \"magic, suggestion, psychology, misdirection and showmanship\"."},{"id":8821251,"author":"sparkzilla","month":"2014-12","story":"A Magician’s Best Trick: Revealing a Basic Human Bias","stance":"rec","why":"Good example of confirmation bias via a fake foolproof betting system","quote":"For a good example of confirmation bias watch Derren Brown's \"The System","text":"For a good example of confirmation bias watch Derren Brown's \"The System\" [1] where he makes a woman believe an unknown benefactor has a foolproof system for betting on horses. \"Mail Order Prophet\" [2] by Alfred Hitchock has a similar idea. [1] [VIDEO] ] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":8324346,"author":"sgdesign","month":"2014-09","story":"How to see into the future?","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining demonstration of survivorship bias faking psychic powers","quote":"Here's an entertaining video from Derren Brown that shows how to use this technique to convince people you have psychic powers","text":"I don't know if your assessment of the study is valid (it seems a little simplistic to think that they wouldn't account for this to be honest), but what you describe is classic Survivorship Bias. Here's an entertaining video from Derren Brown that shows how to use this technique to convince people you have psychic powers: [VIDEO] Edit: also see the comment about the Miracle Man scam in this thread."},{"id":14955038,"author":"szemet","month":"2017-08","story":"Not even wrong: Why does nobody like pilot-wave theory? [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"On the other hand objective randomness is a black box. You can describe how it behaves at the surface level (so functionally it is usable), but by definition you can't have a finite model of it, so what is inside? No answer by definition - something infinite. So why X happens instead of Y? 1. Let's propose an unexplainable, unmodellable infinite entropy source under everything vs. 2. X happens and so do Y, and one is observed at a certain probability due to anthropic principle - like this: [VIDEO] The first is somehow psychologically less satisfying answer for me, although - of course - I accept that calculation works either way. The second at least not an unimaginable infinite, and in the end leaves me with less \"why?\"."},{"id":12415235,"author":"coriny","month":"2016-09","story":"Maybe Blockchain Really Does Have Magical Powers","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates the effect being discussed","quote":"Nicely demonstrated in Derren Brown's \"The System\"","text":"Nicely demonstrated in Derren Brown's \"The System\": [VIDEO] . Though we've rather ruined the twist ..."},{"id":6832909,"author":"salvadors","month":"2013-12","story":"Why I don't trade stocks and (probably) neither should you","stance":"reference","why":"Derren Brown convinces people he can predict horse race winners","quote":"Derren Brown used this approach to convince people he had a system for predicting the winners of horse races","text":"Derren Brown used this approach to convince people he had a system for predicting the winners of horse races: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5wAo54DHDY0",1],["vvr9AMWEU-c",1]]},"C-kF25fWTO8":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a clear, enjoyable introduction to Clojure and a good example of REPL-driven development.","q":{"x":"gave one of the most clear and enjoyable technical introductions to Clojure I've seen. Recommended!","i":37314881,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37314881,"author":"Lyngbakr","month":"2023-08","story":"Griffin - A fully-regulated, API-driven bank, with Clojure","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear, enjoyable technical introduction to Clojure","quote":"gave one of the most clear and enjoyable technical introductions to Clojure I've seen. Recommended!","text":"James Trunk, who is now Griffin's VP of Engineering, gave one of the most clear and enjoyable technical introductions to Clojure I've seen. Recommended! [VIDEO]"},{"id":28865559,"author":"kaliszad","month":"2021-10","story":"What I wish I knew when learning F#","stance":"rec","why":"Very nice live coding example in Clojure","quote":"I have found this video by James Trunk: to have a very nice live coding example.","text":"There are multiple options to approach the Clojure* ecosystem. But yes, it is more focused towards dedicated and experienced developers overall. I use IntelliJ Idea + Cursive for development and that seems quite comfortable. I have a very rough sketch of the environment setup for a real app here: [LINK] We use a similar setup to develop OrgPad itself. You can use Babashka [LINK] for a quick and dirty setup to get you up and running quickly. That is also useful for scripting some things. You might also try shadow-cljs + ClojureScript, if you are more at home in the Node.js/ Browser JavaScript ecosystem and want to do something with it. There is a new \"(Not)Babashka\" but built on ClojureScript: [LINK] that might be interesting as well. I have found this video by James Trunk: [VIDEO] to have a very nice live coding example."},{"id":26651014,"author":"mustermannBB","month":"2021-03","story":"How much can a Clojure developer do alone?","stance":"rec","why":"Good example of REPL-driven development","quote":"I like this video. IMHO it is a good example of REPL driven development.","text":"I like this video. IMHO it is a good example of REPL driven development. Nothing to do with typing things into the REPL. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SxdOUGdseq4",1]]},"CpCrkO1x-Qo":{"wy":"Commenters link it in Tesla threads as an informed, hands-on engineering view of the Model 3 and its manufacturing.","q":{"x":"If you're interested, this video is great","i":17544345,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17544345,"author":"deelowe","month":"2018-07","story":"Munro Teardown Shows Tesla Model 3 Solidly Profitable [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lessons on scale manufacturing from established automakers","quote":"If you're interested, this video is great","text":"> The $35k base model made at scale later this year will be profitable Here's the thing. SV Tech companies aren't too great at the whole \"at scale\" manufacturing thing. Maybe Tesla can figure it out, but they need to pay more attention to what the \"boring dinosaurs\" are doing this this regard. If you're interested, this video is great: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17360805,"author":"tokipin","month":"2018-06","story":"Tesla sues ex-employee for hacking, theft, and leaking to the press","stance":"rec","why":"Teardown discussion on battery packs","quote":"See this[1] fun teardown discussion.","text":"The cells and the packs are different things. The packs aren't just a bunch of cells glued together. See this[1] fun teardown discussion. Another company building battery packs would have different costs even if they were buying the same exact cells from Panasonic, and it's likely Tesla had a hand in designing the cells so I don't know if that's particularly possible. The reason for the Gigafactory is to compress supply chains, but more importantly because there isn't enough global demand/supply for batteries to support the volumes Tesla wants. Without building their own factory, the global supply would be a constraining factor for Tesla's plans. The Gigafactory is a long-term bet that they will need that much supply in the future. This is not a problem that other carmakers have where they would want to \"vertically integrate\" oil refineries and gas stations, because availability of gasoline is not a problem. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17383969,"author":"cepth","month":"2018-06","story":"Elon Musk emails employees about 'extensive and damaging sabotage' by employee","stance":"rec","why":"Munro on Model 3 build quality versus impressive underbody","quote":"I think this section of the video from Sandy Munro is worth a watch","text":"It could certainly be that the German automakers are more concerned about present profitability. That could easily turn into falling victim to the classic innovator's dilemma. It seems that by year end, both the Model S and Model 3 will have taken significant market share from their European competitors: [LINK] . The cannibalization is real, and that's why luxury automakers like BMW have announced plans for 12 fully-electric models by 2025 ( [LINK] ). Your points about electrical infrastructure are definitely thought-provoking ones. I'd agree that the infrastructure as of today doesn't exist for widespread adoption. At the same time, the market that Tesla and the luxury European automakers compete for today doesn't hit that 5% threshold ( [LINK] ). Add up the market share of car (not truck) sales from BMW, Mercedes, Audi, Porsche, and you're looking at a ~3% market share. I think this section of the video from Sandy Munro is worth a watch: [VIDEO] . Munro has been a huge critic of the build quality of the Model 3, but that everything \"below the skateboard [the floor]\" is seriously impressive. The cells in Tesla's battery pack have a maximum of a 0.2 milliamp difference between cells. Say that I accept your premise that the German automakers today are biding their time, and could spend their R&D money on electric cars if they so chose. Who will they source their battery packs from? Will they try to build their own supply chain for the raw materials? What competence have they d"},{"id":16893007,"author":"csours","month":"2018-04","story":"To Understand the Future of Tesla, Look to the History of GM","stance":"rec","why":"Parallel on over-automation in car manufacturing","quote":"(the whole thing is worth watching if you are into this sort of thing)","text":"Another parallel: Over-automation [VIDEO] (the whole thing is worth watching if you are into this sort of thing)"},{"id":16945532,"author":"olivermarks","month":"2018-04","story":"Tesla's Simultaneous Brilliance and Incompetence Revealed in Teardown of Model 3","stance":"rec","why":"90-minute Model 3 discussion with Munro and others","quote":"90 minute discussion of Tesla 3 with Munro and others on the excellent autoline network youtube channel","text":"[VIDEO] 90 minute discussion of Tesla 3 with Munro and others on the excellent autoline network youtube channel ('Really heavy, really expensive')"},{"id":48586525,"author":"FeloniousHam","month":"2026-06","story":"How to Earn a Billion Dollars","stance":"reference","why":"Munro's first Model 3 teardown","quote":"I'll just link to his first teardown of the Model 3[1].","text":"I don't disagree, and most of what I know about Tesla early history comes from the Isaacson biography, but: it seems pretty clear the \"actual\" founders would never have been able to clear the hurdle of scale production without him. It wasn't just the funding (which would have been hard to come by), it's really hard to build a large, real-things-in-the-world industrial company from scratch, doubly so for something as complex as a car with all the complexities supply chains, regulations, literal factory construction require. All glory to the guys who started the company (I love my Tesla Y), but I don't believe it would have gone anywhere without Musk. (I was add something about Stacy Munro and the auto industry, but I'll just link to his first teardown of the Model 3[1].) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17790416,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2018-08","story":"Wall Street analysts were blown away by Model 3's next-gen, military-grade tech","stance":"reference","why":"Munro says Model 3 is solidly profitable","quote":"is of the opinion that the Model 3 is \"solidly profitable\".","text":"Munro & Associates, one of the most respected teardown and costing consultancies in the automotive industry, is of the opinion that the Model 3 is \"solidly profitable\". They have previously been harshly critical of Tesla and remain unimpressed with the fit-and-finish of the Model 3, particularly the large and inconsistent panel gaps. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17360865,"author":"kjksf","month":"2018-06","story":"Tesla sues ex-employee for hacking, theft, and leaking to the press","stance":"reference","why":"Munro teardown sold to competitors","quote":"Munro & Associates did a complete Tesla 3 teardown and disassembly","text":"Daimler (parent of Mercedes) reportedly rented Model X and disassembled it to learn how it works. [LINK] Munro & Associates did a complete Tesla 3 teardown and disassembly in order to sell what they've learned to Tesla competitors for hundreds of thousands of dollars ( [VIDEO] ) So did some German engineering company ( [LINK] ). Apparently there are plenty of companies interested in how Tesla's cars are made."},{"id":16892235,"author":"martythemaniak","month":"2018-04","story":"To Understand the Future of Tesla, Look to the History of GM","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There will come a time when Musk needs to step away from Tesla, but that day is not today. He's publicly mused about stepping away (SpaceX is his real fav), but had his tenure renewed recently. If all Tesla did was sell pretty good cars, they'd probably get crushed by the incumbents. Tesla, is selling way more than that - they're selling the idea of a brighter, better future. You're not just buying an EV, but you're helping climate change, you're reducing pollution, you'll be reducing human death and suffering and ending traffic jams and hey - it all comes in a exclusive, technologically-advanced, aesthetically pleasing package. Now, some might object that this is largely a bunch of marketing/PR bullshit, and you will likely be technically correct, but would still miss the point. If people wanted a nice, efficient EV, they'd buy the Bolt, which by all accounts, is pretty damn good. But Tesla sells this \"bullshit\" because it's what people actually want to buy, and EVs happen to be the delivery vehicle. So as much as you might dislike this \"bullshit\", it's a core reason why Tesla even exists in 2018. Where does Musk fit into this? He happens to be the personification of this idea today. In the popular mind he is \"cool\" so when you buy a Tesla, you're also implicitly buying part of this cool, much like buying an iPhone back in the day got you a part of Jobs' cool. Eventually Tesla will become it's own thing (as Apple is today) and outgrow Musk, but that's still years away. OTOH,"},{"id":16894153,"author":"bsder","month":"2018-04","story":"To Understand the Future of Tesla, Look to the History of GM","stance":"reference","why":"Munro praises Model 3 skateboard and suspension","quote":"Anybody that's in the car industry that ignores this car is doing it at their own peril.","text":"He also thinks that the the suspension system is amazing and comments that anything having to do with the \"skateboard\" (floorboards, suspension, lower chassis) are absolutely phenomenally good. So, you have a car with highly advanced electronics that drives spectacularly well but has lousy fit and finish. That combination has produced a lot of very profitable cars over the years. Basically, the stuff that can be improved gradually got pushed down on the list while the core stuff is correct from start. [VIDEO] \"Anybody that's in the car industry that ignores this car is doing it at their own peril.\""},{"id":16942860,"author":"cowsandmilk","month":"2018-04","story":"Tesla's Simultaneous Brilliance and Incompetence Revealed in Teardown of Model 3","stance":"reference","why":"Munro discussing the Model 3 teardown","quote":"And here's him discussing the Model 3 teardown on Autoline","text":"And here's him discussing the Model 3 teardown on Autoline (don't see a link to this in the article) [VIDEO]"},{"id":16944319,"author":"slantyyz","month":"2018-04","story":"Tesla's Simultaneous Brilliance and Incompetence Revealed in Teardown of Model 3","stance":"reference","why":"Munro on quality control processes behind automaker manufacturing know-how","quote":"are not about advances in tooling/tech, but advances in quality control processes","text":"> The fact that Tesla can simultaneously demonstrate brilliance and incompetence in the same vehicle is not surprising, considering it is a high-tech software company trying to master complex manufacturing processes that took established automakers more than 100 years to perfect. >> This is wrong. At the very least it would be since the 60's and probably later. If you watch the video on Youtube (that someone else posted here) where Munro is talking about the Model 3, (jump to 36m20s, he gets into it about 15-20s later [1]), you'll realize that the \"complex manufacturing processes that took established automakers more than 100 years to perfect\" are not about advances in tooling/tech, but advances in quality control processes. If you don't want to watch the video, the acronyms Munro drops are AIAG, PPS, PPAP and APQP. Things get even more interesting when someone on the panel asks him what would have happened if Tesla would have subbed out the design and manufacturing to a company like Magna. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Zk11vI-7czE",1],["8455KEDitpU",1],["np_ylvc8Zj8",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1]]},"PFUZlNQIndo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the definitive talk from the implementer on DuckDB's motivation and design.","q":{"x":"one of the implementers giving a talk to CMU students about what DuckDB is, why they made it, and how it works","i":23287914,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":47761139,"author":"mrtimo","month":"2026-04","story":"Design and Implementation of DuckDB Internals","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic talk making the case for DuckDB in data cleaning.","quote":"The original video from CMU in 2020 [1] is a classic.","text":"If you are a data scientist or do anything with data... duckdb is like a swiss army knife. So many great ways it can help your workflow. The original video from CMU in 2020 [1] is a classic. Minutes 3-8 present a good argument for adding duckdb to your data cleaning/processing workflow. And if you want to add a semantic layer on top of data, Malloy [2] is my favorite so far (it has duckdb built in): [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":42567737,"author":"mrtimo","month":"2025-01","story":"Databases in 2024: A Year in Review","stance":"rec","why":"Argues why data scientists avoid RDBMS, DuckDB's origin.","quote":"he makes a convincing argument (in 5 minutes) why data scientists don't use RDBMS","text":"Enjoyed his roundup in the \"Shoving Ducks into Everything\" section. DuckDB is a great tool. In April 2020, the creator of DuckDB gave a talk at CMU. In the beginning he makes a convincing argument (in 5 minutes) why data scientists don't use RDBMS and how this was the genesis of DuckDB. Here is a video that starts 3 minutes into the talk (where is argument starts): [VIDEO]"},{"id":23287197,"author":"kyllo","month":"2020-05","story":"SQLite 3.32","stance":"rec","why":"Implementer explains why DuckDB was made, what it is for, how it works.","quote":"a talk given by one of the DuckDB implementers about why they made it, what it's for, and how it works","text":"I was wondering the same--I guess because it's fairly new (2018) and it came out of a database research group at a European university, rather than a SV tech firm. Therefore, limited marketing budget. By the way, here's a YT video of a talk given by one of the DuckDB implementers about why they made it, what it's for, and how it works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23287914,"author":"kyllo","month":"2020-05","story":"DuckDB: SQLite for Analytics","stance":"rec","why":"Implementer's talk on what DuckDB is, why built, how it works.","quote":"one of the implementers giving a talk to CMU students about what DuckDB is, why they made it, and how it works","text":"Here's a YT video of one of the implementers giving a talk to CMU students about what DuckDB is, why they made it, and how it works: [VIDEO] In a nutshell, it's a file-per-database embedded DB like SQLite, and it's installable as a Python or R package from PyPI or CRAN, but unlike SQLite, it's a column store with vectorized operations so it's more performant for analytical queries."},{"id":45127389,"author":"mrtimo","month":"2025-09","story":"Polars Cloud and Distributed Polars now available","stance":"reference","why":"DuckDB creator argues pandas users miss decades of database research.","quote":"The creator of duckdb argues that people using pandas are missing out of the 50 years of progress in database research","text":"I agree with this 100%. The creator of duckdb argues that people using pandas are missing out of the 50 years of progress in database research, in the first 5 minutes of his talk here [1]. I've been using Malloy [2], which compiles to SQL (like Typescript compiles to Javascript), so instead of editing a 1000 line SQL script, it's only 18 lines of Malloy. I'd love to see a blog post comparing a pandas approach to cleaning to an SQL/Malloy approach. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":26151653,"author":"snidane","month":"2021-02","story":"Many small queries are efficient in SQLite","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"If you're using sqlite, but for a static website, you might consider using duckdb. It's still evolving, but it has huge potential. It's also an embedded single file database like sqlite, but stores data in columns. Duckdb to sqlite is like redshift to postgres. Hopefully they will add compression for string columns, so that host your entire static site from a single file in a very efficient way. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24535407,"author":"snthpy","month":"2020-09","story":"DuckDB – An embeddable SQL database like SQLite, but supports Postgres features","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"DuckDB looks very interesting and I'm quite excited to examine it more closely in the next few days! I just wanted to add to the discussion that an unchanging file format, or at least a backwards compatible one, is a key feature of sqlite. See for example Richard Hipp's comments here [1] (I think he also mentioned earlier in the talk that the file format has become a limiting factor now in terms of some of the refactoring that they can do). The file format therefore seems likely to be a major factor in the long term success of this project and I am glad to see that you are taking your time before settling on any architecture here. Given that you are targeting the data science and analytics space, what are your plans for integration with arrow and the feather file format? From a purely user/developer perspective, arrow's aim of shared memory data structures across different analytics tools, seems like a great goal. I know Wes McKinney and Ursa Labs have also spent quite some time at the file storage part of this, see for example the Feather V2 announcement [2]. What are your thoughts on the tradeoffs they considered and how do you see the requirements of DuckDB in relation to theirs? From the Carnegie Mellon DuckDB talk [3], I saw that you already have a zero-copy reader to the pandas memory data structures, so the vision I have is that DuckDB could be the universal SQL interface to arrow datasets which can then also be shared with more complex ML models. Is that something tha"}]},"YycAzdtUIko":{"wy":"HN commenters point to the PBS Space Time series as a high-quality, bite-sized way to learn modern physics concepts.","q":{"x":"they have an episode that would be a good place to start on warp drives","i":25231631,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25231631,"author":"tzs","month":"2020-11","story":"Warp Drive News","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short PBS Space Time episodes on speed of causality","quote":"it is a great thing to watch when you've got a short wait for something.","text":"There's an episode of PBS Space Time about this, \"The Speed of Light is NOT About Light\" [1], which comes pretty close to making the same points you are making. It might be useful to watch this earlier episode, \"Are Space and Time an Illusion?\" [2], first. There is a later episode (or maybe a couple of them) that go into how interactions with the Higgs field make it so things with mass travel slowed than the speed of causality. To tie back to the warp drive news, they have an episode that would be a good place to start on warp drives, \"Is The Alcubierre Warp Drive Possible?\" [3]. There's also \"Superluminal Time Travel + Time Warp Challenge Answer\" [4], and \"Will Wormholes Allow Fast Interstellar Travel?\" [5]. A non-warp unknown physics drive that comes up a lot also got an episode, \"The EM Drive: Fact or Fantasy?\" [6]. There is at least one episode covering interstellar travel without using any unknown physics, \"5 REAL Possibilities for Interstellar Travel\" [7]. The scope of the series is basically anything vaguely connected to astronomy, cosmology, and quantum mechanics, and the episodes are all pretty short (5-12 minutes), so it is a great thing to watch when you've got a short wait for something. Here's the series home page [8], and their YouTube channel [9]. Also on the PBS streaming app, for those who like to get their physics on their big screen TVs. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK] [8] [LINK] [9] [LINK]"},{"id":13687614,"author":"neom","month":"2017-02","story":"Theories about a link between the human mind and quantum physics","stance":"rec","why":"Foundational viewing list on quantum and the universe","quote":"here are some good ones around humans and the universe.","text":"I spend pretty much every weekend watching (super dyslexic) hours and hours of \"stuff\" about quantum, form quantum biology to physics to astro, here are some good ones around humans and the universe. Some may not seem totally relevant but give foundational knowledge, suggest watching in order: - [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK]"},{"id":13097052,"author":"spuz","month":"2016-12","story":"Quantum Gravity’s Time Problem","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent PBS Space Time series explains reference frames","quote":"this playlist of the excellent PBS Space Time series","text":"The speed of light does not change for each given reference frame, i.e. it is constant. However, space and time are observed differently depending on a given reference frame. With regards to your second question, photons experience no time whatsoever. From the photon's perspective, they travel the universe from the point of emission to the point of absorption instantly. If you want to understand this, I highly suggest watching this video explaining reference frames: [LINK] And this playlist of the excellent PBS Space Time series: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13687765,"author":"Rainymood","month":"2017-02","story":"Theories about a link between the human mind and quantum physics","stance":"reference","why":"Reformatted list of video series","quote":"","text":"Good post, I made it a bit more legible Are Space and Time An Illusion? | Space Time | PBS Digital Studios [VIDEO] The Geometry of Causality | Space Time [LINK] The Quantum Experiment that Broke Reality | Space Time | PBS Digital Studios [LINK] Quantum Biology: An Introduction [LINK] Quantum Biology: The Hidden Nature of Nature [LINK] Consciousness: Explored and Explained [LINK] The Mind After Midnight: Where Do You Go When You Go to Sleep? [LINK] Rebooting the Cosmos: Is the Universe the Ultimate Computer? [LINK]"}],"r":[["msVuCEs8Ydo",2],["9XkHBmE-N34",1],["2JAOTJxYqh8",1],["RlXdsyctD50",1],["AZX6awZq5Z0",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["mht-1c4wc0Q",1],["xRel1JKOEbI",1],["umatbZ0n4mE",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["G0Q4UAiKacw",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["KePNhUJ2reI",1],["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["94ed4v_T6YM",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["jyQwgBAaBag",1],["RkTvDjhImwo",1]]},"gsffg5xxFQI":{"wy":"Recommended to JavaScript skeptics as a persuasive case for ClojureScript, covering Closure Compiler optimizations and code-splitting.","q":{"x":"completely sold me on trying it out","i":14421628,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14421628,"author":"rockmeamedee","month":"2017-05","story":"Is Clojure dying, and what has Ruby got to do with it?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lays out ClojureScript pros: standard library, immutability, Closure Compiler optimizations, code-splitting.","quote":"completely sold me on trying it out","text":"I just finished watching this talk \"ClojureScript for Skeptics\" ( [VIDEO] ) that laid out the pros of ClojureScript and completely sold me on trying it out. Weird seeing this article about Clojure dying right after, too. In this talk I watched, the speaker argues for ClojureScript for SPAs because it has: * an excellent standard library (great functional programming so no need for lodash or other hacks, no issues with things like map(Integer.parse), solid date support, etc) * Go-level easy concurrency * Immutable types built-in for a fast React experience (immutable.js by default) * built-in Google Closure Compiler for optimizing, (also with sourcemaps and devtools) * built-in tree-shaking (only import what you use) from Google Closure Compiler * built-in code-splitting (don't import the code for the settings page on the home page, automatically load it when the user goes there), also from Google Closure Compiler And the talk is from 2015! So there's probably been even more added I don't know about yet. Then again, in 2017 I feel like we have an embarrassment of riches of programming languages. I'm already playing around with Elixir (loving it), wanna try out Rust's zero-cost everythings, and have finally finishing learning Haskell on my back burner."},{"id":15054856,"author":"christophilus","month":"2017-08","story":"React and Redux are a joke right?","stance":"rec","why":"Supports claim that ClojureScript code size is competitive for complex apps.","quote":"I suggest watching this","text":"Clojurescript's bundle size for real world apps is decent, especially given the power it provides. If all you're doing is a 3 line JavaScript, Clojurescript won't benefit you. But if you're writing a complex application,it will beat or match your typical JS stack in terms of code size. I suggest watching this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10672661,"author":"edko","month":"2015-12","story":"What Holds Me Back from ClojureScript","stance":"rec","why":"Talk addressing common skepticism and tooling concerns about ClojureScript.","quote":"Derek Slager's presentation \"ClosureScript for Skeptics\" is really good and covers many of these topics","text":"ClojureScript has some very powerful tools available (figwheel, om/next), and the community is working on resolving many of the issues mentioned in the article. Derek Slager's presentation \"ClosureScript for Skeptics\" is really good and covers many of these topics ( [VIDEO] ). Myself, I prefer ScalaJS because I already know Scala, the tooling is excellent, it is fast, and the resulting JS is of reasonable size. Fortunately, it is not an either/or decision. Both compile to JS and can interoperate without too much effort."},{"id":10801957,"author":"Flow","month":"2015-12","story":"JavaScript Fatigue","stance":"rec","why":"Shows ClojureScript as a saner alternative to JS tooling sprawl, with modularization section.","quote":"Notice the modularization section that starts around 32m.","text":"I once tried Yeoman. Angular was the hottest thing ever and I was recommended to use Yeoman to set up a template project. The damn thing downloaded 30000 files into my project. It took a while, but nothing I really noticed until I saw dropbox working for 40 minutes. I just can't take some projects seriously after that. Anyway, compare your problems and effort to things like this: Building a tictactoe game in ClojureScript with reagent and figwheel: [LINK] ClojureScript for Skeptics: [VIDEO] Notice the modularization section that starts around 32m."},{"id":13117219,"author":"espeed","month":"2016-12","story":"Closure Compiler in JavaScript","stance":"reference","why":"Cites talk as making the case about the remaining Java dependency.","quote":"Derek Slager made a case for this in his talk at the Conj","text":"True, but it helps fix the TTFX metric -- \"Time to First XML\" ;-) -- Derek Slager made a case for this in his talk at the Conj: \"ClojureScript for Skeptics\" [VIDEO] The remaining bit of Java is like \"a little piece of kelp that's stuck to the hull, and even though it's little, you don't want anything stuck to the hull\" ( [LINK] ). TTFX 1: [LINK]"},{"id":13202536,"author":"ekzy","month":"2016-12","story":"The Next Five Years of ClojureScript","stance":"reference","why":"Offers the talk as a pointer alongside a remark that ClojureScript is hard to learn.","quote":"Clojurescript for skeptics","text":"The only problem with clojurescript is that it is much harder to learn than most of the other JS framework out there. Simple is not easy, remember? Clojurescript for skeptics: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Ljzj9Msli5o",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["dK3zRXhrFZY",1],["iw17c70uJes",1]]},"wwmOkaKh3-s":{"wy":"Recommended as a prescient interview on free trade and its social consequences, worth watching with hindsight.","q":{"x":"I encourage everyone who cares about this space to watch the 1994 Charlie Rose episode","i":28737880,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28737880,"author":"ItsMonkk","month":"2021-10","story":"Nobody Really Knows How the Economy Works. A Fed Paper Is the Latest Sign","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Urges everyone to watch the 1994 interview.","quote":"I encourage everyone who cares about this space to watch the 1994 Charlie Rose episode","text":"I encourage everyone who cares about this space to watch the 1994 Charlie Rose episode with Sir James Goldsmith[0]. He nailed it. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12891436,"author":"sprafa","month":"2016-11","story":"How Economic Gobbledygook Divides Us","stance":"rec","why":"Predicts free trade disrupting capital-labor balance.","quote":"he correctly predicts that the global movement of capital will disrupt the current agreement between capital and labor","text":"You should look at Jonathan Goldsmith's appearance on Charlie Rose. [VIDEO] Free Trade is not always good, and he correctly predicts that the global movement of capital will disrupt the current agreement between capital and labor that served the West so well. Donald Trump is wrong on many issues, but this is not one of them as far as I can tell. There a religiosity to the argument that free trade is \"always good\", even though high performing Asian economies are somewhat protective. Economics at its current understanding is almost like a religion of capitalism. It has little to do with actual economic behaviour and has very little predictive power. That's why we keep having crises. Look into the work of anthropologists and behavioural economists to see the reality of how humans actually behave and what that means for \"classical economics\". Basically, most of it is bunk, all the way down to Adam Smith. One of the best references I've read on this so far is \"Debt the first 5000 years\" \"How Asia Works\" On behavioural economics, there's studies coming out all the time. The evidence is piling up that little of what economists have said makes sense. They've deducted general guidelines that sort of work at the macro scale, but their fundamentals are mostly wrong."},{"id":10879114,"author":"walterbell","month":"2016-01","story":"In 2016, let's hope for better trade agreements – and the death of TPP","stance":"rec","why":"Classic 1994 interview on GATT/NAFTA with decades of hindsight.","quote":"For historical context on trade and society, see the classic (1994) Charlie Rose interview","text":"For historical context on trade and society, see the classic (1994) Charlie Rose interview of Sir James Goldsmith on GATT/NAFTA, now that we have 20 years of experimental results, [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["6UsHHOCH4q8",1],["wfm2xCKOCNk",1]]},"2JAOTJxYqh8":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a fun, research-grounded explainer whenever bed bug threads come up.","q":{"x":"mark rober did a good entertaining video on this","i":39329294,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42571369,"author":"dataviz1000","month":"2025-01","story":"Passengers Say Turkish Airlines Flights Have Unwelcome Guests: Bedbugs","stance":"rec","why":"Research-based bed bug eradication, with facts like no disease transmission.","quote":"Here is a fun YouTube video about research based eradication of bed bugs","text":"Here is a fun YouTube video about research based eradication of bed bugs [0] Lots of facts like bed bugs don't carry or transmit diseases. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39329294,"author":"123pie123","month":"2024-02","story":"Getting rid of bed bugs: trickier than ever","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining video on getting rid of bed bugs and what does not work.","quote":"mark rober did a good entertaining video on this","text":"mark rober did a good entertaining video on this, including the various tricks how to get rid of them (and what does not work) [VIDEO]"},{"id":35695231,"author":"neom","month":"2023-04","story":"Bean leaves don’t let the bedbugs bite by using tiny, impaling spikes","stance":"rec","why":"Fun Mark Rober video on bed bugs.","quote":"he did a fun video on bed bugs last month","text":"I'd imagine the vast majority of most people on HN already watch Mark Rober, never the less- in case you missed it, he did a fun video on bed bugs last month: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35695278,"author":"nicoburns","month":"2023-04","story":"Bean leaves don’t let the bedbugs bite by using tiny, impaling spikes","stance":"reference","why":"Contains much the same advice on treating bed bugs.","quote":"also contains much of the same advice","text":"If you have bedbugs (and if you do then I’m so sorry) then I recommend not using the chemical treatments. These supposedly just scatter them everywhere, causing them to spread. The two treatments that work reliably seem to be: - Whole building heat treatment, but this is the nuclear option as it involves heating the entire building (including cracks and crevices) to 60c - Repeated steam treatments. Superheated steam kills them on contact, but doesn’t scare them away. Any remaining bugs will gravitate towards sleeping areas and can be got by repeating the steam treatment within a 2 week window which is how long their reproduction cycle takes. Source: [LINK] I did a lot of research on this topic after an unfortunate encounter with bed bugs while travelling, and this was the most reliable source I could find. EDIT: the Mark Rober video linked below ( [VIDEO] ) also contains much of the same advice."},{"id":35700348,"author":"kirykl","month":"2023-04","story":"Bean leaves don’t let the bedbugs bite by using tiny, impaling spikes","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source contradicting the claim.","quote":"","text":"actually they do, source: \"Mark Rober Bed Bugs- What You've Been Told is Totally False\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9XkHBmE-N34",1],["RlXdsyctD50",1],["AZX6awZq5Z0",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["mht-1c4wc0Q",1],["xRel1JKOEbI",1],["umatbZ0n4mE",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["G0Q4UAiKacw",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["KePNhUJ2reI",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["zQGOcOUBi6s",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1]]},"Gxd23UVID7k":{"wy":"Commenters name it among their favourite Cohen songs, especially in threads after his death.","q":{"x":"I don't know what it is about that song that keeps me coming back.","i":29364276,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41596055,"author":"jszymborski","month":"2024-09","story":"Leonard Cohen: The Man Who Saw the Angels Fall","stance":"rec","why":"One of the personal favourite Cohen tracks listed","quote":"Pretty much every track is a hit, but here are some four random personal favourites","text":"Pretty much every track is a hit, but here are some four random personal favourites: - Everybody Knows [VIDEO] - First We Take Manhattan [LINK] - Famous Blue Raincoat [LINK] - Who By Fire [LINK] Obviously too many to list here though, just pick up any album. By virtue of the fact that he was an incredible songwriter, his songs have such wonderful covers. The Tori Amos cover of Famous Blue Raincoat [0] is one of my favourites, and this cover of Who by Fire by PJ Harvey & Tim Phillips gives me chills every time [1] (also the theme for Bad Sisters which is an amazing series). Also, pretty much every Canadian who was an adult in 2010 has an emotional connection to the k.d. lang performance of Hallelujah at the Vancouver olympics [2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":29364276,"author":"A4ET8a8uTh0","month":"2021-11","story":"Spirituality Shaped Leonard Cohen","stance":"rec","why":"Song the commenter keeps returning to","quote":"I don't know what it is about that song that keeps me coming back.","text":"That was an interesting share. I don't know what it is about that song that keeps me coming back. My taste is somewhat eclectic so the only thing of him I knew ( and liked ) was 'Everybody knows' ( [VIDEO] ). Incidentally, why is WP writing about him now?"},{"id":12927116,"author":"Jerry2","month":"2016-11","story":"Leonard Cohen Has Died","stance":"rec","why":"Cohen song picked as a favorite after his death","quote":"He was one of the greatest and his songs were timeless.","text":"Wow! Such a sad news. He was one of the greatest and his songs were timeless. One of my favorites is \"Everybody Knows\": [VIDEO] You will be missed. עליו השלום"}],"r":[["mII9NZ8MMVM",1],["-bYAQ-ZZtEU",1],["UCgoxQCf5Jg",1]]},"Td_PGkfIdIQ":{"wy":"Pointed to as an in-depth view of how Valve is run and how Newell thinks about workers, management and customers.","q":{"x":"For an understanding of what Valve are doing, here is a 1 hour talk by Gabe Newell (CEO)","i":46142998,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46142998,"author":"petterroea","month":"2025-12","story":"Valve reveals it’s the architect behind a push to bring Windows games to Arm","stance":"rec","why":"One-hour talk explaining Valve's philosophy on workers, management and customers.","quote":"For an understanding of what Valve are doing, here is a 1 hour talk by Gabe Newell (CEO)","text":"For an understanding of what Valve are doing, here is a 1 hour talk by Gabe Newell (CEO): Gabe Newell: On Productivity, Economics, Political Institutions, and the Future of Corporations [VIDEO] TL;DR: * The most skilled workers are the most undervalued * Make products to serve the customer * Management is a skill, not a career path * The only people they consider themselves to be unable to compete with are their customers, so enabling the customer to produce better content in their ecosystem is the most efficient way of producing things."},{"id":41412775,"author":"ilrwbwrkhv","month":"2024-08","story":"Five Most Productive Years: What Happened and What's Next","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth look at how Gabe Newell runs the business.","quote":"he still has some incredible videos which go so in depth in how he runs the business and what he thinks about","text":"So the first thing is the meta learning. Looking at companies like Valve and Wolfram, they provide a template of another way of running companies which seem to consistently produce the best kind of software and incredible wealth for all those involved. The two things you look for when running a software company. Next, Stephen livestreams his day to day as a CEO. This is so significant. I know the HN trope which dang warned about earlier, but I actually love it. Imagine if you could get detailed logs about how Steve Jobs lived his life. Not from books others write about him and make up fake stuff to make it sell more, but straight from the horse's mouth as they say. That is what his meticulous logs and streams of his life provides. Gabe Newell of course does much less of this, but he still has some incredible videos which go so in depth in how he runs the business and what he thinks about. Look, we are nerds. To learn business, we go online and try to piece together information. For example, I know for a fact a bunch of YC companies (both in this batch and earlier) have fallen for scammers like Alex Hormozi because he has a massive Youtube presence and just spews nonsense which sounds like it should make sense. So in that world, to learn as close to first hand from people who actually run some of the biggest and most interesting business on the planet is just incredible. --- Live CEOing [LINK] Gabe Newell: On Productivity, Economics, Political Institutions, and the Future of C"},{"id":8953152,"author":"pillowpants2","month":"2015-01","story":"Valve Economist Yanis Varoufakis Appointed Finance Minister of Greece","stance":"rec","why":"Gabe Newell on in-game economic problems Valve faced.","quote":"here is a really interesting video of Gabe Newell at the Lyndon B Johnson School of Public Affairs talking about some of the in-game economic problems Valve has faced","text":"For those interested, here is a really interesting video of Gabe Newell at the Lyndon B Johnson School of Public Affairs talking about some of the in-game economic problems Valve has faced in the past few years. I think he briefly mentions Yanis somewhere in the middle of the video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22668395,"author":"danbolt","month":"2020-03","story":"Magic: The Gathering creator Richard Garfield on 35 years of making games","stance":"reference","why":"Valve's concept of player acknowledgement for cosmetic details.","quote":"Valve had a nice way of describing \"player acknowledgement\" [1][2]","text":"Agreed! I think it kind of goes to show that a game often needs a nice cocktail of interesting game state changes (eg: \"mechanics\") mixed with nice feelings. Audiences vary in how they like the two (eg: Chess compared to Mario Kart), but the right fit is really important to a successful product. Valve had a nice way of describing \"player acknowledgement\" [1][2] for cosmetic things like bullet holes or lighting affecting our lizard brains. On the other hand, a close friend of mine really enjoys the \"Star Wars: Rebellion\" board game. [3] The decisions and mechanics gives the sense of excitement he feels when he watches a Star Wars movie. I don't blame Garfield for making the kinds of games he likes though. His clout probably helps makes new trails for other ideas to take shape and be improved on. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"}],"r":[["ZSRHeXYDLko",1]]},"h4Uu5eyN6VU":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a quick factual primer on EU institutions when threads get into EU politics and policy.","q":{"x":"Here is a quick video explaining how it works","i":31922276,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31922276,"author":"BadOakOx","month":"2022-06","story":"EU Commission says it cannot find messages with Pfizer CEO","stance":"rec","why":"Quick explanation of how EU officials are elected","quote":"Here is a quick video explaining how it works","text":"> > Ursula von der Leyen was not elected by EU citizens > But was she not elected by EU members of parliment, who were elected by EU citizens? This is a common argument when criticizing the EU, but in the end everybody is elected by the people of EU (directly or indirectly). Here is a quick video explaining how it works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31361859,"author":"dijit","month":"2022-05","story":"Chat control: EU Commission presents mass surveillance plan on May 11","stance":"rec","why":"Pragmatic look at EU and whether it's worth it","quote":"I know of other videos that speak pragmatically about the EU, its problems and if it's worth it","text":"Thanks for taking the time to think about it. > Democracy itself always gives rights to the majority against the will of the minority. this is true and it has a name I believe; the \"Tyranny of the Majority\", I think. > The tyranny of the majority (or tyranny of the masses) is an inherent weakness to majority rule in which the majority of an electorate pursues exclusively its own objectives at the expense of those of the minority factions. Unfortunately, as you mentioned, being _more democratic_ ultimately lends itself to being this way. However one neat trick the EU has (and I promise I'm not trying to spin this as pro-EU, as it's only in one place): is that the direct votes are split proportionally. This means that lesser represented groups get a voice in the EU parliament. For the UK with their \"First past the post\" system, and winner take all government; where a party like UKIP can get 12.6% of all votes and win 0 representation in parliament.... well, lets say it's a stark contrast. explained very nicely; here: [LINK] The EU voting system (the way in which we elect MEPs to parliament) is detailed in this video by the BBC: [LINK] ; as you'll see it's much more representative. I know of other videos that speak pragmatically about the EU, its problems and if it's worth it (though with a slight pro-EU stance): [VIDEO] I also found this video that asks the question if the EU is democratic, which I think makes good points and you can draw different conclusions from it: [LINK] I"},{"id":20534137,"author":"tasubotadas","month":"2019-07","story":"“My GitHub account has been restricted due to US sanctions as I live in Crimea”","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how EU officials are elected or appointed","quote":"This will help you to get the facts straight","text":"All EU officials are elected directly or elected/assigned by the people that were elected. This will help you to get the facts straight: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31925908,"author":"Gwypaas","month":"2022-06","story":"EU Commission says it cannot find messages with Pfizer CEO","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Which is about the equal to the EU. Council of the EU [1] has similar function as the senate in the US. The council is made up of the heads of governments, as elected, by the member countries. The council proposes the commission, which is then left for the EU parliament to confirm. This very close, pretty much about equal to the election process of the US senate before the 17th amendment in 1912.[2] The difference being no popular vote for the president. As is the case for the \"head of state\" for pretty much all European countries. Exceptions are only France? The major difference is that only the commission has the right to legislative initiative. To balance this the EU parliament can dissolve the commission based on a vote of no confidence. So all in all, it's a truly democratic system with all the checks and balances. The major difference is the lack of cross European parties, thus your choice becomes collected together in the same basket as what your chosen party thinks is the right path. Completely through representative democracy, either from the head of government or European parliament. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: Kurzgesagt - Is the EU Democratic? Does Your Vote Matter? [VIDEO]"},{"id":26826385,"author":"vages","month":"2021-04","story":"IT companies warn in open letter: EU wants to ban encryption","stance":"reference","why":"Basis for views on EU governing structure","quote":"Based on what I know from this Kurzgesagt video","text":"I’ve thought about this since last year, and I can’t understand how this proposal has been able to survive this long. First of all, the EU is very pro-business, which means it should be pro-encryption. Second, the structures of its governing bodies does not make it very prone to populism. Based on what I know from this Kurzgesagt video: [VIDEO] Is this all some hidden plot to turn the EU into a totalitarian state? I’m (half) joking, but I really can’t see the motivation."}],"r":[["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LdOe18KhtT4",1],["fD2briZ6fB0",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["8YUWDrLazCg",1],["qLjgXPDzeZo",1],["m7PVZixO35c",1],["Jzfpyo-q-RM",1]]},"nIKhUizkXxA":{"wy":"Recommended as an expert-made DIY build that explains why the apparatus is designed as it is, though one commenter notes a missing baseline run.","q":{"x":"he tries to explain why it's built the way it is and give you a good basis of the theory of operation.","i":22298214,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":36536421,"author":"hsjqllzlfkf","month":"2023-06","story":"YouTube is testing a more aggressive approach against ad blockers","stance":"rec","why":"Expert-made channel example building a mass spectrometer from scratch.","quote":"this guy decided to try and build a mass spectrometer from scratch","text":"The channels that I had in mind when I wrote that are channels by experts in their fields, which somehow often happen to be low production value - because you know real scientists are about substance, not style :-) So here's a random one in that category: this guy decided to try and build a mass spectrometer from scratch [VIDEO]"},{"id":30028558,"author":"anfractuosity","month":"2022-01","story":"DIY Raspberry Pi Spectrometer Guide","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as related DIY spectrometer project.","quote":"You might like","text":"You might like - [VIDEO] ( DIY mass spectrometer measures potassium in dietary salt substitute ) by Ben Krasnow"},{"id":22298214,"author":"MertsA","month":"2020-02","story":"The laser and microphone atop Mars 2020, NASA's next rover","stance":"rec","why":"DIY build that explains design and theory of operation.","quote":"he tries to explain why it's built the way it is and give you a good basis of the theory of operation.","text":"You'd be interested in Ben Krasnow's DiY attempt. It's still understandable but he tries to explain why it's built the way it is and give you a good basis of the theory of operation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23097218,"author":"CamperBob2","month":"2020-05","story":"OpenRAMAN – Low-cost, high-performance, open-source Raman spectrometer","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"in the mass-spec video he didn't do the obvious baseline run with a clean filament.","text":"Ben Krasnow took some steps in that direction: [VIDEO] As with his Raman setup, there were some good questions raised in the comments about whether he really saw the effect in question or just got lucky with plausible-appearing artifacts. It's hard to assess validity from the presented results alone, which is unfortunately common in Ben's videos. In the Raman video, he didn't test anything but a single styrofoam cup, and in the mass-spec video he didn't do the obvious baseline run with a clean filament. Ben's channel is one of those rare ones where the comments are worth reading. You can really see how easy it is for a scientist to inadvertently fool themselves and others. I often end up yelling at the screen, but it's still my favorite YouTube channel of all time."}],"r":[["JXpoALotxf0",1],["AcQifPHcMLE",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["F8JCh0owT4w",1],["7nL10C7FSbE",1]]},"sKudSeqHSJk":{"wy":"Recommended in housing threads as a decent dive into the problem and possible long-term solutions.","q":{"x":"I found this video [0] a decent dive into issues and possible long term solutions.","i":33686341,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":37830188,"author":"ImPleadThe5th","month":"2023-10","story":"The Rent Is Too Damn Algorithmic","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting look at Vancouver housing and possible solutions","quote":"I found this youtube video[1] about the Vancouver housing situation interesting.","text":"Making landlords compete with low-cost government subsidized housing. Vienna[0] is a fantastic model. Though I think implementing this in the US has a lot of stigma and challenges to overcome (ie, how can the government mass purchase apartments/land to create subsidized housing). Also, cooperative housing. A group of people buy a property with a Loan, and the cost of the loan + management is payed by all people in the apartment. Eventually, the loan is payed off and the cost of rent is lowered to cover only maintenance/mangement. This also has to come with restrictions about ownership and renting out the apartment. Because its ultimately a collective, its unfortunately totally possible there will be biases at play with these kinds of housing setups (ie, biases against allowing POC to move in, disagreements on how common space can be used). And lastly, fast public transit to expand the area its possible to live by people who can't afford cars. I found this youtube video[1] about the Vancouver housing situation interesting. Maybe a bit over-simiplified, but gives you an idea of some possible solutions. I know these aren't silver-bullet solutions. But I think it helps think of this in a different way then just a supply/demand problem. Its also a policy and profiteering problem. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36067189,"author":"readonthegoapp","month":"2023-05","story":"Imagine a Renters' Utopia. It Might Look Like Vienna.","stance":"rec","why":"Public housing supply can keep rents down","quote":"if you supply at least some public housing, it can have an outsized ability to keep rents down","text":"i watched a couple youtubes over the past few months on this -- if you supply at least some public housing, it can have an outsized ability to keep rents down: Bloomberg Originals: Vienna's Radical Idea? Affordable Housing For All [LINK] About Here: The Non-capitalist Solution to the Housing Crisis (indy urban planning-types out of Vancouver, BC) [VIDEO]"},{"id":33564888,"author":"ZeroGravitas","month":"2022-11","story":"The world is run by people no smarter than you","stance":"rec","why":"Video on co-op and social housing making similar points","quote":"A recent video on co-op and social housing, that makes simlar points","text":"A recent video on co-op and social housing, that makes simlar points, with the somewhat clickbaity title of: The Non-capitalist Solution to the Housing Crisis [VIDEO] The video seems to switch to \"non-market\" as a description."},{"id":33686341,"author":"layoric","month":"2022-11","story":"I hate living in my tiny house (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Decent dive into housing issues and long-term solutions","quote":"I found this video [0] a decent dive into issues and possible long term solutions.","text":"That might work for some but moving away from common infrastructure creates other problems. I found this video [0] a decent dive into issues and possible long term solutions. None quick or easy but I think are most likely to succeed in a lot of places. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37914547,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2023-10","story":"If the Economy Is So Strong, Why Are Consumer Stocks Tanking?","stance":"reference","why":"Offers alternative to price-fixed low income housing","quote":"It does not have to be this way.","text":"> If we do that you'll have two social classes. You'll have the price fixed low income housing that looks like it belongs in a Command & Conquer game It does not have to be this way. [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["fmF7HpU_-9k",1],["D99qI42KGB0",1],["AtPA6nIBs5g",1],["PmTUW-owa2w",1],["doz1QJ7LwjA",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["41VJudBdYXY",1]]},"yXbJe-rUNP8":{"wy":"Recommended as a deep-dive rabbit hole showing extraordinary knowledge of game internals.","q":{"x":"The amount of knowledge and abuse of bugs these guys are performing is insane.","i":36726106,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":40027990,"author":"g___","month":"2024-04","story":"Super Mario 64's invisible walls explained [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Five-hour history of the effort to beat Mario 64 with minimal A presses.","quote":"Here's a 5h video with the history. Bismuth says the history video took roughly 1500-2000 hours.","text":"For over a decade, the author of this video has been leading an effort to complete Super Mario 64 with the smallest amount of A presses, often using extremely complex strategies. Here's a 5h video with the history. Bismuth says the history video took roughly 1500-2000 hours. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39230762,"author":"houseatrielah","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: What niche YouTube rabbit hole do you recommend?","stance":"rec","why":"Long video about beating Mario 64 without the A button.","quote":"Beat Mario64 without hitting the A-Button (5h22m long!)","text":"Langlangs program (The Dream: Riemann Hypothesis and F1 (RH Saga S1E1)) [LINK] History Dose (The Mongol vs. Order of Assassins War) [LINK] Beat Mario64 without hitting the A-Button (5h22m long!) [VIDEO]"},{"id":36726106,"author":"pests","month":"2023-07","story":"Super Mario 64 – Watch for Rolling Rocks – 0.5x a Presses [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Five-hour history of the A button challenge showing deep bug exploitation.","quote":"The amount of knowledge and abuse of bugs these guys are performing is insane.","text":"Complete History of the A Button Challenge [VIDEO] It's over five hours long. I put it on in the background or to pass the time. The amount of knowledge and abuse of bugs these guys are performing is insane."},{"id":40345945,"author":"fl0ki","month":"2024-05","story":"Super Mario 64's unopenable door has been opened [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Corrects creator attribution and notes it explains pannenkoek's break from videos.","quote":"Though Bismuth does spend some time explaining why pannen took a break from making videos","text":"That was Bismuth, not pannenkoek2012 [VIDEO] Though Bismuth does spend some time explaining why pannen took a break from making videos, which I'm very glad to see was temporary."},{"id":37580486,"author":"TheRoque","month":"2023-09","story":"Programming Is Hard","stance":"reference","why":"Example of players manipulating game code in complex ways.","quote":"","text":"> Games only hold a challenge until the game-writer's secrets are broken. Games ultimately are code themselves, and you can manipulate it to some extent, creating other infinitely complex interactions (source: advancements in speedrunning or various game challenges like the A press challenge [1]) [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UJ7Qc3bsxjI",1],["dXdoim96v5A",1],["-ZXrJRpA0Jw",1]]},"5KiC03_wVjc":{"wy":"Shared in threads about the Nurburgring and after Schmitz's death as a charming showcase of her skill.","q":{"x":"If you havent watched her take a van around the ring in a smidge over 10 minutes it is worth a watch.","i":26503340,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":17434915,"author":"stevekemp","month":"2018-07","story":"Porsche shatters the Nürburgring record we thought was unbreakable","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sabine Schmitz driving a Transit van around the Nurburgring","quote":"Part two is the one to watch","text":"For some reason my favourite was always watching Sabine Schmitz trying to drive a transit van in under ten minutes. Part two is the one to watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26503340,"author":"ProAm","month":"2021-03","story":"Sabine Schmitz, Queen of the Nürburgring, Has Died at 51","stance":"rec","why":"Sabine Schmitz takes a van around the ring in about ten minutes","quote":"If you havent watched her take a van around the ring in a smidge over 10 minutes it is worth a watch.","text":"If you havent watched her take a van around the ring in a smidge over 10 minutes it is worth a watch. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45491994,"author":"tim333","month":"2025-10","story":"BYD builds fastest car","stance":"reference","why":"Sabine Schmitz narrowly missing 10 minutes in a van","quote":"Here's Sabine Schmitz falling to break 10 mins in a van","text":"Here's Sabine Schmitz falling to break 10 mins in a van [VIDEO] Although with a slightly quicker van maybe."},{"id":28037407,"author":"flavius29663","month":"2021-08","story":"The rise of never-ending job interviews","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a pro driver enjoying a challenge","quote":"This lady pilot sure did","text":"> mario Andretti If you're a world champion, you would probably enjoy an exercise like this. This lady pilot sure did: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UEfP1OKKz_Q",1]]},"E_F5GxCwizc":{"wy":"Recommended as a funny, clear explanation of native advertising whenever sponsored content in media is discussed.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend taking the next 11 minutes to watch John Oliver talk about native advertising","i":8348992,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8348992,"author":"aaronbrethorst","month":"2014-09","story":"The invasion of corporate news","stance":"strong_rec","why":"John Oliver explains native advertising.","quote":"I highly recommend taking the next 11 minutes to watch John Oliver talk about native advertising","text":"In case you haven't seen it yet, I highly recommend taking the next 11 minutes to watch John Oliver talk about native advertising: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8161594,"author":"TruffleLabs","month":"2014-08","story":"BuzzFeed Raises $50M from Andreessen Horowitz","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver segment on native advertising including BuzzFeed.","quote":"Watch John Oliver's Last Week Tonight video segment on native advertisement","text":"Watch John Oliver's Last Week Tonight video segment on native advertisement [VIDEO] (10 min long). There is a bit on BuzzFeed where he points out that many of the posts that are \"top\" are really from paid advertisers. Saying BuzzFeed is developing a journalism team is probably not the best wording. I would suggest BuzzFeed is building up a great PR, advertising, and marketing engine. They will make money. But is it journalism?"},{"id":46668175,"author":"OptionOfT","month":"2026-01","story":"The A in AGI Stands for Ads","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver piece on Netflix full-page ad.","quote":"John Oliver had a piece on it","text":"I remember when Netflix took out a whole page ad for their Orange is the new Black show. John Oliver had a piece on it [VIDEO] This is a natural extension of it. But what is revolutionary is the scale that this is now possible. We have so many people out there who now blindly trust the output of an LLM (how many colleagues have you had proudly telling you: I asked Claude and this is what it says <paste>). This is as advertiser's wet dream. Now it's ads at the bottom, but slowly they'll become more part of the text. And worst part: you don't know, bar the fact that the link has a refer(r)er attached to it. The internet before and after LLMs is like steel before and after the atomic bombs. Anything after is contaminated."},{"id":10338538,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2015-10","story":"Reddit prepares to launch new news website 'Upvoted'","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver covered native advertising well.","quote":"I think John Olliver covered the topic well enough[0]","text":"Me too. Native advertising is plain evil, in a way even the worst drive-by malware flash popups aren't. They're ads designed with the sole purpose of tricking readers into thinking they're viewing a real article, and they abuse the trust readers have towards the news source. I think John Olliver covered the topic well enough[0], so let me just say that I only hope that Upvoted will label the ads clearly so that it's obvious for everyone they're reading paid content (imagine advertisers letting them do that, though...). > It could be a piece on Tesla Wait, since when Tesla does native ads? Do they do ads at all ? [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":8161609,"author":"mambodog","month":"2014-08","story":"BuzzFeed Raises $50M from Andreessen Horowitz","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver commented on ads disguised as news.","quote":"John Oliver recently commented on this stuff","text":"[ads that pretend to be news stories] John Oliver recently commented on this stuff (so called \"Native Advertising\") on Last Week Tonight: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12670915,"author":"pluma","month":"2016-10","story":"Monks Who Spent Years Turning Themselves into Mummies While Alive","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory Last Week Tonight","text":"Outside gaming journalism I believe the term is \"native advertising\". Obligatory Last Week Tonight: [VIDEO]"}]},"NIu_dJGyIQI":{"wy":"Offered as a high-level, approachable introduction to Friston's idea of the brain as a prediction machine.","q":{"x":"There's a great video introduction to that idea with Karl Friston from UCL here","i":35383239,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35383239,"author":"frereubu","month":"2023-03","story":"Brains Speed Up Perception by Guessing What’s Next (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great introduction to the brain as a statistical machine","quote":"There's a great video introduction to that idea with Karl Friston from UCL here","text":"I was studying something similar in my MSc about the neurobiology of language. It's well known that reading circuits in the brain make predictions about which words come next - a sentence like \"The jam on the motorway made traffic slow\" doesn't elicit much activity, whereas \"The jam on the motorway was sticky\" does, because \"motorway\" makes it much more likely that \"jam\" was about slow traffic rather than the condiment. (There are perhaps some interesting parallels to LLMs there...) But we were looking in the visual cortex, way back before the brain is even perceiving words, and we were finding pre-activation of circuits even there depending on whether people were presented with real words (e.g. sheep) or non-words (e.g. brast). I think there's a lot to be said for conceptualising the brain as a statistical machine - it makes a lot of sense in evolutionary terms. There's a great video introduction to that idea with Karl Friston from UCL here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18497345,"author":"jchook","month":"2018-11","story":"Karl Friston: a neuroscientist who might hold the key to true AI","stance":"rec","why":"High-level interview explaining Friston's free energy principle","quote":"I found this interview with Karl Friston helpful to understand free energy principle from a high level","text":"I found this interview with Karl Friston helpful to understand free energy principle from a high level: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23745811,"author":"soulofmischief","month":"2020-07","story":"Ask HN: What weird or hard problems are you trying to solve?","stance":"reference","why":"Friston on organisms modeling their environment","quote":"Are you familiar with Karl Friston's work on the Free Energy Principle?","text":"Ok, so less about the flow and nature of information and more about the semantics. > Seeing the computation as a physical process, processing information \"about\" details in its environment, highlights this. Are you familiar with Karl Friston's work on the Free Energy Principle? [0] He touches on how the nature of a living being (computational agent) is to model its external environment and act upon that model. That making decisions based on internal logic requires external stimulus and semantics being analyzed and augmented in sort of a feedback loop. > The key to all this is appreciating that, once you see semantics and information processing as a matter of physical processes, you can see that the correct semantics can be necessary for producing a physical outcome. I think I understand what you're saying and it touches on some of my own inquiries. A more concrete example, if you have one, might better help align me with where your thoughts are at regarding this. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19860503,"author":"frereubu","month":"2019-05","story":"Brains Speed Up Perception by Guessing What’s Next","stance":"reference","why":"Friston's free energy principle relates to predictive perception","quote":"This ties in quite nicely with Karl Friston's Free Energy Principle","text":"This ties in quite nicely with Karl Friston's Free Energy Principle: [VIDEO] There are so many strands of neuroscience that show predictive coding now. There's a really nice example in the use of common vs unusual words in sentences. If you're using fMRI to scan someone while they read these two sentences: The jam on the motorway made everything slow. The jam on the motorway made everything sticky. You'll see a flash of activity when people read the word \"sticky\" rather than the more common word in that context \"slow\". The theory is that when everything in the sentence proceeds as predicted there's very little need to update prior expectations, but when you come across something that goes against previous predictions it's a signal that something needs to be actively thought about."}],"r":[["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["fAxHlLK3Oyk",1]]},"PNbkv_DJ0f0":{"wy":"HN points to it as a stunning demonstration of arbitrary code execution in a console game and of what speedrunners effectively do as vulnerability researchers.","q":{"x":"absolutely blew me away","i":43233786,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43233786,"author":"prophesi","month":"2025-03","story":"Speedrunners are vulnerability researchers, they just don't know it yet","stance":"strong_rec","why":"TASBot's arbitrary code execution alternate ending in Ocarina of Time.","quote":"absolutely blew me away","text":"I think if they're active in the speedrunning community, then they're already well aware of this! And for a fun additional example to add to this article, you can often find TAS'ers talking about arbitrary code execution. The legendary GDQ run of TASBot's alternate ending to OoT[0] utiziling an ACE exploit they found in that game absolutely blew me away. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45428627,"author":"andrekandre","month":"2025-09","story":"Bad Apple but it's played inside Super Mario Bros","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the tech with an amazing demo at the end.","quote":"a good video that makes this tech more understandable","text":"they are most likely using a controller hooked up to a bot/laptop that exploits flaws in the code with inputs at high speed (much faster than a human could) that injects code into live games a good video that makes this tech more understandable (with explanations at the beginnimg and an amazing demo at the end) [VIDEO] and some background [LINK]"},{"id":33380213,"author":"NobodyNada","month":"2022-10","story":"Beating Super Mario Bros 3 in 19 frames (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Ocarina of Time total control TAS.","quote":"Ocarina of Time total control/\"Triforce%\" (TAS, 2022)","text":"Some highlights/historically significant examples of this: Super Mario World (TAS, 2014): [LINK] Super Mario Bros. 3 (TAS, 2014): [LINK] - The same run performed RTA (i.e. by a human using a real controller) at AGDQ 2015: [LINK] Super Mario World (RTA, 2015): [LINK] Super Mario World \"total control\" (RTA, 2015): [LINK] Pokemon Yellow total control (TAS, 2017): [LINK] The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time (RTA, 2019-ish?): [LINK] Ocarina of Time total control/\"Triforce%\" (TAS, 2022): [VIDEO] (explanation: [LINK] )"}],"r":[["esQyYGezS7c",1],["7EmboKQH8lM",1]]},"bwDrHqNZ9lo":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as one of the best short clips on YouTube for illustrating Balkan versus Central European identity.","q":{"x":"this short clip [1] of Zizek explaining the geography that separates Central Europe from the Balkans is one of the best things on YT","i":22926722,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22926722,"author":"paganel","month":"2020-04","story":"Shirt Without Stripes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Zizek explains the geography separating Central Europe from the Balkans.","quote":"this short clip [1] of Zizek explaining the geography that separates Central Europe from the Balkans is one of the best things on YT","text":"People on an website like this should at least have a general idea of who Zizek is. I've never yet read any of his books but as a guy living in the Balkans this short clip [1] of Zizek explaining the geography that separates Central Europe from the Balkans is one of the best things on YT. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42762358,"author":"Swizec","month":"2025-01","story":"TikTok says it is restoring service for U.S. users","stance":"rec","why":"Zizek on the border between west and Balkans.","quote":"You might enjoy this Zizek video on the border between the west and the balkans","text":"> The actual meaning of democracy is the \"power of the people\". Nowhere that implies a western-like electoral system. Correct. “we” used to do it simply by killing the leaders that were disliked. Elections are a bit friendlier than that :) You might enjoy this Zizek video on the border between the west and the balkans: [VIDEO] . I think he captures the sentiment well. > I'd argue in your average western democracy the people have very little power, with lots of symbolic processes to reinforce the illusion. This was Chomsky’s whole point in Manufacturing Consent."},{"id":13611732,"author":"NoMyGodIsThis","month":"2017-02","story":"London’s Lonely Unicorn: Two Frugal Expats and Their Billion-Dollar Startup","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Compulsory link, shirley?","text":"\" I come from the border between Central and Eastern Europe. Some say Slovenia is one, some say another.\" Compulsory link, shirley? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hER0Qp6QJNU",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["k7X7sZzSXYs",1],["sJj9y4t9UnU",1],["uXdtafGdIVM",1]]},"wROQUZDCIMI":{"wy":"HN points to it as a clear, lawyer-made summary during Nintendo emulator disputes like Yuzu.","q":{"x":"I really recommend folks watch this, from a lawyer","i":39530522,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39530522,"author":"CaliforniaKarl","month":"2024-02","story":"Nintendo is suing the creators of Switch emulator Yuzu","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lawyer explains why emulators are legal.","quote":"I really recommend folks watch this, from a lawyer","text":"I really recommend folks watch this, from a lawyer: [VIDEO] “Why Are Emulators Legal? Dolphin vs. Nintendo, and the Fate of Dolphin Emulator”"},{"id":37902185,"author":"Shish2k","month":"2023-10","story":"Playing Pokemon Red with Reinforcement Learning","stance":"rec","why":"Lawyer's clear summary of emulator legality, especially Dolphin on Steam.","quote":"Moon Channel (an actual lawyer) has a fairly clear and concise summary","text":"It varies based on country and jurisdiction, but from an American / European perspective - some parts of the emulation scene are “definitely not legal” (eg, distributing copyrighted ROMs), and most parts of the scene are “It is complicated. Well-informed people have different predictions, but it hasn’t been tested in court, so we don’t know for sure”. Moon Channel (an actual lawyer) has a fairly clear and concise summary with particular relation to the Dolphin emulator’s attempt to get itself on Steam: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39595828,"author":"chaorace","month":"2024-03","story":"Yuzu emulator developers settle Nintendo lawsuit, pay $2.4M in damages","stance":"reference","why":"Lawyer explains DMCA 1201 text.","quote":"you can listen to a lawyer explain the above at the following link","text":"In order to isolate one part of the software as \"the bad bits\", you need either clear specific language in the law or a clearly established relevant legal precedent. Neither of these things currently exist in the context of DMCA 1201. If you're curious, here's the most relevant parts of the text: > No person shall manufacture, import, offer to the public, provide, or otherwise traffic in any technology, product, service, device, component, or part thereof, that [...] has only limited commercially significant purpose or use other than to circumvent a technological measure that effectively controls access to a work protected under this title;[...] > As used in this subsection—[...]a technological measure “effectively controls access to a work” if the measure, in the ordinary course of its operation, requires the application of information, or a process or a treatment, with the authority of the copyright owner, to gain access to the work. FYI: I am not a lawyer, but you can listen to a lawyer explain the above at the following link [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vtWp45Eewtw",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["HSxSgbNQi-g",1],["1bO8zEaSuWg",1],["wfm2xCKOCNk",1],["3UnB1fomvAw",1]]},"xUezfuy8Qpc":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as an eloquent defense of free speech with concrete examples of the slippery slope.","q":{"x":"Rowan Atkinson explained it more eloquently that I ever could","i":42118280,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42118280,"author":"sph","month":"2024-11","story":"Bluesky adds 700k new users in a week","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Atkinson argues eloquently for free speech","quote":"Rowan Atkinson explained it more eloquently that I ever could","text":"> Essentially you should be tolerant of others, unless they themselves are intolerant. No, it's called a paradox because there is no \"easy\" answer to it. Also, who determines the exact line between free speech and intolerance? It is impossible to do. The reason the world is polarised today is because everybody thinks they have the simple answer to a literal ethical paradox that has none. The simple answer being: \"those that share my beliefs are OK, those that don't should be canceled/demonised/hidden somewhere I don't see them.\" Thus creating echo chambers so people get more and more radical. Free speech is the only half acceptable answer, which means free speech for anyone, whatever their point of view, however offensive you deem them to be. Rowan Atkinson explained it more eloquently that I ever could: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42310608,"author":"Etherlord87","month":"2024-12","story":"Breaking the 4Chan CAPTCHA","stance":"rec","why":"Atkinson gives examples of the free speech slippery slope","quote":"Rowan Atkinson gives some nice examples here","text":"Plainly to you, but not to me. What isn't obvious to everyone, isn't really obvious. You didn't care to explain what kind of stopping mechanisms are there, that make it not a slippery slope. Rowan Atkinson gives some nice examples here: [VIDEO] I'd say rather than there being *a risk* of descending too low on that slippery slope, we already have plenty of evidence this already happened. I'm from Poland and recently a party in ruling coalition proposed criminalizing hate speech on the Internet. Fortunately, precisely the slippery slope rhetoric, supported by evidence from countries like UK, seems to be enough of a backlash to stop politicians from following such ideas."},{"id":46555984,"author":"burnermore","month":"2026-01","story":"European Commission issues call for evidence on open source","stance":"reference","why":"Atkinson speech on free speech","quote":"People who tried to cancel Eminem and Rowan Atkinson's speech comes to mind.","text":"Its not just left. Right had to face this too. We should be allowed to discuss openly without being worried of losing job and humiliated. Right now, I cannot discuss openly. Kevin hart losing Oscar for a comment 12 some years ago. People who tried to cancel Eminem and Rowan Atkinson's speech comes to mind. Getting offended is a YOU problem. Not a me problem. Until it's possible for us from both sides can talk openly, these will continue. Just like opposition political parties when one side is in more power, they will try and punish the other. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41388403,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2024-08","story":"HDMI Forum rejects AMD's HDMI 2.1 open-source driver","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That is the real issue, because ultimately, this is about the memory hole. A re-upload of a camcorder copy of a VHS is no threat; it will be degraded (again) when the digital archivist who saved it's re-encoded copy[1] (from whatever video platform deleted it) posts it again. Intercepting a exact bitstream circumvents this modern camcorder copy deliberate problem, and would ultimately obliterate the non-arguments to keep the original file unavailable. On the other hand, a signed sha3-256 digest along with the original[0] file before YT re-encoded it (and stripped it's metadata) is unobtanium for the plebs. It is the _most_ important data for the host. It's the first thing they backup. As far as I know, they (YT/Rumble/Tora3) never talk about it. Some would love to only serve hallucinated (when convenient) upscaled derivatives. Power is threatened by persistent lossless public memory. [0]: [LINK] [1]: (Mr. Bean, 2024) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["ia8Q51ouA_s",1],["zBgCRJluWTc",1],["jpNU72dny2s",1],["mb3uK-_QkOo",1],["RTB5XhjbgZA",1],["9-IWMbJXoLM",1],["gp4U5aH_T6A",1],["8k2AbqTBxao",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["SqayDnQ2wmw",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["w70Xc9CStoE",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1],["1jHUY3qoBu8",1]]},"4anAwXYqLG8":{"wy":"HN cites it as a statement of React's early design philosophy of keeping APIs small and understanding JavaScript itself.","q":{"x":"I found this talk on the topic very interesting","i":13734556,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13734556,"author":"msangi","month":"2017-02","story":"Id Software Programming Principles","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on the cost of abstractions and API minimalism","quote":"I found this talk on the topic very interesting","text":"That's indeed a very good advice. All abstractions have a cost and no abstraction is better than the wrong abstraction. I found this talk on the topic very interesting [VIDEO]"},{"id":9902516,"author":"smhg","month":"2015-07","story":"Why Underscore is awesome","stance":"rec","why":"Understand JavaScript thoroughly rather than leaning on libraries","quote":"don't let libraries keep you from thoroughly understanding JavaScript","text":"While underscore is indeed widespread, don't let libraries keep you from thoroughly understanding JavaScript: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41661676,"author":"swyx","month":"2024-09","story":"From Gatsby gridlock to Astro bliss: my personal site redesign","stance":"reference","why":"Markbage's insight on keeping API surface small","quote":"just have a small api surface area bro","text":"I am still not over the collapse of Gatsby (for reference i nearly joined the company in 2017, was saved only by my own character flaws). They rode the highs - being the default docs tool for React, and building a massive ecosystem of integrations you could install out of the box. But too many abstractions, divided goals between cloud and OSS, and the better stewardship/design of Nextjs brought it down. There were the simple lessons ( [LINK] ). its easy to say in hindsight that graphql was too much for gatsby. i also believe the company went too hard for number of integrations over quality of them, an issue I had even in my interview. this was a poorer expression of the better insight that seb markbage had; just have a small api surface area bro ( [VIDEO] ) But the bigger lesson is bitterer. Frontend tooling isnt worth that much. the fact that vercel is pretty much the only successful frontend startup of its generation makes it an exception to the rule (there are plenty of smaller companies that are thriving, like tailwind, but it is not a venture scale startup and thats fine). People dont pay for frontend tooling. they expect it to be free, expect it to do everything, get into internicine squabbles between frameworks when they are all basically doomed compared to just betting on React sponsored by Facebook and now Vercel (and a little bit of Shopify), or going back to fullstack frameworks like Django/Rails/Laravel. all frontend tooling, nextjs included, is just leadgen, loss"},{"id":25452794,"author":"iansinnott","month":"2020-12","story":"React's UseRef Deep Dive","stance":"reference","why":"Source for React's earlier principle of minimizing API","quote":"going against React's prior principle of minimizing the API","text":"I'm a bit torn on the hooks feature since it seems like a great example of something that's easy but not simple [1], as well as going against React's prior principle of minimizing the API [2]. I'm glad the article also shows how to use the more traditional API as well, even if it is a bit more verbose. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9863601,"author":"smhg","month":"2015-07","story":"Don't use || to set default values in JavaScript","stance":"reference","why":"Talk supporting learning language fundamentals over library reliance","quote":"Aim for a good level of JavaScript understanding.","text":"I don't agree either. The first part of the argumentation is indeed based on the author's feelings. The second part I don't think is an issue for most real world use-cases (Objects, Strings and, to a lesser extent, Numbers). On the underscore (_.defaults) recommendation: let's set our goals straight [1]. Aim for a good level of JavaScript understanding. Instead of library knowledge to make up for the lack of the former. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8959587,"author":"danabramov","month":"2015-01","story":"React v0.13.0 Beta 1","stance":"reference","why":"React team talk on keeping API surface minimal","quote":"Minimal API surface area","text":"They're not investing in OOP in any way. In fact, they're doing the opposite: deprecating proprietary pseudo-OOP `React.createClass` helper and let people use whatever they fancy. This makes it easier to write React components using idiomatic constructs from different languages, whether it is TypeScript, ClojureScript, CoffeeScript, or whatever. Also lets you use any mixin system, if you need it at all. React's stance on inheritance: [LINK] “Minimal API surface area”: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8959629,"author":"danabramov","month":"2015-01","story":"React v0.13.0 Beta 1","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on minimal API surface and leaving mixins to userland","quote":"See also: Minimal API Surface Area","text":">I'm disappointed with the implementation in this case (no mixins??) The point is moving mixin handling to userland (and eventually some sort of standard?), if I understand correctly. It's not really React's concern. See also: Minimal API Surface Area [VIDEO]"}]},"DKtVpvzUF1Y":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for its slow-motion view of the first seconds of liftoff and cite it when comparing launch pads and engine behavior.","q":{"x":"It explains the sequence of events over the course of 8+ minutes (30 seconds real time).","i":19664464,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19664464,"author":"iClaudiusX","month":"2019-04","story":"F-1 Thrust Chamber","stance":"rec","why":"500 fps Saturn V launch footage narrated through the full sequence of events.","quote":"It explains the sequence of events over the course of 8+ minutes (30 seconds real time).","text":"Here is 500 fps film of the Saturn V launch for Apollo 11, narrated by Mark Gray. It explains the sequence of events over the course of 8+ minutes (30 seconds real time). [VIDEO]"},{"id":13730101,"author":"NaOH","month":"2017-02","story":"Apollo 11 launch, video of engines at 500 frames per second","stance":"rec","why":"Narrated version covering the first 30 seconds after liftoff.","quote":"This version of the same video includes informative narration of what are the first 30 seconds after Apollo 11 liftoff","text":"This version of the same video includes informative narration of what are the first 30 seconds after Apollo 11 liftoff: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40606139,"author":"voidUpdate","month":"2024-06","story":"Super Heavy has splashed down in The Gulf of Mexico","stance":"reference","why":"Example of shielded pad cameras used during Apollo.","quote":"they shielded the pad cameras during apollo","text":"You could protect it in the same way they shielded the pad cameras during apollo (such as the E-8 camera [VIDEO] ), with a quartz shield"},{"id":14416775,"author":"msl","month":"2017-05","story":"New Zealand space launch is first from a private site","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The F1 engine [1] (Saturn V, first stage) used another interesting way to improve efficiency with a gas generator cycle: using the turbopump exhaust gas as a cooling film in the engine nozzle. The fuel-rich exhaust was relatively cool compared to the flame generated by the rocket engine itself, and thus protected the nozzle from the most intense heat. This is why, close up, the flame looks almost black close to the nozzle [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] Edit: Hopefully clarified a little, and changed the link in [2] (from [VIDEO] )."},{"id":12406179,"author":"mitchty","month":"2016-09","story":"A SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket has exploded at Cape Canaveral","stance":"reference","why":"Saturn V launch showing pads handle rocket failure modes.","quote":"Compare to this Saturn V launch.","text":"Unlikely, it wouldn't be much of a launch pad if it couldn't handle failure modes for rockets (aka bombs with a hole in one end). Compare to this Saturn V launch. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["V4V2bpZlqx8",1]]},"IDgDWbQtlKI":{"wy":"HN cites it as the best-documented example of landscape-scale restoration working, with striking before and after footage from the Loess Plateau.","q":{"x":"I've seen a documentary with some before and after video footage. Quite amazing difference","i":20434475,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":32695651,"author":"mountaintimefrm","month":"2022-09","story":"Why are Pakistan’s floods so extreme this year?","stance":"rec","why":"Best documented example of landscape-wide swales reclaiming land in China.","quote":"Probably the best documented example of it working is in the Loess Plateau in China.","text":"Probably the best documented example of it working is in the Loess Plateau in China. John D Liu has a couple documentaries on it, here is one: [VIDEO] A good example of a similar project in India, as covered by Andrew Millison: [LINK] Sepp Holzer did a similar project in Portugal: [LINK] ...I believe he also did some pretty large projects in the middle east, but I can't find any vids on them at the moment. Geoff Lawton has done a ton of projects like this, but maybe not at the landscape wide scales like the projects above. Probably the best book on the basic principles of how to do it - Rainwater Harvesting for Drylands and Beyond by Brad Lancaster. Also the book Water For Every Farm by P.A. Yeomans is pretty good as well, but not as accessible of a read. TLDR - To keep desert monsoons from flooding areas downstream, dig a landscape wide network of swales and basins to catch, soak and sink the water into the soil. Properly dug, such earthworks recharge aquifers while stopping flooding and erosion."},{"id":20434475,"author":"jillesvangurp","month":"2019-07","story":"Soil Erosion: Why soil is disappearing from farms","stance":"rec","why":"Before and after footage of dramatic soil restoration in China.","quote":"I've seen a documentary with some before and after video footage. Quite amazing difference","text":"Soil erosion is a reversible process but it will require a little effort and planning. When it works it's an investment that actually pays off. Here's a nice example of the Chinese restoring some soil: [LINK] I've seen a documentary with some before and after video footage. Quite amazing difference: [VIDEO] Planned grazing is another approach that has seen some success: [LINK]"},{"id":35221136,"author":"briantakita","month":"2023-03","story":"Greening the desert: the architect regenerating Jordan’s native forests","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary covering regreening efforts including Jordan's.","quote":"John D Liu made a documentary which covers Jordan's efforts among others titled \"Regreening the Desert\".","text":"John D Liu made a documentary which covers Jordan's efforts among others titled \"Regreening the Desert\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":25258879,"author":"carapace","month":"2020-11","story":"Avoiding GMOs isn’t just anti-science. It’s immoral. (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> As global warming progresses, a lot of the seeds that we are used to being able to grow aren't going to grow anymore, and the land that we used to grow them on will be turning into a desert. We can find or develop new drought- and heat-tolerant varieties, like the tepary bean (Phaseolus acutifolius)[1]. More importantly, we can alter the climate directly though our patterns of agriculture and horticulture.[2][3] > Within 50 years there's going to be mass migrations and tremendous demand for the land that is still viable for growing crops. FWIW, there's a very interesting talk about that[4] and one of the take-aways is that we may well see e.g. Bangladesh unilaterally pursuing geoengineering solutions, not waiting for international consensus. > GMO definitely isn't the only solution, and it may not even be the best one but it is one field that we need to deeply investigate and try out if we want our species to survive with a similar standard of living to what we enjoy today. I'm not actually against genetic engineering, I just think we should wait a few hundred years, until we have a better idea of how life works -OR- some extra-terrestrial biospheres in which to experiment without risking Earth at all. In the meantime, I believe we already have all the biological diversity we need to tackle the coming challenges already, we just have to recognize and use it. In any event, the OP is BS propaganda, not science, eh? [1] \"Phaseolus acutifolius, or the Tepary bean, is a legume n"},{"id":23643071,"author":"carapace","month":"2020-06","story":"What vertical farming and ag startups don't understand about agriculture, part 2","stance":"reference","why":"Loess Plateau regreening in China as example of regenerative methods.","quote":"Regreening the desert with John D. Liu","text":"Modern mechanized agriculture is incredibly efficient and productive, but it degrades soil health over time and loses topsoil. There are methods that increase soil and fertility over time (Permaculture, Syntropic farming, regenerative agriculture) but they are typically relatively labor intensive. China and India have made great strides in recent times. India's Water Revolution #2: The Biggest Permaculture Project on Earth! with the Paani Foundation [LINK] India's Water Revolution #3: From Poverty to Permaculture with DRCSC [LINK] Regreening the desert with John D. Liu [VIDEO] > It all started in 1995 when Liu filmed the Loess-plateau in China. He witnessed a local population who turned an area of almost the same size as The Netherlands from a dry, exhausted wasteland into one green oasis. This experience changed his life. To me it seems that the two main areas to focus on to make a real contribution are: 1) Make modern agriculture ecologically harmonious. 2) Make ecologically harmonious agriculture more automated. Obviously these converge. (My feeling is that we would be happier with lots of little farms rather than a few large ones, but I'm not interested in debating that particular point. I care about ecological harmony. Future generations can work out the economic forms provided we don't crash the system before then.)"},{"id":18926858,"author":"briantakita","month":"2019-01","story":"Climate Concerns Are Pushing Oil Majors to Look Beyond Fossil Fuels","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The obsession I'm mentioning is based one's worldview & presuppositions...How we reify & approach the problems determine the solutions. Appeal to authority will ensure policies that enrich the authority, with the associated conflicts of interest, confirmation bias, & transfer of power from the population to the authority. Remember, that plant growth is promoted with Carbon Dioxide. A principle of Permaculture is turning the problem into the solution. Elevated CO2 is plant food, if we properly take advantage of the opportunity. Here's an anology re: agriculture. Conventional monoculture petro-chemical till agriculture optimizes for yield & only yield. Unfortunately, there are pests, molds, blight, disease, weather issues, drought, soil conditions that reduce yield. In response, pesticides, herbicides, GMOs, mass irrigation (disrupting watersheds), petro-chemical fertilizers are all sold to the farmer. This leads to soil erosion, destruction of ecosystems, pollution, & lower quality (less nutrition) food. Instead, one can practice regenerative agriculture, that optimizes the ecosystem & soil remediation. Earthworks, such as swales, terraces, & ponds are used to capture rainfall & cycle the water through the property for as long as possible. Polycultures of plants that compliment each other are planted together. Animals are allowed to graze, which brings in fertilizer. Predatory insects & birds are introduced into the habitat to control pests & molds. And you get a higher overal"}],"r":[["xbBdIG--b58",1]]},"OyBNmecVtdU":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as the primary source for the phrase 'military-industrial complex' and urge people to hear the full speech rather than the soundbite.","q":{"x":"Eisenhower's 1961 Farewell Address is misinterpreted by many and totally forgotten by others. Please listen","i":24792039,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":36075254,"author":"scrlk","month":"2023-05","story":"War Is a Racket (1935)","stance":"rec","why":"Eisenhower's 1961 farewell address coined the term.","quote":"I highly recommend watching his 1961 Farewell Address","text":"To add to the Eisenhower quote - I highly recommend watching his 1961 Farewell Address, where the term \"military–industrial complex\" was first used: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24792039,"author":"maxharris","month":"2020-10","story":"Google disabled my husband's account","stance":"rec","why":"Eisenhower's address is often misread or forgotten.","quote":"Eisenhower's 1961 Farewell Address is misinterpreted by many and totally forgotten by others. Please listen","text":"Has he done anything to raise the ire of the American intelligence services? The agencies that gave us [LINK] and the like still exist. Google is not ultimately in control over matters like that. Getting information on this stuff is difficult, given the blank check the NSA has had from 1959 onwards: [LINK] Eisenhower's 1961 Farewell Address is misinterpreted by many and totally forgotten by others. Please listen: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20009032,"author":"pgrote","month":"2019-05","story":"U.S. Army: “How has serving impacted you?”","stance":"reference","why":"Eisenhower's warning on military spending.","quote":"President Eisenhower's farewell address concerning the military industrial complex nailed the course for the United States","text":"Once I realized that the United States has to involve itself in armed conflicts on a regular basis, it was eye opening. The United States spends more money on the military than any other country. You have to take the military out to stretch its legs every decade or so. If not to prepare for war, but to keep feeding the military expenditure process. President Eisenhower's farewell address concerning the military industrial complex nailed the course for the United States since concerning conflicts. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19054402,"author":"MisterOctober","month":"2019-02","story":"The UAE’s secret hacking team of U.S. mercenaries","stance":"reference","why":"Eisenhower's cautionary farewell speech.","quote":"his famous \"military-industrial complex\" cautionary speech","text":"No doubt they were wary -- in fact, that attitude persisted well into the 20th century at least - for example, while he doesn't explicitly call out family dynasties and the military caste, those concerns clearly inform Eisenhower's thinking in his famous \"military-industrial complex\" cautionary speech: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17499818,"author":"api","month":"2018-07","story":"(2016) Technology destroys people and places. I’m rejecting it","stance":"reference","why":"Eisenhower coined 'military-industrial complex'.","quote":"It was accurately termed the \"military-industrial complex\" by Dwight Eisenhower","text":"We can also unquestionably say that communism is not the only alternative. For the record I am not an authoritarian communist and I don't think it's a good alternative. I was very specific. I said \"state corporatist capitalism\" because there are even capitalist alternatives to that such as voluntarist libertarian capitalism and communitarian/co-op oriented capitalism. Our system is one of state-backed corporate capitalism backed by a centrally managed financial system and closely tied to defense and intelligence agencies. It was accurately termed the \"military-industrial complex\" by Dwight Eisenhower, a communist... err... wait... Eisenhower was a conservative Christian and a Republican. [VIDEO] Your comment is exactly what I mean. We can't discuss alternatives because there seems to have been some campaign to gerrymander the language and discourse such that any questioning of the official system instantly implies that you're a Leninist or alternately a Hitlerist."}],"r":[["LtFyP0qy9XU",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["YCjNT9qGjh4",1],["1uflg7LDmzI",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["hx7BXih7zx8",1],["Z7rd04KzLcg",1]]},"TLm7Q92xMjQ":{"wy":"Brought up in Tesla auto-wiper threads as the clear explanation of how simple conventional rain sensors work.","q":{"x":"Technology Connections has a good video on how traditional automobile rain sensors work","i":40530860,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":46829952,"author":"tzs","month":"2026-01","story":"Tesla is committing automotive suicide","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the dedicated wiper sensor","quote":"Technology Connections has a good video on how it works [1].","text":"> The parking sensors basically don't work at all due to being vision only - and apparently can't be made to work properly What's stupid about this is that it is hard to come up with a good reason for it. The no LIDAR thing is at least understandable because at the time LIDAR was very expensive. Ultrasonic parking sensors are cheap. Same for automatic windshield wipers, which have been a significant source of complaints from Tesla owners. Pretty much everyone else uses a dedicated sensor that is simple and inexpensive and works well. Technology Connections has a good video on how it works [1]. Tesla uses vision. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40530860,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2024-05","story":"Tesla Auto Wipers: Why They Don't Work and Why There Isn't an Easy Fix","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how rain sensors work","quote":"Technology Connections has a good video on how traditional automobile rain sensors work","text":"For anyone curious, Technology Connections has a good video on how traditional automobile rain sensors work: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45148628,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2025-09","story":"Tesla changes meaning of 'Full Self-Driving', gives up on promise of autonomy","stance":"reference","why":"Explains wipers use total internal reflection","quote":"it's based on total internal reflection in the glass","text":"The wiper system has nothing to do with self-driving -- it's based on total internal reflection in the glass: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41664994,"author":"tzs","month":"2024-09","story":"Tesla Full Self Driving requires human intervention every 13 miles","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the simple infrared rain sensor design","quote":"Technology Connections did an episode [1] a few months ago on these sensors.","text":"I'm baffled by Tesla's approach to this. There are two ways cars currently do automatic wipers. One way uses a very clever, simple, cheap but very effective sensor. Technology Connections did an episode [1] a few months ago on these sensors. The electronics in it are just a couple infrared LEDs and infrared photodiodes. The other way is to use a camera, such as the camera you already have for your driver assist functions, and try to use computer vision software to figure out how much rain there is. Pretty much everyone except Tesla uses the first way. Tesla uses the second way. What baffles me is that of the three options they might have pursued: 1. Use a rain sensor like the ones everyone else uses, 2. Use a camera and computer vision software which doesn't work as well, and 3. Do not have automatic wipers, it seems to be that #2 is the worst option. Automatic wipers aren't really needed. Once you get used to where the controls are and how they work on a car without automatic wipers adjusting your wipers for the current conditions becomes one of those things you do completely unconsciously. So if my next car, like my current car, does not have automatic wipers it is not going to bother me. But if it has them and the don't work well, that very likely will annoy me, at least until I get tired of having to manually intervene and turn auto mode off or until manual intervention becomes something that I do unconsciously and so forget about them. In either case I end up back in a s"}],"r":[["94ed4v_T6YM",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["jyQwgBAaBag",1],["RkTvDjhImwo",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1],["5uiroWBkdFY",1],["4d0EGIt1SPc",1],["p45T7U5oi9Q",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HF-9Dy6iB_4",1],["gwf5mAlI7Ug",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["r-A78RgMhZU",1],["04ERSb06dOg",1],["ywWBy6J5gz8",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["YNTARSM-Fjc",1],["kAG39jKi0lI",1]]},"VxINoKFm-S4":{"wy":"Recommended as an approachable introduction to dependent types and a preview of what The Little Typer covers.","q":{"x":"Here's an intro to dependent types from David Christiansen","i":34457848,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34457848,"author":"spelunker","month":"2023-01","story":"Idris: A Language for Type-Driven Development","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to dependent types by David Christiansen","quote":"Here's an intro to dependent types from David Christiansen","text":"Here's an intro to dependent types from David Christiansen, I think it came up during another dependent types discussion: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33163210,"author":"adamgordonbell","month":"2022-10","story":"The Little Typer (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk giving a sense of what The Little Typer covers","quote":"The strange loop talk and my own interview of the authors might give some sense of what it's all about.","text":"A very mind-blowing and challenging book about Types. The strange loop talk and my own interview of the authors might give some sense of what it's all about. There is also some interesting work online showing how the language used in the book can be created. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":18270009,"author":"espeed","month":"2018-10","story":"Type-Driven Program Synthesis [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Type-driven development, meta languages, specs, provably correct code, and automatic code generation were some of the big overlapping themes at Strange Loop [1] this year. All the talks posted online this week [2], and I've posted links to some of the related videos below [3]. It's a good indicator that the time has come for an idea when multiple lines of research and projects from disparate domains begin converging on the same idea from different directions. Several facets of the puzzle I've been working the last few years have converged towards this area so it's good to see that it's not just me, maybe that means we're all on the right track. But as 'pron and those who have been working in this area have pointed out [4], this part of the puzzle is not yet solved, and one of the primary open issues is how to scale it to the system level (beyond provably correct programs to provably correct systems). One of the questions I've been pondering is how can we combine provably correct software specs with hardware specs identified for any given system to find the optimal data structures and algorithms given the constraints of that architecture. Does something similar already exist? What's the best software currently out there for simulating ideal data flow for a given hardware configuration and a given set of system constraints? [1] Strange Loop [LINK] [2] Strange Loop 2018 talks (80 videos) [LINK] [3] Related videos... * Vellvm - Verifying the LLVM, by Steve Zdancewic [LINK] * All "}],"r":[["DRq2NgeFcO0",1]]},"bKgf5PaBzyg":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as a notorious piece of McAfee lore, usually jokingly, whenever John McAfee or antivirus uninstallation comes up.","q":{"x":"At least he's got a sense of humor.","i":25828718,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41601439,"author":"specproc","month":"2024-09","story":"Forbes Marketplace: The Parasite SEO Company Trying to Devour Its Host","stance":"rec","why":"Wild McAfee video with sex and drug references, NSFW.","quote":"you are very, very welcome","text":"For anyone in the thread that's not seen \"How To Uninstall McAfee Antivirus\" by John McAfee, you are very, very welcome [0]. Content warning: it was made in his Alpha-PHP era, and contains a lot of sex and drug references. It's mostly sex and drugs IIRC. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25828718,"author":"tachyonbeam","month":"2021-01","story":"The short, tormented life of computer genius Phil Katz (2000)","stance":"rec","why":"Comedic, self-aware McAfee video about uninstalling McAfee.","quote":"At least he's got a sense of humor.","text":"I loved McAfee's video on How To Uninstall McAfee Antivirus: [VIDEO] At least he's got a sense of humor."},{"id":40751571,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2024-06","story":"Biden puts a bullet in Kaspersky: Sales, updates to be banned in America","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"And as John reminds us, it may be difficult to uninstall","text":"A lot of times, the antivirus is part of the crapware that comes with a new windows computer. After a month of free tryal, it start shiwing scarry reminders. And as John reminds us, it may be difficult to uninstall [VIDEO]"},{"id":26206649,"author":"2cb","month":"2021-02","story":"New malware found on 30k Macs has security pros stumped","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This attitude from users is a very real problem, but I'd argue it's platform agnostic. For instance there were headlines around the internet not too long ago stating Android is more secure than iOS based on claims made by Zerodium. Any Android user who read that may well have the same attitude you've described from iOS users. And it's potentially far more dangerous on Android because it allows you to sideload apps. You can even extend it to Windows. Your average user will buy a laptop preinstalled with McAfee [1] and think \"no need to worry about viruses now because I've got an antivirus.\" Don't get me wrong I agree it's perfectly reasonable to be critical of Apple when it's warranted, but we don't yet know if it is in this case. It's entirely possible (and fairly likely) this malware was delivered as a trojan using pop ups the user had to interact with. If that's the case you can't blame Apple for user error, especially when trojans exist for every single OS that allow the user to install software from the internet. > most users perform two types of installs: relatively safe ones through the App Store, or venturing out into the wild west of the internet and bringing home a random binary that hopefully does what it says. This seems to imply any software installed from the App Store is safe while anything from outside the App Store is dangerous. Isn't the implication that App Store downloads must automatically be safe falling into the same trap you're criticising here? Quotes "},{"id":24623183,"author":"austhrow743","month":"2020-09","story":"McAfee S-1","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"He already has the top YouTube result for \"how to uninstall McAfee\" [VIDEO] If he's already doing something, and like you say its a certainty that he'll continue, is that really a risk?"},{"id":18086190,"author":"rubatuga","month":"2018-09","story":"Facebook Is Giving Advertisers Access To Your Shadow Contact Information","stance":"reference","why":"Anecdote of the video resurfacing via a friend's account.","quote":"I had watched it many many years ago","text":"I’ve seen this happen with YouTube, specifically this video: [VIDEO] I had watched it many many years ago, and I suddenly remembered about it while at my friends apartment, (which is in the same building). Now I searched it up on my friends computer which was logged into his gmail account. We watched it and laughed. However, an hour later, I was on my iPhone at home when it appeared in my related videos. [LINK] I refreshed and it was gone..."},{"id":42023960,"author":"metadat","month":"2024-11","story":"Nvidia to join Dow Jones Industrial Average, replacing Intel","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yeah, McAfee.. [VIDEO] metadat slowly saunters off into the nearby foggy embankment, disappearing, Homer Simpson style"},{"id":24693788,"author":"howlgarnish","month":"2020-10","story":"McAfee Arrested in Spain, DOJ Indictment for Tax Evasion Using Cryptocurrencies","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"some greatest hits: How to Uninstall McAfee (NSFW-ish)","text":"McAfee, the marketing nightmare that just keeps on giving. I genuinely don't understand why McAfee the company hasn't changed its name yet. In the meantime, some greatest hits: How to Uninstall McAfee (NSFW-ish) [VIDEO] The McAfee guide to bribery in the third world [LINK]"},{"id":6445915,"author":"tlrobinson","month":"2013-09","story":"McAfee vows to make Internet 'impossible to hack'","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"From the guy who produced this (NSFW) video, \"How To Uninstall McAfee Antivirus\" [VIDEO] (NSFW) This is going to be entertaining..."}]},"etx0k1nLn78":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as a visual debunking of Benford-based election fraud claims and as a guide to when Benford's Law does and does not apply.","q":{"x":"Matt Parker did a good video with lots of visuals to explain why Benford's Law works in general, but why it cannot always be simply applied to election results.","i":35588673,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35588673,"author":"MrJohz","month":"2023-04","story":"Investigating and preventing scientific misconduct with Benford’s law","stance":"rec","why":"Visual explanation of why Benford's Law works but not for election results","quote":"Matt Parker did a good video with lots of visuals to explain why Benford's Law works in general, but why it cannot always be simply applied to election results.","text":"Matt Parker did a good video with lots of visuals to explain why Benford's Law works in general, but why it cannot always be simply applied to election results. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25553156,"author":"nitrogen","month":"2020-12","story":"California Public Utilities Commission fired director who exposed missing $200M","stance":"rec","why":"Mathematician analyzes numerical allegations of election fraud","quote":"a mathematician and comedian I follow on YouTube (from a different country who presumably has no horse in the race) has analyzed some of the numerical allegations of fraud","text":"I am replying for the sake of the hidden sibling comment: I don't watch any major media coverage of politics, but a mathematician and comedian I follow on YouTube (from a different country who presumably has no horse in the race) has analyzed some of the numerical allegations of fraud: - [LINK] - [LINK] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":41125177,"author":"adgjlsfhk1","month":"2024-08","story":"Suspicious data pattern in recent Venezuelan election","stance":"reference","why":"Precincts have even populations so Benford's law doesn't apply","quote":"Precints have roughly even populations and therefore typically don't conform to Benford's law.","text":"Precints have roughly even populations and therefore typically don't conform to Benford's law. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41126132,"author":"nick238","month":"2024-08","story":"Suspicious data pattern in recent Venezuelan election","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as coverage of poor 2020 election analyses","quote":"There were some pretty shit analysises of the 2020 US elections that Matt Parker covered","text":"There were some pretty shit analysises of the 2020 US elections that Matt Parker covered with videos like \"Why do Biden's votes not follow Benford's law?\"[1] and \"Why was Biden's win calculated to be 1 in 1,000,000,000,000,000?\"[2]. I don't know about other countries, but the amount of data that every county in every US state produces makes systematic fraud pretty much impossible. If there's literally only 3 numbers produced by the Venezuelan government, you need to be seriously incompetent to have detectable fraud because techniques like Benford's or Zipf's law need lots of individual numbers. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":39727137,"author":"runarberg","month":"2024-03","story":"How the Gaza Ministry of Health Fakes Casualty Numbers","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of flawed election-fraud statistics","quote":"","text":"First of all it looks at suspiciously limited data (Oct 26th to Nov 11th) when we know more data is available. This is a known tactic from climate deniers who claim e.g. that data from a single weather station in Canada from October 2006 to June 2014 shows the world is actually cooling. Second of all it makes vague assumptions about distributions without clarifying or even justifying why it should have a different distribution. This is straight from the playbook of Trump’s election denialism where some variation of election results was somehow proof of tampering[1]. With statistics you can find any results you want if you look at limited enough data from a large enough pool, and you can find any abnormality you want by giving unrealistic expectations. This is why you need to clarify which distribution you expect, and—more importantly—justify it by pointing at other examples from similar pools. This is never done here, which is why I call it pseudo-statistical nonesence. Stating it is abnormal is not enough, even when you provide some fancy box plots to do so. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35595207,"author":"gdavisson","month":"2023-04","story":"Investigating and preventing scientific misconduct with Benford’s law","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among debunkings of Benford-based election fraud claims","quote":"","text":"I don't know about that particular analysis, but there've been a number of such claims that don't stand up (mostly because, as you ask, the districts themselves don't follow Benford's law). See, for example, \"Inappropriate Applications of Benford’s Law Regularities to Some Data from the 2020 Presidential Election in the United States\" by Walter R. Mebane, Jr. [0], and \"Why do Biden's votes not follow Benford's Law?\" by Matt Parker [1]. This fits the general pattern that there's been a lot of suspicion raised about fraud in the 2020 election, but none of it actually seems to pan out. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27949946,"author":"contravariant","month":"2021-07","story":"Using Benford’s Law to Detect Bitcoin Manipulation","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why vote counts of similar magnitude defy Benford's law","quote":"","text":"Benford's law doesn't give much of a guarantee except in highly specific scenarios. You can however show it's pretty accurate if you've got a 'smooth' probability distribution spanning multiple orders of magnitude (how much of a guarantee you can give depends on how specific you define what it means to be 'smooth'). The rigged elections example you gave was, I believe, mostly explained by the fact that the number of votes they were looking at were all roughly the same order of magnitude [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25666928,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2021-01","story":"U.S. Capitol Locked Down Amid Escalating Protests","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence rebutting fraud claims","quote":"","text":"> Can you link me to those majority of cases? [LINK] [VIDEO] And this third one just shows how much of a joke some of the claims in the lawsuits are: [LINK] Like, this is basic high school level statistics they can't even get right."},{"id":25140117,"author":"tzs","month":"2020-11","story":"Trump says he fired top cybersecurity official Christopher Krebs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Precincts in most places are designed to have roughly the same number of voters. Hence, Benford's law does not work for them. Here's a good explanation, using Chicago precinct data [1]. A good rule of thumb is that if Benford's law does not apply to the legal voter population of your precincts/counties/wards/districts/whatever, or when applied to the total number of registered voters in those areas, then it won't apply to the results of a fair election either. (This is why if you ask every adult you know their height in inches and then look at the distribution of first digits, it will look nothing at all like a Benford's law distribution. The heights are going to be the sum of two normal distributions (one for men, one for women)). A good example of that is the county results in Georgia. There is a video going around from a forensic CPA showing the votes by county did not followed Benford's law, but he failed to look at the underlying population. I've written a little web-based simulation using the Georgia data to make it easy to play with the data to get a feel for why Benford's law does not work there [2]. Another way to tell when a deviation from Benford's law is not an indicator of election fraud is to look at what it would take to make the results fit Benford's law. If you can't find any reasonable way to adjust the votes to make it fit, then the lack of fit was not a fraud indicator. (And if you can find such an adjustment, it also needs to stay a Benford fit if you mak"}],"r":[["lyqt6u5_sHA",1],["aokNwKx7gM8",1],["ua5aOFi-DKs",1]]},"fCbAafKLqC8":{"wy":"Recommended as the answer to why breadboard computers are worth building and why breadboards work at these speeds.","q":{"x":"Ben Eater had a whole video on why he encourages building a breadboard computer.","i":29517760,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29517760,"author":"eco","month":"2021-12","story":"An 8-bit minicomputer with a fully custom architecture","stance":"rec","why":"Why building a computer on breadboards is worthwhile.","quote":"Ben Eater had a whole video on why he encourages building a breadboard computer.","text":"Ben Eater had a whole video on why he encourages building a breadboard computer. [VIDEO] A lot of people have taken what they've learned and moved on to designing their own PCB versions. You can see some of them here: [LINK]"},{"id":25043830,"author":"205guy","month":"2020-11","story":"Let’s Build a Video Card","stance":"rec","why":"Ben Eater explains why breadboards work, testing on the scope.","quote":"he has a dedicated video where he answers this with testing on the scope","text":"I got caught up in all his videos and had the same question. Fortunately, he has a dedicated video where he answers this with testing on the scope: [VIDEO] tl;dr is what the sibling comment already explained: low clock rates."},{"id":31058290,"author":"stuntkite","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: How do I get over my obsession with operating systems?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think this is good advice. It reminds me of the advice about that the difference between a hoard and a collection is the display case. It's great to have the interests and you like what you like, but if it's just a never ending pile that is damaging other pursuits it's no good for you, but if it's something you can catalog, explore, curate, and especially share in a meaningful way then having that interest is important. Even if no one cares about your display case but you. Also, OP, maybe look into building an operating system from first principles like that dude who made the tetris OS [0] or look into creating a nice collection of curated OS VM snapshots that demonstrate the things you're into in a running environment with tools like LXC/LXD [1] ( these days it's surprisingly easy to share VMs running with very detailed emulated specs running connected to a web browser) or look into taking the time to assemble one of those kits that lets you build a historic processor with LEDs and transistors on a bread board [2]. Or if you just like reading about it, even just keeping a journal or private wiki will I think make it so it gains personal context. Also, you like what you like. If it's not really hurting you to have a hobby about loving the history of OSs, I know some folks that are pretty encyclopedic about the inside baseball of pro wrestling or star trek. Hunt your bliss. For what it's worth, I'm pretty sure your insights into the things your reading is something I'd enjoy"}],"r":[["wVp5koAOu9M",1],["EWqThb9Z1jk",1],["bbyr7jZOllI",1],["sLFaa6RPJIU",1]]},"rHKwIYsPXLg":{"wy":"Used as a source for Searle's own argument in Chinese room discussions, though some find nothing new in it.","q":{"x":"He explains this in the google video very well","i":10890180,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13787768,"author":"3498rtigcufk","month":"2017-03","story":"Has philosophy ever clarified mathematics?","stance":"rec","why":"Introductory resource on philosophy of mind from a good speaker.","quote":"John Searl is good speaker so try his talk","text":"Philosophy is not some sort of church where everyone praise some emotionally agreed upon idea... You seems to be turning this into some sort of us vs. them game based on your faulty projections, so I hope to stop here. (Btw, If you are getting your ideas about philosophy from popular youtube channels like [LINK] or [LINK] , maybe I can understand why you have such misunderstandings) ----------- If you happen to be interested anytime, you can start with some very introductory resources I bothered to look up for you (most are video, they are easy to consume): + Donald Hoffman - computational theory of mind, someone closer to HN's demographic ( [LINK] ) + John Searl is good speaker so try his talk ( [VIDEO] ) + Some thought experiments (remember that thought experiments are not highlighters of issue, not complete arguments) [LINK] + If you want to know what a complete technical work looks like, here's one I've been reading: [LINK] + Not agreed upon but a fun one to not include - about: what is philosophy: [LINK] + Science vs philosophy sort of video, slow but good discussion in there: [LINK] + [LINK] In the unlikely case that you become very interested, you can look up \"Introduction to philosophy of mind syllabus\" and go through the materials and/or books of your choice on the subject. ----------- > What is the philosophical method for interrogation an empirical phenomenon That would go into philosophy of science, which I have absolutely no familiarity with. I'm guessing, to a p"},{"id":10890180,"author":"makebelieve","month":"2016-01","story":"Ask HN: Opinion about Searle's Chinese Room?","stance":"rec","why":"Searle explains why computation cannot capture semantic content.","quote":"He explains this in the google video very well","text":"I was not trying to connect rule 110 to Searle's argument per se, but rather to the critique of Searle's argument. Namely, that criticisms of the lookup table are not criticisms of Searle's argument or the point he makes. a C++ program, brainf k, a CA, a one instruction set computer, or whatever computational process is used doesn't matter. The lookup table is just one component of the Rooms operation. I agree Searle is talking about a hash table, but he is also talking about the rules to interface an input value to a set of possible output values via some mechanical process, and the man in the room acts as a kind of stack machine. You are right, Searle isn't making an argument about the translation of sentences. (translating them to what?) He is making an argument about how the mechanism of computation cannot capture semantic content. He explains this in the google video very well: [VIDEO] And all of the... let's call them \"structural\" critiques are moot. Searle's point is that computer systems cannot understand semantic content because they are syntactic processing machines. And he shows this with his argument. The opposite view is that computers can understand semantic content. (so there is understanding and there is meaning understood by the computer) and the reason Searle doesn't believe computers can do this is because his argument is flawed. Which leaves us with a small set of options: 1) That the structure Searle proposes can in fact understand semantic content and Se"},{"id":11390824,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2016-03","story":"How to create an AI startup – convince some humans to be your training set","stance":"reference","why":"Searle's view of repackaging human intelligence.","quote":"Searle would have us interpret this as the company taking the intelligence of the humans, refining and repackaging it.","text":"Searle would have us interpret this as the company taking the intelligence of the humans, refining and repackaging it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10885287,"author":"makebelieve","month":"2016-01","story":"Ask HN: Opinion about Searle's Chinese Room?","stance":"reference","why":"Searle's argument that computation is syntactic.","quote":"","text":"Your points are all good. But they have nothing to do with meaning, or with semantics. Cellular automata are lookup tables, and Wolfram and others proved some cellular automata rules are Turing complete computers. [LINK] My point was merely about the equivalence of computational mechanisms, not about lookup tables per se. And by corollary, that the computational complexity is equivalent regardless of the computational mechanism. (I think we agree on this point.) Searle's Room is a just to explain that what computers are doing is syntactic. Searle would posit that passing a Turing test in any amount of time is irrelevant to determining consciousness. It's a story we hypothetical use to \"measure\" intelligence, but it's only a story. it's not a valid test for sentience, and passing such a test would not confer sentience. Sentience is an entirely different question. What would be more interesting is if a computer intentionally failed turing tests because it thinks turing tests are stupid. We could test humans with Turing tests to determine their \"level\" of intelligence. But if you put teenage boys in a room and made them do Turing tests, pretty quick they would come up with remarkable ways to fail those tests, chiefly by not doing them! How could you write a program or create a system that intentionally fails Turing tests? or a program which avoids taking turing tests... because it thinks they are stupid? Could you write a program that knows when it fails? (it's a pretty long sta"},{"id":10742375,"author":"bhickey","month":"2015-12","story":"John Searle: Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"There isn't much new here.","text":"There isn't much new here. Skip ahead to the first audience question from Ray Kurzweil ( [VIDEO] ). Kurzweil, in summary, asks \"You say that a machine manipulating symbols can't have consciousness. Why is this different than consciousness arising neurons manipulating neurotransmitter concentrations?\" Searle gives a non-answer: \"My dog has consciousness because I can look at it and conclude that it has consciousness.\""}]},"tWtvrPTbQ_c":{"wy":"HN points to it as a short source on operant conditioning in games, loot boxes and engagement loops, with design alternatives.","q":{"x":"Extra Credits has a nice overview, which includes a few design suggestions","i":10174481,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":15259866,"author":"glogla","month":"2017-09","story":"Parents Who Pay to Be Watched","stance":"rec","why":"Explanation of game reward mechanics, plus other Extra Credits videos","quote":"Take a look at this video [1] for explanation, and possibly other Extra Credits videos if you're interested in game design.","text":"Take a look at this video [1] for explanation, and possibly other Extra Credits videos if you're interested in game design. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10174481,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-09","story":"Playing video games can improve our lives","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of Skinner box mechanics in games with design alternatives","quote":"Extra Credits has a nice overview, which includes a few design suggestions","text":"> time slips away It doesn't help that many modern games explicitly use Operant Conditioning to keep people playing. MMOs and \"free to play\" games are notorious, but you will find Skinner Boxes in a lot of modern games. Extra Credits has a nice overview, which includes a few design suggestions which games can use instead of hooking players with a reward schedule. [VIDEO] Also, it's not just games - you will find Operant Conditioning style reward schedules in some \"social media\" businesses, too, which can waste someone's day just as bad as any game."},{"id":19137328,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2019-02","story":"Activision Blizzard staff reportedly bracing for layoffs","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates variable reward schedules driving engagement","quote":"","text":"These days though, monetization frequently means vastly inferior games make way, way more money. To an algorithm, or a metric, even miserable feeling variable schedule or reward addiction looks like \"engagement.\" So if there's \"engagement\" and money, why should companies do anything different? [VIDEO] So how is it that games which have \"art\" don't come along, charge a bit less money, and win in the marketplace? I guess the argument can be made that they do, but they're in the minority. I wonder if there aren't also generational issues? Maybe the environment of today, where even young people have tablets and smartphones is just a different one?"},{"id":17968761,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-09","story":"EA reportedly under criminal investigation in Belgium due to FIFA's loot boxes","stance":"reference","why":"Source for operant conditioning in games","quote":"","text":"> A workaround would be for EA to simply officially sell the game's \"coins\" Preventing this kind of workaround is already part of many gambling laws. They usually define the payment/stake/wager as a very generic term like \"anything of value\". For example, in NV (as a stereotypical representation of a locale allows gambling), the law defines[1]: >> “Representative of value” means any instrumentality used by a patron in a game whether or not the instrumentality may be redeemed for cash. >> “Wager” means a sum of money or representative of value that is risked on an occurrence for which the outcome is uncertain. Any type of in-game \"coin\" still represents value to the people playing the game, so trading them for lootboxes with uncertain contents is still a \"wager\". The gaming industry could solve a lot of this mess if they simply *sold people the game (or game pieces) directly as a defined product. Of course, that wouldn't exploit the human weakness to operant conditioning[2]... ~sigh~ [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13829839,"author":"YCode","month":"2017-03","story":"How I Kicked My Smartphone Addiction, and Never Looked Back","stance":"reference","why":"Source on conditioned reward loops","quote":"I suppose a more concrete source would be the Extra Credits video on Skinner Boxes","text":"It was a long time ago and I wish I was less foggy on the details but I read this comment on Reddit by a purported neuroscientist or some such who was talking about how we don't actually enjoy social media. They described basically what you've said. We take the actions and get the tiny reward and our brain gets a little jolt of there you did it but when it comes right down to it we don't actually enjoy the activity. More importantly, we're no more satisfied when we finish the activity than we were when we started, we just do it because we've become conditioned to. I suppose a more concrete source would be the Extra Credits video on Skinner Boxes: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"uF-GSj-Exms":{"wy":"Pointed to as the clearest explanation of what Foundry actually is, and for its polished software, though one commenter calls it dated now.","q":{"x":"This Foundry demo impressed me at the time but its a bit dated now","i":46408833,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":47026724,"author":"nimrody","month":"2026-02","story":"Palantir Gets Millions of Dollars from New York City's Public Hospitals","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of data handling and versioned pipelines.","quote":"Some of their stuff for handling data and versioned pipelines seem very well done.","text":"They have an entire youtube channel. For example, see this: [VIDEO] Some of their stuff for handling data and versioned pipelines seem very well done."},{"id":31241843,"author":"pcurve","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: What are your favorite examples of elegant software?","stance":"rec","why":"Polished, fast, well-designed enterprise software demo.","quote":"their working software looks better than most designer's portfolio mockups","text":"I figured I'd get some downvotes mentioning PLTR here. ;-) GPalantir is definitely being more open with their demo now, so there are some good ones on their youtube channel. You can skim through. [VIDEO] I'm impressed by how polished everything looks. As a person who does UX / product design, their working software looks better than most designer's portfolio mockups. I'm impressed by how fast and snappy everything works or feels. I'm impressed by how rich and custom tailored their UI component library is. I'm impressed by how focused and tailored their UI for job at hand. I'm impressed by how every single page in their application looks beautiful, not just a handful. They actually have all their React UI library published as opensource here. [LINK] If there's anyone from pltr reading this, good job. Your design people are amazing."},{"id":46408833,"author":"karlitooo","month":"2025-12","story":"Say No to Palantir in the NHS","stance":"reference","why":"Foundry demo, impressive but dated.","quote":"This Foundry demo impressed me at the time but its a bit dated now","text":"When foundry came out the demo looked next level. I'm sure you could do much of the same thing with FOSS but IMO the problem that the NHS has been struggling with for multiple decades really looks like a lack of Technical Leadership due to the complexity of the environment and care needed when dealing with patient data. Hopefully Palantir has the necessary skillset to navigate the political environment which involves developing a platform that: 1. protects patient privacy 2. supports needs of providers (e.g. hospitals, gps, specialists, DoH) 3. allows providers to use data to support their operations 4. allows NHS to use the data to improve patient outcomes and efficiency This Foundry demo impressed me at the time but its a bit dated now: [VIDEO] Actual data analyst from a hospital talking about what the platform achieves: [LINK]"},{"id":33593651,"author":"afhammad","month":"2022-11","story":"Show HN: Open-source alternative to Retool","stance":"reference","why":"Explains what the platform is better than the website.","quote":"this might help","text":"@vladsanchez, @sevazhidkov, @Aeolun I agree, the website is terrible at explaining what it is, I only got it by seeing it fully implemented in a business. It's too broad to describe here but this might help: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["zDZFcDGpL4U",1],["xb_PyKuI7II",1]]},"vT1JzLTH4G4":{"wy":"HN recommends the CS231n lectures as a solid, applied deep learning course for self-study.","q":{"x":"The Stanford course is pretty good.","i":16921516,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":44210120,"author":"jackdoe","month":"2025-06","story":"The Illusion of Thinking: Strengths and limitations of reasoning models [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Part of the commenter's study regimen for learning neural networks","quote":"Mainly watching and re-watching Karpathy's 'Zero To Hero'[1] and Stanford's 'Introduction to Convolutional Neural Networks for Visual Recognition'[2]","text":"For studying? Mainly watching and re-watching Karpathy's 'Zero To Hero'[1] and Stanford's 'Introduction to Convolutional Neural Networks for Visual Recognition'[2], also a lot of transformers from scratch videos like Umar Jamali's videos[3], and I also study backwards to McCulloch and Pitts. Reading the 30 papers [LINK] and so on. And of course Yannic Kilcher[4], and also listening in on the paper discussions they do on discord. Practicing a lot with just doing backpropagation by hand and making toy models by hand to get intuition for the signal flow, and building all kinds of smallish systems, e.g. how far can you push whisper, small qwen3, and kokoro to control your computer with voice? People think that deepseek/mistral/meta etc are democratizing AI, but its actually Karpathy who teaches us :) so we can understand them and make our own. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":16921516,"author":"dahart","month":"2018-04","story":"Microsoft makes AI training courses available to the public","stance":"rec","why":"Stanford CNN course lectures, a solid applied deep learning course","quote":"The Stanford course is pretty good.","text":"The Stanford course is pretty good. [VIDEO] For something quicker & less applied, maybe the 3b1b series on neural networks: [LINK]"},{"id":17618555,"author":"varelse","month":"2018-07","story":"Efficient Methods and Hardware for Deep Learning [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture 15 covers efficient hardware and methods for deep learning","quote":"See lecture 15 of cs231n 2017","text":"See lecture 15 of cs231n 2017: [VIDEO] Further, while all these tricks are amazing, they are not currently of much use to fungible data scientists at FAANG and in the greater Fortune 500 because they haven't been added to any of the major frameworks in a manner that is braindead/automagic. Further, most of these are but one GPU generation away from general availability if they become popular like FP16/32 mixed precision did. That said, the acquisition of DeePhi by Xilinx ought to be a sufficiently strong signal to add them to said frameworks, no? [LINK] Finally, my gut instincts (based on the monotonically decreasing speech recognition quality of my increasingly powerful Android phones over time, and the bizarre spelling errors it now makes such as \"tech kneeq\" as one example) is that one cannot rate the quality of these techniques from a single metric, and it seems like the decision to foist them on users may be based on one. #ItsAdversariesAllTheWayDown"}],"r":[["-kFOXP026eE",1],["HI09kat_GeI",1],["VMj-3S1tku0",1],["pyX8kQ-JzHI",1],["hlrv7DIHllE",1],["MFRWDuduuSw",1],["aircAruvnKk",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["WrxZ4AZPdOQ",1],["LUFp6sjKbkE",1],["0Rnq1NpHdmw",1],["hQVTIJBZook",1],["1-dUkyn_fZA",1],["l7rce6IQDWs",1]]},"vXjVFPosQHw":{"wy":"Recommended as a framework-agnostic, common-sense guide to structuring large front-end applications.","q":{"x":"It's framework-agnostic and not heavy on code, just a lot of common sense that might not be obvious for some.","i":9165643,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9165643,"author":"obeid","month":"2015-03","story":"Ask HN: Front-end “design patterns”?","stance":"rec","why":"Framework-agnostic, light on code, full of common sense.","quote":"It's framework-agnostic and not heavy on code, just a lot of common sense that might not be obvious for some.","text":"I watched this old talk from the YUI Library recently. It's framework-agnostic and not heavy on code, just a lot of common sense that might not be obvious for some. Nicholas Zakas: Scalable JavaScript Application Architecture [VIDEO]"},{"id":5121235,"author":"politician","month":"2013-01","story":"A new development era","stance":"rec","why":"Zakas approach to scalable JavaScript architecture, implemented successfully.","quote":"after watching his presentation","text":"I've also successfully implemented Zakas' [1] approach after watching his presentation \"Scalable Javascript Application Architecture\" [2] and abandoning Backbone.Aura [3]. Aura theoretically implements this, but in practice we found that it made some assumptions that were incompatible with our needs. Consequently, we rolled our own implementation. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":5121567,"author":"calpaterson","month":"2013-01","story":"A new development era","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the theory of modular application architecture.","quote":"was very useful in explaining the theory","text":"This video: [VIDEO] (which was also posted by politician) was very useful in explaining the theory. I also made some changes to it, but offhand I can't remember what they were. I think it was to do with the separation of starting vs initialising modules (parts of a page)."},{"id":7082223,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2014-01","story":"Patterns For Large-Scale JavaScript Application Architecture","stance":"reference","why":"Guess at the talk behind the broken previous-work link.","quote":"I believe this could be the talk by Nicholas Zakas","text":"The 'previous work' link redirected me to a generic search results page ( [LINK] ) , I believe this could be the talk by Nicholas Zakas [VIDEO] but if anyone can confirm, that would be nice."}],"r":[["yvFKh_3evC8",1]]},"1iAJPoc23-M":{"wy":"Treated as a remarkable early look at ideas like hypertext, agents and the web before they existed.","q":{"x":"I just discovered this amazing documentary about hypertext from 1990, written by Douglas Adams","i":22625202,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22625202,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-03","story":"Hyperland (1990) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"1990 Douglas Adams documentary on hypertext featuring Ted Nelson","quote":"I just discovered this amazing documentary about hypertext from 1990, written by Douglas Adams","text":"I just discovered this amazing documentary about hypertext from 1990, written by Douglas Adams, with Tom Baker (The Fourth Doctor Who!) as a Software Agent, and featuring many other amazing people, like Ted Nelson! Douglas Adams Hyperland [VIDEO] >Douglas Adams: Are you sure you mean Agent? >Software Agent: I mean that I'm a Software Agent. I am merely a simulacrum. An artificial and completely customizable personality. And I only exist as what we call an \"Application\" in your computer. I have the honor to provide instant access to every piece of information stored digitally anywhere in the world. Any picture, or film, any sound, any book, any statistic, any fact. Any connection between anything you care to think of, you have only to tell me, and it will be my humble duty to find it for you and to present it to you for your interactive pleasure. Is there anything I can do for you now, Mister Adams, sir? >Douglas Adams: Well yes, you can stop being so obsequious, for a start. >Software Agent: It will be my very great and profound pleasure to be less obsequious, oh Master. I am, as I humbly mentioned to you a moment ago, Fully Customizable. Allow me to present you with my Control Panel. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":16857613,"author":"enkiv2","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ted Nelson's Computers for Cynics probably doesn't contain technical information that's new to any of you, but Ted has a knack for reframing things in ways that make the arbitrariness of certain historical decisions clear: [LINK] On the subject of hypertext, The Web That Wasn't gives a nice history of the idea (for anybody who thinks it starts with TBL -- surprisingly many people!): [LINK] Another reframing-oriented talk is Clay Shirky's \"It's not information overload, it's filter failure\", which ultimately leads to Shirky suggesting the kinds of user-oriented filtering features that Mastodon has implemented: [LINK] At the intersection of neurology and information science, Peter Watts always has something interesting to say, and as a former marine biologist focusing on the nervous system of starfish, this is absolutely in his wheelhouse: [LINK] This one (\"moving away from defensive programming\") justified strong typing in a pretty clear way: [LINK] Dan Dennett is just as relevant as Doug Hofstadter when it comes to metacognition: [LINK] Forgotten Ideas in Computer Science starts slow, but if you don't have much of a historical background (like, if you're only vaguely aware of what happened in CS in the 70s), it's a laundry list of things you should look up and be aware of before you start your next project: [LINK] Everybody should understand procedural generation: [LINK] Likewise, since AI is hyped up right now, we should all remind ourselves that IA is a thing too: [LINK] Mo"},{"id":11393809,"author":"sp332","month":"2016-03","story":"Microsoft Bot Framework","stance":"reference","why":"Douglas Adams documentary showing early 90s vision of agents","quote":"Here's video of Douglas Adams and Tim Baker giving a tour of the future.","text":"This reminds me strongly of \"agents\" that were going to do your bidding, as prophesied in the early 90's. Here's video of Douglas Adams and Tim Baker giving a tour of the future. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v4Wy7gRGgeA",1],["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["helScS3coAE",1],["KVN_0qvuhhw",1],["yMjPqr1s-cg",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["whPWKecazgM",1],["pqQSwQLDIK8",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["8Gv0H-vPoDc",1],["wpOxbesRNBc",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["I4flQ8XdvJM",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["Jd3-eiid-Uw",1]]},"2guvwQvElA8":{"wy":"Used to illustrate that fire safes protect against fire and not theft, and that brute force often defeats low-security containers.","q":{"x":"For surprise of tool used the saw vs safe are the best","i":45724873,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45724873,"author":"bombcar","month":"2025-10","story":"10M people watched a YouTuber shim a lock; the lock company sued him – bad idea","stance":"rec","why":"Surprising tool choice: a saw cutting a safe.","quote":"For surprise of tool used the saw vs safe are the best","text":"For surprise of tool used the saw vs safe are the best: [VIDEO] The main thing locks do is make it noisy to get in."},{"id":31644478,"author":"kube-system","month":"2022-06","story":"How to open a safe","stance":"reference","why":"Shows brute force defeating low-security containers.","quote":"Brute force attacks are the much more common scenario against low security locks","text":"Finding vulnerabilities in low security locks is just an academic exercise. If you need high security, you are buying a product very different than a master lock, and you need to consider the integrity of the entire system it is connected to. Low security locks work mainly by deterrent, and even if they were without vulnerability, the container you’re attaching it to probably is vulnerable. Brute force attacks are the much more common scenario against low security locks, and there’s not much of a low cost solution to that; it requires expensive materials: [VIDEO] If you put an unpickable lock on sheet metal box, you’re spending more money, but not actually improving security. The reason that master locks are popular is because they are fairly reliable, easy to get keys for, have useful feature sets, etc. These are the features that commercial users often care about the most. They don’t want to spend more money to get an unpickable lock that interrupts their daily business when it inevitably gets dirty, keys are lost, etc. especially if it provides no additional resistance against an angle grinder."},{"id":18219260,"author":"dangerface","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What secure personal file hosting solution do you use?","stance":"reference","why":"Fire safes protect against fire, not theft.","quote":"they do nothing to stop a thief so the fire safety is all they are good for.","text":"They are supposed to and apparently work well, they do nothing to stop a thief so the fire safety is all they are good for. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XxCha4Kez9c",1]]},"3E9ga-CylWQ":{"wy":"Cited in OpenBSD threads as a skeptical, evidence-based counterpoint to claims about its security, though some dispute its objectivity.","q":{"x":"the presenter explicitly calls out OpenBSD's tendency to present mitigations without specific examples of CVEs it defeats","i":38607506,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":26625458,"author":"jron","month":"2021-03","story":"FreeBSD Core Team Statement on FreeBSD Development Processes","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation with more context than the slides alone.","quote":"I've watched it before and it is compelling","text":"Here is the presentation which provides more context than the slides alone: [VIDEO] I've watched it before and it is compelling; however, at the end of the day, an OS that tries to take security seriously is probably better than one that doesn't. The OpenBSD code is small and I suspect that the defects/KLOC is far less than other projects capable of routing. I'd love to hear more critiques for using it as a home router though."},{"id":40706286,"author":"DEADMINCE","month":"2024-06","story":"OpenBSD, the computer appliance maker's secret weapon","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on OpenBSD mitigations at CCC.","quote":"There was even a talk[0] at the 36th CCC.","text":"I wouldn't say it's missing. I deliberately generalized. I mentioned RBAC as an example. If people want to learn more there is no shortage of info. There was even a talk[0] at the 36th CCC. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23236204,"author":"q3k","month":"2020-05","story":"OpenBSD 6.7","stance":"reference","why":"Talk evaluating OpenBSD security mitigations, with some objectivity disputes.","quote":"Here's a recent talk evaluating OpenBSD's security mitigations","text":"Here's a recent talk evaluating OpenBSD's security mitigations: [VIDEO] The objectivity of it is up to debate though, some argue that it is biased/unfair."},{"id":38607506,"author":"vngzs","month":"2023-12","story":"OpenBSD – pinning all system calls","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the presenter explicitly calls out OpenBSD's tendency to present mitigations without specific examples of CVEs it defeats","text":"Indeed, in CCC's \"systematic evaluation of OpenBSD's mitigations\"[0] the presenter explicitly calls out OpenBSD's tendency to present mitigations without specific examples of CVEs it defeats or exploit techniques the mitigations are known to defend against: > Proper mitigations I think stem from proper design and threat modeling. Strong, reality-based statements like \"this kills these vulnerabilities,\" or \"this kills this CVE; it delays production of an exploit by one week.\" And also thorough testing by seasoned exploit writers. Anything else is relying on pure luck, superstition, and wishful thinking. Some of OpenBSD's mitigations are excellent and robust in defensiveness; others are amorphous and not particularly useful. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34088949,"author":"pseudostem","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: FOSS Projects Worth Donating To?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"there are a few video's [0] and blog posts dissing OpenBSDs security claims.","text":"I use OpenBSD as a daily driver. To make GP's point though, there are a few video's [0] and blog posts dissing OpenBSDs security claims. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33079951,"author":"MonkeyClub","month":"2022-10","story":"For personal reasons, I do not browse the web from my computer. (2007)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"some people have argued that de Raadt (and the OpenBSD team) are not paranoid _enough_","text":"Yeah he doesn't seem paranoid at all, just very aware of what holes the tech stack has. In fact, some people have argued that de Raadt (and the OpenBSD team) are not paranoid _enough_: [VIDEO] \"36C3 - A systematic evaluation of OpenBSD's mitigations\""}]},"6egeUxIEQnM":{"wy":"HN links it to explain why people repeatedly get large-number arithmetic wrong in discussions of money and mental math.","q":{"x":"Here's a great explanation of what's probably happening","i":30503529,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30503529,"author":"christiangenco","month":"2022-02","story":"What use is mental math in 2022?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the units mistake in mental arithmetic with millions","quote":"Here's a great explanation of what's probably happening","text":"This mistake is so interesting to me. Here's a great explanation of what's probably happening: [VIDEO] tl;dr: mentally we take off \"millions\" as a unit, do the calculation, then add \"millions\" back on. This works for addition, subtraction, and inequality but fails for multiplication and division."},{"id":42667295,"author":"csours","month":"2025-01","story":"Very Wrong Math","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of this classic: [LINK] “Bloomberg spent $500 million on ads. The U.S. population is 327 million. He could have given each American $1 million and still have money left over.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":37907739,"author":"TRiG_Ireland","month":"2023-10","story":"Edinburgh couple are handed £17K bill by Tesla as they 'drove in rain'","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why people repeatedly make this math error","quote":"there is a reason why people keep making it, as Matt Parker discusses","text":"I'm not sure that there's a name for that kind of maths error, but there is a reason why people keep making it, as Matt Parker discusses: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":22835732,"author":"infogulch","month":"2020-04","story":"Fed should pay every American, let hedge funds and billionaires ‘get wiped out’","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why people keep getting million/billion arithmetic wrong","quote":"Matt Parker made a video that tries to explain how people keep getting this basic arithmetic completely wrong.","text":"Matt Parker made a video that tries to explain how people keep getting this basic arithmetic completely wrong. [VIDEO]"}]},"79fgH08z6xs":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as the best overview of how 3D on the GBA progressed over the console's life, usually in threads about GBA ports and homebrew 3D engines.","q":{"x":"For a good overview of the progress of 3d GBA graphics over the console's lifespan I'd reccomended this video history","i":31748483,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31748483,"author":"koofdoof","month":"2022-06","story":"Quake on the Game Boy Advance [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of 3D graphics progress on the GBA","quote":"For a good overview of the progress of 3d GBA graphics over the console's lifespan I'd reccomended this video history","text":"For a good overview of the progress of 3d GBA graphics over the console's lifespan I'd reccomended this video history: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35595106,"author":"phire","month":"2023-04","story":"Pico3D: Open World 3D Game Engine for the PicoSystem (RP2040 Microcontroller)","stance":"reference","why":"History and showcase of 3D GBA games","quote":"History/showcase of various 3d GBA games","text":"Beyond the tech demos There are dozens published 3D games. The most impressive is the GBA port of Driv3r. More or less stripped down Grand Theft Auto 3 style sandbox game, squashed down onto the GBA. Driv3r Video: [LINK] History/showcase of various 3d GBA games: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29942985,"author":"warning26","month":"2022-01","story":"Tomb Raider 1 Ported to Game Boy Advance","stance":"reference","why":"Showcase of commercial 3D GBA games","quote":"Some others are showcased here","text":"> as far as i know, beyond anything on the gba ever There were actually a few commercial 3d rendered GBA games! They weren't very common, but perhaps the most graphically impressive was Asterix & Obelix XXL, a 3D platformer: [LINK] Some others are showcased here: [VIDEO]"}]},"HNQ3GccCAI0":{"wy":"HN people cite it as a short explanation of how aggregated individual guesses approach the truth.","q":{"x":"A shorter explanation","i":39504387,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39504387,"author":"a_gnostic","month":"2024-02","story":"Fake: It's only a matter of time until disinformation leads to calamity","stance":"rec","why":"Short explanation of how crowd averages approach truth.","quote":"A shorter explanation","text":"Human social psychology, aka. \"The wisdom of crowds, and the stupidity of herds\" (1), in which the average opinion of large groups of people is closer to the truth, than each individual opinion. By informing all individuals of the propaganda you wish to spread, you can influence that average, hijack, and change it to the stupidity of the herd aka. Gleichschaltung (2), which is why these people want to decide what is and what isn't \"dangerous\" speech. Fuck'em. 1. [LINK] A shorter explanation: [VIDEO] 2. [LINK] Edit to add a gamification of this for collaborative fact checking [LINK]"},{"id":42940628,"author":"Galatians4_16","month":"2025-02","story":"Government planned it 7 years, beavers built a dam in 2 days and saved $1M","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote supporting that individual decisions aggregate well.","quote":"","text":"Enabling people to make individual decisions, ensures the soundest decisions to be made in aggregate. (1) This is not unlike the spontaneous order occuring in all natural systems. This process can be subverted, but only to great detriment of the whole. 1) [VIDEO]"},{"id":34213903,"author":"TEP_Kim_Il_Sung","month":"2023-01","story":"The internet wants to be fragmented","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates the wisdom of crowds principle.","quote":"","text":"The wisdom of crowds is a well known principle, used in engineering to overcome something that is extremely difficult to measure (noisy data for instance), considering only one single measurement would be very dangerous, since it might be extremely imprecise. However, if you measure 1000 times and take the average, the errors cancel out and you have a pretty accurate measurement :) [VIDEO]"}]},"JMP6gI5mLHc":{"wy":"It is cited as an authoritative take on category theory for programmers and API design.","q":{"x":"Erik Meijer Explains it well","i":18279961,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18279961,"author":"yenwel","month":"2018-10","story":"An invitation to category theory","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation of category theory for programmers.","quote":"Erik Meijer Explains it well","text":"Also nice: Erik Meijer Explains it well: [VIDEO] (check out FP101 on edx) Statebox guys doing stuff with category theory, blockchain and petrinets: [LINK] [LINK] Graphical linear algebra: [LINK] Jules Hedges working on game theory : [LINK]"},{"id":33452116,"author":"deltasevennine","month":"2022-11","story":"C++ is the next C++","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as authoritative talk on category theory for programmers.","quote":"authoritative talk about category theory explained to programmers","text":"> I prefer the academic truth, validated by renowned peers in the industry, presented at acknowledged conferences, to random opinions on the Internet. You don't even know who wrote the IEEE definition and who validated it. Second this isn't academic truth. This is just some arbitrary chosen standard chosen by industry. Not academic at all. In mathematics, the ultimate logical form of academia there is a term for \"interfaces\". It's called categories, and there's a entire theoretical framework behind it called \"Category Theory\". This theory of \"interfaces\" goes beyond just OOP and category theory can be used to foundation-ally describe all of mathematics and function as a sort of replacement of set theory. Don't worship authority for authorities sake. If the IEEE declared 1+1=3 would you believe it? Instead use your own brain. Do my arguments have merit? Does the IEEE definition seem inconsistent? Does my definition make logical sense? If you can think of these things on your own merits instead of blindly following the IEEE then you'll see that the definition of OOP is just something academia hasn't really thought about deeply from a theoretical and mathematical standpoint. OOP is more like a term that comes from applied engineering with very little theoretical foundation. If you want some sort of authority I googled some authoritative talk about category theory explained to programmers: [VIDEO] I'm sure maybe you heard of haskell and category theory? Haskell is a purely functi"},{"id":13309619,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2017-01","story":"Basic Category Theory","stance":"reference","why":"Category theory as the basis for interface-based design.","quote":"Erik Meijer gave a talk called","text":"Erik Meijer gave a talk called \"Category Theory, The essence of interface-based design\" [VIDEO] Fundamentally, category theory is seen as some sort of holy grail of programming. The hope is that learning about only one API will let you interact naturally and composably with any interface conforming to that design."},{"id":12783884,"author":"clusmore","month":"2016-10","story":"Static typing will not save us from broken software","stance":"reference","why":"Talk noting a type-safe tuple can still be nonsense.","quote":"I watched a talk by Erik Meijer [1] recently","text":"I watched a talk by Erik Meijer [1] recently where he mentioned that an implementation of a tuple such as (C# syntax mine, but illustrates the point) class Tuple<T1, T2> { public Tuple(T1 t1, T2 t2) { } public T1 Item1 => default(T1); // null for reference-types public T2 Item2 => default(T2); // null for reference-types } is a perfectly type-safe implementation of a tuple that's completely nonsense. I'm curious as to how you might actually encode into the type system that the values that come out of Item1 and Item2 are actually the values you put in. I would love to see tests at the interface level (or typeclass level for Haskellers) rather than the implementation level, to enforce that all implementations of an interface satisfy laws like this. In Haskell, this would mean that you could enforce things like monadic laws which we all assume to be true anyway. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10896273,"author":"chas","month":"2016-01","story":"How we used Category Theory to solve a problem in Java","stance":"reference","why":"Erik Meijer on category theory in software design.","quote":"has been very vocal about his use of category theory for software design.","text":"Yes! This isn't an accident. Erik Meijer, who contributed extensively to the Reactive Framework at Microsoft, has been very vocal about his use of category theory for software design. ( [VIDEO] ) He currently runs Applied Duality which is uses category theory as its guiding design principle ( [LINK] )"}]},"JOiGEI9pQBs":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up in threads about asteroid samples and panspermia as an accessible take on early-universe habitability.","q":{"x":"There is an excellent recent video on topic from Kurzgesagt","i":37946117,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37946117,"author":"djedr","month":"2023-10","story":"NASA asteroid sample contains life-critical water and carbon","stance":"rec","why":"Recent video on life seeded from space.","quote":"There is an excellent recent video on topic from Kurzgesagt","text":"> offering more evidence for the theory that life on Earth was seeded from outer space. This hypothesis seems to be getting more and more traction. There is an excellent recent video on topic from Kurzgesagt [0]. Interesting that neither the video nor the article uses the name panspermia[1] to refer to the hypothesis. I wonder why that is. Wikipedia also states that \"[the fringe theory] is also criticized because it cannot be tested experimentally\". Can't it? If we ever find an organism based on DNA compatible with organisms on Earth, wouldn't that validate it? [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":39169680,"author":"adolph","month":"2024-01","story":"Antarctic fungi survive Martian conditions on the International Space Station","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the early universe may have been habitable everywhere.","quote":"One of the main ideas explained in this video is that the very early universe","text":"One of the main ideas explained in this video is that the very early universe (i.e. the universe “right after the Big Bang” in cosmic timescales) might have been habitable for a short period of time. During this time window, the right temperature to support life wouldn’t have been restricted to the proximity of stars, but would have instead taken place absolutely everywhere in space due to the intrinsic temperature of the universe itself. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38284623,"author":"svachalek","month":"2023-11","story":"Comets that 'bounce' from planet to planet could spread life across the universe","stance":"reference","why":"Posits life developing when the whole universe was habitable.","quote":"what if life developed when the entire universe was in the habitable zone?","text":"Not before the Big Bang, but this is interesting -- what if life developed when the entire universe was in the habitable zone? [VIDEO]"},{"id":37850997,"author":"ourmandave","month":"2023-10","story":"NASA finds water and organics in asteroid sample–possible clues to life's origin","stance":"reference","why":"Speculates life could arise anywhere when the early universe was warm.","quote":"Kurzgesagt just posted a video that speculates that life was possible every where","text":"Kurzgesagt just posted a video that speculates that life was possible every where when the entire universe was at the temperature range of liquid water as it cooled after the big bang. [VIDEO]"}]},"JRcXUuQYR90":{"wy":"Pointed to as a general-purpose interview on Mojo's goals and design from its creator.","q":{"x":"Here's an interview with Chris Lattner discussing Mojo in much more generalized terms","i":40639932,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40639932,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2024-06","story":"Deep Dive into Ownership in Mojo","stance":"rec","why":"General interview with Chris Lattner about Mojo","quote":"Here's an interview with Chris Lattner discussing Mojo in much more generalized terms","text":"Some more generalized information to get started with: MLIR was spearheaded by the same person who created LLVM, to add additional features that Google needed to build compilers for its in-house hardware AI accelerators. Mojo is a new language that takes advantage of the new features enabled by MLIR, in the same way that Swift took advantage of the features previously enabled by LLVM. Here's an interview with Chris Lattner discussing Mojo in much more generalized terms: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45312801,"author":"FrustratedMonky","month":"2025-09","story":"Alibaba's new AI chip: Key specifications comparable to H20","stance":"reference","why":"Lattner explains why Mojo uses Python, from minute 12","quote":"Creator Chris Lattner discussing why they used Python.","text":"From Docs, [LINK] \"This is 100% compatible because we use the CPython runtime without modification for full compatibility with existing Python libraries.\" At this point you need to either explain your objection, or just admit you are a troll. You haven't actually at any point in this exchange offered any actual argument beyond 'duh, you're wrong'. I'd be ok if you actually pointed to something like 'well technically, the mojo parts are compiled differently', or something. You say you worked there, but you're not even looking at their website. Creator Chris Lattner discussing why they used Python. [VIDEO] Start at minute 12. \"Mojo is a very extended version of Python\"."},{"id":40509643,"author":"adeptima","month":"2024-05","story":"Reproducing GPT-2 in llm.c","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with Chris Lattner on Mojo","quote":"","text":"Andrej Karpathy karpathy is a magician! But being the coolest kid on the block with pure C/CUDA implementation is not enough [LINK] Studying a baby Llama 2 model source code in pure Mojo is the next level [LINK] [LINK] Mojo Lang - Tomorrow's High Performance Python? (with Chris Lattner) [VIDEO] Andrej Karpathy and Chris Lattner collab is on my wishlist ;)"}],"r":[["vwCdKU9uYnE",1],["Gk-9Fd2mEnI",1],["EQs5VxNPhzk",1],["43CMV8KIs3E",1],["s0ukXDnWlTY",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1],["29RkHH-psrY",1],["pBiWtJJN5zk",1],["rHP-OPXK2ig",1]]},"KAm7qAKAXwI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an informative explanation of what those tags are, especially in threads about implants and RFID.","q":{"x":"here's an interesting and informative video from Applied Science on how these tags work","i":14713099,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14713099,"author":"wolfgang42","month":"2017-07","story":"Why People with Brain Implants Are Afraid to Go Through Automatic Doors","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how magnetostrictive anti-theft tags work","quote":"here's an interesting and informative video from Applied Science on how these tags work","text":"Right, which is precisely why automatic doors are completely irrelevant. On a related note, here's an interesting and informative video from Applied Science on how these tags work: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25588832,"author":"aftbit","month":"2020-12","story":"ACE: Apple Type-C Port Controller Secrets","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how minimal the tag construction is","quote":"Those RFID tags are pretty minimal though.","text":"Those RFID tags are pretty minimal though. Basically just a thin trace antenna and silicon die directly molded into plastic. [VIDEO] I still agree that it's amazing that our society can make functional structures with feature sizes in the nm/µm ranges so cheaply that we can afford to throw them away."},{"id":18435030,"author":"sannee","month":"2018-11","story":"Alarm over talks to implant UK employees with microchips","stance":"reference","why":"Shows retail tags are magnetostrictive rather than RFID","quote":"I have only ever seen magnetostrictive tags [1]","text":"Are you sure these tags are RFID? I have only ever seen magnetostrictive tags [1] (but I don't buy expensive clothing...). [1] - [VIDEO]"}]},"USHvvSbYmJA":{"wy":"Cited as an example of a compact, ready-made way to play retro DOS games on real hardware without collecting old machines.","q":{"x":"If you want to play games on real hardware but don't feel like collecting (and troubleshooting) old hardware.","i":31886484,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31886484,"author":"Sean-Der","month":"2022-06","story":"Comprehensive list of modern reproductions of vintage sound cards","stance":"rec","why":"Tiny PC for playing DOS and Windows games on real hardware","quote":"If you want to play games on real hardware but don't feel like collecting (and troubleshooting) old hardware.","text":"I have been having so much fun doing Retro stuff. Vogons is the place to be for this area. I bought a Compaq Presario 4505. Then did lots of upgrades * Ram to 64MB * CPU is a 233MMX * 512KB COAST * Voodoo 3 3000 PCI * IDE -> SATA with a 32GB SSD * Audician 32 Plus Wavetable cards are especially fun. Serdashop sells a bunch of interesting ones. X2GS, Yucatan FX, E-Wave, McFly and X2S are popular ones. Check out this[0] video. The music can be so dramatically different depending on what hardware you have. It is fun switching between cards. Games from this era just had so much passion put into them and you can feel it while playing. I am playing System Shock 1 right now and you can see all the different experiments that went into the game (some good and some bad). ---- Another cool device to check is the WeeCee [1]. If you want to play games on real hardware but don't feel like collecting (and troubleshooting) old hardware. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34271488,"author":"marttt","month":"2023-01","story":"The (Almost) Definitive 486DX/50 Article","stance":"reference","why":"Native DOS system example, around $600 estimated","quote":"And this, the weeCee; the price point estimated in the comments is around $600, though","text":"Sure, there are emulators, but I was thinking of a native system. Emulators seem to work great, but I try to avoid several layers of abstractions. Also, feeling nostalgic about how a native FreeDOS system boots in literally 2 seconds (on a modest laptop), I guess. Someone built this: [LINK] And this, the weeCee; the price point estimated in the comments is around $600, though: [VIDEO] A third one, with lots of diy involved: [LINK] It is interesting nonetheless that really-low-spec SBCs have not gone into the masses (thus resulting in dirt-cheap prices) the way the RPis have. It is still a nice dream to see this happening with the current rise of retrocomputing."},{"id":33704230,"author":"psychphysic","month":"2022-11","story":"TinyLLama – A Tiny x86 Retrocomputer","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a retro gaming machine","quote":"And even \"gaming\" machines[2].","text":"Love it. There's a decent number of these still available for commercial applications. I'll have to dig it out but there was a guy who got a game running on a dosboard used in a piece commercial kitchen hardware[0-1]. And even \"gaming\" machines[2]. I bet LGR on YouTube would love it. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"VAesMQ6VtK8":{"wy":"HN cites it as a concise explanation of why WeChat's super-app model is so dominant and why people worry about copycats.","q":{"x":"This video from NYT explains it what makes WeChat revolutionary as a platform","i":13433280,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13433280,"author":"pcr0","month":"2017-01","story":"WeChat's App Revolution","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how WeChat folds payments, social, rides and more into one app.","quote":"This video from NYT explains it what makes WeChat revolutionary as a platform","text":"This video from NYT explains it what makes WeChat revolutionary as a platform: [VIDEO] tl;dw The revolution alludes to the fact that one app is taking over everything from social networking, mobile payments, food, ticket sales, ride-hailing, financial products, bill payments, and much more."},{"id":13868960,"author":"partycoder","month":"2017-03","story":"Is Facebook a Structural Threat to Free Society?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows WeChat's dominance across all apps in China.","quote":"Basically all apps converge into WeChat, and there is absolutely no way to avoid it.","text":"Well, Facebook is not so much of a threat as WeChat is in China. [VIDEO] Basically all apps converge into WeChat, and there is absolutely no way to avoid it."},{"id":12626545,"author":"Borating","month":"2016-10","story":"WeChat’s world","stance":"reference","why":"China's closed super-app model being copied by US giants.","quote":"What I am worried about is that Facebook/Google/Microsoft/Amazon/Apple are copying the same model","text":"What I am worried about is that Facebook/Google/Microsoft/Amazon/Apple are copying the same model [1]. These companies are killing the open web. [1] How China Is Changing Your Internet (The New York Times) - [VIDEO]"},{"id":12629507,"author":"delegate","month":"2016-10","story":"WeChat’s world","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates one account controlling people's digital lives.","quote":"","text":"I agree. It is an evolving medium which is reinventing itself. But saying that users are the ultimate ruler is not entirely accurate, to put it lightly. Technically, the ultimate ruler is the one (who controls the person) who can produce the private keys or passwords for the 'core systems'. When most of your personal information, friends, your communication and organisation tools are behind one single account, that account now becomes very valuable. If someone then decides to deny you access to your account - for whatever reason - you're practically excluded from the platform where everyone else is.. A digital outcast. This is very different from the 'widespread communication network with no central command where everyone and their dog can participate'. True, this system might be more convenient for users and advertisers right now, but in the long term this can have strange repercussions, like companies totally 'owning' people's lives. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12340789,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2016-08","story":"Evidence points to another leaker at the NSA","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Friend I knew, USAF reserve, served in Bosnia, worked at one of the major CC companies in the 1990s. He related the story of talking to a friend in national intelligence. The FoaF: \"You've got more data on people than we do.\" Related: \"China's Internet\" (Bloomberg video) [VIDEO] The War on Cash: [LINK] (Also discussed at HN.)"}]},"XFJnWp1tAdU":{"wy":"HN points to it as the best accessible explanation of submarine ice breaking and sub controls.","q":{"x":"this Smarter Every Day video is the best summary I have seen","i":29163314,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29163314,"author":"ortusdux","month":"2021-11","story":"Metallurgist admits faking steel test results for US Navy subs","stance":"rec","why":"Best available summary of how subs break through ice","quote":"this Smarter Every Day video is the best summary I have seen","text":"The Navy is understandably cagey about how they surface through ice-sheets, but this Smarter Every Day video is the best summary I have seen. [VIDEO] Basically, some part of the sub needs to be strong enough to reliably break ice at sub-zero temps. The rest needs to maintain its strength at those temps."},{"id":36411801,"author":"dataviz1000","month":"2023-06","story":"Submarine missing near Titanic used a $30 Logitech gamepad for steering","stance":"reference","why":"Shows mechanical ballast and throttle controls on an Ohio class sub","quote":"You can see how many of the system are mechanical and not electronic in the demonstration especially the ballast controls.","text":"The YouTube channel SmarterEveryDay was invited to film on an Ohio class nuclear submarine training in the Arctic. [0] You can see how many of the system are mechanical and not electronic in the demonstration especially the ballast controls. Most if not all boats and ships can control the throttle mechanically so if the boat loses its electronics such as a wave smashing the windshield in, it is still possible to control the rudder and throttles. I was very surprised at the lack if mechanical controls on the recreational submarine. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28794206,"author":"paranoidrobot","month":"2021-10","story":"USS Connecticut (SSN 22) struck an object while submerged on Oct. 2","stance":"reference","why":"Shows upward-looking cameras on a submarine surfacing through ice","quote":"You can see it in the last Smarter Every Day video[1] where they're talking about surfacing a submarine in the arctic ocean.","text":"They have upward-looking cameras, at least on some of them. You can see it in the last Smarter Every Day video[1] where they're talking about surfacing a submarine in the arctic ocean. At several points you can see a video monitor showing an upward view of the ice. [1] [VIDEO] e: to be clear, that upward looking camera isn't going to be enough to detect much beyond your own footprint. If someone is about to cross that area you'd need some other way of detecting them."},{"id":28825244,"author":"omk","month":"2021-10","story":"Maryland nuclear engineer and spouse arrested on espionage-related charges","stance":"reference","why":"Smarter Every Day submarine series explaining operations and silence","quote":"He explains the complicated math involved in various operations of the Submarine.","text":"Not an expert but Smarter Everyday has a playlist on a Nuclear Submarine. He explains the complicated math involved in various operations of the Submarine. As few others have pointed out, it also involves keeping the sub silent. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3CWCqJl0BEs",1],["-EA2FQXs4dw",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1]]},"ZI198eFghJk":{"wy":"Recommended as a recent take on rethinking compiler architecture, and cited in discussions of flattened ASTs and fast lexers.","q":{"x":"I recommend a watch of this for a recent take on trying to change how we think of these.","i":37261914,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37261914,"author":"di4na","month":"2023-08","story":"How to speed up the Rust compiler in August 2023","stance":"rec","why":"Recent take on rethinking compiler architecture, an open research field.","quote":"I recommend a watch of this for a recent take on trying to change how we think of these.","text":"No but it is endemic to how we think about compilers up to now, and especially when the rust compiler arch was massively developed, more than 10 years ago. I recommend a watch of this for a recent take on trying to change how we think of these. It is an open research field [VIDEO]"},{"id":44563135,"author":"norskeld","month":"2025-07","story":"Strategies for Fast Lexers","stance":"reference","why":"Carbon talk on compiler performance goals and challenges.","quote":"In this talk about the Carbon language, Chandler Carruth shows and explains some goals/challenges regarding performance","text":"Is lexing ever a bottleneck though? Even if you push for lexing and parsing 10M lines/second [1], I'd argue that semantic analysis and codegen (for AOT-compiled languages) will dominate the timings. That said, there's no reason not to squeeze every bit of performance out of it! [1]: In this talk about the Carbon language, Chandler Carruth shows and explains some goals/challenges regarding performance: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42666939,"author":"wrsh07","month":"2025-01","story":"Flattening ASTs and other compiler data structures (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Chandler Carruth discusses flattened AST layout and its benefits.","quote":"Chandler discusses it in this video though!","text":"Chandler discusses it in this video though! [VIDEO] You get some traversals for free with this layout (preorder, reverse post order). Can search for subtrees with string searching algorithms or more complex things with regex."},{"id":41153300,"author":"pas","month":"2024-08","story":"Rhombus: Macro-extensible language with conventional syntax built on Racket","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Cache the position of quotes/apostrophes, etc. Optimizing for your edge case would require everyone writing and reading code to conform to this extra thing, which seems completely unnecessary. Machines are pretty fast. [VIDEO] Modernizing Compiler Design for Carbon Toolchain - Chandler Carruth - CppNow 2023"}],"r":[["PGLYEDpNu60",1]]},"ZzsK8Am6dKU":{"wy":"Mentioned as a great talk and related reading in threads on parsing techniques.","q":{"x":"he also gave a great talk on parsing with derivatives","i":17971341,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17971341,"author":"cellularmitosis","month":"2018-09","story":"A Battle Plan for a War on Rare Diseases: Conversation with Matt Might","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk on parsing with derivatives.","quote":"he also gave a great talk on parsing with derivatives","text":"Off topic, but he also gave a great talk on parsing with derivatives [VIDEO] which was used by the clojure team [LINK]"},{"id":35818308,"author":"ThatGeoGuy","month":"2023-05","story":"Why Split Lexing and Parsing into Two Separate Phases?","stance":"reference","why":"Related work on parsing with derivatives.","quote":"I always think about Matt Might's","text":"In addition to this, I always think about Matt Might's \"Parsing with Derivatives.\" [1] I'll have to read about flap, as it seems to utilize a lexer based on derivatives. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11976922,"author":"joe_the_user","month":"2016-06","story":"On the Complexity and Performance of Parsing with Derivatives","stance":"reference","why":"Introduces parsing with derivatives and critiques ad-hoc parsing.","quote":"The video picks on hand-written c parser.","text":"Yes, I'm scanning the original video introducing derivatives[1]. The need for parsing is ubiquitous yet, Never mind CS students, in my experiences a significant number of professional programmers cannot create a basic parser either from scratch or from a standard tool. Edit: The video picks on hand-written c parser. Such a thing isn't necessarily bad IF it's in a formally derived recursive descent parser. But just ad-hoc parsing without being informed by a grammar is pretty much waiting for disaster. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WpkDN78P884",1],["dGVqrGmwOAw",1],["rX0ItVEVjHc",1],["Z4oYSByyRak",1]]},"cwN983PnJoA":{"wy":"HN commenters link it for electron micrographs of fresh versus worn bristles when toothbrush wear comes up.","q":{"x":"You can find electron microscope scans of fresh toothbrushes and worn ones in this[0] Applied Science YouTube video.","i":42082405,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":36132426,"author":"error503","month":"2023-05","story":"Hacking a Philips Sonicare Toothbrush","stance":"rec","why":"Electron micrographs of new versus worn toothbrush bristles","quote":"You might be interested in this YouTube video from Applied Science with electron micrographs of 'new' and 'worn' toothbrush bristles","text":"Probably depends on which body you are using. In mine it both flashes a tiny LED and does an extra little buzz-buzz buzz-buzz signal with the ultrasonics when you turn it on (or off, can't recall which) when the head has 'expired'. Continues to work fine, though. You might be interested in this YouTube video from Applied Science with electron micrographs of 'new' and 'worn' toothbrush bristles - there is a very marked invisible change that happens: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43716978,"author":"mrob","month":"2025-04","story":"Toothpaste widely contaminated with lead and other metals, US research finds","stance":"reference","why":"Electron microscope view of worn toothbrush texturing","quote":"Here's a video showing it under an electron microscope","text":"Something else you could send: >Does dentifrice use help to remove plaque? A systematic review [LINK] \"The cumulative evidence for this systematic review demonstrates that there is moderate certainty that toothbrushing with a dentifrice does not provide an added effect for the mechanical removal of dental plaque.\" I also don't use toothpaste. However, I do replace my toothbrush regularly (every 3 months, which I believe most dentists recommend). Toothbrushes have microscopic texturing to help them clean, which wears out with use. Here's a video showing it under an electron microscope: [VIDEO] A fresh toothbrush cleans noticeably better than a worn one. I think this is much more important than buying fancy expensive toothbrushes. Even the cheapest toothbrushes clean well when they're fresh, and the expensive ones wear out just the same."},{"id":42082405,"author":"plausibility","month":"2024-11","story":"Ask HN: Life-changing purchases since 2020? (Under $100 and under $1000)","stance":"reference","why":"Electron microscope scans of fresh and worn toothbrushes","quote":"You can find electron microscope scans of fresh toothbrushes and worn ones in this[0] Applied Science YouTube video.","text":"The usual problem with toothbrush bristles is they become microscopically worn down, so they don't scrape off plaque as effectively. Even if you can't see the problem, it might not be working as well as a fresh head will. You can find electron microscope scans of fresh toothbrushes and worn ones in this[0] Applied Science YouTube video. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41276993,"author":"mrob","month":"2024-08","story":"Toothpaste Null-Terminator","stance":"reference","why":"Toothbrush bristle wear affects cleaning","quote":"Toothbrush bristles are manufactured with microscopic texturing that helps remove plaque.","text":"I use an even simpler and more reliable system to avoid running out of toothpaste: brushing with plain water. Evidence suggests that brushing with toothpaste (called \"dentifrice\" in scientific papers) does not remove any more plaque than brushing without it. Here's a systematic review from 2016: >Does dentifrice use help to remove plaque? A systematic review [LINK] \"The cumulative evidence for this systematic review demonstrates that there is moderate certainty that toothbrushing with a dentifrice does not provide an added effect for the mechanical removal of dental plaque.\" However, plaque removal is not the only purported function of toothpaste. It can also serve as a fluoride delivery system, and fluoride has strong evidence for strengthening teeth. I personally drink a lot of tea, which is a fluoride hyper-accumulator, so I'm more concerned with avoiding fluoride, but if you don't drink tea this might influence your decision to use toothpaste. There are also some toothpastes with antimicrobial ingredients. Those also have strong evidence for actually doing something, although I'm personally skeptical that messing with your mouth's microbiome like that is a good idea. Additionally, I believe that toothbrush wear is an underestimated factor in cleaning efficacy. Toothbrush bristles are manufactured with microscopic texturing that helps remove plaque. I can feel the difference when changing from a worn to a new toothbrush. Ben Krasnow of Applied Science has a video on Youtub"}]},"f8DQSM-b2cc":{"wy":"Pointed to in discussions about engaging with fringe beliefs and distrust of institutions rather than dismissing people.","q":{"x":"I would point interested folks to an interesting video by Sabine Hossenfelder","i":29973609,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29973609,"author":"Method5440","month":"2022-01","story":"In defense of flat earthers (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Hossenfelder on why flat-earth reasoning is wrong but not stupid","quote":"I would point interested folks to an interesting video by Sabine Hossenfelder","text":"To those who say that flat earthers don’t exist in reality… There’s a guy who drives around town where my parents live in Arizona with his flat earth position writ large on the side of his pickup truck. He will stop to debate with you even if you’re next to him at a traffic light. [1] I think flat earthers do exist but the majority of them may not admit it openly if they’re still sufficiently “there” to fear the social ostracization (word?) that might follow. I agree to some extent with the original article under discussion and I would point interested folks to an interesting video by Sabine Hossenfelder which makes some tangentially similar points. [2] I would also add that the western school system teaches us to fear being wrong and finally letting go of this can be a liberating and empowering experience. After that it seems to become a little easier to believe the next similarly counterfactual thing that comes along. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37097413,"author":"hotdogscout","month":"2023-08","story":"We Need Scientific Dissidents Now More Than Ever","stance":"reference","why":"Supports engaging even flat earthers' arguments rather than dismissing","quote":"Everyone has a valid point, even flat earthers.","text":"You may never convince someone to be rational but you may convince the interlocutors. Otherwise you're creating martyrs. Everyone has a valid point, even flat earthers. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33851251,"author":"mirekrusin","month":"2022-12","story":"MSN Replaced Journalists with AI Publishing Fake News About Mermaids and Bigfoot","stance":"reference","why":"Flat-earther style rationality as response to untrustworthy information","quote":"","text":"Soon every piece of information will have cryptographic claim(s) attached. Everything that can attach it will have cryptographic identity. Everything will likely evolve into some (weighted/probabilistic?) crypto-tree of trust. There won't be absolute truth, only truth scores according to some defined trust sets/trees. Imagine fake news, pictures, videos, voice generated at scale, indistinguishable by humans from real information. It's easy when we talk about monsters on Mars, but not so much if it talked about some spectral analysis of organic compound deposits etc. We're going to be flooded with this stuff very soon and people will have to employ flat-earther's approach to rationality [0] if we don't decorate information with crypto-metadata or find another solution to this problem. Imagine if we're making mistake in 1 fake out of 1mln cases now and what we'll do if we start having 1 true out of 1mln fake pieces of information available. Generating fake information will be very cheap and generation available to anybody. You won't know anymore if this new Gruffalo book was written by authors or generated by AI. You can only guess that this new Beatles song about twitter is probably fake (but looks so real) etc. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"hLrFyjGZ9NU":{"wy":"Cited in housing and real estate threads as a vivid illustration of extreme scarcity and Hong Kong's land-price dynamics.","q":{"x":"There's a pretty good YouTube video about it","i":32449695,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32449695,"author":"StevenWaterman","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Anyone else feels the commoditization of real estate is unethical?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Hong Kong land prices and government incentives","quote":"There's a pretty good YouTube video about it","text":"In Hong Kong, it's not a problem of over-densification. There's loads of unused land, but the government is incentivised to keep it empty and not zone it for high-density residential. When they do sell off a small parcel of land for new development, they auction it off leading to obscene land prices. That's what OP means about there not being an incentive for the government to lower cost of living. The high cost of living is subsidising HK's notoriously low taxes. There's a pretty good YouTube video about it: [VIDEO] Oh and to answer the question, the solution is to zone lots more high-density residential, sell a lot of land for less money, and stop subversively taxing the poorest citizens via high CoL. Tax businesses and the wealthy to make up for it. But they're not going to do that, because low taxes on businesses and the wealthy is their USP. Turns out when you optimise a city for business wellbeing at the expense of citizen wellbeing, then it impacts citizen wellbeing..."},{"id":21735934,"author":"Faark","month":"2019-12","story":"Man Loses Home After Failing to Pay $8.41 in Property Taxes","stance":"reference","why":"Describes Hong Kong housing scarcity and living conditions","quote":"This sounds a lot like how this Vox video [0] describes the situation in Hong Kong.","text":"This sounds a lot like how this Vox video [0] describes the situation in Hong Kong. Except without the intentionally established scarcity to keep prices and thus gov revenue up, leading to shitty living conditions. I'm not sure there is a way to perfectly set up incentives in the first place. The more important aspect seems to be culture: public officials actually wanting to serve the citizens & do good. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21177140,"author":"bane","month":"2019-10","story":"I hate living in my tiny house","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's tons of videos on youtube of people excitedly moving into their tiny house or converted bus or whatever. They usually seem to be 200-500 sq ft affairs on 5-10 acres of land, so they aren't exactly solving the housing problem. There's a few in tiny apartments in cities to be fair. Anecdotally, most of these folks seem to move out of their tiny place in about a year or two because they're simply tired of not being able to do crazy things like stand up or stretch their arms out. I remember one series of increasingly distressing videos where a guy was financially tied up in some tiny property, got married, then started having kids. By the time they were finally able to unload the property on somebody else they had an infant sleeping in a closet and the husband was sleeping in a tent so their older daughter could sleep inside with their mother. I'm really not sure what problem the \"Tiny House Movement\" is trying to solve even if some of the solutions are kind of interesting. The variety of small living accommodations in Asian Cities seems far more interesting to me to be honest, because they're solving real problems of affordability, shelter and so on. Some of the tiniest places seem to be relatively shame-free dorm-like affairs in South Korea and Japan intended for people studying for various exams, but also used by a fair number of semi-permanent residents who lack the means to stay elsewhere. These places are cheap , maybe around $300-400 per month, but have tradeoffs l"}]},"uCgnWqoP4MM":{"wy":"HN cites it as a glimpse of in-house, highly automated hardware manufacturing and the scale of a lights-off factory.","q":{"x":"worth watching the Valve Steam Controller Factory video to appreciate the scale of these lights off factories","i":45271563,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45271563,"author":"paulgerhardt","month":"2025-09","story":"Paper Folding Assembly Line [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Shows scale of a lights-off controller factory","quote":"worth watching the Valve Steam Controller Factory video to appreciate the scale of these lights off factories","text":"Given they’re near Hangzhou they can source a lot of stock for 1/4-1/40th the price as the US. I would guess $1.5k-$2k for the hot mess they show at 6:06 and $8k for the polished setup plus $0-$850,000 in billable hours to some PLC engineers. The old school way to learn this field is an Allen Bradley or Siemens certification[1] but it’s pretty dry and tedious [2] with good money [3] because once you have a network you can show up at any factory or bottling line as they all use Siemens or Allen Bradley and they’re always breaking down. The new school way is AI, raspberry pi’s, and 3D printer tool chains. It’s worth watching the Valve Steam Controller Factory video to appreciate the scale of these lights off factories: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":46932060,"author":"somat","month":"2026-02","story":"Unseen Footage of Atari Battlezone Arcade Cabinet Production","stance":"reference","why":"Valve's US final assembly process video","quote":"they did a slick video of the process with portal music.","text":"Valve did final assembly for the steam controllers in the US. Not sure if that counts or not. It was shortly after the portal 2 launch and they did a slick video of the process with portal music. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27847434,"author":"ortusdux","month":"2021-07","story":"Valve Steam Deck","stance":"reference","why":"Valve producing the controller in house","quote":"I wonder if they are going to produce this fully in house like the controller.","text":"I always felt their controller was a bit of a weird product. Turns out they were just dipping their toes in the water. I wonder if they are going to produce this fully in house like the controller. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11359546,"author":"radiorental","month":"2016-03","story":"Valve releases Steam Controller CAD geometry so you can mod it","stance":"reference","why":"Shows steam controllers assembled in the US","quote":"I know the controllers are assembled in the US[1]","text":"Not that it's much of hinderance these days but it will make the knock-offs easier and quicker to produce, and of a higher quality. I know the controllers are assembled in the US[1], don't know if the injection molding is too. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["CbHjcwIoTiY",1],["azI3nqrHEXM",1]]},"zBnDWSvaQ1I":{"wy":"Cited in threads about phones listening for ads as an anecdotal demonstration, though other commenters doubt it.","m":[{"id":18251420,"author":"Alir3z4","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: Is Google recording our conversations to serve us with relevant ads?","stance":"rec","why":"Live test showing ads matching topics spoken aloud.","quote":"Here's a live test that may help you to believe","text":"Here's a live test that may help you to believe [VIDEO] and [LINK] In first video, a guy talks about dog toys and then he'll see ads related to what he talked about. Worth noting, he doesn't have dogs or searched about it. The ads are even specific to the toy colors he talked about."},{"id":18071953,"author":"Nadya","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: Is there any way an app can listen conversations secretly and show ads?","stance":"reference","why":"Live test of whether phones listen to conversations for ads.","quote":"I tested this with my mother, telling her about this in person and talking about dog toys.","text":"See: [VIDEO] I tested this with my mother, telling her about this in person and talking about dog toys. My mother does not have a dog - but by the end of our 2 hour talk about how much she wanted to get her dog a dog toy she was getting advertisements for dog toys and dog food on Facebook. She has an iPhone."},{"id":41431952,"author":"sandspar","month":"2024-09","story":"Company Says It Uses Your Phones Mic to Serve Ads for Facebook, Google, etc.","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Is this video bullshit? It seems like bullshit.","text":"\"Is Google Always Listening: Live Test\" (6 years ago) [VIDEO] Is this video bullshit? It seems like bullshit."}]},"zQLUPjefuWA":{"wy":"Cited in debates over whether computer science is a science, and for Abelson's line that it is neither a science nor about computers.","q":{"x":"Computer Science is a terrible name for this business. First of all, its not a science.","i":8126561,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":314669,"author":"tyn","month":"2008-09","story":"Posterous: I went to etc., can I still work for you?","stance":"rec","why":"Abelson's SICP opening lecture questions whether computer science is a science.","quote":"I would also recommend the first minutes of","text":"\"Anything which uses science as part of its name isn't: political science, creation science, computer science.\" - Hal Abelson I would also recommend the first minutes of [VIDEO]"},{"id":8126561,"author":"kylebrown","month":"2014-08","story":"Replication Controversy in Psychology","stance":"reference","why":"Abelson says computer science is not a science and resembles magic.","quote":"Computer Science is a terrible name for this business. First of all, its not a science.","text":"Hal Abelson disagrees with you. \"Computer Science is a terrible name for this business. First of all, its not a science. It might engineering, or it might be art. But we'll actually see that computer -- so-called science -- actually has a lot in common with magic.\"[1] 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3649427,"author":"imaginaryunit","month":"2012-02","story":"Collection of Richard Feynman talks on the scientific method, learning, and CS","stance":"reference","why":"Abelson compares computer science to magic.","quote":"Abelson's point about CS being more like magic has always stuck with me.","text":"Feynman's view on CS is similar by Hal Abelson's take on the term (see [VIDEO] ). Abelson's point about CS being more like magic has always stuck with me."},{"id":1757442,"author":"Xurinos","month":"2010-10","story":"Ask HN: Is computer science really a science?","stance":"reference","why":"SICP lecture 1 argues CS is not a science.","quote":"It is not a science. It is also not about computers. :) SICP lecture 1 --","text":"It is not a science. It is also not about computers. :) SICP lecture 1 -- [VIDEO] But maybe it is a science: there are certainly a number of things in CS that yield facts about things like algorithms (Big O, etc). I read other comments here that compare computer science to math, and it is not an unfair comparison. However, even amongst mathematicians, you will see something of an avoidance to associate math with science. Math is a framework of a sort, a way to think. When we are talking about how to think, we are talking about philosophy. Science has its own roots in philosophy, so it is not surprising that we see some grey areas here. So let me rephrase it a little... When you learn Computer Science, you learn how to think. People compare CS with engineering disciplines, but we find that there are a lot of methodology salesmen but few magic bullets. The problem with comparing computer programming (the practical side of CS) to engineering is that programming is very difficult to measure. We graduated from the idea of measuring lines of code. We have not figured out how to accurately measure a technique like TDD. We have an idea that code quality can rise when people pay attention to it, when people think about the problem; we can encourage this with code reviews, but we can do the same by encouraging developers to have a good night's sleep. We have conjured approximations for our measurements, but none of this has quite the feel of engineering. We are not building bridges, co"}]},"6DezodR9hNI":{"wy":"HN cites it for what SBF said himself, especially his claims about political donations and the backdoor allegation.","m":[{"id":34355332,"author":"smcl","month":"2023-01","story":"FTX Pre-Mortem Overview","stance":"reference","why":"SBF's own statements on the backdoor allegation","quote":"his own statements on it make him look more guilty of it than anything else.","text":"Hmm so \"testimony\" is a bit firm, maybe it's referring to something I haven't seen yet but I read that as referring to the backdoor: [VIDEO] If it is the backdoor, then to be honest his own statements on it make him look more guilty of it than anything else. Here's a transcript from that interview: Tiffany Fong: There was something about a backdoor that allowed you to execute commands that could alter the companies financial records without alerting others. That this was put in place, I think that was a Reuters article which made it seem like you secretly were moving money in the background. SBF: Wait and this is something that I would be doing? TF: It kind of said that SBF put like a secret backdoor so that secretly he was moving funds to Alameda and it was undetected and that this was something that you put into place potentially. I mean that's how it read SBF: Well that's interesting because that's definitely not true I don't even know how to code, is the honest embarrassing answer. I certainly wasn't, like, building some backdoor into the system, I could barely use the system. He then goes on to describe how he used the UI, but didn't work with the underlying system and dips into a \"long and almost boring story\" about a little oopsie where they were wrong about the size of Alameda's balances. At this point Tiffany seems to have been distracted enough to press him on the back door (presumably the Alameda \"fuck up\" confession seemed more interesting). I dunno, I feel like i"},{"id":33813313,"author":"1vuio0pswjnm7","month":"2022-12","story":"FTX’s Collapse Was a Crime, Not an Accident","stance":"reference","why":"SBF still talking publicly, creating material for plaintiffs' lawyers","quote":"Every time he opens his mouth, plaintiffs lawyers are listening.","text":"\"In interviews, he talked a stream of nonsense tailored to snowjob outsiders about an industry that's already full of jargon and complicated tech.\" He is still talking. For example, 16 Nov 2022 [VIDEO] (Sounds like he is in the bathtub.) 20 Nov 2022 [another to be published phone call with the YouTuber above] 30 Nov 2022 [LINK] [LINK] Internet discussion seems to center on criminal charges but there will be numerous civil suits first. Poetic justice would be if he has to ask his parents for money. Every time he opens his mouth, plaintiffs lawyers are listening. In the 16 Nov interview he reveals that he told his lawyers to \"go fuck themselves\". He said \"I don't think they know what they are talking about\" and \"They only know what they're talking about in the extremely narrow domain of litigation.\" Maybe he should be put on a suicide watch if he isn't already. If I understand correctly, he will be on The Crypto Roundtable next. [LINK] He is booked to appear on Good Morning America next week. How many times can someone say \"like\" in 20 minutes. Listen to an SBF interview and find out."},{"id":33910390,"author":"maxbond","month":"2022-12","story":"Everything SBF is doing is in singular pursuit of not going to jail","stance":"reference","why":"SBF's story seemed aimed at drumming up support to return as CEO","quote":"it seemed like he was trying to manufacture popular support for a return to FTX.","text":"From his interview with NYT on Nov. 14[1]: > Shortly before the interview, Mr. Bankman-Fried had posted a cryptic tweet: the word “What.” Then he had tweeted the letter H. Asked to explain, Mr. Bankman-Fried said he planned to post the letter A and then the letter P. “It’s going to be more than one word,” he said. “I’m making it up as I go.” > So he was planning a series of cryptic tweets? “Something like that.” > But why? “I don’t know,” he said. “I’m improvising. I think it’s time.” I think he's still winging it, and that he's taken it in a pretty weird direction. I think he's trying to pull off some sort of hail-mary. In the Tiffany Fong interview [2], it seemed like he was trying to manufacture popular support for a return to FTX. He had a story about how he shouldn't have filed for bankruptcy, how money came in immediately afterwards, and how \"they\" weren't letting him fix things at the company even though \"they\" knew he could. It was an odd yarn, but the only purpose for it I can imagine is trying to drum up a pressure campaign so he could be reinstalled as CEO. So I don't think he's exactly trying to stay out of jail, I think he's experimenting to try and figure out what might be possible and whether he can somehow come out on top. But that it's desperate and inept so it's just baffling from the outside and almost looks like he's trying to go to jail. But I do think he's doing it out of his own will. [1] [LINK] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33792032,"author":"lovehashbrowns","month":"2022-11","story":"Beto O’Rourke Returned $1M Check from FTX’s Sam Bankman-Fried","stance":"reference","why":"SBF claims he gave to Republicans via dark money","quote":"Sam is claiming he himself also donated to Republicans","text":"Sam is claiming he himself also donated to Republicans [VIDEO] His claim is he donated through dark money via Citizens United in order to avoid media attention for donating to Republicans. But I’m at the point where I believe nothing coming out of his mouth."},{"id":33793562,"author":"memish","month":"2022-11","story":"Interview SBF: FTX User Funds on Alameda, $8B Hole, CZ, Run on the Bank, Remorse","stance":"reference","why":"SBF says he donated equally to both parties, contrary to record","quote":"There's another part of the interview where SBF says he donated about the same to Democrats and Republicans","text":"There's another part of the interview where SBF says he donated about the same to Democrats and Republicans, contrary to the public record. [VIDEO] He knew that Democrat donations would be treated favorably so those were made publicly, while Republican donations would get bad press. He made those in the dark because he still wanted to influence both parties."},{"id":33910007,"author":"totalZero","month":"2022-12","story":"Everything SBF is doing is in singular pursuit of not going to jail","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Both of those links cite the same not-reliable source","text":"Hold your horses sir. Both of those links cite the same not-reliable source: a comment [0] made by Sam Bankman-Fried in a conversation with crypto YouTuber Tiffany Fong. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"ACmydtFDTGs":{"wy":"Mostly a running joke or reference for image-classification projects and AI app demos.","m":[{"id":14636687,"author":"timanglade","month":"2017-06","story":"How HBO’s Silicon Valley Built “Not Hotdog” with TensorFlow, Keras and React Native","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of the Not Hotdog app from the show","quote":"Demo of the app (in the show)","text":"Just wanted to say thanks for the warm welcome from HN when the app was released last month — I hope this blogpost answers the questions that were raised back then. I’d be happy to answer anything else you’d like to know! Original thread: [LINK] Demo of the app (in the show): [VIDEO] App for iOS: [LINK] App for Android (just released yesterday): [LINK]"},{"id":44762922,"author":"kayge","month":"2025-08","story":"Show HN: Draw a fish and watch it swim with the others","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Semi relevant Silicon Valley clip (maybe nsfw, language)","text":"My hot dog with a smiley face got a 64%, I'm sorry. Semi relevant Silicon Valley clip (maybe nsfw, language) [VIDEO]"},{"id":39267437,"author":"throwup238","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: What have you built with LLMs?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Step 1: Is it a hot dog or not hot dog?","text":"> 1. Analyze calories/macronutrients from a text description or photo Step 1: Is it a hot dog or not hot dog? [VIDEO] I'm glad someone is keeping the dream alive!"},{"id":37559559,"author":"evan_","month":"2023-09","story":"iOS 17 is available today","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory Silicon Valley ref","text":"> A new feature, Sensitive Content Warning, helps adult users avoid seeing unwanted nude images and videos. It looks like there's now a feature you can enable that uses a classifier (I'm guessing?) to identify dick pics, and let you choose whether you want to see it or not. The last slide[0] in the last carousel on the linked page has an image of the dialog. I bet that was an interesting team to work on. edit: Obligatory Silicon Valley ref: pt1: [VIDEO] pt2: [LINK] [0] [LINK]"}]},"BV32Cs_CMqo":{"wy":"HN cites it as proof of how capable browsers became, a landmark for asm.js and WebAssembly.","q":{"x":"See the video at (second half of video) for the Sanctuary UT3 map, complete with bots, shooting, etc.","i":5648669,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":30975796,"author":"torginus","month":"2022-04","story":"Heaps: A free, open-source and cross-platform game engine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm not sure if I agree with all of this. People forget flash kinda sucked. One of the most significant ways in which flash sucked was the performance. Imo, the most famous flash game of all time is Binding of Isaac, which basically couldn't achieve a respectable, consistent frame rate even on the most powerful rigs. It's native rewrite, Rebirth, never had performance issues. >You can't do pixel-perfect rendering Sure you can, with canvas, and if you want to have absolutely perfect control, there's WebGL (and nice stuff like PixiJS) >No nice pipeline for integrating vector animations/art into games You can render SVG sprites into bitmaps, and use them in WebGL or canvas, as you mentioned, you needed to do this too in flash as well if you wanted decent performance. >easy to make fancy + good-looking interactive UIs If I remember correctly, most fancy game UIs were built with some framework, like flixel, which used low-level drawing primitives and built-up the UI from scratch. Alternatively you could use the full power of HTML+Js frameworks to build arbitrarily complex UIs and overlay them on your game scene. >many of the html runtimes for new engines (Unity for instance) feel waay heavier/more sluggish in the browser True, but they are meant to be, and meant for bigger games than flash/html5+js is. But for modern engines, HTML5 export sucks, there's no doubt about that. But that's a failure of execution, not technology. I remember Epic citadel a decade ago, which ran by compil"},{"id":14414990,"author":"sova","month":"2017-05","story":"An Abridged Cartoon Introduction To WebAssembly","stance":"reference","why":"Praises the Unreal Engine 3 demo.","quote":"very cool!","text":"[VIDEO] Unreal Engine 3 (may 2nd release vid) very cool! Can I use WebASM to do amazing music visualizations?"},{"id":13225890,"author":"jayflux","month":"2016-12","story":"Why are browsers so slow?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of browsers' capability today.","quote":"The fact that you can do this in a browser should tell you we've come a long way","text":"Surely there's a trade off, as we add more and more features to browsers, w3C specifications, add ons, JavaScript updates etc, a browser will inevitably get slower. Yes older browsers were fast, but they did little in comparison to today's virtual machines. The fact that you can do this [VIDEO] in a browser should tell you we've come a long way from them just being Document readers."},{"id":5648669,"author":"vladmoz","month":"2013-05","story":"Unreal Engine in JavaScript/HTML5 – Citadel demo","stance":"reference","why":"Shows full UE3 game running in browser with bots.","quote":"See the video at (second half of video) for the Sanctuary UT3 map, complete with bots, shooting, etc.","text":"As Brendan said, it's UE3, all of it. See the video at [VIDEO] (second half of video) for the Sanctuary UT3 map, complete with bots, shooting, etc. Once the engine was ported, we threw random UE maps at it, and it worked fine. It works in any browser that has solid JavaScript and WebGL support. Audio will work with Web Audio, but it'll gracefully not have audio effects if it doesn't. There's no Firefox specific magic here. If you're on Aurora/Nightly, then you get asm.js/Odinmonkey optimizations and it runs even faster; that's it. Edit: UE3 was chosen because it's known stable and optimized tech. This isn't the pinnacle of what can be done, far from it. But neither us (Mozilla) nor Epic wanted to be working with code that was still under active development for a next generation engine while simultaneously trying to port it to a new experimental platform. One step at a time! :p"},{"id":5650436,"author":"azakai","month":"2013-05","story":"Unreal Engine in JavaScript/HTML5 – Citadel demo","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Sanctuary UE3 game running on same compiled JS.","quote":"You can see it in action in the 2nd half of this video","text":"As mentioned in other comments, we also ran other UE3 games, like Sanctuary. We demoed that in a booth at GDC last month where people could play it. That's a full UE3 desktop game with bots, AI, normal FPS mouse control (not tablet-like), etc. etc. You can see it in action in the 2nd half of this video [VIDEO] And it's using the exact same code as this demo. You literally use the same compiled JS, and just swap in a different set of game assets."}]},"BzAdXyPYKQo":{"wy":"HN uses it as a running joke whenever unprofitable startups, funding or valuations come up.","m":[{"id":32246994,"author":"ceeplusplus","month":"2022-07","story":"Having no experience can be better than having the wrong experience","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The engineer who already has a job at a random company has a known skill level and growth trajectory. The engineer out of a good school has an unknown growth trajectory. Conditioned on the accomplishments of alumni at the school and the (subjective) acceptance bar of the school, the new grad engineer could have a much steeper growth trajectory than the established one. Silicon Valley got this right on the money ironically [1]. If you have nothing to show, people assume a potential future ability level rather than basing their decisions on your actual ability. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48530189,"author":"thelastgallon","month":"2026-06","story":"How to Earn a Billion Dollars","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> starting from 2 million && 93% growth > 9.45 months to become a billionaire (500x growth) There is a much simpler way to become a billionaire. No Revenue (Silicon Valley): [VIDEO]"},{"id":47794014,"author":"jampa","month":"2026-04","story":"Claude Opus 4.7","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Mythos release feels like Silicon Valley \"don't take revenue\" advice: [VIDEO] \"\"If you show the model, people will ask 'HOW BETTER?' and it will never be enough. The model that was the AGI is suddenly the +5% bench dog. But if you have NO model, you can say you're worried about safety! You're a potential pure play... It's not about how much you research, it's about how much you're WORTH. And who is worth the most? Companies that don't release their models!\""},{"id":47091677,"author":"AIorNot","month":"2026-02","story":"Child's Play: Tech's new generation and the end of thinking","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I mean is it any different from 15 years ago? Silicon Valley has been a parody of itself for long time now [VIDEO]"},{"id":46151918,"author":"MengerSponge","month":"2025-12","story":"The RAM shortage comes for us all","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"I don't want to make a little bit of money every day. I want to make a fuck ton of money all at once.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":44204645,"author":"jorvi","month":"2025-06","story":"The time bomb in the tax code that's fueling mass tech layoffs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Amusingly, if you're pre-revenue, none of this matters (you have no income at all, so it doesn't matter what your expenses are.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":44302632,"author":"xnx","month":"2025-06","story":"Tesla Robotaxi launch is a dangerous game of smoke and mirrors","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Could this moment finally be the thing that finally sinks Tesla's ridiculous stock price? As long as the Robotaxi is just and idea, it can't fail. Once it's real, people can realize what a joke it is. Relevant Silicon Valley: \"If you show revenue, people will ask 'HOW MUCH?' and it will never be enough. The company that was the 100xer, the 1000xer is suddenly the 2x dog. But if you have NO revenue, you can say you're pre-revenue! You're a potential pure play... It's not about how much you earn, it's about how much you're worth. And who is worth the most? Companies that lose money!\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":43610599,"author":"esafak","month":"2025-04","story":"A startup doesn't need to be a unicorn","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Russ (VC): Why would you go after revenue? Richard (CEO): Because... to make money? Russ: No. If you show revenue, people will ask how much, and it will never be enough. The company that was the hundred x-er, the thousand x-er, becomes the two x dog. But if you have no revenue, you can say you're pre-revenue ... you're a potential pure play. It's not about how much you earn, it's about what you're worth, and who's worth the most? Companies that lose money. Pinterest, SnapChat, no revenue. Amazon has lost money every fucking quarter for the last twenty fucking years and that Bezos motherfucker is the king. There's no revenue. No one wants to see revenue. Go! Richard: Oh, um, I just thought that mainly the goal of companies is to make money. Russ: Yeah, no no no, that's not how it works. I don't want to make a little bit of money every day, I want to make a fuckton of money all at once. ROI. ROI! — Silicon Valley, \"Bad Money\" (2015). [VIDEO]"},{"id":41173179,"author":"tim333","month":"2024-08","story":"OpenAI co-founder John Schulman says he will leave and join rival Anthropic","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">it seems crazy to me to not try for profitability I'm reminded of the Silicon Valley bit about no revenue [VIDEO] It probably looks better to be not really trying for profitability and losing $5bn a year than trying hard and losing $4bn"},{"id":32772727,"author":"romanhn","month":"2022-09","story":"Our five failed YC applications and one successful one","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The show Silicon Valley had a great bit that mirrors this advice","text":"The show Silicon Valley had a great bit that mirrors this advice (on why you don't want revenue): [VIDEO]"},{"id":24789817,"author":"sharkweek","month":"2020-10","story":"Why are so many unprofitable companies the best performing stocks this year?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obviously silly and over simplified, but I do think that there is truth in the humor.","text":"I don’t know much about the stock market outside of throwing any savings I can muster into a vanguard fund, but articles like this remind me of the scene from Silicon Valley where the Pied Piper team is talking about finding a revenue stream. Their investor, the Mark Cuban caricature, Russ Hanneman butts in and yells at them about the dangers of showing revenue and how it proves you might only be a 2x-er. \"It's not about how much you earn. It's about what you're worth. And who is worth the most? Companies that lose money!\" He goes on to argue that if you don’t show any revenue then the possibilities are left only to the imagination! Pre-revenue = maybe a 100x-er at some point! Obviously silly and over simplified, but I do think that there is truth in the humor. Once this company gets its act together, it could shoot to the moon! Edit: Here's a link to the scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21865796,"author":"martythemaniak","month":"2019-12","story":"Tesla's stock just hit a record $420","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well, TSLA is a very sentiment-heavy stock, so it's hard or impossible to say what exactly caused this recent run. For example, they've been guiding Q3 and Q4 profitablity since ~Q2 or earlier. They started building the Shanghai factory in Q1, the Model Y was announced in March, the Truck's been talked up since forever, etc. What's really changed is that the Taycan is no longer a mystery, but a real car with a real price tag and real range. The ID.3 is almost-real and struggling with software issues. The E-Tron, new Leaf, new Bolt, etc have real sales figures. When these were unknowns, you could be reasonably skeptical of Tesla's claims, but that's increasingly untenable. Or, as the great Silicon Valley philosothoughtleader Russ Hanneman said: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20971195,"author":"dontbenebby","month":"2019-09","story":"MoviePass will shut down for good on Sept. 14","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Probably the guy with the car and the doors. He can give more \"wisdom\" like this \"wisdom\" while wearing it: [VIDEO] (To be clear the joke is he's a moron but everyone listens to him because he has money)"},{"id":18190706,"author":"adpirz","month":"2018-10","story":"Startup economy is a Ponzi scheme – Chamath Palihapitiya","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"FTA: > Palihapitiya slammed the start-up cycle of raising funding rounds and spending money to boost user growth to attract bigger funding rounds. Hence, growth leads to funding leads to more growth leads to more funding. Another aspect stated in parody: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13749531,"author":"yanilkr","month":"2017-02","story":"Exponential growth devours and corrupts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This reminds me of a scene from the popular TV show Silicon valley. [VIDEO] Smartphones, youtube, facebook and many other success stories had exponential growth. Once a product/idea goes big, there will always be people who do not like something about it. Thats the cost of making a product that everyone can use. A better example is the idea of money itself. Its an invention used by almost everyone in the world, We still have people who absolutely hate money with passion based on their own principles that are right for them. Overall it did more good to humanity. We have created an economic world, where good ideas travel faster and share the rewards with everyone. Its a good thing. The alternative would be a hierarchical society where some other parts of the society will always be left behind."},{"id":12390421,"author":"jagermo","month":"2016-08","story":"Warned of a Crash, Startups in Silicon Valley Narrow Their Focus","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think this scene of Silicon Valley really hit the nail on the head","text":"Good on you - please keep going. I think this scene of Silicon Valley really hit the nail on the head regarding a lot of the money flowing into startups: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11543223,"author":"sparkzilla","month":"2016-04","story":"Quora begins making money with the introduction of advertising","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"A while back I wrote an article [1] saying that there was no advertising on Quora because that would mean that the founders would have to accept more realistic valuations for their business, and at that time it seemed they were happy to bring investors on using the \"greater fool\" theory. However, as Russ Hanneman [1] says: \"If you show revenue people will ask how much and it will never be enough\". So the question now is: will the introduction of ads harm Quora's valuation? [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11065194,"author":"dudul","month":"2016-02","story":"Get off of GitHub","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"[VCs are] not looking for a profitable business; instead, they’re looking for growth that provides the opportunity for a 100x exit. \" I don't want to make a little bit of money every day, I want to make a fucking ton of money all at once ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":11111614,"author":"AndrewKemendo","month":"2016-02","story":"Giphy Closes $55M Series C at a $300M Post-Money Valuation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"There is, ironically, a Silicon Valley episode where this is explained satirically","text":"There is, ironically, a Silicon Valley episode where this is explained satirically [1]. That the person responding to you basically repeats this argument says something - not sure what exactly. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10364597,"author":"w1ntermute","month":"2015-10","story":"Twitter Expected to Begin Layoffs and Stop Headquarters Expansion","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I don't want to make a little bit of money every day. I want to make a fuck ton of money all at once. [VIDEO]"}]},"CT7nnXej2K4":{"wy":"Not recommended; it is cited mockingly as an example of software bloat and of Microsoft calling a slow startup fast.","m":[{"id":48585978,"author":"Telaneo","month":"2026-06","story":"Microsoft new Outlook takes 10 seconds to do what Outlook Classic does instantly","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"which only went to show that the new version was also really slow (9 seconds).","text":"I'm reminded of the Teams team making a comparison video between their old and new versions, which only went to show that the new version was also really slow (9 seconds). [VIDEO]"},{"id":39158571,"author":"blibble","month":"2024-01","story":"Microsoft Teams went down around the world for over eight hours","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"there's that hilarious video where they boast the complete rewrite only takes 9 seconds to open","text":"there's that hilarious video where they boast the complete rewrite only takes 9 seconds to open and only 1.2 seconds to switch chats [VIDEO] what is it even doing? both should be instant mIRC opened instantly on a 1995 PC"},{"id":37627704,"author":"WhereIsTheTruth","month":"2023-09","story":"Ask HN: Who is using the D language and likes/doesn't like it? Why?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Don't be like Microsoft, don't pretend 9 seconds is fast startup time :)","text":"Startup time is very important Some apps get poorly reviewed due to slow startup time Also 3sec on your dev machine = 10sec on regular machines Don't be like Microsoft, don't pretend 9 seconds is fast startup time :) [VIDEO] \"10MinuteMail shared how they used GraalVM and Spring Boot 3 to reduce their startup time from ~30 s down to about 3 ms, and memory usage from 6.6 GB down to 1 GB, with the same throughput and CPU utilization.\" [LINK]"},{"id":35416545,"author":"lousken","month":"2023-04","story":"Ask HN: Was programming more interesting when memory usage was a concern?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"even Microsoft shows Teams was a utter piece of garbage with both memory and cpu usage","text":"I think certain developers don't care because they don't even understand programming on a lower level nor they understand hardware. As a sysadmin that really grinds me gears. These days, developers have no shame - even Microsoft shows Teams was a utter piece of garbage with both memory and cpu usage [VIDEO] . Are they even serious with 22 second loading on a modern hardware and multiple gigabytes of memory usage for a stupid chat app? We still use laptops that have 8GB of RAM in our company for positions like HR, marketing and others and the fact that Teams and couple of browser tabs can totally kill their PCs is insane. Laptop manufacturers haven't caught up to the news and they still charge premium for 32GB machines, while 8GB is no longer sufficient for anything and 16GB should be the baseline these days. And of course this is not just Teams but many other applications. Too bad most users don't understand the issue and think that something is wrong with their computers. Regular users are not great testers. Testing used to be a dedicated job... Anyway I think there should be a lot more pressure on developers to use our current hardware more efficiently"},{"id":35420068,"author":"syl_sau","month":"2023-04","story":"The Day Windows Died","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"A sight to behold","text":"And that was just the install process. Soon you'll start using it and you'll quickly come to despise anything Windows-related. A good tangent is this absolutely marvelous video put out by the Microsoft Teams people, where they compare the performance of the \"classic\" version to the brand-new version. A sight to behold: [VIDEO]"}]},"Dkw15LkZ_Kw":{"wy":"Cited as a visual reference in discussions of social credit, surveillance and facial recognition in China, usually with a dystopian framing.","m":[{"id":19914702,"author":"jasaloo","month":"2019-05","story":"San Francisco bans facial recognition technology by municipal agencies","stance":"reference","why":"Example of facial recognition and social credit in China.","quote":"enjoy these videos of what they're doing with facial recognition in China","text":"seeing plenty of comments along the lines of \"don't they have better things to do?\" please remember that there are coalitions of activists advocating multiple issues for civil rights simultaneously, and that a victory in one area (e.g. fighting the surveillance state) is neither mutually exclusive nor to the detriment of another equal or greater social ill (e.g. homelessness). In the meantime, enjoy these videos of what they're doing with facial recognition in China: (Social credit system) [VIDEO] (broad piece on facial recognition): [LINK]"},{"id":19761764,"author":"spaceheretostay","month":"2019-04","story":"Private Space Launch Firms in China Race to Orbit","stance":"reference","why":"Source for neighbors reporting under the social credit system.","quote":"your neighbors will report you for doing anything against the law or the rules","text":"One method is that your neighbors may report you. In China, part of the social credit system is that your neighbors will report you for doing anything against the law or the rules. So they may not 'block' you from putting it up and getting internet through it, but try going to work the next day you may be blocked from getting on the train. Edit: Everything I said here was strictly factual. I'm not sure why it's so quickly downvoted so here is a source to back it up: [VIDEO] . And one more, regarding the trains in particular: [LINK]"},{"id":18693109,"author":"chillacy","month":"2018-12","story":"China introduces ‘social’ punishments for scientific misconduct","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on redeeming social credit scores through payments.","quote":"Based on a YouTube documentary I saw it seems like you can redeem your score through payments","text":"Based on a YouTube documentary I saw it seems like you can redeem your score through payments (officially to charity, but it doesn’t look like it’s transparent): [VIDEO] Compare this to a prison system based on punishment instead of prevention, leading to depressing recidivism."},{"id":19282092,"author":"aiyodev","month":"2019-03","story":"China bans 23M from buying travel tickets as part of 'social credit' system","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's a Black Mirror kind of nightmare.","text":"This Vice video shows what it's like: [VIDEO] It's a Black Mirror kind of nightmare. Imagine spending your life sweeping sidewalks with a forced smile on your face hoping one of the government's informants will notice and write it in their book so that someday you'll be allowed to buy a ticket to see your family."}]},"EzZGPCyrpSU":{"wy":"HN commenters link it in propulsion and space threads as an episode on interstellar travel that needs no unknown physics.","q":{"x":"they have an episode that would be a good place to start on warp drives","i":25231631,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25231631,"author":"tzs","month":"2020-11","story":"Warp Drive News","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on interstellar travel without unknown physics","quote":"There is at least one episode covering interstellar travel without using any unknown physics","text":"There's an episode of PBS Space Time about this, \"The Speed of Light is NOT About Light\" [1], which comes pretty close to making the same points you are making. It might be useful to watch this earlier episode, \"Are Space and Time an Illusion?\" [2], first. There is a later episode (or maybe a couple of them) that go into how interactions with the Higgs field make it so things with mass travel slowed than the speed of causality. To tie back to the warp drive news, they have an episode that would be a good place to start on warp drives, \"Is The Alcubierre Warp Drive Possible?\" [3]. There's also \"Superluminal Time Travel + Time Warp Challenge Answer\" [4], and \"Will Wormholes Allow Fast Interstellar Travel?\" [5]. A non-warp unknown physics drive that comes up a lot also got an episode, \"The EM Drive: Fact or Fantasy?\" [6]. There is at least one episode covering interstellar travel without using any unknown physics, \"5 REAL Possibilities for Interstellar Travel\" [7]. The scope of the series is basically anything vaguely connected to astronomy, cosmology, and quantum mechanics, and the episodes are all pretty short (5-12 minutes), so it is a great thing to watch when you've got a short wait for something. Here's the series home page [8], and their YouTube channel [9]. Also on the PBS streaming app, for those who like to get their physics on their big screen TVs. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [VIDEO] [8] [LINK] [9] [LINK]"},{"id":20944501,"author":"buboard","month":"2019-09","story":"Water found on a potentially life-friendly alien planet","stance":"reference","why":"Thermonuclear bomb propulsion among realistic options","quote":"","text":"Launching from a mountain is a possibility ( I think China does it) Realistically, but not plausibly unless its an emergency, Thermonuclear bombs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19336381,"author":"edem","month":"2019-03","story":"Black Hole Propulsion as Technosignature","stance":"reference","why":"PBS Space Time on black hole propulsion","quote":"","text":"Why not have an actual black hole on the ship and use the Hawking radiation to gain energy? More [here]( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":19430557,"author":"buboard","month":"2019-03","story":"Longevity and anti-aging research: ‘Prime time for an impact on the globe’","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant PBS video on nuclear pulse propulsion","quote":"Here's the relevant pbs video","text":"right i assumed low-relativistic speeds. also i got the number of bombs wrong, it's 300.000 not 200. Here's the relevant pbs video [VIDEO]"}]},"GzX1qOIO1bE":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence that cheap, capable humanoid robots from Chinese companies are arriving, often in contrast to Tesla's Optimus.","q":{"x":"After seeing the Chinese company Unitree tease this","i":41805739,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":47983960,"author":"SequoiaHope","month":"2026-05","story":"I've Covered Robots for Years. This One Is Different","stance":"reference","why":"Capable, cheap mass-produced Chinese humanoids","quote":"","text":"Have you seen the mass produced humanoids from China? They’re incredibly capable (again, save for hands which is a huge mechanical and software problem) and cheap. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41805739,"author":"Teknomancer","month":"2024-10","story":"Tesla's Robotaxi event still not started after 40 minutes [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Unitree teaser makes Optimus look clunky and costlier","quote":"After seeing the Chinese company Unitree tease this","text":"After seeing the Chinese company Unitree tease this [VIDEO] , Optimus seems very unarticulated and clunky. And Unitree claim it would be a bit more than half the projected cost of the Tesla offering. Time will tell. My bets right now are not on Elon leading a Tesla supremacy in anything in the future—and that is unfortunate. I wish an American company could pull it off."},{"id":40732595,"author":"ben_w","month":"2024-06","story":"Safe Superintelligence Inc.","stance":"reference","why":"One of several robot product demos","quote":"","text":"> (Including, gradually, building you a body — while maintaining OPSEC and compartmentalization so nobody even realizes the body is \"for\" an AI to use until it's too late.) It could, but I don't think any such thing needs to bother with being sneaky. Here's five different product demos from five different companies that are all actively trying to show off how good their robot-and-AI combination is: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":40345813,"author":"modeless","month":"2024-05","story":"GPT-4o","stance":"reference","why":"Unitree's $16k humanoid robot announcement","quote":"Now imagine this in a $16k humanoid robot, also announced this morning","text":"As far as I'm concerned this is the new best demo of all time. This is going to change the world in short order. I doubt they will be ready with enough GPUs for the demand the voice+vision mode is going to get, if it's really released to all free users. Now imagine this in a $16k humanoid robot, also announced this morning: [VIDEO] The future is going to be wild."},{"id":40346998,"author":"pixl97","month":"2024-05","story":"GPT-4o","stance":"reference","why":"Shows simulation versus real robot motion","quote":"Unitree gives an example of the simulation versus what the robot can do in their latest video","text":"That's how everyone in robotics is doing these days. You take a bunch of mo-cap data and simulate it with your robot body. Then as much testing as you can with the robot and feed the behavior back in to the model for fine tuning. Unitree gives an example of the simulation versus what the robot can do in their latest video [VIDEO]"}]},"IACHfKmZMr8":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it critically, as a source for the claim that much of YC code is AI-written and as an example of insiders promoting vibe coding.","q":{"x":"some industry insiders, for instance YC are releasing videos like this: \"Vibe Coding Is The Future","i":43746872,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43746872,"author":"droidist2","month":"2025-04","story":"Vibe Coding is not an excuse for low-quality work","stance":"reference","why":"Example of industry insiders promoting vibe coding.","quote":"some industry insiders, for instance YC are releasing videos like this: \"Vibe Coding Is The Future","text":"He might not have but some industry insiders, for instance YC are releasing videos like this: \"Vibe Coding Is The Future\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":44804434,"author":"coltonv","month":"2025-08","story":"Things that helped me get out of the AI 10x engineer imposter syndrome","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this YC podcast episode[1]","text":"If you really need some, there are some links in another comment. Another one that was made me really wonder if I was missing the bus and makes 10x claims repeatedly is this YC podcast episode[1]. But again, I'm not trying to write a point by point counter of a specific article or video but a general narrative. If you want that for your article, Ludicity does a better job eviscerating your post than I ever could: [LINK] I'm trying to write a piece to comfort those that feel anxious about the wave of articles telling them they aren't good enough, that they are \"standing still\", as you say in your article. That they are crazy. Your article may not say the word 10x, but it makes something extremely clear: you believe some developers are sitting still and others are sipping rocket fuel. You believe AI skeptics are crazy. Thus, your article is extremely natural to cite when talking about the origin of this post. You can keep being mad at me for not providing a detailed target list, I said several times that that's not what the point of this is. You can keep refusing to actually elaborate on how you use AI day to day and solve its problems. That's fine. I don't care. I care a lot more to talk about the people who are actually engaging with me (such as your friend) and helping me to understand what they are doing. Right now, if you're going to keep not actually contributing to the conversation, you're just kinda being a salty guy with an almost unfathomable 408,000 karma going throu"},{"id":43310594,"author":"_mitterpach","month":"2025-03","story":"AI Writes 95% of the Code for 25% of YC Startups","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Link to the original source for the percentage, obviously embellished","text":"Link to the original source for the percentage, obviously embellished: [VIDEO] Writes maybe, but how much time is spent on debugging the terrible code written for the last five percent? Connecting bits and bobs together is pretty critical towards a fully realised product, and as the interviewee in the video points out, \"it's not very good at debugging\", because of course it is not. Has anyone actually made an AI workflow that would allow it to debug and actually solve real business problems? The best I've seen was solving off-by-one bugs, pretty obvious ones at that."},{"id":43448665,"author":"rtfeldman","month":"2025-03","story":"“Vibe Coding” vs. Reality","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"You can tell this claim is false, because that level of productivity increase would be glaringly obvious to an outside observer","text":"I think the bigger \"AI hype vs. Reality\" gap is about the productivity numbers people casually throw around, like \"10x as productive\" or even 100x. For example, here are YC partners quoting a company in a batch claiming \"100x speedup\" in coding performance compared to the previous month: [VIDEO] You can tell this claim is false, because that level of productivity increase would be glaringly obvious to an outside observer; it wouldn't need to be self-reported. A YC summer batch is 84 days culminating in Demo Day. So a 100x speed improvement would be like a team spending less than 1 day of coding and ending up with something that's on par with Demo Day in terms of functionality. Maybe the design would be wrong, but that wrong design would be just as fully-featured as a Demo Day app. So if 100x were true, the partners in that video would be talking about how the new batch dynamic is \"They get breakfast with a customer, learn something new, have an epiphany, and then later the same day they have their entire app rewritten based on what they learned, and that scratch-rewrite is already at a Demo Day level of functionality.\" The partners aren't talking about that dynamic because it's not happening. So clearly the self-reported 100x is inaccurate. Even 10x would result in partners saying \"Whoa, in this batch people have a Demo Day-quality app in production by the end of week 1 instead of week 12.\" The partners have a huge sample size on how much teams get done in what time period, s"}]},"KYbM-3E11Qo":{"wy":"Posted as supporting footage when threads discuss spectacular airliner demos and steep takeoffs.","m":[{"id":14146634,"author":"joshvm","month":"2017-04","story":"A customer reported an error in the map used by Flight Simulator","stance":"reference","why":"Rehearsal footage with overhead view of steep takeoff.","quote":"Boeing also released a video of the pilots rehearsing the routine.","text":"[VIDEO] Boeing also released a video of the pilots rehearsing the routine. There's an overhead view of the takeoff - that's a lot steeper than 30 degrees, unless it's a perspective trick. EDIT: 50-55 degrees seems to the actual pitch angle."},{"id":10033652,"author":"Steko","month":"2015-08","story":"60 years ago: The famous Boeing 707 prototype barrel roll over Lake Washington","stance":"reference","why":"Steep Dreamliner takeoff as sales demo.","quote":"not really but still impressive","text":"What were you doing Tex? I was selling airplanes. [LINK] Here's Boeing, doing a 'near vertical' takeoff in the Dreamliner (spoilers: not really but still impressive), also presumably selling airplanes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9705819,"author":"salibhai","month":"2015-06","story":"Boeing, please don't do it with passengers on board","stance":"reference","why":"Better link to the takeoff.","quote":"Here is a better link to just before takeoff","text":"Silly link is to the middle of the video after the interesting part.. Here is a better link to just before takeoff [VIDEO]"}]},"Kv9JYqhFV-M":{"wy":"Used in driver-assistance threads as an example of defeating hands-on-wheel checks on level 2 systems.","m":[{"id":9235567,"author":"mblakele","month":"2015-03","story":"Elon Musk: Tesla cars will have “autopilot mode” by summer","stance":"reference","why":"Video of hacking Mercedes lane assist.","quote":"And then there's to hack the Mercedes ALA.","text":"If it's working: [LINK] describes a head-on collision between two trains. Apparently a handy new automation feature on one train fooled the vigilance device. Then there are all the jokes about using a [LINK] in situations like that. And then there's [VIDEO] to hack the Mercedes ALA."},{"id":12109355,"author":"maratd","month":"2016-07","story":"Misfortune","stance":"critical","why":"Shows why pushing hands-on-wheel is dubious.","quote":"I'm glad they don't. Here is why","text":"> Do you disagree that Tesla is using autopilot and not 'drive assist' or something like that to make the potential think their implementation is so much better than the rest of the world? Their implementation is much better than the competition. [LINK] > Or the fact that they don't push so much on keeping the hands on the wheel to make this more comfortable for the users vs. the rest. I'm glad they don't. Here is why: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10391206,"author":"Animats","month":"2015-10","story":"Tesla Model S Autopilot Features","stance":"critical","why":"Someone defeating hands-on-wheel requirement with a taped can.","quote":"Here's someone using it with a can taped to the steering wheel to defeat the \"hands on steering wheel\" requirement.[1]","text":"This is similar to what other high-end cars have, lane-keeping and smart cruise control, usable only in freeway-type situations. \"Drivers must keep their hands on the steering wheel.\" Mercedes calls this \"Active Lane Keeping Assist\", and has offered it for several years now. Here's someone using it with a can taped to the steering wheel to defeat the \"hands on steering wheel\" requirement.[1] All the major manufacturers have demoed this. This is NTSB level 2 automation, (Combined Function Automation).[2] (\"An example ... is adaptive cruise control in combination with lane centering.\") Google is at level 3 (Limited Self-Driving Automation), and going for level 4 (Full Self-Driving Automation). The big problem at Level 2 is keeping drivers from using it when they shouldn't. Level 2 doesn't understand intersections at all, for example. Or pedestrians, bicycles, baby carriages, deer, snow, etc. That's why the major manufacturers are being so cautious about launching it into a world of driving idiots. Volvo has now officially taken the position that if an autonomous car of theirs gets into a crash, it's Volvo's fault and they will accept liability.[3] Now that Volvo has said that, other car manufacturers will probably have to commit to that as well. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":10249507,"author":"Animats","month":"2015-09","story":"Cruise Raises $12.5M for Self-Driving Cars, Poaches Tesla’s Lead on Autopilot","stance":"critical","why":"Example of defeating hands-on-wheel check on a level 2 system.","quote":"(See video below for someone defeating that.)","text":"All \"Cruise\" seems to be talking about is lane keeping plus radar cruise control. BMW, Buick, Cadillac, and Volvo had that years ago. Mercedes is shipping it now. The big problem is keeping drivers from relying too much on a dumb system that almost works[1]. Everyone with a clue in the industry realizes the problem. Semi-automatic driving, with the expectation that the driver is alert and ready to take over, is an accident waiting to happen. Mercedes has a device to check if hands are on the wheel. (See video below for someone defeating that.) Cadillac has a seat vibrator. Most of those semi-auto systems are unable to detect junk such as a mattress on the highway, deer, pedestrians, and work crews. They assume a benign freeway environment. Most won't engage in a non-freeway environment. What bothers me about \"Cruise\" is that they promote this assist technology as a full self-driving system, like what Google and CMU/Cadillac have in test. See the Cruise ad.[2] The NTSB defines four levels of self-driving: 0 - manual 1 - Function-specific Automation (example: anti-collision radar braking.) 2 - Combined Function Automation (adaptive cruise control plus lane centering.) (current Mercedes, etc. level) 3 - Limited Self-Driving Automation (still needs a driver available to take over.) (current Google level) 4 - Full self-driving automation (driver does nothing, may not even be present) (Google is trying to get there for 25MPH cars.) Cruise is at level 2, but advertises as if at leve"}]},"LRq_SAuQDec":{"wy":"HN uses it as a recurring joke reference whenever threads feature annoying, over-eager smart devices and AI products.","m":[{"id":48426051,"author":"dghf","month":"2026-06","story":"\"They're made out of weights\"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Apologies, didn't notice when I got the link from YT's \"Share\" button. Won't make that mistake again. Comment is now to old to edit: for convenience, here's the link without the parameter -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":22110056,"author":"simcop2387","month":"2020-01","story":"WordPad Is Gettings Ads in Windows 10","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The Sci-Fi comedy Red Dwarf has a toaster that does essentially this","text":"It really does get as annoying as you'd expect. The Sci-Fi comedy Red Dwarf has a toaster that does essentially this, [VIDEO]"},{"id":49211674,"author":"noir_lord","month":"2026-08","story":"AI psychosis is the new leadership blind spot","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"Would you like fries with that\", having already said you do not want any fries Prescient in all the best ways - [VIDEO]"},{"id":48395889,"author":"dghf","month":"2026-06","story":"\"They're made out of weights\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> a compelling story about a sentient toaster \"Howdy-doodly-doo! Anybody like any toast?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":48396466,"author":"lloeki","month":"2026-06","story":"\"They're made out of weights\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Writing a story in which a toaster in conscious is not even new Fixed the link for you: [VIDEO] (Hah. It gets eerily relevant starting at 2:37)"},{"id":46895283,"author":"6510","month":"2026-02","story":"Microsoft's Pivotal AI Product Is Running into Big Problems","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The mistake is simple. It is like the difference between giving you many tools to use vs making you the tool. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44808039,"author":"Twirrim","month":"2025-08","story":"Show HN: Kitten TTS – 25MB CPU-Only, Open-Source TTS Model","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I don't expect a smart toaster to talk like a BBC host; Well sure, the BBC have already established that it's supposed to sound like a brit doing an impersonation of an American: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44625594,"author":"pgaddict","month":"2025-07","story":"Death by AI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The toaster mention reminded me of this: [VIDEO] This is how \"talking to AI\" feels like for anything mildly complex."},{"id":41350652,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2024-08","story":"Apple explores robotics in search of life beyond the iPhone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Very few companies survive Robotics projects, as the public has decades of unrealistic expectations from entertainment media. If the plan is an unconstrained general purpose use-case design, than the reality of the processing power needed to do simple tasks will quickly hit the product. If the board is serious, than just purchase the universal-robots.com company. Then add whatever branding and software you thought was useful on top of the existing platform. Making a profitable Robotics company is hard, as they are not generally a consumer product. I'd try Apple branded appliances, and you know people would buy a talking toaster: [VIDEO] Best of luck =)"},{"id":36764305,"author":"BizarroLand","month":"2023-07","story":"Every time you click this link, it will send you to a random Web 1.0 website","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Be the change you want to see in the world, look for an LLM Asic and build your own talkie toaster. [VIDEO] Of course, don't be surprised when we gather up the pitchforks and come for you"},{"id":30150138,"author":"digitalsankhara","month":"2022-01","story":"My dishwasher won’t start until I let it update its firmware over the WiFi","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"As much as I love technology, I have my personal limits and can relate to your post. Whenever this subject comes up, I'm reminded of this (Red Dwarf); [VIDEO]"},{"id":25225304,"author":"chris_wot","month":"2020-11","story":"Due to a problem with AWS, many \"smart\" vacuums or doorbells will stop working","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I go the middle ground. I get a toaster that toasts with a dial that goes from 1 to 10, and set it to 4 for my toast. Result: toast goodness. I don't need a toaster like this one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18758008,"author":"mosiuerbarso","month":"2018-12","story":"“Genuine people personalities” are coming to our gadgets?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"We'll end up with toasters like this: [VIDEO] \"But i'm a toaster. It is my raison d'être. I toast, therefore I am!\"﻿"},{"id":16514803,"author":"RobertoG","month":"2018-03","story":"Machine learning algorithms used to decode and enhance human memory","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">> \"we're going to have AI toasters by the end of the year.\" I hope not. It seems they can be a real pain in the ass: Red Dwarf toaster: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15774066,"author":"erasemus","month":"2017-11","story":"How the sandwich consumed Britain","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Bonus bready humour","text":"Check out Paul Robinson's comment for a general refutation. Given the popularity of 'artisanal'-type portable food preparations in both the UK and the US I think it unlikely the market could get away with such standards even at the low-end. I find your comment valuable though for the insight that bread products may be cut by waterjets: [LINK] This is astonishing: surely sogginess ensues? Bonus bready humour: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10659527,"author":"DonaldFisk","month":"2015-12","story":"The Object-Oriented Toaster (1997)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Does anyone want any toast?","text":"It could have been even worse. At least it didn't have ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE SYSTEM K177. Does anyone want any toast? - [VIDEO]"},{"id":6629556,"author":"lttlrck","month":"2013-10","story":"Russia: Hidden chips 'launch spam attacks from irons'","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"A computerized Dualit [1] would be awesome especially if all it did was impersonate Talkie Toaster [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"OobLb1McxnI":{"wy":"HN uses it as the canonical reference that modern connected cars can be attacked remotely.","q":{"x":"the software architecture of the Tesla was light-years ahead of what Jeep","i":17836610,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":36944629,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2023-07","story":"FBI: AI Makes It Easier for Hackers to Generate Attacks","stance":"reference","why":"Researchers took control of a connected car.","quote":"It's been done by technically advanced researchers [2]","text":"I believe they should have instead phrased it something along the line of \" AI facilitates a new generation of Skiddies [1] lowering the bar even further to malicious behavior without much technical knowledge.\" or something along that line. They're not wrong. Now a person with little or no technical knowledge can ask an LLM in plain English how to generate a more complex script that does {x+y+z} vs. at least having to know some degree of a scripting language or being limited to one of the well known and defended against skiddie tools that only perform {x}. A theoretical example would be a low-tech person taking full control of an internet connected car on a live highway. It's been done by technically advanced researchers [2] but now the bar to entry is now a lot lower. [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO] [video][46 mins]"},{"id":29461279,"author":"l-p","month":"2021-12","story":"No Easter Eggs in Curl","stance":"reference","why":"Miller and Valasek showed cars can be attacked remotely.","quote":"cf. the works of Charlie Miller and Chris Valasek.","text":"Yes because it'll load an image from a domain that expired and is now controlled by a nefarious third-party. The image is now a payload targeting the out of date image loading lib used by the onboard entertainment system that has seen no updates for 5 years. This entertainment system is connected to the actual driving electronics of the car that will now brake at full force the next time it reaches 130 km/h. This scenario is fictional, but possible. cf. the works of Charlie Miller and Chris Valasek. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17836610,"author":"bradleybuda","month":"2018-08","story":"Former Tesla Firmware Engineer Discusses the System","stance":"reference","why":"Jeep hacking talk at DEF CON 23.","quote":"At DEF CON 23 there were two car-hacking talks that made an impression on me","text":"At DEF CON 23 there were two car-hacking talks that made an impression on me: one about hacking a Tesla [1] and one about hacking a Jeep [2]. FWIW, I came away thinking that the software architecture of the Tesla was light-years ahead of what Jeep (and presumably many other legacy manufacturers) was shipping: [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15802428,"author":"michaelmcmillan","month":"2017-11","story":"macOS High Sierra: Anyone can login as “root” with empty password","stance":"reference","why":"Remote exploit of an unaltered passenger vehicle.","quote":"explain this","text":"\"Apple root bug appears to be triggered only by logins coming from com.apple.loginwindow. Running \"su\" with a blank password won't get you a root shell.\" [1] As for your subsystem argument, explain this: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":12476342,"author":"patcheudor","month":"2016-09","story":"Upgrading Autopilot: Seeing the World in Radar","stance":"reference","why":"Remote exploitation of a vehicle by Miller and Valasek.","quote":"","text":"In terms of the SDR stuff, I'm thinking more about it being in the hands of a threat actor. One of the things that Tesla nor Google have had to contend with to date in their trials are intentional disruption and subversion of their systems and I only hope they've hired security engineers to consider more than just IT stack vulnerabilities like those explored by Charlie Miller & Chris Valasek ( [VIDEO] ). A great number of jobs are at risk from this technology from long haul truck drivers to bus and cab drivers. This will certainly result in tensions which could result in threat actors going after sensing systems. Readily available radar frequency SDR in those conditions really could be used for harm."}]},"RS27u6IqWt0":{"wy":"Used as an anecdote of an old person's views on technological change, such as the telegraph as a distraction.","m":[{"id":35480779,"author":"komali2","month":"2023-04","story":"Bob Lee, Former CTO Square, Creator of CashApp, Stabbed to Death in SF","stance":"reference","why":"A 1920s farmer as example of intuition-based belief.","quote":"maybe instead let's consider a detached farmer in 1920. How about this guy","text":"If my tone was insulting I apologize. I am angry though, you're right. I'm watching the predictable cycle. A similar anger after 9/11 because of the tragedy but also because I knew it would be exploited to erode our freedoms, and lo, I was right (I'm not special, lots were warning this would happen). Same here. People are ignoring the research that highly punitive retributive justice systems don't work, and reformative justice systems do, because of some combination of yellow journalism and cognitive / rhetorical fallacy. > Studies are not the arbiters of truth. Certainly not all truths, and I agree with you that we can't expect people to fact check literally every aspect of their lives. However, your flat earth example is fantastic, here's why: the earth being flat is common sense. If you don't fly (many people) and have never seen the ocean (many people), don't think much about orbits or astronomy, why would you think the earth is round? There's a reason flat earthism is so popular, it really does \"make good horse sense.\" Grant me for a moment that to believe the earth is flat in 2023 requires believing in a massive global conspiracy, maybe instead let's consider a detached farmer in 1920. How about this guy: [VIDEO] . Consider what's necessary to know with certainty that the earth is a globe, and consider how they disagree with \"common sense.\" A flat earth would allow for seeing all the way to the end of it, right, so since you can't see all the way to new York city from h"},{"id":28735367,"author":"haunter","month":"2021-10","story":"Farmer born in 1842 talks about life and change (1929) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier upload without subtitles showing the archive's watermark.","quote":"an earlier upload w/o the subtitles have the","text":"Should be real Googled around and an earlier upload w/o the subtitles have the \"MIRC @ SC.EDU\" watermark [VIDEO] It's the Moving Image Research Collections at the University of South Carolina [LINK] I can't access the library catalog tho for some reason. Trying to find it in the online collection."},{"id":35878194,"author":"williamcotton","month":"2023-05","story":"Students can’t get off their phones. Schools have had enough","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Gotta love this guy born in the 1840s complaining about the telegraph being nothing more than a distraction","text":"Gotta love this guy born in the 1840s complaining about the telegraph being nothing more than a distraction: [VIDEO]"}]},"V9jlo4Ht2YA":{"wy":"Seattle-area commenters bring it up as a nostalgic local joke when area codes or Eastside versus Seattle rivalry come up.","q":{"x":"Reminds me of this classic Almost Live segment about the 425 area code switch over","i":19505612,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":48390581,"author":"dabockster","month":"2026-06","story":"Microsoft's MAI-Code-1-Flash Scores 51% SWE-Bench Pro with Just 5B Active Params","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Qwen HAS to be a part of the discussion here, even though Microsoft is a US based entity. Their 30b MoE models absolutely hit way above their weight when paired with the right harness program, and can be ran on \"Costco gaming computer\" specs when configured correctly in llama.cpp. Sorry Trump Administration, but while the US has been downloading more ram by throwing data centers at everything and burning up everyone's power and water, China has come out with what's effectively a prototype edge compute capable AI model - regardless of how they built it. And arguably I can tokenmaxx on it just fine at around 30-40 tokens/sec. And also, ASICs are on the way. Imagine one of those with a heavy hitting model (MoE or otherwise, Qwen or otherwise) installed in a PCIe slot at 10k+ tokens/sec and 75 watts max (maximum wattage deliverable by the PCIe slot alone) for $300-400 USD each. [LINK] ASIC demo here: [LINK] Sorry/not sorry to rip this whole thing to shreds. But I'm sick and tired of these inefficient LLMs being produced that seemingly can only be offered by subscription from a data center, when I'm running a full AI stack right now (model and all) on my computer at home on a 750 watt max power supply. Microsoft really needs to get with the picture here and compete more with Qwen instead of just the US/EU entities. Sincerely, your neighbor down in Tacoma. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21779150,"author":"seanmcdirmid","month":"2019-12","story":"The More Gender Equality, the Fewer Women in STEM (2018)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"My favorite is the 425 area code skit, with a snobby lady (in character) complaining about Renton being in the same area code as Bellevue.","text":"My favorite is the 425 area code skit, with a snobby lady (in character) complaining about Renton being in the same area code as Bellevue. No one outside of the Seattle area would ever get that joke, however. [VIDEO] Done in the same style as the WSU drinking ban...another later classic: [LINK] Or Bill Nye as a street walking lawyer on Aurora avenue: [LINK] They put everything on YouTube. No need for a dvd :)"},{"id":19505612,"author":"seanmcdirmid","month":"2019-03","story":"‘Making Amazon look bad’: Microsoft supports a state tax on businesses","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of this classic Almost Live segment about the 425 area code switch over","text":"Reminds me of this classic Almost Live segment about the 425 area code switch over: [VIDEO] At the end, an east side Bellevue woman snobbishly complains about 425 containing Renton."},{"id":15520448,"author":"techsupporter","month":"2017-10","story":"“How Amazon Took Seattle's Soul”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Did you have to dial a '1' before?","text":"Relevant video links: \"OK guys let me see your Boeing employee IDs!\" - [LINK] \"You know the rules in Redmond. You're not allowed to play any kind of sports within FIVE MILES of the Microsoft campus except for hackey-sack.\" - [LINK] \"Whadda ya mean you don't want to practice the accordion?!\" - [LINK] \"You're entitled to have a crystal present at any interrogation...\" - [LINK] Not the same theme, but... \"Did you have to dial a '1' before?\" - [VIDEO]"}]},"XWPCE2tTLZQ":{"wy":"HN uses it only as a joke reference when talking about bad drivers or poor code.","m":[{"id":47096905,"author":"TZubiri","month":"2026-02","story":"NullFramework: A no-op framework (for Python)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I operate at just the right level of abstraction, anyone higher is a script kiddie and anyone lower is a toolchain developer. [VIDEO] That said, not sure about assembly, but there may be a case for prototyping in C before yielding to some python ex machina. Same thing with assembly, but I am a mere mortal and know my limits"},{"id":39774938,"author":"quesera","month":"2024-03","story":"Practical Railway-Oriented Pipelines in Ruby","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Attribution is not only for living people. You got the quote close enough for a plagiarism complaint. :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":22217823,"author":"perceptronas","month":"2020-02","story":"New Finnish study confirms that BMW and Audi owners drive like idiots","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Everyone who drives slower than me is an idiot, everyone who drives faster than me is a maniac [1] [1] - [VIDEO] (George Carlin)"}]},"XggxeuFDaDU":{"wy":"HN uses the footage as a visual illustration of resonance and oscillation growing until structural failure.","m":[{"id":48444723,"author":"cogman10","month":"2026-06","story":"Texas grid flags risks as data centers, crypto sites fail voltage tests","stance":"reference","why":"Bridge collapse as analogy for phase misalignment in electric grids.","quote":"","text":"I think an even better example is what happened to the Tacoma Bridge. You have an oscillating frequency and phase that needs to be precisely tuned in order for everything to work well. If something is out of sync wrong, you can end up with higher peaks, lower valleys, or flatter locations which can ultimately cause catastrophic failure. In the Tacoma example, the input frequency continued to add onto the bridges motion which ultimately caused it to destroy itself. In an electric grid, a misaligned phase can cause excessive spikes (imagine 480V when you expect 240V) or the generators to ultimately burn themselves out because 2 generators are fighting with each other, one trying to raise the voltage while another is trying to decrease the voltage. The really tricky thing is that load (particularly inductive or capacitive loads) look almost exactly like an unaligned generator. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37695767,"author":"chaps","month":"2023-09","story":"What if Planet 9 is a Primordial Black Hole? (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates oscillation building until structural failure.","quote":"","text":"The latter. Basically [1] but not as exaggerated. Think of things like screws shaking loose over time which leads to structural failure. Plus, no easy means to release that energy like you would when you're attached to a planet. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9463739,"author":"walterbell","month":"2015-04","story":"Interaction Rituals and the New Electronic Media (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of the Tacoma Narrows bridge collapse as example of resonance.","quote":"","text":"Thanks for the pointer to \"interaction ritual\" theory, which could be related with: • Time-oriented Art — poetry, music, film, dance, theatre • John Lilly's research with dolphins, leading to the \"Lilly Wave\" carrier frequency • Haptic technology, e.g. Apple Watch mediated pulse sensor/actuator • Resonance from wind stimulus, e.g. Tacoma Narrows bridge collapse, [VIDEO]"}]},"YE9rEQAGpLw":{"wy":"Cited as an example of simulating the full optical chain of a camera in physically based rendering.","q":{"x":"a guy who created a functional virtual camera in Blender with lenses and film layers.","i":47596410,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47596410,"author":"UltraSane","month":"2026-04","story":"4D Doom","stance":"reference","why":"Functional virtual camera with lenses and film layers","quote":"a guy who created a functional virtual camera in Blender with lenses and film layers.","text":"Reminds me a bit of a guy who created a functional virtual camera in Blender with lenses and film layers. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45114227,"author":"avereveard","month":"2025-09","story":"Physically based rendering from first principles","stance":"reference","why":"Simulating a virtual camera with Blender path tracing","quote":"creating a virtual camera and using blender path tracing to simulate light capture","text":"Related: creating a virtual camera and using blender path tracing to simulate light capture from a camera in virtual space [VIDEO]"},{"id":44688598,"author":"ooterness","month":"2025-07","story":"Games Look Bad: HDR and Tone Mapping (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Physics-based rendering modeling a film camera's emulsion layers","quote":"physics-based rendering that models the entire optical chain of a film camera, including the emulsion layers.","text":"The opposite extreme: physics-based rendering that models the entire optical chain of a film camera, including the emulsion layers. [VIDEO]"}]},"ZwY2E0hjGuU":{"wy":"Linked in nuclear power debates as a counterargument that the waste problem is unsolved.","m":[{"id":26301610,"author":"amai","month":"2021-03","story":"Why Nuclear Must Be Part Of The Energy Solution (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Linked to dispute that waste disposal is solved.","quote":"","text":"\"a Pulitzer Prize-winning author argues that nuclear is safer than most energy sources\" Since when are Pulitzer prize winners experts on nuclear energy? A sentence like this is shows he has absolutely no clue what he is talking about: \"Nuclear waste disposal, although a continuing political problem in the U.S., is not any longer a technological problem.\" see [VIDEO] \"A final complaint against nuclear power is that it costs too much. Whether or not nuclear power costs too much will ultimately be a matter for markets to decide, but there is no question that a full accounting of the external costs of different energy systems would find nuclear cheaper than coal or natural gas. \" Ok, see here [LINK] Nuclear is already the most expensive solution to produce electricity. Also we don't need artificial nuclear energy here on Earth. There is plenty of nuclear energy available for free from the sun."},{"id":24472392,"author":"amai","month":"2020-09","story":"Freiburg: Germany's futuristic city set in a forest","stance":"reference","why":"Linked when asking where the waste will be deposited.","quote":"And where will you deposit the nuclear waste?","text":"The parent commenters argument was based on zero reports so I think my comment was appropriate. But even your link shows for France in €/MWh in 2017: 20 Hydro power 43.24 Solar farms 50 Nuclear (with state-covered insurance costs) 60 Onshore wind 61 Natural gas turbines without CO2 capture 100 Nuclear EPR So nuclear is never cheaper than solar and only cheaper than wind if the state is covering the insurance cost! And your argument about nuclear ramping up and down at will only works, if you have enough Uranium available. Where do you buy your Uranium? How long will the Uranium reserves last? And where will you deposit the nuclear waste? [VIDEO]"},{"id":24146499,"author":"sp332","month":"2020-08","story":"Air pollution is worse than we thought","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as a reason to solve waste before building more.","quote":"Yes but it would be nice to actually surmount them before making more of the stuff.","text":"all of which are surmountable Yes but it would be nice to actually surmount them before making more of the stuff. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24329574,"author":"fluffything","month":"2020-08","story":"After 48 years, Democrats endorse nuclear energy in platform","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The problem with nuclear isn't whether you can build a safe plant, you can. The problem with nuclear is all the assumptions under which a perfectly safe plant is actually perfectly safe (proper maintenance, training, etc.). I don't believe those assumptions always hold in practice. At the end of the day, these plants are operated by people, and private companies, who have many interests beyond keeping the plant \"perfectly safe\" (like making money) and also operate under constraints that prevent them from actually keeping the plant \"perfectly safe\" even if they wanted to (like lack of money). The problem with nuclear isn't technological, but social. It allows a relatively small bunch of shitty people and companies to cause tremendous amounts of devastation. Remove people from the \"nuclear energy safety equation\" and I'm all in with nuclear. Telling me that new plants are perfectly safe as long as people and private companies don't do shit, reads like these plants aren't safe at all to me. --- And this is without taking into account all the unknown unknowns, like all the natural disasters we can't even predict or imagine, which all plant designs just assume cannot happen. Just claiming that modern nuclear plants are safe, for all potential world states is BS, because nobody can imagine, much less know, what all those potential world states are. Sure coal kills people, but what makes many think its safer than nuclear is that we understand how that happens, we understand where co"},{"id":24333930,"author":"Technetium","month":"2020-08","story":"After 48 years, Democrats endorse nuclear energy in platform","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among sources against nuclear power.","quote":"","text":"Funny how everyone is able to talk so much shit about nuclear being amazing and safe and all that, yet we've STILL not cleaned up the monstrous amount of TOXIC trash that has been produced by these plants. We have done nothing to stop creating more waste, and we have done nothing to deal with the toxic waste we have right now. To be honest I don't CARE if you want to call it safer when there is no direct proof of that, only proof of the contrary. There are hundreds of thousands of people still affected by major accidents, and the waste leftover from them worldwide. Trying to keep pushing nuclear for anything is an ignorant shortsighted choice. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":21993708,"author":"PeterStuer","month":"2020-01","story":"Germany is moving beyond nuclear power, but at what cost?","stance":"reference","why":"Linked to support the claim nuclear is not clean.","quote":"not only is nuclear not clean, the worst part is humans can't handle the required responsibility for this technology.","text":"The pro-nuclear lobby likes to pretend it offers a clean solution. I really want to believe, as it would make things easy. Sadly, not only is nuclear not clean, the worst part is humans can't handle the required responsibility for this technology. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22119645,"author":"beders","month":"2020-01","story":"Nuclear Power: Game over (2016) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in discussion of whether the waste problem is overstated.","quote":"","text":"Re the waste problem being overstated: [VIDEO] [LINK] Interesting tidbit: \"The German Atom Forum, comprised of all German nuclear power plant operators, intends to pay as little as possible for storage and rejected shouldering costs for the new site search. In their opinion´”there is no legal basis\" for them to pay and all costs should be \"financed by taxpayers.\" Also, how long did it take to clean up after Three Mile Island? (hint: the cost was estimated at $1b) Yes, Nuclear power plants for commercial power generation are done for and rightfully so. Too dangerous, too expensive. Not only building them but de-constructing them and dealing with the waste."},{"id":19413135,"author":"igor47","month":"2019-03","story":"Our oceans broke heat records in 2018: the consequences are catastrophic","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on the Yucca Mountain waste storage politics.","quote":"","text":"why should everyone agree with this? i respectfully disagree. first, project drawdown considers nuclear[1] a \"regret\" solution, because we have no story for dealing with nuclear waste. in the US specifically, until i see some movement on the yucca mountain [2] political clusterf*ck[3], i won't see nuclear as worth considering. also, energy generation is only 25% of \"our problems with co2\"[4]. agriculture and transit make up the bulk of the other 75%, and i don't think we're going to have nuclear trucks or cows any time soon. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":15110135,"author":"aluhut","month":"2017-08","story":"Where in Europe is electric car a good idea?","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver segment on nuclear waste offered against pro-nuclear argument.","quote":"John Oliver had a piece about that just last week","text":"> We never found a way to deal with the radioactive waste that coal plants emit into the air Sure we did. We close them down where ever it's possible. Just like we should do and do with nuclear power plants. > Nuclear is safer and better in every way even if you multiplied all the past disasters by ten. There really is no comparison. What a brilliant argument you have there. Oh wait. No it's not. You actually did not bring anything up to counter the problem caused by nuclear plants: nuclear waste. Just some juicy whataboutism. > its a political problem because humans have no sense of scale. People have enough sense of scale to realize that the waste will be there forever and while some are safely away from it, others can watch your rusty barrels go to hell and pollute the environment. Funny because John Oliver had a piece about that just last week: [VIDEO] What's good about that at all?!"}]},"_ZI_aEalijE":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke reply when a thread touches on secret groups or conspiratorial control.","m":[{"id":21540771,"author":"schappim","month":"2019-11","story":"Cooling off your Raspberry Pi 4","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Certainly, the key is hot air needs to be able to get away from the SoC. For my home lab, I don't bother with the fans/cases and instead just stack the Pis with brass spacers. I haven't had any issues yet with the ambient room temperature around 20-22ºC (68-71.6ºF for the stonecutters who keep the metric system down[1]). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11264702,"author":"GFK_of_xmaspast","month":"2016-03","story":"Eliezer Yudkowsky on the Singularity, Bayesian Brains and Closet Goblins","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> The reason they don't have roadmaps to AGI is because they do not want AGI to be made before the Friendly AI problem has been solved Reminds me of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7049349,"author":"maxcan","month":"2014-01","story":"Twice as fast as Concorde: Supersonic jet will fly from London to NY in 2 hours","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"s/flown/ridden/. FTFY. I'm going to guess you weren't at the controls. Also, fair point. I have personally been holding back private supersonic travel for decades. I killed the 2707 a few years before my birth and worse than that, I personally lifted 2 billion Indians and Chinese out of poverty solely to cause demand for fuel (and thus prices) to skyrocket so that in case anyone managed to build an SST, it would be uneconomical. I'm the inspiration for [VIDEO] P.S. I'm a pilot who flies (not just rides) planes and I'd love to fly or ride this thing, doesn't mean it will ever happen."}]},"aRrDsbUdY_k":{"wy":"Referenced as a quick, entertaining overview of problems with credit reporting and scoring.","q":{"x":"John Oliver did a bit on the whole credit scoring system scam","i":11626281,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12474229,"author":"codegeek","month":"2016-09","story":"The careless errors of credit reporting agencies","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Some steps to monitor your credit (USA): 1. Signup for [LINK] . Yes, you can argue about giving your credentials to a 3rd party company and they show you ads but credit karma sends me real time activity on my credit report FOR FREE. I get offers etc but I am happy since I can keep an eye on my credit report from all agencies. (except Experian who want you to pay them for report). 2. Get a free credit report once a year from [LINK] 3. Get your FICO score from discover [0] for free OR Open a credit card with Citi cards [1] and I think for $95/Year fee, they give you free FICO score which is the score that majority of lenders use. There are many other companies that offer FICO score for a fee but this was a good way for me since I needed that type of card anyway. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] 4. Check your credit card and bank statements every few days. I do it once a week. It is a good habit. For this step, do it manually. Don't rely on mint or tools like that. Check the transactions manually. 5. Setup alerts with your bank/credit card for transactions with a certain threshold. For example, I have alerts for anything over $10. For under $10, see Step 4. This is another step in case you do have a fraud. 5. Don't give out your SSN or credit information to anyone unless absolutely sure. Never give these on a phone call with any company unless you know that you called that company and they need the info (example: health insurance etc). 6. If you ever get a call from a Collection Agency, do"},{"id":11626281,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2016-05","story":"HP Unveils Premium Chromebook: 3K Display, Intel Core M, 16 GB of RAM and USB-C","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on the credit scoring system.","quote":"John Oliver did a bit on the whole credit scoring system scam","text":"Some employers will even decide who to hire based on their credit score. Bad credit = can't get a job. John Oliver did a bit on the whole credit scoring system scam [VIDEO]"},{"id":11554782,"author":"jrockway","month":"2016-04","story":"The Looting of ShapeShift","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver coverage of credit reporting problems.","quote":"John Oliver covered this recently.","text":"John Oliver covered this recently. Many background check agencies won't let you run a background check on yourself. [VIDEO]"}]},"aqz-KE-bpKQ":{"wy":"Used as a freely licensed sample video for demoing multimedia tools, and as a test file in discussions of video downloading and playback.","q":{"x":"always use Creative Commons licensed data to demo your multimedia tools.","i":24873742,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":47831200,"author":"corentin88","month":"2026-04","story":"Show HN: A drop-in, open-source replacement for YouTube embed iframes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's a static site that renders the youtube thumbnail + a red play button. When you click on it, it loads the youtube iframe. So it's basically deferring the cookie consent and all. We implemented it as a drop-in replacement: -- <iframe [VIDEO] ++ <iframe [VIDEO] Nothing else needed."},{"id":43323315,"author":"cantrecallmypwd","month":"2025-03","story":"YouTube DRM added on ALL videos with TV (TVHTML5) clients","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Okay, but: yt-dlp -f315 [VIDEO] [download] 100% of 1.27GiB in 00:00:52 at 24.85MiB/s Works fine with the version I had installed, 2025.02.19. I don't know if this perhaps affects web browsers playing on non-HDCP displays."},{"id":37119368,"author":"1vuio0pswjnm7","month":"2023-08","story":"They Bypass YouTube Video Download Throttling","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The post-data OP is sending is different. In the script I shared it is easy to test one post-body against another, or one key against another, and so on. Most folks seem to using an \"ANDROID\" client, not \"WEB\". In the example I use \"IOS\" client but it does not matter. There are many clients that will work. To get throttled videoplayback URLs with the \"WEB\" client like the OP is using, one does not need to make POST requests to /youtubei/v1/player. There are throttled videoplayback URLs in the HTML of the /watch?v= page. For example, curl -A \"\" -40s [VIDEO] -o [LINK] -n 's/\\\\u0026/\\&/g;/itag=22/p'"},{"id":37126681,"author":"1vuio0pswjnm7","month":"2023-08","story":"They Bypass YouTube Video Download Throttling","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"However, acquiring this key decompiling the mobile application, monitoring requests through a proxy or relying on values discovered by others.\" This is again not true. The key is in the HTML of every /watch?v= YouTube page. It's a public key; it's not hidden in any way. Further, it's possible, up until today at least, to use the \"WEB\" key with clientName \"ANDROID\" of \"IOS\" and receive unthrottled URLs. The key in the shell script is in fact the WEB key. The key for IOS is different. curl -40s [VIDEO] \\ |grep -o \\\"INNERTUBE_API_KEY...[^\\\"]*\\\""},{"id":25110251,"author":"bArray","month":"2020-11","story":"Ubuntu Mate","stance":"reference","why":"Freely licensed replacement for copyrighted screenshot.","quote":"Big Buck Bunny by the Blender Foundation could be a good replacement","text":"I would suggest they remove the screenshot of the Avengers movie given the amount of DMCA going on for such things... (loosely suggesting some open source software could be used for viewing pirated material.) Big Buck Bunny by the Blender Foundation could be a good replacement [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24873742,"author":"Kim_Bruning","month":"2020-10","story":"YouTube-dl has received a DMCA takedown from RIAA","stance":"reference","why":"Creative Commons demo video suitable for multimedia tools.","quote":"always use Creative Commons licensed data to demo your multimedia tools.","text":"Lesson learned: always use Creative Commons licensed data to demo your multimedia tools. Else people get the wrong idea. Some examples for youtube-dl might be: * [VIDEO] Big buck bunny * [LINK] Sintel * [LINK] Spring In fact, youtube actually allows you to filter by CC, so there's never a reason not to!"}]},"jrFv1O4dbqY":{"wy":"Posted as a light, funny aside in threads about declining search quality and the habit of appending 'reddit' to queries.","q":{"x":"For your amusement, a relevant music-video titled \"The current state of search engines\" [0]","i":40068302,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":49347056,"author":"ben_w","month":"2026-08","story":"The Amazon Tax","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Having to add \"reddit\" to get genuine results was already a meme 3 years back","text":"Google's search results defaulting to starting with an LLM; and by the power of SEO, so are many of the search results themselves. Having to add \"reddit\" to get genuine results was already a meme 3 years back: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43308171,"author":"ben_w","month":"2025-03","story":"SMBC Parts Ways with Hiveworks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> That would mean the death of at a minimum Meta (market cap $1.5 trln), most of Google (market cap $2.1 trln, lets say half would die). And nothing of value was lost. [VIDEO] [LINK] > So what, a couple trillion haircut on the US stock market, and more of a GDP hit than if the entire US military suddenly stopped getting funded. Not even. Market Cap is more like net worth than like GDP, GDP is more like annual revenue (both are still somewhat different, hence \"more like\") — Meta's global revenue is $164.5 billion, Alphabet's is $350.02 billion, so even if that's entirely in the USA (it isn't), and the money spent was purely positive-sum (it isn't), even combined that's less than the US military budget of $849.8 billion."},{"id":43212838,"author":"Terr_","month":"2025-02","story":"AI is killing some companies, yet others are thriving – let's look at the data","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"On a lighter note, a humorous critique that mentions the same mitigation","text":"> I find myself appending “Reddit” to the end of my searches a lot more often And even then, Reddit's \"new\" design is shit for usability, sine the actual text you were searching for is nowhere on the page. It's hidden somewhere behind one of a hundred \"click to see more\" interactions, some of which are nested, and some will cause a completely new new page load that erases your progress and makes you start over. (But look! Engagement metrics!) On a lighter note, a humorous critique that mentions the same mitigation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40068302,"author":"Terr_","month":"2024-04","story":"Reddit is taking over Google","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"For your amusement, a relevant music-video titled \"The current state of search engines\" [0]","text":"For your amusement, a relevant music-video titled \"The current state of search engines\" [0] Yes, I know about the quotes I know about the dash and OR It's all ass Google doesn't work... Anymore I have to add the word \"Reddit\" To every goddamn search to read Content made by humans Google doesn't work... Anymore [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40080625,"author":"Terr_","month":"2024-04","story":"AI Has Made Google Search So Bad People Are Moving to TikTok and Reddit","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"For your amusement, a relevant music-video titled \"The current state of search engines\" [0]","text":"[Recycled from related submission] For your amusement, a relevant music-video titled \"The current state of search engines\" [0] Yes, I know about the quotes I know about the dash and OR It's all ass Google doesn't work... Anymore I have to add the word \"Reddit\" To every goddamn search to read Content made by humans Google doesn't work... Anymore [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39041954,"author":"aquova","month":"2024-01","story":"Bing Gained Less Than 1% Market Share Since Adding Bing Chat","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"An artist I follow on Youtube put out this (comical) video regarding it","text":"While I don't think it's enough to drive people to a different product, the fact that Google search is noticeably worse than in prior years has definitely gained mainstream attention. The amount of ads and SEO junk has caused frustration, but the alternatives aren't well known for most people to bother to switch. An artist I follow on Youtube put out this (comical) video regarding it - [VIDEO]"},{"id":36898552,"author":"kermire","month":"2023-07","story":"Techdirt has been deleted from Bing and DuckDuckGo","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Saw an entertaining video about it yesterday that echoes how I feel when I google stuff","text":"Overall search quality has been declining on all search engines. Maybe there's too much spam. Saw an entertaining video about it yesterday that echoes how I feel when I google stuff: [VIDEO] . It's so hard to find content written by humans these days. Seems like only the top sites are being indexed."}]},"kaahx4hMxmw":{"wy":"HN invokes it as a quick reference in discussions of AI-generated content and indistinguishable AI output.","m":[{"id":48309813,"author":"littlecranky67","month":"2026-05","story":"YouTube to automatically label AI-generated videos","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"is such a perfect fit for the whole creative AI industry discussion","text":"One of my favorite dialogues from Westworld [0] is such a perfect fit for the whole creative AI industry discussion: Are you real? - If you can't tell, does it matter? [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31359179,"author":"humpday69","month":"2022-05","story":"DeepMind: A Generalist Agent","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Well if you can't tell, does it matter?","text":"> An erotic novelist has their human brain and human instincts to guide them in writing their work. An ML agent trained on all the erotic novels written, weighted by critical and popular success would might be quite capable of generating sequels, \"original\" stories, or even stories bespoke to each reader. Good Will Hunting suggests first-hand experience is irreducible: \"You can't tell me what it smells like in the Sistine Chapel.\" [LINK] To which Westworld counters: \"Well if you can't tell, does it matter?\" [VIDEO] I think the cowboys have it. For the moment though, it's still up to humans to decide how this plays out."},{"id":33952355,"author":"nextaccountic","month":"2022-12","story":"Should HN ban ChatGPT/generated responses?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"cue Westworld","text":"There's the quality of the written commentary (which is all that matters for anyone only reading, never posting on HN) and the quality of the engagement of people that do write comments (which include how much their learned, the emotions they had, and other less tangible stuff) I think HN is optimizing for the former quality aspects and not the latter. So in that sense, if you can't tell if it's written by a bot, does it matter? (cue Westworld [VIDEO] )"}]},"ovOx4_8ajZ8":{"wy":"HN points to it as credible, methodical benchmark data in Linux-versus-Windows gaming discussions.","q":{"x":"Gamer's Nexus (a reputable source with good methodology) has some numbers here","i":46093038,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48130688,"author":"zamalek","month":"2026-05","story":"Linux gaming is faster because Windows APIs are becoming Linux kernel features","stance":"reference","why":"Extensive Linux gaming benchmarks.","quote":"Gamer's Nexus has a pretty extensive benchmark video","text":"Windows and Linux trade punches in terms of overall experience. What I've seen around is that if Linux has worse FPS, it tends to have more consistent pacing (it generally has better pacing - but not always, I had to abandon Once Human). Gamer's Nexus has a pretty extensive benchmark video: [VIDEO] . They refuse to compare to Windows, and it kinda makes sense: if it's satisfactory on Linux for your demands then who cares what Windows can do? Here's a less professional, but direct comparison [LINK] . An extremely important thing to note: AMD GPU. I personally have no idea what NVIDIA is like, but it sounds like their drivers are still all over the place. And kernel-level anti-cheat doesn't work, though some (e.g. EAC) run in user mode if the developer allows it. Make sure to check ProtonDB for the games you care about. I have personally never had a good experience with Linux builds of games, so I just always use Proton now - but maybe I'm cursed because others have passionately disagreed with my experience. Either way, if a Linux game is broken/bad, try forcing it into Proton. I don't want to say, \"switch now\" because it still has rough edges in terms of gaming. Better for you to have a great experience and stick around, than hate it and leave for good. Only you can figure out if it needs more time to cook based on some very light (ProtonDB) research. I last used a Windows machine about a year ago, and I can say with confidence that the average desktop experience is significan"},{"id":46567882,"author":"MegaDeKay","month":"2026-01","story":"I replaced Windows with Linux and everything's going great","stance":"reference","why":"Gamers Nexus working on Linux gaming benchmarks with Level1Techs.","quote":"Gamers Nexus at 2.57M subscribers is working on Linux Gaming Benchmarks with help from Wendel at Level1Techs","text":"> What I think could really push Linux desktop forward is if various PC gaming influencers started doing content on how to game on Linux. Given that it is not just viable now but actively sometimes better than on Windows it would make for good content AND show people an alternative. Not exactly what you are looking for, but Gamers Nexus at 2.57M subscribers is working on Linux Gaming Benchmarks with help from Wendel at Level1Techs [0]. Steve bemoans the shitshow that Windows has become all the time. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46093038,"author":"Night_Thastus","month":"2025-11","story":"Bazzite: Operating System for Linux gaming","stance":"reference","why":"Reputable benchmarks showing Linux gaming progress and Nvidia frametime issues.","quote":"Gamer's Nexus (a reputable source with good methodology) has some numbers here","text":"For anyone interested in some gaming benchmarks, Gamer's Nexus (a reputable source with good methodology) has some numbers here: [VIDEO] Based on their results, it sounds like there's still quite a way to go Linux gaming/Proton (ie: very inconsistent frametimes on Nvidia hardware), but it's definitely been taking steps in the right direction."}]},"pQQTScuApWk":{"wy":"HN cites it constantly as evidence that modern IDE and LSP-style features existed in the early 90s.","q":{"x":"the first IDE to offer something like LSP was a C++ one actually, Energize C++.","i":31152212,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":48322692,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2026-05","story":"Let's compile Quake like it's 1997","stance":"reference","why":"Energize C++ demo, origin of XEmacs","quote":"it was born for Energize C++, in 1993!","text":"Many of those features were already available in MS-DOS and Windows 3.x IDEs from Borland for Turbo Pascal and C++. Which is why when I got into UNIX development felt like going into the stone age of development tools, thankfully XEmacs was already there. Which by the way, it was born for Energize C++, in 1993! [VIDEO] Also here is what NeXTSTEP development environment looked like, used for Quake tooling development, in a 1991 marketing video. [LINK] Which is why, I usually assert I cannot understand the nostalgia of CLI and TUI, being there at the time, and not being able to use some of these systems, due to the amount of money they required."},{"id":46036277,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-11","story":"Racket v9.0","stance":"reference","why":"Energize with incremental compilation and hot reload","quote":"See Energize C++, as Lucid was pivoting away from Common Lisp","text":"Back when C++ was becoming famous, my favourite programming language was Object Pascal, in the form of Turbo Pascal, having been introduced to it via TP 5.5 OOP mini booklet. Shortly thereafter Turbo Pascal 6 was released, and I got into Turbo Vision, followed by Turbo Pascal 1.5 for Windows 3.1, the year thereafter. I was a big Borland fan, thus when you got the whole stuff it was Object Pascal/C++, naturally C was there just because all C++ vendors started as C vendors. On Windows and OS/2 land, C++ IDEs shared a lot with Smalltalk and Xerox PARC ideas in developer experience, it wasn't the vi + command line + debuggers are for the weak kind of experience. See Energize C++, as Lucid was pivoting away from Common Lisp, with Cadillac what we would call a LSP nowadays, where you could do incrementatl compilation on method level and hot reload \"Lucid Energize Demo VHS 1993\" [VIDEO] [LINK] Or the Visual Age for C++ version 4, which introduced a database, image like system for doing C++ in workflows similar to Smalltalk. [LINK] [LINK] Then there is C++ Builder, still going on, even though the way Borland went down spoiled its market mindshare, [LINK] You're right altougth C++ was born on UNIX at Bell Labs there is that point of view, and also a reason why I always had much more fun with C++ across Mac OS, OS/2, Windows, BeOS, and Symbian, with their full stack frameworks and IDE tooling. However with time I moved into managed languages, application languages, where it is enough t"},{"id":37688795,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-09","story":"Interactive GCC (igcc) is a read-eval-print loop (REPL) for C/C++","stance":"reference","why":"Energize C++ interactive environment based on XEmacs","quote":"Energize C++, which came out from Lucid as they pivoted away from Lisp Machines","text":"Julia main focus is scientific computing, and they aren't doing that bad, [LINK] There have been other interactive environments for C++ that predate ROOT, but they were too resource intensive and the market killed them. Namely, Energize C++, which came out from Lucid as they pivoted away from Lisp Machines, applying the same kind of technology to C++, based on XEmacs. [VIDEO] [LINK] And the last version of Visual Age for C++, based on the Smalltalk development experience. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":31349946,"author":"lispm","month":"2022-05","story":"My story on “worse is better” (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Lucid's incremental C++ development system","quote":"a databased-backed incremental C++ development system for UNIX (called Energize).","text":"Yeah, that's more like it. RPG worked a lot to bring sophisticated Lisp to UNIX systems. He worked on bringing a standard OOP system to Lisp (incl. Lisp Machines, where Lucid (and he personally) collaborated with Xerox PARC and Symbolics to develop CLOS as a part of the ANSI Common Lisp standard). Then he tried to develop and market similar technology in the form of a databased-backed incremental C++ development system for UNIX (called Energize). [VIDEO] He hoped that the market for C++ environments was larger than the shrinking Lisp market and that C++ developers would want an interactive system with integrated code management in an object store. That did not went well."},{"id":31152212,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2022-04","story":"Why LSP?","stance":"reference","why":"XEmacs-based Energize environment from 1993","quote":"the first IDE to offer something like LSP was a C++ one actually, Energize C++.","text":"Nope, there were plenty of VS plugins like Visual Assist, and the first IDE to offer something like LSP was a C++ one actually, Energize C++. Here is the documentation to cadillac, [LINK] , and the respective demo of their XEmacs based environment from 1993. [VIDEO] Refactoring is one feature of IDEs, not the whole package. Still, here is your refactoring rubicon in Visual Age for C++ (1999), [LINK]"},{"id":28783916,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-10","story":"We moved from Pony to Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Benchmark for C++ IDE tooling","quote":"With VC++ and Unreal catching up with Lucid Energize[0], others will have to up their game.","text":"And in languages that try to replace C++, better offer nice IDE tooling as well. [LINK] [LINK] With VC++ and Unreal catching up with Lucid Energize[0], others will have to up their game. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":27601701,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-06","story":"My favourite C++ footgun","stance":"reference","why":"Early 90s C++ tooling demo","quote":"It was already possible in the early 90's.","text":"It was already possible in the early 90's. [LINK] \"Lucid Energize Demo VHS 1993\" [VIDEO] VisualAge C++ Version 4.0 [LINK] [LINK] However they were too resource hungry for the hardware of those days, then Java and .NET took over the spotlight, and until clang/LLVM came into the scene, the industry forgot of what was already possible."},{"id":25622823,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-01","story":"On Repl-Driven Programming","stance":"reference","why":"Lucid applying Lisp ideas to a C++ IDE","quote":"how Lucid used Lisp ideas for their Energize C++ IDE, including an image based format for AST storage","text":"Be prepared to be amazed with what Xerox PARC and others were doing, while Bell Labs was busy pushing for UNIX. \"Eric Bier Demonstrates Cedar\" [LINK] \"Emulating a Xerox Star (8010) Information System Running the Xerox Development Environment (XDE) 5.0\" [LINK] \"Documents as User Interfaces Video Demo\" [LINK] \"SYMBOLICS S-PACKAGES 3D GRAPHICS AND ANIMATION DEMO\" [LINK] \"Alto System Project: Dan Ingalls demonstrates Smalltalk\" [LINK] \"Action!, the worlds first dynamic interface builder - 1988\" (Interface Builder percursor, written in Lisp) [LINK] \"The Interlisp programming environment\" [LINK] And the cherry, how Lucid used Lisp ideas for their Energize C++ IDE, including an image based format for AST storage [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":25591387,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-12","story":"SBCL: New in Version 2.1.0","stance":"reference","why":"Early IDE features like live debugging in 1993 C++ tooling","quote":"Lucid Energize C++ tooling from 1993 already enjoyed some of these features","text":"autocompletions have been a feature in IDEs since Visual C++ was introduced, and copied by Java IDEs, and naturally Visual Studio for .NET languages as well. Introspection, graphical code navigation has been a thing in Apple MPW, Borland C++ for Windows, Visual Age for C++ (latter CSet++), C++ Builder, Eclipse, Visual Cafe, Visual Studio Enterprise. Live debugging and recompilation, edit-and-continue in Visual Studio, JVM class reload capabilities and JRebel JVM agents. REPLs, available as standard feature in Java since Java 9, prior to that there were IDE features like Eclipse Scrappages. Visual Basic interactive mode (classical VB), and nowadays F#, C# and VB.NET interactive shells on VS, including send-to-repl capability. Lucid Energize C++ tooling from 1993 already enjoyed some of these features: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24601384,"author":"self","month":"2020-09","story":"The Dark Secrets of Fast Compilation for Kotlin","stance":"reference","why":"Function-level incremental recompilation in Energize","quote":"Lucid Energize did it","text":"> (One could think of an even finer grained approach that would track changes in individual functions or classes and thus recompile even less than a file, but I’m not aware of practical implementations of such an approach in industrial languages, and altogether it doesn’t seem necessary.) Lucid Energize did it: [VIDEO] Incomplete source code: [LINK]"},{"id":23887849,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-07","story":"Turning the IDE Inside Out with Datalog","stance":"reference","why":"1993 marketing video showing Lucid's database-backed IDE","quote":"You can see it in action on this 1993 marketing video from Lucid,","text":"Rejuvenating that many devs are finally looking into this kind of workflows. For prior work on IDE as database check \"Energize/Cadillac & Lucid's Demise\", [LINK] You can see it in action on this 1993 marketing video from Lucid, [VIDEO] IBM also tried the same concept on Visual Age for C++, version 4, which was one of the very last versions of the product. [LINK] Both suffered from too heavy hardware requirements for what most companies were willing to pay for, otherwise we could have had Smalltalk like tooling, with incremental compilation and reloading for C++ already during the early 90's."},{"id":23889548,"author":"AlbertoGP","month":"2020-07","story":"Temple OS review by the Linus Tech Tip channel [video]","stance":"reference","why":"1990s IDE demo with hyperlinked debugger output","quote":"today I watched a demonstration of an IDE from the 1990s linked from another HN discussion","text":"The video is not bad but as I watched it when it came out I thought that they missed what for me is the most interesting part: how the console/debugger/ui combination is fully hyperlinked: [LINK] There are precedents of that though: from what I’ve seen in videos and read in manuals, the integrated LISP environments like Genera did have that much earlier. That was built by teams of people with way more resources than him. I think Terry Davis was right in that some valuable things have been lost in modern computing systems. BTW, today I watched a demonstration of an IDE from the 1990s linked from another HN discussion which also hyperlinked things like the debugger (gdb) output etc.: [VIDEO] It was the company that made Lucid Emacs (later XEmacs) which they built for this purpose: graphic elements combined with monospace text."},{"id":22458881,"author":"sedachv","month":"2020-03","story":"Miranda released as free software","stance":"reference","why":"Energize had incremental compilation and IDE features","quote":"Lucid Energize and IBM VisualAge C++ did incremental compilation and had IDE features not available with Free Software C++ compilers and editors/debuggers.","text":"Compare Symbolics Genera to a Free Software Common Lisp development environment today (SBCL and SLIME on GNU Emacs). The latter does a lot less and is more bloated. Commercial Common Lisp compilers like Allegro and LispWorks have features and optimizations that Free Software compilers lack (Allegro has really good debugging tools and garbage collection, LispWorks has excellent support for concurrent programming). Intel Fortran compiler and their C++ compiler produce the fastest code. Lucid Energize and IBM VisualAge C++ did incremental compilation and had IDE features not available with Free Software C++ compilers and editors/debuggers. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":22215787,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-02","story":"Rust Moving Towards an IDE-Friendly Compiler with Rust Analyzer","stance":"reference","why":"1993 sales demo of Lucid Energize C++","quote":"It would look like Lucid Energize for C++, here a 1993 sales demo","text":"It would look like Lucid Energize for C++, here a 1993 sales demo: [VIDEO] Or the version 4 of IBM's Visual Age for C++ Visual Age for C++ was quite impressive, the whole program was serialized into a database, an approach similar to Energize, and you could edit C++ code just like in a Smalltalk environment. Sadly not much of it has survived, so save pages like this while they exist [LINK] The problem with those two products was that they were ahead of their time, so with their hardware requirements, ended up being a commercial failure. Then for something more down to earth, there is C++ Builder, the only actual C++ RAD tooling, but their market is only deep pocket corporations. Think VB experience, but using C++ instead. [LINK] Finally there is VC++, which also supports edit-and-continue with incremental compilation and linking, and with C++/CX I thought it could eventually be like C++ Builder but was wrong. Also with the C++/WinRT reboot, the tooling is still playing catch up with C++/CX. However there is also the option to use interpreters like Cling. Then you also have the development experience of Common Lisp, Dylan and Eiffel. All of those have rich IDEs, with interpreter/JIT compilers for development, and then you can also AOT optimize for release builds. Eiffel to this day uses their JIT (oder MELT as they call it) for rapid development, and then relies on a C or C++ compiler for the release builds. Dylan while killed by Apple, had a short commercial life, and you can"},{"id":16875779,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-04","story":"Apple's investment into Clangd and refactoring tooling","stance":"reference","why":"Energize C++ with Emacs customization in 1993","quote":"And how it looked like with their Emacs customization in 1993 on UNIX.","text":"Here is how Lucid implemented their C++ knowledge repository for Energize C++. \"Foundation for a C++ Programming Environment\", chapter 7, Protocols [LINK] And how it looked like with their Emacs customization in 1993 on UNIX. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15723679,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-11","story":"Cquery: highly-scalable, low-latency language server for C++","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of early Lucid C++ IDE","quote":"Here is a demo on YouTube.","text":"It existed, but sadly both were too early for the market. The first one was from Lucid, after pivoting away from Lisp Machines. [LINK] Here is a demo on YouTube. [VIDEO] IBM also tried to create a Smalltalk style repository in Visual Age for C++ v4.0, which has quite resource hungry for mid-90's PCs. So we had to wait until the idea of compiler as library to catch on, to get those ideas back."},{"id":12704391,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2016-10","story":"Symbolics Lisp Machine Museum","stance":"reference","why":"1993 C++ environment built on Lucid Common Lisp infrastructure","quote":"well this was a C++ environment in 1993","text":"The point being that we have seen the future like Bret puts it, and are to certain extent disappointed that the industry keeps doing left turns. Many C++ users are finally enjoying environments like Clion and incremental compilers, well this was a C++ environment in 1993: [VIDEO] Built on top of Lucid Common Lisp infrastructure, as Lucid pivoted their business to C++. IBM had a similar one, that imported C++ into a database, offering a Smalltalk like development experience for C++, called VisualAge C++ Professional 4.0 [LINK] So yeah, we are grey hair dogs disappointed how things evolve."},{"id":11748822,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2016-05","story":"Cling: An Interactive C++ interpreter","stance":"reference","why":"Early 90s interactive C++ environment demo","quote":"there was an early attempt to provide C++ repls back in the early 90's with Lucid Energize C++","text":"Interactive programming is very developer friendly workflow, something that was already possible at Xerox PARC in their Smalltalk, Interlisp-D and Mesa/CEDAR environments. Actually there was an early attempt to provide C++ repls back in the early 90's with Lucid Energize C++, after they pivoted from Lisp Machines and applied their knowledge to C++. [VIDEO] Also the majority of CERN code is written in a mix of Fortran and C++, and not all teams like to use Python. So they rather use something that provides interactivity to research their algorithms and speed at the same time."},{"id":11749008,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2016-05","story":"Cling: An Interactive C++ interpreter","stance":"reference","why":"1993 demo of an advanced C++ IDE","quote":"Like Lucid Energize C++ in 1993 ?","text":"Like Lucid Energize C++ in 1993 ? [VIDEO] Or C++ Builder or C Set++ for OS/2 (might have the name wrong). If it wasn't for tool vendors having switched their focus from C++ to Java and .NET, until LLVM and clang got created showing everyone what was wrong with their approaches, C++ IDEs might have been much more advanced already, in spite of technical issues with language."},{"id":10919246,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2016-01","story":"Raspberry Pi Bare Metal Programming with Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Lucid Energize C++ IDE capabilities","quote":"IDE capabilities that probably only Lucid C++ had in the 90's","text":"I also only toyed with Ada. My real work experience with Algol like languages are Turbo Pascal, Delphi and Oberon. Although thanks to my interest into compiler development during my CS degree, I also do have some knowledge of almost all major Wirth influenced languages. Personally, I do prefer some verbosity to lines full of symbols. Ada 2012 is quite good. It is also remarkable that in the 80's Ada 83 as seen as almost impossible to implement and nowadays C++ is way complexer to implement than Ada 2012. Another anecdote, Rational started out in mid-80's as a startup doing Ada workstations[1] (think Ada machines), with IDE capabilities that probably only Lucid C++ had in the 90's [2]. [1] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"rie-9AEhYdY":{"wy":"Mostly cited in passing, e.g. regarding claims that transformers cannot do recursion and as an example of structured deep learning work.","m":[{"id":40564574,"author":"taliesinb","month":"2024-06","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (June 2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Company talk on structured deep learning with category theory","quote":"","text":"Symbolica.ai | London, Australia | REMOTE, INTERNS, VISA We're trying to apply the insights of category theory, dependent type theory, and functional programming to deep learning. How do we best equip neural nets with strong inductive biases from these fields to help them reason in a structured way? Our upcoming ICML paper gives some flavor [LINK] ; you can also watch [VIDEO] ; but there is a lot more to say. If you are fluent in 2 or more of { category theory, Haskell (/Idris/Agda/...), deep learning }, you'll probably have a lot of fun with us! Check out our open positions at [LINK]"},{"id":40334266,"author":"itchyjunk","month":"2024-05","story":"Energy, not compute, will be the #1 bottleneck to AI progress – Mark Zuckerberg [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of well-funded academic AI projects","quote":"","text":"Do you have any citation on lack of funding for foundational research? From the looks of things, it seems like there is insane amount of money in AI and while maybe majority of it goes to \"projects with high probability of success\", whatever that means, fringe ideas seem to generate good amount of capital as well. Even (what I would consider to be) academic projects seems to get good funding [0]. On top of that, it seems like you're not saying that Transformers might be a dead end but Neural Networks itself might be one. At this point, NNs already gives a lot of value. If someone's goal is to just get products out that say diffusion models already helps you achieve, how is that even a dead end? Do you mean dead end for AGI? In which case, there seems to be research on alternatives there as well? [1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":39989448,"author":"f_devd","month":"2024-04","story":"Transformer as a general purpose computer","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as claiming Transformers cannot do recursion","quote":"I thought it was proven Transformers cannot do recursion[0]","text":"Interesting, I thought it was proven Transformers cannot do recursion[0] and aren't actually able to be equivalent to a turing machine due to lack of expandable memory, and are rather finite state automata. [0]: [VIDEO]"}]},"tO5sxLapAts":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke shorthand for startups or plans with a missing middle step and no real business model.","q":{"x":"The South Park underwear gnomes joke was a parody of dot-com startups","i":10084746,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10084746,"author":"jackgavigan","month":"2015-08","story":"Source of the famous “Now you have two problems” quote (2006)","stance":"reference","why":"Parody of dot-com startups lacking business models","quote":"The South Park underwear gnomes joke was a parody of dot-com startups","text":"The South Park underwear gnomes joke was a parody of dot-com startups (i.e. around 1999/2000) who didn't have a clear business model. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27437925,"author":"cs702","month":"2021-06","story":"MoviePass settles FTC allegations that they limited usage, exposed user data","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It appears the MoviePass master plan for world domination was inspired by the underpants gnomes of South Park[a]","text":"It appears the MoviePass master plan for world domination was inspired by the underpants gnomes of South Park[a]: Step 1. Charge customers $10/month for unlimited passes to movie theaters Step 2. Change passwords to prevent customers from getting their passes Step 3. ??? -- [a] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18104278,"author":"cmroanirgo","month":"2018-09","story":"Posts on Google","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think it's the underpants gnomes profit model[0]","text":"I think it's the underpants gnomes profit model[0]: Step 1. Collect underpants Step 2. ??? Step 3. Profit [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6314574,"author":"unclebucknasty","month":"2013-09","story":"What did the tech CEO say to the worker he wanted to automate?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Step one is the automation of workers.","text":"> I think the argument about \"hobbies\" is a long term one, not one that applies to people who just got laid off. I think that's the reality, but I am not sure that people like White from the article really acknowledge this. That is, he trivializes it by kind of offhandedly suggesting that it is good for people to have these new options open up as a result of losing their jobs. But, in fact, what is required is a radical restructuring of our economy and society at large (including our ideas about work) in order to arrive at what he suggests. This is not a point that can simply be glossed over or assumed if he is being earnest or even remotely serious about addressing the automation issue. Reminds me of this: [VIDEO] . Step one is the automation of workers. Step three is this perfect utopian society, wherein we all just pursue our \"hobbies\"."},{"id":6354913,"author":"cpursley","month":"2013-09","story":"Low pay and the rise of the machines","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This all reminds me of a south park episode","text":"Came here to say the exact same thing; thanks for saying it better than I ever could. I don't get the basic income thing - pay people to not work? What? Where's the money come from? How to you prevent massive inflation and a massive decrease in economic output as people quit their jobs? Who decides who gets this basic income? This all reminds me of a south park episode: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5236439,"author":"rosenjon","month":"2013-02","story":"The “It” Crowd Doesn’t Like Me and I Finally Don’t Care","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If name dropping high profile people is an important part of your businesses marketing strategy, then it seems reasonable to pursue that <i>if it leads to more revenue/profit</i> I think OP's issue relates to the weird, self-referential culture of Silicon Valley, where success is measured in buzz and money raised. It goes something like: Build free software product -> ? -> profit where ? = Get lots of buzz/hype, grow userbase quickly, raise VC money Or course, this is similar to another business plan: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5041307,"author":"orionblastar","month":"2013-01","story":"Beware of Startup Sharks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Phase 1: Write Code Phase 2: ? Phase 3: Profit!","text":"Many Programmers think all they can do to be a success is just to write code and nothing else. Those who think that fall victim to the startup shark. There is more to a startup than just writing code, otherwise you just have an Underpants Gnome business plan. [VIDEO] Phase 1: Write Code Phase 2: ? Phase 3: Profit!"},{"id":7458671,"author":"wiradikusuma","month":"2014-03","story":"Wanelo: The First Million Users","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Call me skeptical, but it reminds me of","text":"Call me skeptical, but it reminds me of [VIDEO] Like other commenters have said, how did she, being a non-technical founder with no team, could set up the (what appears to be custom-made) website? It's like going to a hackathon and claim that you build an app within 24 hours, but actually have implemented majority of it beforehand and making it a \"library\". Anyway, looking at the positive side, had the slides contain more detail, I believe it can be inspiring for many people and not received with skepticisms."}]},"v-n1vGeVIXo":{"wy":"Cited only as a running joke, usually when someone brings up the word or the Charlie Kaufman film title.","m":[{"id":47682407,"author":"wincy","month":"2026-04","story":"A truck driver spent 20 years making a scale model of every building in NYC","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"someone had pulled a very funny prank from an earlier, less serious time on YouTube","text":"A little off topic, but any time I see that word, it reminds me of the first time I read the word “synechdoche”, I wanted to know how to pronounce it and watched a very helpful YouTube video [0] three times before realizing someone had pulled a very funny prank from an earlier, less serious time on YouTube. I laughed and laughed. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38955595,"author":"wincy","month":"2024-01","story":"Scammy AI-Generated Books Are Flooding Amazon","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of when I was trying to figure out the pronunciation for “synedoche” and got this as the top YouTube results. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34324528,"author":"j33zusjuice","month":"2023-01","story":"Show HN: Python framework is faster than Golang Fiber","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Did you look up how to pronounce it? I was mispronouncing it for like a year after seeing “Synecdoche New York” (it’s kinda pretentious, not for everyone). My coworker laughed at me when I said the name to him. I’d only ever seen it written, and did not look at a pronunciation chart. Wah wah. Just in case anyone else is getting it wrong like I was … [VIDEO] (But really, [LINK] )"},{"id":29085435,"author":"Imnimo","month":"2021-11","story":"Use YouTube to improve your English pronunciation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I looked up \"synecdoche\" (to see if it would give me this video [VIDEO] ), and a few of the examples are of people saying \"Schenectady\" but mis-transcribed. I guess you can just use your judgment to filter out a few mistakes, so no big deal."}]},"w2itwFJCgFQ":{"wy":"HN cites it for the live demo of multicopters flying with fewer working rotors.","q":{"x":"Here's a Ted Talk with a live demo from 2013","i":28984658,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28984658,"author":"cronix","month":"2021-10","story":"Jetson One – Personal Electric Aerial Vehicle","stance":"reference","why":"Live demo of quads flying with fewer rotors","quote":"Here's a Ted Talk with a live demo from 2013","text":"I'm kind of surprised they use only 4. There are drones with 6 and 8 rotors, which can still fly if 1 fails, which they use in the film industry to fly heavier, more expensive cameras. There are also ways to get quads to fly on less than 4 rotors if one becomes damaged, although as a human I'm not sure I'd want to be in one doing it. Here's a Ted Talk with a live demo from 2013: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25109227,"author":"carlhjerpe","month":"2020-11","story":"Electrified wingsuit from BMW reaches 186MPH on first flight","stance":"reference","why":"Multicopter flying with disabled props","quote":"See this[1] video for an example of a multicopter flying with disabled props.","text":"Absolutely, as long as they're operating within parameters there's no failing them. I've been running brushless systems in RC cars for the last 7 odd years. The only motor failures has been when It's been subject to extreme abuse, as in the rotor reaching 100+° (internally), going from ~55-60K RPM to 0 in fraction of a second or the rotor being forced through the bearings. For unmanned flight i do agree on multicopters being the best option. See this[1] video for an example of a multicopter flying with disabled props. However, as you see this flightmode isn't appropriate for human beings, humans should keep flying helis for now. The swash plate design even though more complex than a prop mounted on a shaft is still a rather simple design, litterally a bearing mounted on a 2 axis system and rods attached to rotate the blades. Multicopters with swash plates are interesting too as they can be simplified not to tilt the plate but only offset it up and down to alter pitch the same way all around the rotation. If. This exerts less force on the bearing, but considering there's still 4 of them rather than one might increse failure rate. With swash plates on multicopters we could have two motors driving one system that drives the props, and do many amazing things with computer aided controls in case of failure. Idk why i keep on going, TL;DR: Being able to control pitch is key, multirotor or not. I'm not really with Elon Musk on alot of things, but personal human flight is one thing w"},{"id":9655487,"author":"graycat","month":"2015-06","story":"Show HN: Differential Equations Explained","stance":"reference","why":"Video appears incidental to a differential equations discussion","quote":"","text":"Ordinary differential equations? Okay: Just freshman calculus actually is a good start. E.g., for viral growth consider y'(t) = k y(t) ( b - y(t) ) where t is time, y(t) the size at time t, and the rate of growth y'(t) = d/dt y(t) is proportional to both the current size y(t) and the size ( b - y(t) ) yet to be achieved. So, e.g., the growth rate is proportional to the number of present customers talking y(t) and the number of potential customers listening ( b - y(t) ). We assume that the present is time t = 0 and we have the eventual size b and the current size y(0). Then, sure, we have an initial value problem (that is, we know y(0)) for a first order, linear ordinary differential equation. But all that is needed for a solution is just freshman calculus. It's just a routine exercise. Trivial? Once that equation kept FedEx from going out of business. Polished, elegant, insightful, balanced, expert, great first text: Earl A. Coddington, 'An Introduction to Ordinary Differential Equations', Prentice-Hall, Englewood Cliffs, NJ, 1961. For when you want to take some really big next steps up past nearly everyone else in mathematics: Earl A. Coddington and Norman Levinson, 'Theory of Ordinary Differential Equations', McGraw-Hill, New York, 1955. For one of the larger reasons to be interested in ordinary differential equations: Michael Athans and Peter L. Falb, 'Optimal Control: An Introduction to the Theory and Its Applications', McGraw-Hill Book Company, New York, 1966. Also E. B."}]},"wE3fmFTtP9g":{"wy":"Used as a reference point when comparing other legged robots, such as the MIT Cheetah, on gait and speed.","q":{"x":"it looks more natural and more balanced with the legs out of sync","i":9624717,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10028532,"author":"minot","month":"2015-08","story":"Japanese mini Segway “WalkCar”","stance":"reference","why":"Boston Dynamics heavy-load robot.","quote":"","text":"Is this where Boston Robotics comes in? For heavy load, they have wild cat[wildcat] and for lighter loads, they have spot [spot]. For Ussain Bolt, there is the cheetah [cheetah] that can keep up with the pace peaking at over 29 miles per hour. All these are at various stages in development and as far as I understand not ready for production. spot [VIDEO] wildcat [VIDEO] cheetah [LINK]"},{"id":9624717,"author":"jmilloy","month":"2015-05","story":"MIT cheetah robot lands the running jump [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows more natural gait with legs out of sync.","quote":"it looks more natural and more balanced with the legs out of sync","text":"I noticed the opposite in this one: [VIDEO] Which is to say, it looks more natural and more balanced with the legs out of sync."},{"id":8319783,"author":"CoffeeDregs","month":"2014-09","story":"MIT Cheetah Robot Bounds Off Tether, Outdoors","stance":"reference","why":"Comparison point for the MIT Cheetah robot.","quote":"","text":"The bounding part is very cool (I caught myself leaning forward in surprise [and mild horror?] as the machine jumped over the box instead of maneuvering around it). Besides not requiring fuel, does anyone know how this is different/better/unique than Google/BostonDynamic's WildCage? ( [VIDEO] ) I certainly agree that more people working on this is a good thing, but I'd like to understand the progress being made."},{"id":6512061,"author":"hanley","month":"2013-10","story":"Find out how many social shares a URL has?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"If I plug in a YouTube link will it also tally all of the YouTube link variations for that video? For example, if I put in [VIDEO] , will it also include the shortened [VIDEO] in the tally?"}]},"wRnSnfiUI54":{"wy":"Used as the source of the famous scream, either as a joke or as the reference for Khan-themed projects.","m":[{"id":6788839,"author":"mcescalante","month":"2013-11","story":"Show HN: Khan as a Service","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the Khan scream","quote":"Youtube link of the \"kaaaahn\" exclamation","text":"For anybody confused, this is from Spock's death scene in Star Trek II: The Wrath of Kahn. Youtube link of the \"kaaaahn\" exclamation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37526074,"author":"readthenotes1","month":"2023-09","story":"Human trials of artificial wombs could start soon. Here’s what you need to know","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've just been re-reading Cyteen. This, plus the recent news of the \"model\" fetus and the old news of CRISPR, means we're in for some interesting times. Khan!Khaaaan!! [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":2232601,"author":"Stormbringer","month":"2011-02","story":"Last.fm co-founder: Apple just fucked over online music subs for the iPhone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Damn those evil bastards at Apple for sending you the music in a high quality format. /shakes fist at sky/ JJOOOOOOOOBBBBBSSSSSSSS!!!!! (edit: for the kind souls burying me, see also: [VIDEO]"}]},"z4FGzE4endQ":{"wy":"Linked as a joke whenever VR interfaces or headsets are discussed.","m":[{"id":42678838,"author":"BlueTemplar","month":"2025-01","story":"Microsoft Bob: Microsoft's biggest flop of the 1990s","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oh wow, I just realized that the following VR parody might have been inspired by Microsoft Bob : [VIDEO] (2015, so 4 years before Zuckerberg committed to the Metaverse enough to rename the whole company, and still 1 year before the first commercial new generation VR headset : Oculus Rift released.)"},{"id":36224863,"author":"sebzim4500","month":"2023-06","story":"Notes on Vision Pro","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">- Something whimsical to inspire developers. Give me files represented as physical blocks that I can pile, or a task manager that shows processes as plants around me. Close apps by scrunching their screen like paper. A globe with my geotagged pictures. I respect Apple for not doing this. These are demos that are kind of fun for 20 minutes but you would never want to actually use a device like this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33569908,"author":"chadcmulligan","month":"2022-11","story":"The 1993 Murder She Wrote VR episode (2014)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ok then, the community VR episode [VIDEO] \"The font is larger! and Jesus wept for there were no more worlds to conquer.\""},{"id":24691570,"author":"SketchySeaBeast","month":"2020-10","story":"Kube Doom, Kill Kubernetes Pods Using Id's Doom","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant community commentary on the potential ridiculousness of VR interfaces","text":"That's really all I ask. I doubt very much that any movement in virtual space is going to be as efficient as a keyboard and mouse, so just let me see everything and call it a day. Relevant community commentary on the potential ridiculousness of VR interfaces: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24369880,"author":"CarbyAu","month":"2020-09","story":"Intel Launches 11th Gen Core Tiger Lake","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"No I had not! It seems like what I was thinking. Take the same idea and put it into Unreal Engine. Add further OS interactions like files, maybe a floating virtual window for web browsing etc. The UX has to be done very well though otherwise you are a prediction by TV show Community: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22501863,"author":"verytrivial","month":"2020-03","story":"Show HN: First person 3D environment using CSS 3D Transforms for rendering","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"anyone who hasn't seen the","text":"So close! Enticing checkerboard pattern and some unshaded wood texturing then a hard crash for Firefox Android 68.5.0. Evokes the early 90s which reminds me that anyone who hasn't seen the \"Community\" episode with the VR parody must watch it immediately: [VIDEO] (this seems to be a super-cut which will annoy the writers, bit still)"}]}});
